      "analysis": {
        "exploitability_evidence": "The flagged sink is stmt.executeUpdate(sql) inside the helper method executeSQLCommand(String sql) in DatabaseHelper.java. The method itself performs no string formatting or concatenation - it simply forwards whatever 'sql' string it receives to a raw java.sql.Statement (not a PreparedStatement). No caller of executeSQLCommand is shown in the provided context, so the actual origin of the 'sql' argument (e.g., whether it is built from hard-coded literals or concatenated with servlet request parameters) cannot be determined from the code shown. There is no sanitizer or validator visible on this path, but there is also no confirmed attacker-controlled source reaching this parameter in the given snippet.",
        "verdict": "Inconclusive",
        "confidence": "medium",
        "severity_assessment": "medium",
        "root_cause": "The helper method uses a Statement instead of a PreparedStatement and executes the given SQL string as-is, which is unsafe if any caller builds 'sql' via concatenation with untrusted input. However, the callers/source of the 'sql' parameter are not included in the provided code context, so taint from an attacker-controlled source cannot be confirmed or ruled out from the shown code alone.",
        "impact": "If any caller constructs the 'sql' argument using unsanitized user input via string concatenation, this sink would allow SQL injection, potentially enabling data exfiltration, modification, or destruction in the underlying database.",
        "remediation": [
          "Audit all call sites of executeSQLCommand to confirm whether the 'sql' argument is ever built from user-controlled input.",
          "Refactor executeSQLCommand (and its callers) to use PreparedStatement with bound parameters instead of raw Statement.executeUpdate(String).",
          "If dynamic SQL is unavoidable, apply strict allow-list validation or parameterization for any variable parts of the query.",
          "Add unit/integration tests confirming that injected SQL metacharacters in inputs do not alter query structure."
        ]
      }
