      "analysis": {
        "exploitability_evidence": "The value passed to Class.forName (variable 'which') is built from props.getProperty(\"thing\") where props is loaded from a classpath resource file 'thing.properties' via ThingFactory.class.getClassLoader().getResourceAsStream(...). This is a bundled configuration resource shipped with the application, not an attacker-controlled input (no HttpServletRequest, no external parameter, no network input feeds into this value). There is no reachable path from any external/user-controlled source to this sink in the code shown.",
        "verdict": "False Positive",
        "confidence": "high",
        "severity_assessment": "low",
        "root_cause": "The class name used in reflection is derived from a local properties file bundled with the application (thing.properties on the classpath), not from any externally-controlled input such as HTTP parameters, headers, or other request data. CWE-470 requires attacker control over the value used to select the class/method, which is absent here.",
        "impact": "None in practice: since the properties file is a trusted, build-time artifact and not user-modifiable at runtime through any exposed interface, there's no realistic way for an attacker to influence which class gets instantiated.",
        "remediation": [
          "If thing.properties can ever be replaced or influenced by untrusted input (e.g., via file upload or a writable deployment directory), treat that as the real vulnerability point and restrict file permissions.",
          "Consider using an allow-list/enum mapping instead of raw Class.forName if the properties file's source ever becomes less trusted.",
          "No change needed for the current reflection call itself given the fully trusted source of the class name."
        ]
      }
