          "cwe_top25": 0.1,
          "taint_source": 0.1,
          "source_sink_combo": 0.2
        }
      },
      "analysis": {
        "exploitability_evidence": "The static block in Utils.java (lines 137-157) unconditionally (on non-Windows systems) builds a PosixFilePermission set that includes GROUP_READ, GROUP_EXECUTE, OTHERS_READ, and OTHERS_EXECUTE alongside OWNER_READ/WRITE/EXECUTE, then applies it via Files.setPosixFilePermissions(script.toPath(), perms) to insecureCmd.sh. This is a hardcoded misconfiguration, not a tainted-input flow: the sink (setPosixFilePermissions) receives a permission set that grants read and execute to the file's group and to all other local users on the system. There is no sanitizer or validation applied to this permission set before it reaches the sink, and the script name ('insecureCmd.sh') suggests it may contain sensitive/test command logic. While the permissions do not include GROUP_WRITE/OTHERS_WRITE (so it's not literally 'rwx for all users' as the message states), granting world read+execute on a script violates least privilege and is a demonstrable, always-executed misconfiguration in the code shown.",
        "verdict": "True Positive",
        "confidence": "medium",
        "severity_assessment": "low",
        "root_cause": "The code explicitly constructs a permission set including OTHERS_READ and OTHERS_EXECUTE (and GROUP_READ/GROUP_EXECUTE) and applies it unconditionally to a script file via Files.setPosixFilePermissions, granting broader access than necessary (violates least privilege), even though full write access is not granted to group/others.",
        "impact": "Any local user or process on the host can read and execute insecureCmd.sh, potentially exposing script contents (which may reveal internal logic, paths, or command patterns) or allowing unauthorized execution/tampering vectors if the file is later modified by another writable process, increasing risk of information disclosure or local privilege abuse.",
        "remediation": [
          "Restrict permissions to only what is required (e.g., OWNER_READ, OWNER_EXECUTE, and only add GROUP/OTHERS permissions if genuinely needed).",
          "Avoid granting OTHERS_READ/OTHERS_EXECUTE unless the script must be run by other system users.",
          "Consider setting permissions at deployment/build time via file system ACLs rather than hardcoding permissive bits in application code.",
          "Review whether insecureCmd.sh needs to be world-readable/executable at all, or if it can be restricted to the application's own user/group."
        ]
      }
    },
