          "source_sink_combo": 0.2
        }
      },
      "analysis": {
        "exploitability_evidence": "The flagged sink is Files.setPosixFilePermissions(script.toPath(), perms) where perms is built from a hard-coded HashSet of PosixFilePermission constants: OWNER_READ, OWNER_WRITE, OWNER_EXECUTE, GROUP_READ, GROUP_EXECUTE, OTHERS_READ, OTHERS_EXECUTE. This maps to the octal mode 755 (rwxr-xr-x) \u2014 the value is a compile-time constant set, not attacker-controlled input, so there is no external source flowing into this sink. Critically, OTHERS_WRITE and GROUP_WRITE are NOT included, meaning only the file owner can write to the script; group and others only get read+execute. The scanner's message describes 'read, write, and execute for all users' which does not match the actual permission bits being set here. The comment in the code explains this is intentionally restoring execute bits lost when the script is exploded from the WAR, which is a legitimate operational need for the benchmark harness to run insecureCmd.sh.",
        "verdict": "False Positive",
        "confidence": "medium",
        "severity_assessment": "low",
        "root_cause": "The rule appears to pattern-match on the presence of READ+WRITE+EXECUTE PosixFilePermission constants anywhere in the set without correctly attributing them to per-actor-class scope. The actual computed mode is 755, which only grants write access to the owner; group and others get only read+execute, so this is not an overly permissive 777-style configuration. The value being set is a static, non-attacker-controlled literal.",
        "impact": "If exploitable, overly permissive file permissions on a script could allow unauthorized users to modify or execute privileged scripts, potentially leading to privilege escalation. However, since group/others do not have write access here, this specific risk is not realized.",
        "remediation": [
          "Confirm the mode is intentionally 755 for an executable script and document why group/other execute access is required.",
          "If broader execute access is unnecessary, restrict to OWNER_READ/OWNER_EXECUTE only or use a narrower set (e.g., 750).",
          "Avoid granting OTHERS_READ/OTHERS_EXECUTE unless the script must be world-executable; consider restricting to the application's service account/group.",
          "Add a comment in code justifying the permission bits to reduce false positives from static analysis tools in the future."
        ]
      }
    },
