      "analysis": {
        "exploitability_evidence": "The flagged sink is the SSLConnectionSocketFactory constructor call in Utils.getSSLFactory() (lines 423-425). The protocol array passed is hardcoded to `new String[] {\"TLSv1\"}` \u2014 this is a constant, not attacker-controlled data, but it is a fixed insecure configuration: TLSv1 is deprecated (RFC 8996) and vulnerable to BEAST/POODLE-class downgrade and padding-oracle attacks; no TLSv1.2/1.3 is offered as an alternative. There is no sanitizer or override elsewhere in the shown code that changes this list before it reaches the SSLConnectionSocketFactory constructor, so any HTTPS connection built via getSSLFactory() will negotiate only the weak TLSv1 protocol. Additionally NoopHostnameVerifier.INSTANCE disables hostname verification, compounding transport-security weakness, though that is a separate CWE not directly the one flagged.",
        "verdict": "True Positive",
        "confidence": "high",
        "severity_assessment": "medium",
        "root_cause": "The code explicitly restricts the SSL/TLS handshake to the deprecated TLSv1 protocol only, instead of allowing modern secure protocols (TLSv1.2/TLSv1.3), creating a hardcoded insecure transport configuration.",
        "impact": "Any HTTPS connections established through this factory are limited to a deprecated protocol susceptible to known cryptographic weaknesses (e.g., BEAST) and downgrade attacks, potentially allowing an on-path attacker to intercept or tamper with sensitive data in transit.",
        "remediation": [
          "Replace the hardcoded protocol array with modern protocols, e.g. new String[] {\"TLSv1.2\", \"TLSv1.3\"}.",
          "Remove or replace NoopHostnameVerifier.INSTANCE with proper hostname verification (e.g., DefaultHostnameVerifier).",
          "Centralize TLS configuration and enforce a minimum protocol version policy across the codebase.",
          "Add automated tests/config checks to prevent regressions to deprecated protocol versions."
        ]
      }
    },
