      "analysis": {
        "exploitability_evidence": "Attacker-controlled source: cookie named 'BenchmarkTest00001' read via request.getCookies(); its value is URL-decoded (java.net.URLDecoder.decode) into 'param'. This param is directly concatenated onto Utils.TESTFILES_DIR to build 'fileName', which is passed unmodified into 'new java.io.File(fileName)' and then 'new java.io.FileInputStream(...)' \u2014 the flagged sink. There is no path canonicalization, no check for '../' sequences, and no allow-list validation of the filename component before it reaches the file-open sink. The only sanitization present (ESAPI encoder().encodeForHTML) is applied later, only to the fileName string when it's echoed back in the HTTP response body \u2014 that's an output-encoding step for XSS/HTML context, not a path-traversal sanitizer, and it doesn't affect the file path used to open the file. Therefore user input flows unsanitized into a file-path sink.",
        "verdict": "True Positive",
        "confidence": "high",
        "severity_assessment": "high",
        "root_cause": "User-supplied cookie value is decoded and directly concatenated into a file path used to open a file, without validating/canonicalizing the resulting path or stripping directory traversal sequences ('../').",
        "impact": "An attacker can supply a cookie value containing '../' sequences (or absolute path fragments) to escape the intended TESTFILES_DIR directory and read arbitrary files readable by the application process, leading to disclosure of sensitive files (e.g., configuration files, credentials, source code).",
        "remediation": [
          "Canonicalize the resulting File path (e.g., File.getCanonicalPath()) and verify it still resides within the intended base directory (TESTFILES_DIR) before opening it.",
          "Reject or strip path traversal sequences and path separators from the cookie value, or better, use org.apache.commons.io.FilenameUtils.getName(param) to reduce input to a bare filename with no directory components.",
          "Use an allow-list of permitted filenames if the set of valid files is finite/known.",
          "Avoid building file paths from user input entirely; instead map an opaque token/id supplied by the user to a server-side lookup table of allowed file paths."
        ]
      }
    },
