        "exploitability_evidence": "Source: request.getHeaders(\"Referer\") \u2014 user-controlled HTTP header value is extracted into 'param'. It is then URL-decoded via java.net.URLDecoder.decode(param, \"UTF-8\") (decoding does not neutralize HTML/script content, it only reverses percent-encoding). The decoded value is passed directly as the format string to response.getWriter().format(Locale.US, param, obj) at line 56. There is no HTML-encoding, output escaping, or allowlist validation applied to 'param' anywhere on this path before it reaches the Writer sink, which writes directly into the HTTP response body bypassing any templating/escaping layer. An attacker who controls the Referer header (e.g., via a crafted HTTP request, a malicious link with a controlled referrer, or a cross-origin request they initiate) can inject arbitrary HTML/JavaScript that gets reflected verbatim in the response.",
        "verdict": "True Positive",
        "confidence": "high",
        "severity_assessment": "medium",
        "root_cause": "Attacker-controlled Referer header value is URL-decoded and written directly to the response Writer without any HTML output encoding or sanitization, allowing injected markup/script to be reflected in the page.",
        "impact": "Reflected Cross-Site Scripting: an attacker able to control or spoof the Referer header (e.g., via a crafted request, malicious redirecting page, or tooling that lets them set arbitrary headers) can inject HTML/JavaScript that executes in the context of the vulnerable site when the response is rendered, enabling session hijacking, credential theft, or defacement.",
        "remediation": [
          "Avoid writing user-controlled data directly via response.getWriter(); use a view/templating technology that automatically HTML-encodes output (e.g., JSP EL, JSF, Thymeleaf).",
          "If manual output is required, HTML-encode the value using a vetted library (e.g., OWASP Java Encoder: Encode.forHtml(param)) before writing it to the response.",
          "Do not use untrusted input as a format string argument to String.format/Formatter; treat it strictly as data, not as the format pattern.",
          "Set appropriate Content-Security-Policy headers as defense-in-depth against reflected XSS."
        ]
      }
