      "analysis": {
        "exploitability_evidence": "Source: request.getParameterMap() retrieves attacker-controlled parameter 'BenchmarkTest00028' into `param`. This value is concatenated directly into `fileName = Utils.TESTFILES_DIR + param` with no validation, then passed to `new java.io.FileOutputStream(fileName, false)` \u2014 the sink. The only processing applied to `fileName` is `ESAPI.encoder().encodeForHTML(fileName)`, which is used solely for the HTTP response text, not for the file path used in the FileOutputStream call. HTML encoding does not strip or block '../' sequences, absolute paths, or null bytes, so it provides no protection against path traversal in the file system operation. No canonicalization check (e.g., verifying the resolved path is within TESTFILES_DIR) or use of FilenameUtils.getName() is present on the path used in the sink.",
        "verdict": "True Positive",
        "confidence": "high",
        "severity_assessment": "high",
        "root_cause": "User--supplied request parameter is concatenated into a file path used to open/write a file, without out any path traversal sanitization or canonical-path containment check before that write; the HTML-encoding applied is irrelevant to the filesystem sink.",
        "impact": "An attacker can supply a parameter value containing '../' sequences (or an absolute path) to write/overwrite arbitrary files outside the intended TESTFILES_DIR directory, potentially leading to data corruption, remote code execution (e.g., overwriting web-accessible files or config files), or denial of service.",
        "remediation": [
          "Use FilenameUtils.getName(param) or similar to strip directory components from user input before building the file path.",
          "After constructing the full path, canonicalize it (File.getCanonicalPath()) and verify it starts with the intended base directory (TESTFILES_DIR) before opening the FileOutputStream.",
          "Maintain an allow-list of permitted file names or use a generated/mapped identifier instead of directly using user input in file paths.",
          "Reject or reject requests where the resolved path escapes the intended directory."
        ]
      }
    },
