      "analysis": {
        "exploitability_evidence": "Source: request.getParameterMap() at line 44 populates 'map', and param is set from map.get(\"BenchmarkTest00031\")[0] \u2014 fully attacker-controlled via query/POST parameter. This raw 'param' value flows directly into request.getSession().putValue(\"userid\", param) at line 52 with no validation or sanitization applied before the session write. The only sanitizer present, Utils.encodeForHTML(param), is applied afterward solely for the HTML output on the next lines \u2014 it does not sanitize the value stored in the session. Thus tainted user input is persisted into the session attribute unsanitized, matching the CWE-501 trust boundary violation pattern exactly.",
        "verdict": "True Positive",
        "confidence": "high",
        "severity_assessment": "low",
        "root_cause": "User-supplied HTTP parameter is stored directly into the HttpSession via putValue() without any validation, sanitization, or type-checking, blurring the trust boundary between session (trusted) and request (untrusted) data.",
        "impact": "Untrusted data persisted in the session may later be treated as trusted by other application code (e.g., used in decisions, rendered elsewhere without encoding, or used in downstream sinks), potentially enabling stored XSS, injection, or logic bypass if that trust assumption is violated.",
        "remediation": [
          "Validate and sanitize/normalize the parameter value before storing it in the session (e.g., whitelist allowed characters/format for 'userid').",
          "Avoid storing raw request parameters in the session; instead store a vetted/typed representation.",
          "Apply context-appropriate encoding at every point the session value is later used, not just at this one display site.",
          "Use the modern setAttribute API and treat all session data as needing validation on write, not just on read."
        ]
      }
    },
