      "analysis": {
        "exploitability_evidence": "Source: HTTP request parameter names, enumerated via request.getParameterNames()/getParameterValues(). If any parameter value equals the literal 'BenchmarkTest00036', the parameter NAME (attacker-controlled) is stored in 'param'. This 'param' is then written directly to the response via response.getWriter().write(param.toCharArray(), 0, length) with no HTML encoding, escaping, or use of ESAPI. There is no sanitizer or encoder on this path before the Writer sink, so any HTML/script content submitted as a parameter name will be reflected verbatim in the response body.",
        "verdict": "True Positive",
        "confidence": "high",
        "severity_assessment": "high",
        "root_cause": "User-supplied parameter name is written directly to the HttpServletResponse Writer without any output encoding, bypassing HTML escaping.",
        "impact": "Reflected Cross-Site Scripting allowing an attacker to inject arbitrary HTML/JavaScript that executes in the victim's browser, potentially leading to session hijacking, credential theft, or defacement.",
        "remediation": [
          "Encode all user-controlled output with a context-aware encoder (e.g., ESAPI.encoder().encodeForHTML()) before writing to the response.",
          "Avoid writing directly to the raw OutputStream/Writer; use a templating engine (JSP/JSF) that auto-escapes output.",
          "Set appropriate Content-Security-Policy headers as defense in depth."
        ]
      }
    },
