        "root_cause": "User-supplied HTTP parameter is used to construct a java.io.File object and subsequently queried with .exists() without any path traversal sanitization or canonicalization/whitelisting; the only sanitization present (HTML encoding) is irrelevant to file path safety.",
        "impact": "An attacker can supply path traversal sequences (e.g., '../../etc/passwd') to probe for the existence of arbitrary files on the server's filesystem, enabling information disclosure about the file system layout, potential enumeration of sensitive files, and could be a stepping stone to further attacks (e.g., combined with other endpoints that read/write file contents).",
        "remediation": [
          "Canonicalize the file path (File.getCanonicalPath()) and verify it resides within an expected base directory before use.",
          "Use FilenameUtils.getName() or similar to strip any directory components from user input.",
          "Maintain a whitelist of allowed file names/paths and reject any input not matching the whitelist.",
          "Avoid directly using user input to construct File objects; use an indirect reference (e.g., a lookup key mapped to a safe file path) instead."
