      "analysis": {
        "exploitability_evidence": "Source: `request.getParameterValues(\"BenchmarkTest00045\")` provides attacker-controlled `param`. This is concatenated directly with `Utils.TESTFILES_DIR` to build `fileName` with no validation, filtering, or canonicalization of path traversal sequences like '../'. `fileName` then flows directly into `new java.io.FileInputStream(fileName)`, which opens the file at that path. The only sanitizer present, `ESAPI.encoder().encodeForHTML(fileName)`, is applied afterward purely to the string echoed in the HTTP response body (an XSS mitigation) \u2014 it does not affect or precede the FileInputStream call, so it provides no protection against path traversal at the file-open sink.",
        "verdict": "True Positive",
        "confidence": "high",
        "severity_assessment": "high",
        "root_cause": "User-supplied request parameter is concatenated into a file path with no path traversal sanitization (e.g., no canonicalization, no rejection of '../' sequences, no allow-list check) before being used to open a file via FileInputStream.",
        "impact": "An attacker can supply a parameter value like '../../../../etc/passwd' to read arbitrary files on the filesystem accessible to the application process, leading to disclosure of sensitive data, configuration files, or credentials.",
        "remediation": [
          "Canonicalize the resolved file path (e.g., File.getCanonicalPath()) and verify it resides within the intended base directory (TESTFILES_DIR) before opening it.",
          "Use an allow-list of permitted file names or strip path traversal sequences using a vetted utility such as org.apache.commons.io.FilenameUtils.getName(...) to strip directory components from user input.",
          "Reject any input containing path separators or '..' sequences outright before building the file path.",
          "Avoid directly using user input as part of file system paths; map user input to a safe internal identifier when possible."
        ]
      }
    },
