      "analysis": {
        "exploitability_evidence": "Source: request cookie named 'BenchmarkTest00060' -> theCookie.getValue() is URL-decoded into `param`. `param` is then Base64-encoded and immediately Base64-decoded back into `bar` (a no-op transformation that does not strip '../' sequences). `bar` is used directly as the child path in `new File(new File(TESTFILES_DIR), bar)` with no canonicalization, path-traversal filtering, or allow-list check. The resulting File object's existence is checked and its path is echoed back (HTML-encoded, but that does not mitigate path traversal). No sanitizer applicable to filesystem path traversal (e.g., getCanonicalPath comparison, rejecting '..', or FilenameUtils.getName) is applied on this path.",
        "verdict": "True Positive",
        "confidence": "high",
        "severity_assessment": "medium",
        "root_cause": "User-controlled cookie value flows unsanitized into a File path constructor; the only transformation applied (Base64 encode/decode round-trip) is a no-op and does not neutralize '../' sequences, allowing directory traversal.",
        "impact": "An attacker can supply a cookie value containing '../' sequences to make the application check for or reference arbitrary files outside the intended TESTFILES_DIR, potentially revealing file existence/information about the filesystem structure (information disclosure), and in variants where the file is opened/read, full arbitrary file read.",
        "remediation": [
          "Canonicalize the resulting File path (getCanonicalPath) and verify it still resides within the intended base directory before use.",
          "Reject or strip path traversal sequences ('../', absolute paths) from user input.",
          "Use org.apache.commons.io.FilenameUtils.getName(...) to extract only the filename portion from user input before combining with the base directory.",
          "Prefer a whitelist of allowed filenames rather than accepting arbitrary user-supplied path segments."
        ]
      }
    },
