      "analysis": {
        "exploitability_evidence": "Source: HTTP cookie named 'BenchmarkTest00061' read via request.getCookies() (line ~59), value URL-decoded into 'param'. This flows into 'bar' via a Base64 encode/decode round-trip (org.apache.commons.codec.binary.Base64.decodeBase64(encodeBase64(param.getBytes()))) which is a no-op transformation that does not sanitize path-traversal sequences like '../'. 'bar' is then used directly as the parent path in 'new java.io.File(bar, \"/Test.txt\")' (the flagged sink at line 75). No path canonicalization, whitelist check, or FilenameUtils.getName() call is applied to 'bar' before constructing the File object. The subsequent encodeForHTML() call only protects the HTML output context, not the file path construction, so it is not an adequate sanitizer for this CWE-22 sink. An attacker can set the cookie value to a traversal sequence (e.g. '../../../../etc') to make fileTarget resolve outside the intended directory, and the application then discloses via fileTarget.exists() whether such a path exists on the filesystem.",
        "verdict": "True Positive",
        "confidence": "high"
      }
