      "analysis": {
        "exploitability_evidence": "Source: theCookie.getValue() decoded via URLDecoder.decode() into 'param'. This flows into the ternary: bar = (7*18)+num > 200 ? \"This_should_always_happen\" : param; with num=106, (7*18)+106 = 232 > 200 is always true, so bar is ALWAYS assigned the constant string \"This_should_always_happen\" and the tainted 'param' branch is unreachable dead code. fileName = TESTFILES_DIR + bar therefore always resolves to a fixed, non-attacker-controlled path before reaching the FileOutputStream sink at line 92.",
        "verdict": "False Positive",
        "confidence": "high",
        "severity_assessment": "low",
        "root_cause": "The conditional expression controlling which value is assigned to 'bar' is a tautology (constant arithmetic always evaluates true), so the branch that would use the tainted, user-controlled cookie value is never executed. The FileOutputStream is always constructed with a hardcoded string, not attacker input.",
        "impact": "None in practice \u2014 the flagged sink never receives attacker-controlled data due to the always-true condition, so path traversal is not achievable through this code path.",
        "remediation": [
          "No fix required functionally, but for defense-in-depth and to avoid confusing future maintainers, remove dead/unreachable tainted code paths.",
          "If the intent was genuinely to use user input, validate/canonicalize the path with FilenameUtils.getName() or verify the resolved path stays within the intended directory before use in FileOutputStream."
        ]
      }
    },
