      "analysis": {
        "exploitability_evidence": "Source: HTTP cookie 'BenchmarkTest00077' value read via request.getCookies(), URL-decoded and stored in 'param'. Flow: param -> bar (via switch statement where switchTarget='C' from guess.charAt(2), executing case 'C'/'D' branch, so bar=param) -> concatenated as 'echo ' + bar into argList -> ProcessBuilder(argList) -> pb.start() at line 97. No sanitization, whitelist validation, or shell-metacharacter escaping is applied to param/bar between the cookie extraction and command execution. URLDecoder.decode only performs URL-decoding, providing no protection against shell metacharacters (e.g., ;, |, &, `, $()). Because the command is executed via 'sh -c' or 'cmd.exe /c', any shell metacharacters in the cookie value will be interpreted by the shell, enabling command injection.",
        "verdict": "True Positive",
        "confidence": "high",
        "severity_assessment": "high",
        "root_cause": "User-controlled cookie value flows unsanitized into a ProcessBuilder command executed through a shell interpreter (sh -c / cmd.exe /c), allowing injection of arbitrary shell metacharacters and commands.",
        "impact": "An attacker can execute arbitrary OS commands on the server by injecting shell metacharacters into the cookie value, potentially leading to full server compromise, data exfiltration, or denial of service.",
        "remediation": [
          "Avoid passing user-controlled input to shell-invoking APIs like ProcessBuilder with 'sh -c'/'cmd.exe /c'.",
          "If command execution is unavoidable, use a strict whitelist/allowlist of permitted values for 'bar' and reject anything else.",
          "Prefer passing arguments directly to ProcessBuilder without going through a shell, and avoid string concatenation of untrusted input into the command line.",
          "Use ESAPI or similar validators to strictly validate the cookie value against expected patterns before use in any command context.",
          "Consider using Java APIs (e.g., built-in echo functionality) instead of shelling out for simple output tasks."
        ]
      }
    },
