        "small_model": {
          "key": "org/owasp/benchmark/testcode/BenchmarkTest00118.java:90:13-90:88:java.lang.security.audit.xss.no-direct-response-writer.no-direct-response-writer",
          "decision": "escalate",
          "reason": "User-controlled cookie value flows into XPath expression and then directly into response.getWriter() without HTML encoding in the success path, creating XSS vulnerability.",
          "error": null,
          "elapsed_seconds": 1.428
        }
      },
      "analysis": {
        "exploitability_evidence": "The cookie value 'param' is URL-decoded and assigned to 'bar' only in the else branch of 'if ((7 * 42) - num > 200) bar = \"This_should_always_happen\"; else bar = param;'. Evaluating the constant expression: (7*42)-86 = 294-86 = 208, and 208 > 200 is always true, so the if-branch always executes and bar is always hardcoded to \"This_should_always_happen\". The else branch that assigns the tainted cookie value to bar is unreachable dead code. Consequently, the XPath expression and the resulting 'result' string written via response.getWriter().println(...) at line 90 are always built from the constant string, never from attacker-controlled input. The cookie value never reaches the flagged sink.",
        "verdict": "False Positive",
        "confidence": "high",
        "severity_assessment": "low",
        "root_cause": "An always-true conditional guard (arithmetic constant comparison that never evaluates to false) prevents the tainted branch (bar = param) from ever executing, so the user-controlled cookie value never reaches the response writer sink.",
        "impact": "None in current code, since the vulnerable data path is dead code and cannot be triggered by any request.",
        "remediation": [
          "No immediate fix required since the tainted branch is unreachable, but the dead/dummy conditional should be removed or clarified to avoid confusing future maintainers or automated tools.",
          "If the intent was to sometimes use user input, ensure HTML-encoding (e.g., ESAPI.encoder().encodeForHTML) is applied to any XPath query result derived from user input before writing to the response, consistent with the encoding already used in the catch block."
        ]
      }
    },
