      "analysis": {
        "exploitability_evidence": "Source: request.getHeader(\"BenchmarkTest00131\") -> URL-decoded into 'param'. Flow to sink: bar is assigned via a switch on switchTarget = \"ABC\".charAt(1), which is always the constant char 'B'. The switch's case 'B' branch always executes, setting bar = \"bob\" (a hardcoded literal), and the case 'A'/'C'/'D' branches that assign bar = param are unreachable dead code. Consequently java.io.File fileTarget = new File(bar) is always constructed with the constant \"bob\", never with attacker-controlled 'param'. The path-traversal sink is therefore never reached with tainted data.",
        "verdict": "False Positive",
        "confidence": "high",
        "severity_assessment": "low",
        "root_cause": "The switch statement's control variable (switchTarget) is derived from a hardcoded string \"ABC\".charAt(1), which always evaluates to 'B'. This makes the case that assigns the tainted 'param' value to 'bar' unreachable; only the constant-assignment case ('B' -> \"bob\") executes at runtime, so the file path used in the File constructor is never influenced by user input.",
        "impact": "None in practice: because the vulnerable code path is unreachable, there is no real path traversal risk from this specific code as written. If the switch logic were ever changed to depend on actual runtime/user-controlled data, this would become exploitable.",
        "remediation": [
          "Treat this as a benign/dead-code finding for this Benchmark test case; no functional fix required unless the switch condition changes.",
          "If similar patterns exist elsewhere with real conditional logic reachable by user input, validate/canonicalize the path with something like FilenameUtils.getName() or verify the resolved File is within an allowed base directory before use.",
          "Add unit/integration tests asserting the switch always resolves to the safe branch to prevent future regressions."
        ]
      }
    },
