      "analysis": {
        "exploitability_evidence": "Source: request.getHeader(\"BenchmarkTest00133\") is attacker-controlled. It flows into 'param', is URL-decoded, then via a ternary whose condition (7*42-106>200) is always false, 'bar' is always assigned param's value unmodified. 'bar' is then concatenated into fileName = TESTFILES_DIR + bar and passed directly to new java.io.FileInputStream(fileName) at line 63. No path traversal sanitization (e.g., stripping '../', canonicalization, or FilenameUtils.getName) is applied to 'bar' or 'fileName' before the FileInputStream call. The only sanitization present, ESAPI encodeForHTML, is applied afterward to the fileName for display/output purposes, not to prevent path traversal in the file access itself. Thus a header value like '../../../../etc/passwd' would be decoded and passed straight to FileInputStream, allowing arbitrary file read outside the intended directory.",
        "verdict": "True Positive",
        "confidence": "high",
        "severity_assessment": "high",
        "root_cause": "User-controlled HTTP header value is URL-decoded and concatenated into a file path without any validation, normalization, or restriction (e.g., disallowing '../' sequences or verifying the resolved path stays within the intended base directory) before being used to open a file via FileInputStream.",
        "impact": "An attacker can supply a header value containing directory traversal sequences (e.g., '../../../../etc/passwd') to read arbitrary files on the filesystem that the application process has permission to access, potentially exposing sensitive configuration files, credentials, or other confidential data.",
        "remediation": [
          "Canonicalize the resolved file path (e.g., using File.getCanonicalPath()) and verify it is still within the intended TESTFILES_DIR before opening it.",
          "Reject or strip path traversal sequences ('../', absolute paths) from user input before use in file operations.",
          "Use org.apache.commons.io.FilenameUtils.getName() to extract only the file name component, discarding any directory traversal payload.",
          "Maintain an allow-list of permitted file names and validate the user input against it rather than directly using raw input in a path."
        ]
      }
    },
