      "analysis": {
        "exploitability_evidence": "Source: request.getHeader(\"Referer\") (attacker-controlled HTTP header) is assigned to param, then URL-decoded via java.net.URLDecoder.decode. A conditional (always true given the fixed arithmetic 500/42+196>200) assigns param to bar with no escaping/sanitization applied. bar is placed into an Object[] and passed to response.getWriter().format(\"Formatted like: %1$s and %2$s.\", obj), which writes the raw string directly to the HTTP response body via the raw Writer, bypassing any HTML-encoding view layer. No sanitizer, encoder, or validator is applied to bar between the tainted source and the sink.",
        "verdict": "True Positive",
        "confidence": "high",
        "severity_assessment": "medium",
        "root_cause": "User-controlled Referer header value is decoded and written directly to the response output stream via getWriter().format() without any HTML output encoding, allowing injected script/markup to be reflected in the response.",
        "impact": "An attacker who can control the Referer header (e.g., via a crafted link, proxy, or intermediary that reflects it) can inject arbitrary HTML/JavaScript that executes in the victim's browser context, leading to reflected XSS: session hijacking, credential theft, or defacement.",
        "remediation": [
          "HTML-encode/escape the value of bar before writing it to the response (e.g., using OWASP Java Encoder's Encode.forHtml()).",
          "Avoid writing directly to the raw Writer/OutputStream for dynamic content; use a templating engine (JSP/JSF/Thymeleaf) that auto-escapes output.",
          "Set appropriate Content-Security-Policy headers to reduce impact of any residual XSS.",
          "Do not use the X-XSS-Protection header as a substitute for proper output encoding (it is deprecated in modern browsers)."
        ]
      }
    },
