          "reason": "User-controlled request header (Referer) is URL-decoded and written directly to response via printf without HTML encoding, creating a direct XSS vector.",
          "error": null,
          "elapsed_seconds": 1.337
        }
      },
      "analysis": {
        "exploitability_evidence": "Source: request.getHeader(\"Referer\") (attacker-controlled HTTP header) at line 44/45. It is URL-decoded via URLDecoder.decode at line 49 (decoding does not neutralize HTML/JS metacharacters). The value is assigned to 'param' then to 'bar' via an if/else where the condition (500/42)+196>200 is a constant expression that always evaluates true, so bar always equals the tainted param. 'bar' is placed into an Object[] and passed to response.getWriter().printf(Locale.US, \"Formatted like: %1$s and %2$s.\", obj) at line 60. printf performs no HTML encoding; the format string simply substitutes the raw string. No sanitizer, encoder, or validator is applied anywhere on this path, and output is written directly via getWriter() bypassing any templating/auto-escaping layer.",
        "verdict": "True Positive",
        "confidence": "high",
        "severity_assessment": "medium",
        "root_cause": "User-controlled Referer header value is URL-decoded and written directly to the HTTP response body via getWriter().printf without any HTML/output encoding or validation, allowing injection of arbitrary HTML/JavaScript.",
        "impact": "An attacker who controls the Referer header (e.g., via a crafted link, proxy, or intercepted request replay) can inject arbitrary script/HTML into the response, resulting in reflected Cross-Site Scripting (XSS) that can lead to session hijacking, credential theft, or other client-side attacks against victims who load the crafted response.",
        "remediation": [
          "HTML-encode the Referer value before writing it into the response (e.g., use OWASP Java Encoder's Encode.forHtml() or similar) instead of raw printf.",
          "Avoid writing user-controlled data directly via response.getWriter(); use a templating engine (JSP/JSF/Thymeleaf) that auto-escapes output.",
          "Do not trust the Referer header for any output; treat all HTTP headers as untrusted input and validate/sanitize before use.",
          "Set appropriate Content-Security-Policy headers as defense-in-depth against reflected XSS."
        ]
      }
    },
