        "confidence": "high",
        "severity_assessment": "high",
        "root_cause": "User-controlled HTTP header value is concatenated directly into a SQL query string instead of being passed as a bound parameter, defeating the protection that PreparedStatement would otherwise provide.",
        "impact": "An attacker can inject arbitrary SQL via the 'BenchmarkTest00192' HTTP header (e.g., closing the quoted PASSWORD literal and appending malicious SQL), potentially bypassing authentication, exfiltrating data from the USERS table, or modifying/deleting data depending on DB permissions.",
        "remediation": [
          "Bind the PASSWORD value as a parameterized placeholder (e.g., 'PASSWORD=?') and use statement.setString(2, bar) instead of string concatenation.",
          "Avoid building SQL strings via concatenation of user input entirely; use only bind variables for all user-supplied values.",
          "Validate/allow-list the expected format of the header value (e.g., alphanumeric only) as defense in depth.",
          "Ensure all PreparedStatement parameters are set via setX methods rather than embedded in the SQL text."
