      "analysis": {
        "exploitability_evidence": "Source: request.getHeader(\"BenchmarkTest00195\") (line ~44). Value is URL-decoded and assigned to param, then assigned to bar via a ternary whose condition (7*42-106=188 > 200) is always false, so bar always equals the raw decoded header value. bar is directly concatenated into the SQL string: \"SELECT * from USERS where USERNAME='foo' and PASSWORD='\" + bar + \"'\" (line 58-59) with no escaping/parameterization, then passed to DatabaseHelper.JDBCtemplate.execute(sql) (the flagged sink at line 61). The only encoding present, ESAPI.encoder().encodeForHTML(sql), is applied afterward only to the string written to the HTTP response for display purposes \u2014 it does not sanitize the sql variable before execution. No parameterized query, prepared statement, or SQL-specific sanitizer is used on the path to the sink.",
        "verdict": "True Positive",
        "confidence": "high",
        "severity_assessment": "high",
        "root_cause": "User-controlled HTTP header value is concatenated directly into a SQL query string without parameterization or SQL-specific sanitization before being executed via JDBCtemplate.execute().",
        "impact": "An attacker can inject arbitrary SQL via the 'BenchmarkTest00195' HTTP header, potentially bypassing authentication, extracting or modifying data, or in some database configurations executing further malicious operations.",
        "remediation": [
          "Use parameterized queries / PreparedStatement (or Spring JdbcTemplate's query methods with bind parameters) instead of string concatenation.",
          "Avoid building SQL strings from raw user input; if dynamic SQL is unavoidable, use a strict allow-list validator for the input.",
          "Do not rely on HTML output encoding (ESAPI encodeForHTML) as a defense against SQL injection \u2014 it only protects against XSS in the response body."
        ]
      }
