      "analysis": {
        "exploitability_evidence": "Source: request.getHeader(\"BenchmarkTest00203\") is attacker-controlled. It's URL-decoded, then assigned to 'param'. The ternary '(7*42)-num>200 ? ... : param' evaluates 188>200 which is always false, so bar=param unconditionally (dead branch never taken). 'sql' is built via string concatenation: \"INSERT INTO users (username, password) VALUES ('foo','\" + bar + \"')\" with no escaping/parameterization. This raw sql string is passed to statement.executeUpdate(sql, ...) via DatabaseHelper.getSqlStatement() which returns a plain java.sql.Statement (not PreparedStatement). No sanitizer, validator, or parameterized query is applied anywhere on this path before the SQL sink. The only encoding present (ESAPI encodeForHTML in outputUpdateComplete) is applied to the sql string for HTML output response, not to neutralize SQL metacharacters before execution \u2014 irrelevant to the SQL injection sink.",
        "verdict": "True Positive",
        "confidence": "high",
        "severity_assessment": "high",
        "root_cause": "User-controlled HTTP header value is concatenated directly into a SQL statement string and executed via a plain Statement.executeUpdate() call, with no input sanitization, escaping, or use of parameterized queries/PreparedStatement.",
        "impact": "An attacker can inject arbitrary SQL via the 'BenchmarkTest00203' HTTP header, potentially manipulating the INSERT statement to alter data, exfiltrate data via error-based/UNION techniques (if the DB supports it in this context), or in more permissive drivers achieve stacked query execution, leading to unauthorized data modification, disclosure, or database compromise.",
        "remediation": [
          "Use PreparedStatement with parameterized queries instead of building SQL via string concatenation.",
          "Bind the header value as a parameter (e.g., ps.setString(1, bar)) rather than concatenating into the SQL string.",
          "Apply strict input validation/allow-listing on the header value if it must be used to construct dynamic SQL fragments (though parameterization is preferred).",
          "Avoid using raw java.sql.Statement for any query built from user input."
        ]
      }
    },
