      "analysis": {
        "exploitability_evidence": "Source: request.getHeader(\"BenchmarkTest00203\") - an attacker-controlled HTTP header value. It's URL-decoded and assigned to param. The ternary condition `(7 * 42) - num > 200` evaluates to 294-106=188 > 200 which is false, so bar is always assigned the raw param value (no sanitization branch is ever effectively active for benign input). This bar is then directly concatenated into the SQL string: `\"INSERT INTO users (username, password) VALUES ('foo','\" + bar + \"')\"`. This sql string is executed via statement.executeUpdate(sql, ...) using a plain java.sql.Statement (from DatabaseHelper.getSqlStatement(), confirmed non-parameterized in the shown body) rather than a PreparedStatement. No sanitizer, escaping, or validation is applied to bar/param before it reaches the SQL sink. The only downstream encoding (ESAPI.encodeForHTML in outputUpdateComplete) is for HTML output, not SQL, and occurs after the query has already executed \u2014 it does not mitigate the injection.",
        "verdict": "True Positive",
        "confidence": "high",
        "severity_assessment": "high",
        "root_cause": "User-supplied HTTP header value is decoded and concatenated directly into a SQL INSERT statement string without parameterization or input sanitization, then executed via a raw Statement object.",
        "impact": "An attacker can inject arbitrary SQL via the BenchmarkTest00203 header, potentially allowing data exfiltration, modification of the users table, authentication bypass, or further database compromise depending on DB permissions and engine.",
        "remediation": [
          "Use java.sql.PreparedStatement with parameterized queries (e.g., connection.prepareStatement(\"INSERT INTO users (username, password) VALUES (?, ?)\")) instead of string concatenation.",
          "Bind the header-derived value via setString() rather than embedding it directly in the SQL text.",
          "Apply strict allow-list input validation on the header value if it must conform to a specific format.",
          "Avoid trusting HTTP headers as safe input; treat all headers as attacker-controlled."
        ]
      }
    },
