          "signal:xss": 0.15,
          "taint_source": 0.1,
          "sanitizer_hint": -0.1
        }
      },
      "analysis": {
        "exploitability_evidence": "Attacker-controlled source: an HTTP request header name (obtained via request.getHeaderNames()/getHeaders(name)) is assigned to 'param' and then to 'bar' with no validation of its content. 'bar' is passed directly as the child path segment into 'new java.io.File(new java.io.File(Utils.TESTFILES_DIR), bar)' at lines 63-65, allowing sequences like '../' to escape the intended TESTFILES_DIR. The only processing applied afterward is ESAPI's encoder().encodeForHTML() on the resulting file path string, which is an output-encoding step for safe HTML rendering \u2014 it does not sanitize or restrict the path used to construct/query the File object. There is no call to FilenameUtils.getName(), no whitelist check, and no path-canonicalization/prefix check before the File is used in fileTarget.exists(). Hence the traversal-vulnerable File object is built directly from unsanitized attacker input.",
        "verdict": "True Positive",
        "confidence": "high",
        "severity_assessment": "medium",
        "root_cause": "User-supplied HTTP header name is used unsanitized to build a java.io.File path, allowing directory traversal via '../' sequences; the only mitigation present (ESAPI encodeForHTML) sanitizes for HTML output context, not for filesystem path safety.",
        "impact": "An attacker can supply a crafted, non-standard HTTP header name containing path traversal sequences to probe for the existence of arbitrary files on the filesystem outside the intended TESTFILES_DIR, leading to information disclosure about the file system layout; if similar constructs are reused elsewhere for read/write operations, this could escalate to arbitrary file read/write.",
        "remediation": [
          "Sanitize the header-derived value using org.apache.commons.io.FilenameUtils.getName() to strip any directory components before using it in a File path.",
          "Validate the resulting path against a canonical, whitelisted base directory (e.g., verify File.getCanonicalPath() starts with the expected base directory) before performing any file operations.",
          "Reject or ignore header values containing '..' or path separators outright.",
          "Avoid deriving file names directly from arbitrary HTTP header names; if functionality requires it, use a strict allow-list of expected values."
        ]
      }
    },
