      "analysis": {
        "exploitability_evidence": "Source: HTTP request header name obtained via request.getHeaderNames()/getHeaders(name) \u2014 fully attacker-controlled and not URL-decoded (comment explicitly notes this). This value is stored in 'param', then unconditionally assigned to 'bar' (the if-condition (500/42)+196>200 is always true, so bar=param always). 'bar' is passed unsanitized into 'new java.io.File(Utils.TESTFILES_DIR, bar)', which is the path-traversal sink (CWE-22). No path validation, canonicalization, or use of FilenameUtils.getName() is applied to 'bar' before it reaches the File constructor. The only sanitization present, ESAPI encoder().encodeForHTML(...), is applied to fileTarget.toString() solely for safe HTML output \u2014 it does not neutralize '../' sequences in the underlying path used to construct/query the File object, so it provides no protection for the path-traversal sink itself.",
        "verdict": "True Positive",
        "confidence": "high",
        "severity_assessment": "medium",
        "root_cause": "An attacker-controlled HTTP header name is used directly to build a java.io.File path (via File(String parent, String child)) without any normalization, canonicalization, or whitelist validation, allowing directory traversal sequences ('../') to escape the intended base directory (Utils.TESTFILES_DIR).",
        "impact": "An attacker can supply a crafted header name containing '../' sequences to make the servlet check for the existence of arbitrary files outside the intended TESTFILES_DIR (file existence/information disclosure oracle). Depending on how fileTarget is used elsewhere or in related code paths, this pattern could also enable more severe file read/write if extended, and at minimum leaks whether arbitrary files exist on the filesystem.",
        "remediation": [
          "Do not use raw, attacker-supplied header names (or any request-derived data) directly as file path components.",
          "Use org.apache.commons.io.FilenameUtils.getName(bar) or similar to strip any directory components before building the File object.",
          "Canonicalize the resulting File (getCanonicalPath()) and verify it still resides within the intended base directory (Utils.TESTFILES_DIR) before use.",
          "Reject or sanitize any input containing '..', '/', '\\\\', or other path-traversal metacharacters.",
          "Apply the same validation regardless of whether the value is later HTML-encoded for display \u2014 HTML encoding does not mitigate path traversal."
        ]
      }
