        "exploitability_evidence": "Source: request.getHeaderNames()/getHeaders(name) - the name of the first non-standard HTTP header is attacker-controlled and assigned to 'param'. This flows through a switch statement (case 'A' or 'C'/'D', which is the actual path taken since guess.charAt(2)=='C') into 'bar' unmodified. 'bar' is then concatenated directly into a file path string used to build a java.net.URI and subsequently a java.io.File(fileURI) object at line ~102. No path traversal sanitization (e.g., canonicalization check, FilenameUtils.getName, or rejection of '../' sequences) is applied to 'bar' before it reaches the File constructor. The only sanitization present, ESAPI encodeForHTML(), is applied afterward to the file's toString() output for HTML display purposes only \u2014 it does not sanitize the path used to construct the File object and is inadequate for CWE-22 protection. Thus an attacker can supply a header name containing '../' sequences to escape the intended TESTFILES_DIR and reference/probe arbitrary files on the filesystem via fileTarget.exists().",
