        "exploitability_evidence": "Source: request.getHeaderNames()/getHeaders(name) - attacker fully controls the name of any non-standard HTTP header sent in the request. That header name is stored in 'param'. The ternary condition (7*42-106>200) evaluates to false (188>200 is false), so 'bar = param' unconditionally, meaning bar is fully attacker-controlled. Sink: fileName = Utils.TESTFILES_DIR + bar; fis = new FileInputStream(new File(fileName)) at line 73. No path validation, canonicalization, or filtering of '../' sequences is performed on 'bar' or 'fileName' before it reaches the FileInputStream constructor. The only encoding applied (ESAPI encoder().encodeForHTML) is used solely to safely render the fileName/error message back into the HTML response - it does not sanitize path traversal characters and is irrelevant to the file-open sink, which already executed before encoding. HTTP header names can be set by an attacker to arbitrary strings (e.g., via curl or a raw socket) including sequences like '../../../etc/passwd', so this constitutes a genuine path traversal vulnerability allowing arbitrary file read from the filesystem relative to TESTFILES_DIR.",
