      "analysis": {
        "exploitability_evidence": "Source: request.getHeaderNames() enumerates all HTTP header names sent by the client; the code picks the first non-standard header name into 'param'. Through the switch statement (deterministic case 'C' since guess=\"ABC\").charAt(2)=='C'), 'bar' is assigned 'param' unmodified. Then fileName = TESTFILES_DIR + bar concatenates the raw attacker-controlled header name onto a base directory with no path traversal filtering (no call to FilenameUtils.getName, no check for '../', no whitelist). This fileName is passed directly to 'new java.io.FileOutputStream(fileName)' \u2014 the flagged sink. The only sanitizer present, ESAPI.encoder().encodeForHTML(fileName), is applied afterward purely for the HTML response text, not before the file open, and even if applied would not neutralize path traversal sequences for a filesystem sink. Thus an attacker can send a custom HTTP header name containing '../' sequences to write/create files outside the intended TESTFILES_DIR.",
        "verdict": "True Positive",
        "confidence": "high",
        "severity_assessment": "high",
        "root_cause": "User-controlled data (an arbitrary HTTP header name) is concatenated into a file path with no path traversal sanitization or canonicalization before being used to open a FileOutputStream.",
        "impact": "An attacker can supply a crafted header name containing directory traversal sequences (e.g. '../../etc/passwd' or arbitrary paths) causing the application to create/overwrite files outside the intended directory, potentially leading to file corruption, arbitrary file write, or in some environments remote code execution (e.g., dropping a web shell in a webroot).",
        "remediation": [
          "Never build file paths directly from user input; use FilenameUtils.getName() to strip any directory components before use.",
          "Validate the resulting file name against a strict allow-list of expected file names/extensions.",
          "Resolve the final path with a canonicalization step and verify it stays within TESTFILES_DIR (e.g., File.getCanonicalPath() startsWith check).",
          "Reject header names/values containing path separators or '..' sequences before use in filesystem operations."
        ]
      }
    },
