      "analysis": {
        "exploitability_evidence": "Source: request.getHeaderNames() enumerates all incoming HTTP header names; the loop selects the first non-standard header name into 'param'. Because guess=\"ABC\" and switchTarget=guess.charAt(2)=='C', control flow reaches case 'C'/'D' and sets bar = param (attacker-controlled header name). fileName = Utils.TESTFILES_DIR + bar is then passed directly to new java.io.FileOutputStream(fileName) with no path canonicalization, allow-list check, or use of FilenameUtils.getName(). The only sanitizer present, ESAPI.encoder().encodeForHTML(fileName), is applied afterward only to the string written back in the HTTP response, not to the value used to open the file, so it does not protect the sink. This is a genuine unsanitized taint flow from an HTTP request source to a file path sink.",
        "verdict": "True Positive",
        "confidence": "medium",
        "severity_assessment": "medium",
        "root_cause": "User-influenced data (an HTTP header name) is concatenated into a file path and used to open a file without any path traversal validation, normalization, or restriction to a safe base directory before the FileOutputStream call.",
        "impact": "An attacker able to send a request with a crafted header name (e.g. containing '../' sequences, subject to what the underlying HTTP stack/parser permits) could cause the application to write files outside the intended TESTFILES_DIR, potentially overwriting arbitrary files reachable by the process or leaking directory structure information.",
        "remediation": [
          "Canonicalize the resulting file path (e.g., File.getCanonicalPath()) and verify it still resides within the intended base directory before use.",
          "Reject or strip path traversal sequences ('../', absolute paths) from any value used to build a file path.",
          "Use org.apache.commons.io.FilenameUtils.getName(...) or similar to extract only the file name component, discarding any directory traversal segments.",
          "Avoid deriving file paths from arbitrary/unstructured request data such as header names; use a fixed allow-list of expected values instead."
        ]
      }
    },
