          "sanitizer_hint": -0.1
        }
      },
      "analysis": {
        "exploitability_evidence": "Source: request.getHeaderNames()/request.getHeaders(name) supplies an arbitrary HTTP header name fully controlled by the client. This value is assigned to `param`, then `bar = param.split(\" \")[0]` (still attacker-controlled, no sanitization applied). `bar` is then passed directly to `request.getSession().putValue(\"userid\", bar)` at line 65. No validation, allow-listing, or encoding is applied to `bar` before it is stored in the session. The later call to `Utils.encodeForHTML(bar)` only affects the HTTP response body and does not sanitize or replace the value already stored in the session object \u2014 the trust boundary violation (storing raw untrusted data as a session attribute) has already occurred by that point.",
        "verdict": "True Positive",
        "confidence": "high",
        "severity_assessment": "low",
        "root_cause": "Unvalidated data derived from an HTTP request header is stored directly into the HttpSession via putValue() without any sanitization or validation, violating the trust boundary between untrusted user input and the session storage.",
        "impact": "Untrusted data persisted in the session can later be treated as trusted by other application logic, potentially leading to session poisoning, logic errors, or downstream injection issues if the value is used in a sensitive context without re-validation.",
        "remediation": [
          "Validate/allow-list header names before using them as session attribute values.",
          "Apply appropriate sanitization or encoding before storing any user-controlled data in the session.",
          "Avoid using raw HTTP header content as session attribute values; use internally generated identifiers instead."
        ]
      }
    },
