      "analysis": {
        "exploitability_evidence": "The candidate source is the first non-standard HTTP header name captured into 'param' via request.getHeaderNames()/getHeaders(name). However, 'param' is only assigned to 'bar' in the 'A', 'C', or 'D' branches of a switch on switchTarget, which is hard-coded as guess.charAt(1) where guess=\"ABC\" \u2014 i.e., switchTarget is always the literal 'B'. The 'B' case unconditionally sets bar = \"bob\", so the tainted param value can never reach 'bar'. The sink request.getSession().setAttribute(\"userid\", bar) therefore always receives the constant \"bob\", not attacker-controlled header data. The taint path is structurally dead code.",
