      "analysis": {
        "exploitability_evidence": "Source: request.getHeaders(\"BenchmarkTest00262\") returns an attacker-controlled HTTP header value. It is URL-decoded via java.net.URLDecoder.decode(param, \"UTF-8\") (decoding does not sanitize, it only reverses percent-encoding). Because guess=\"ABC\" and switchTarget='C', the switch statement's case 'C' branch executes 'bar = param;', propagating the tainted value unchanged. bar is then passed directly into 'new java.io.File(bar)' with no path normalization, whitelist check, or canonicalization against a base directory. The only sanitization present, ESAPI encodeForHTML(), is applied afterward solely to the string written into the HTTP response body (mitigating XSS) and has no effect on the path used to construct the File object or on fileTarget.exists(). Thus the file existence check operates on a fully attacker-controlled path, including sequences like '../' that could probe or leak information about arbitrary files on the filesystem.",
        "verdict": "True Positive",
        "confidence": "high"
      }
    },
