      "analysis": {
        "exploitability_evidence": "Source: request.getHeaders(\"BenchmarkTest00263\") header value assigned to param, then URL-decoded. This tainted param is only assigned to 'bar' inside switch cases 'A', 'C', or 'D'. However switchTarget is computed as guess.charAt(1) where guess is the constant string \"ABC\", so switchTarget is always the constant 'B'. The switch statement therefore always executes case 'B', which sets bar = \"bob\" (a hardcoded constant), and the branches that would assign the tainted param to bar are unreachable dead code. fileName is built as Utils.TESTFILES_DIR + bar, which is always TESTFILES_DIR + \"bob\", never influenced by user input, before reaching the FileInputStream sink at line 79.",
        "verdict": "False Positive",
        "confidence": "high",
        "severity_assessment": "low",
        "root_cause": "The switch statement's controlling variable (switchTarget) is derived from a hardcoded constant string (\"ABC\").charAt(1), which always evaluates to 'B'. This makes the case labels 'A', 'C', 'D' -- the only branches that assign the tainted header value to 'bar' -- unreachable dead code. As a result, 'bar' is always the literal \"bob\", so the file path passed to FileInputStream is never influenced by attacker input.",
        "impact": "No real-world impact since the vulnerable code path is unreachable; the file path used at the sink is always a hardcoded value.",
        "remediation": [
          "Confirm this is intentional dead-code test scaffolding (as in OWASP Benchmark's 'true negative' variants) rather than production logic.",
          "If similar patterns exist in real code, remove dead branches or ensure the discriminant is genuinely derived from runtime/user data if the branch is meant to be reachable.",
          "Regardless, apply canonicalization and path validation (e.g., verify resolved path is within TESTFILES_DIR) before any file access to defend against future refactoring that could re-enable the tainted branch."
        ]
      }
