      "analysis": {
        "exploitability_evidence": "Source: request.getHeaders(\"BenchmarkTest00264\") reads an attacker-controlled HTTP header. Its first value is URL-decoded via java.net.URLDecoder.decode(param, \"UTF-8\") and assigned to 'bar' through a trivial always-true conditional (no sanitization effect). 'bar' is then concatenated directly into fileName = Utils.TESTFILES_DIR + bar and passed to new java.io.FileInputStream(new java.io.File(fileName)) \u2014 the flagged sink. No path canonicalization, no allow-list check, no use of FilenameUtils.getName or similar to strip directory traversal sequences is performed before the file is opened. The only sanitization present (ESAPI encodeForHTML) is applied later to the fileName string only when echoing it back in the HTTP response, which is output encoding for HTML context and provides no protection against path traversal at the file-open sink. Thus a header value like '../../etc/passwd' (URL-encoded) would traverse outside TESTFILES_DIR and be opened.",
        "verdict": "True Positive",
        "confidence": "high",
        "severity_assessment": "high",
        "root_cause": "User-controlled HTTP header value is decoded and concatenated into a file path without any path traversal sanitization (no canonicalization, no allow-listing, no stripping of '../' sequences) before being used to construct a File/FileInputStream.",
        "impact": "An attacker can supply a crafted header value containing '../' sequences to read arbitrary files on the filesystem accessible to the application process, leading to disclosure of sensitive files (e.g., configuration files, credentials, source code).",
        "remediation": [
          "Use java.nio.file.Path.normalize() combined with a strict check that the resolved path stays within the intended base directory (TESTFILES_DIR) before opening the file.",
          "Alternatively, use org.apache.commons.io.FilenameUtils.getName(bar) to strip any directory components and only allow a bare filename.",
          "Maintain an allow-list of permitted filenames or use a mapping (e.g., an ID to filename) rather than accepting raw user input as part of the path.",
          "Reject requests where the decoded header value contains path traversal sequences ('..', absolute path indicators, etc.)."
        ]
      }
    },
