      "analysis": {
        "exploitability_evidence": "Source: request.getHeaders(\"Referer\") retrieves the attacker-controllable Referer HTTP header value into `param`. It is URL-decoded (java.net.URLDecoder.decode), which does not perform any HTML/JS escaping. The subsequent switch statement is deterministic (guess=\"ABC\", switchTarget=guess.charAt(2)=='C'), so the 'C'/'D' branch (bar = param) is always taken, and `bar` always equals the decoded Referer value. This value is passed unescaped to response.getWriter().print(bar), which is a raw servlet output stream write \u2014 no HTML entity or contextual encoding is applied anywhere on this path (no ESAPI/OWASP encoder, no Encode.forHtml, no template escaping). Referer header can be freely set by an attacker (e.g., via a raw HTTP client, CSRF-style requests, or crafted resources) making this a genuine reflected XSS sink.",
        "verdict": "True Positive",
        "confidence": "high",
        "severity_assessment": "medium",
        "root_cause": "Attacker-controlled Referer header value is decoded and written directly to the HTTP response body via response.getWriter().print() without any output encoding or HTML escaping, bypassing template-based auto-escaping.",
        "impact": "An attacker can craft a request with a malicious Referer header containing HTML/JavaScript payload; when reflected back unescaped in the response, this results in reflected Cross-Site Scripting (XSS), allowing script execution in the victim's browser session, session hijacking, or credential theft.",
        "remediation": [
          "HTML-encode the Referer value before writing it to the response (e.g., using OWASP ESAPI Encoder.encodeForHTML or Java's built-in HtmlUtils/StringEscapeUtils).",
          "Avoid writing raw request data via getWriter().print(); use a templating engine (JSP/JSF) that auto-escapes output.",
          "Apply context-aware output encoding matching where the data is placed (HTML body, attribute, JS, etc.).",
          "Consider validating/whitelisting expected header formats rather than trusting arbitrary header content."
        ]
      }
    },
