      "analysis": {
        "exploitability_evidence": "Source: 'Referer' header via request.getHeaders('Referer') -> param (URL-decoded, attacker-controlled). This flows into a ternary: bar = (7*18)+num > 200 ? \"This_should_always_happen\" : param. Evaluating the condition: 7*18=126, plus num=106 gives 232, which is always > 200. Therefore the ternary always evaluates to the constant string \"This_should_always_happen\" and 'param' (the tainted value) is never assigned to 'bar'. The sink response.getWriter().printf(Locale.US, bar, obj) therefore always operates on a hardcoded constant, not on attacker input. No taint reaches the sink in any actual execution path.",
        "verdict": "False Positive",
        "confidence": "high",
        "severity_assessment": "info",
        "root_cause": "The conditional expression used to select between the tainted value and a constant is a tautology (always evaluates true due to fixed arithmetic on constants), so the tainted branch is dead code and never executed. This is a classic OWASP Benchmark 'false positive by design' test case.",
        "impact": "None \u2014 the tainted data path is unreachable at runtime, so no XSS can occur via this specific code path.",
        "remediation": [
          "No fix required for this instance since the branch is unreachable; however, if the logic were ever changed to make the condition data-dependent, the tainted 'param' value must be HTML-encoded (e.g., via OWASP Java Encoder) before being written via printf/getWriter.",
          "As a general hardening measure, avoid writing directly to the response Writer/OutputStream with unescaped data; use a templating engine or explicit output encoding for any user-controlled content."
        ]
      }
