          "key": "org/owasp/benchmark/testcode/BenchmarkTest00287.java:59:13-59:69:java.lang.security.audit.xss.no-direct-response-writer.no-direct-response-writer",
          "decision": "escalate",
          "reason": "User-controlled Referer header is URL-decoded and written directly to response writer without HTML escaping, enabling XSS.",
          "error": null,
          "elapsed_seconds": 1.289
        }
      },
      "analysis": {
        "exploitability_evidence": "Source: request.getHeaders(\"Referer\") at line 44 reads the attacker-controlled Referer HTTP header. The first header value is assigned to 'param', then URL-decoded via java.net.URLDecoder.decode(param, \"UTF-8\") at line 50 (decoding does not neutralize HTML/JS metacharacters like <, >, \", '). The decoded value is copied to 'bar' and, at line 59, written directly to the response via response.getWriter().write(bar.toCharArray(), 0, length) with no HTML-escaping, output encoding, or validation applied anywhere on the path. Content-Type is text/html, so any injected markup/script in the Referer header will be reflected and executed in the victim's browser.",
        "verdict": "True Positive",
        "confidence": "high",
        "severity_assessment": "high",
        "root_cause": "The application takes an attacker-controlled HTTP header (Referer) and does URL-decodOD(which no does not perform HTML/output encoding) then writes it raw string directly to the response body via the raw OutputStream/Writer, bypassing any templating engine's automatic escaping.",
        "impact": ": attacker can craft a request with a malicious Referer header (e.g. containing a <script> payload) that gets reflected verbatim in the HTTP response, resulting in reflected Cross-Site Scripting (XSS). This can lead to session hijacking, credential theft, defacement, or further client-side attacks against victims who click a crafted link or whose browser sends the malicious Referer.",
        "remediation": [
          "HTML-encode the header value before writing it to the response (e.g., use OWASP Java Encoder's Encode.forHtml() or similar) prior to output.",
          "Avoid writing raw bytes/chars directly to the response Writer/OutputStream for dynamic content; use a templating/view technology (JSP, Thymeleaf, etc.) that auto-escapes output.",
          "If the Referer value must be reflected, validate/sanitize it against an allowlist and encode for the specific output context (HTML body, attribute, JS, etc.).",
          "Add a strong Content-Security-Policy header to reduce impact of any residual XSS.",
          "Do not rely on the X-XSS-Protection header for protection; it is deprecated in modern browsers."
        ]
      }
    },
