      "analysis": {
        "exploitability_evidence": "Source: request.getHeaders(\"Referer\") retrieves the attacker-controlled Referer HTTP header (line 44). This value is stored in 'param', then URL-decoded via java.net.URLDecoder.decode(param, \"UTF-8\") (line 50) \u2014 decoding does not sanitize or escape HTML, it only reverses URL encoding. The decoded value is then wrapped in a StringBuilder and concatenated with the literal '_SafeStuff' suffix (lines 52-53), producing 'bar'. This 'bar' string is then written directly to the HTTP response via response.getWriter().write(bar) (line 57) \u2014 the exact sink flagged by the rule. No HTML entity encoding, output encoding, or allow-list validation is applied anywhere on this path; the only transformation is URL-decoding and string concatenation with a fixed suffix, neither of which neutralizes HTML/JS special characters like <, >, \", '. Additionally, setHeader(\"X-XSS-Protection\", \"0\") explicitly disables the browser's built-in XSS auditor, removing a potential mitigating control. An attacker who controls the Referer header (trivial via a crafted HTTP request or malicious linking page) can inject arbitrary HTML/JavaScript that will be reflected verbatim in the response body, resulting in reflected XSS.",
        "verdict": "True Positive",
        "confidence": "high",
        "severity_assessment": "medium",
        "root_cause": "Attacker-controlled Referer header value is URL-decoded and concatenated into an output string that is written directly to the HTTP response via getWriter().write() without any HTML/JS output encoding or sanitization.",
        "impact": "An attacker can craft a request with a malicious Referer header (or lure a victim via a page with a script-controlled Referer) causing arbitrary JavaScript execution in the victim's browser session, leading to session hijacking, credential theft, defacement, or further client-side attacks.",
        "remediation": [
          "HTML-encode the Referer value before writing it into the response (e.g., use OWASP Java Encoder's Encode.forHtml() or similar context-appropriate encoder).",
          "Avoid writing raw, unencoded request-derived data directly via response.getWriter(); use a templating engine (JSP/JSF) that auto-escapes output.",
          "Do not reflect the Referer header back to the client unless strictly necessary; if needed, validate against an expected format/allow-list.",
          "Re-enable or properly configure X-XSS-Protection header (though this is a defense-in-depth measure, not a substitute for proper output encoding)."
        ]
      }
