      "analysis": {
        "exploitability_evidence": "Source: request.getHeaders(\"BenchmarkTest00295\") returns an attacker-controlled HTTP header value, captured into 'param'. It is URL-decoded (java.net.URLDecoder.decode) which does not sanitize shell metacharacters, only decodes URL encoding. Then 'bar' is assigned via a ternary that always evaluates to false (7*42-106=188, not >200), so bar always equals param (i.e., attacker input, undiminished). This bar is concatenated into args[2] = \"echo \" + bar, and args[] is passed to `new ProcessBuilder(args)` at line 75, then pb.start() executes it. There is no whitelist, escaping, or removal of shell metacharacters (e.g., ;, |, &&, $()) applied to bar before it reaches the ProcessBuilder sink. Although ProcessBuilder with an array avoids shell parsing on some platforms directly, here args[0]/args[1] are 'sh'/'-c' or 'cmd.exe'/'/c', explicitly invoking a shell interpreter with the tainted string as the command argument, which is a classic OS command injection to a shell -c invocation, fully attacker-controllable.",
        "verdict": "True Positive",
        "confidence": "high",
        "severity_assessment": "high",
        "root_cause": "Untrusted HTTP header value is decoded and passed unsanitized into a shell command executed via ProcessBuilder(\"sh\",\"-c\", \"echo \"+bar) or (\"cmd.exe\",\"/c\", ...), allowing shell metacharacter injection.",
        "impact": "An attacker can inject arbitrary shell commands via a crafted HTTP header value, leading to remote code execution on the server, potentially resulting in full system compromise, data exfiltration, or lateral movement.",
        "remediation": [
          "Avoid invoking a shell (\"sh -c\", \"cmd.exe /c\") with user-supplied input; if executing OS commands is unavoidable, invoke the target binary directly with an argument array (no shell) so metacharacters aren't interpreted.",
          "Validate/whitelist the header value against a strict allow-list of expected characters or values before use.",
          "Use ESAPI or similar to strip/reject shell metacharacters (;, |, &, $, `, >, <, etc.) if a shell must be used.",
          "Consider avoiding exec of external OS commands entirely if functionality can be implemented in-process."
        ]
      }
    },
