      "analysis": {
        "exploitability_evidence": "Source: request.getHeaders(\"BenchmarkTest00303\") - an attacker-controlled HTTP header value assigned to 'param'. It is URL-decoded and then run through Base64.encodeBase64/decodeBase64 which is a no-op round-trip (encodeBase64 followed by decodeBase64 restores the original bytes) - not a sanitizer for shell metacharacters. The resulting 'bar' variable is concatenated directly into args used for Runtime.exec(args): on non-Windows, args = {\"sh\",\"-c\", cmd + bar} where cmd+bar becomes the command string interpreted by 'sh -c', allowing shell metacharacters (e.g. ';', '|', '&&') in bar to inject arbitrary commands. No whitelist, regex validation, or shell-escaping is applied to 'bar' before it reaches the exec sink.",
        "verdict": "True Positive",
        "confidence": "high",
        "severity_assessment": "high",
        "root_cause": "User-supplied HTTP header value flows through URL-decoding and a no-op Base64 encode/decode round trip (which does not neutralize shell metacharacters) directly into a command string executed via Runtime.exec with 'sh -c', enabling OS command injection.",
        "impact": "An attacker can inject arbitrary OS commands by supplying shell metacharacters in the 'BenchmarkTest00303' HTTP header, potentially leading to remote code execution, data exfiltration, or full system compromise.",
        "remediation": [
          "Avoid passing user input to shell interpreters (sh -c/cmd.exe /c); use APIs that take command and arguments as separate array elements without a shell wrapper.",
          "Validate/whitelist the header value against a strict allow-list of expected characters (e.g., alphanumeric only) before use.",
          "If shell invocation is unavoidable, use proper OS-level escaping libraries designed for command argument quoting.",
          "Consider using ProcessBuilder without invoking a shell interpreter, and pass arguments as separate array elements to avoid metacharacter interpretation."
        ]
      }
