      "analysis": {
        "exploitability_evidence": "Source: request.getHeaders(\"BenchmarkTest00324\") returns attacker-controlled HTTP header values. The first header value is taken (param), URL-decoded, then passed through HtmlUtils.htmlEscape(param) to produce 'bar', which is stored in the session via request.getSession().putValue(\"userid\", bar). HtmlUtils.htmlEscape only encodes HTML metacharacters (<,>,&,\",') for safe display in an HTML context \u2014 it does not validate or restrict the semantic content of the value, so raw attacker-controlled text (with escaped special chars) is still stored as trusted session state. No validation (e.g., whitelist/format check) is performed before the session write, so the trust boundary between untrusted HTTP input and the session object is crossed.",
        "verdict": "True Positive",
        "confidence": "medium",
        "severity_assessment": "low",
        "root_cause": "User-supplied HTTP header data is placed into an HttpSession attribute after only HTML-escaping (a sanitizer intended for HTML output, not session trust validation), so no actual validation of the value's legitimacy occurs before it becomes part of trusted session state.",
        "impact": "Storing unvalidated, attacker-influenced data in the session can lead to trust boundary violations where later code assumes session attributes are trustworthy, potentially enabling session data poisoning, logic errors, or downstream injection issues if the session value is used in security-sensitive decisions.",
        "remediation": [
          "Validate and canonicalize header input against an expected format/whitelist before storing it in the session.",
          "Avoid using HTML-escaping as a substitute for input validation when the sanitized value's purpose is not HTML output.",
          "Use dedicated input validation libraries (e.g., regex allow-lists) for data that will be persisted in session state.",
          "Consider marking data derived from user input as untrusted throughout its lifecycle and re-validate before using it in trust-sensitive operations."
        ]
      }
    },
