        "severity_assessment": "low",
        "root_cause": "Attacker-controlled HTTP header data is written into the HttpSession without any validation or reclassification step establishing it as trusted; HTML-escaping is an output encoder for a different sink (HTML rendering) and does not mitigate the trust-boundary concern of mixing tainted request data with trusted session state.",
        "impact": "Storing unvalidated request data in session attributes can lead to session data poisoning, incorrect authorization/business logic decisions if the value is later trusted implicitly, and inconsistent trust assumptions across the application that read this session attribute expecting sanitized/trusted content.",
        "remediation": [
          "Validate/normalize the header value against an expected format or allow-list before storing it in the session.",
          "Avoid storing raw request-derived values under trust-implying session keys like 'userid'; use a dedicated 'untrustedUserId' style key or perform explicit business validation first.",
          "If the value must be stored, document and enforce that any code reading it treats it as still-untrusted, or perform canonicalization/validation immediately after decoding."
        ]
      }
