      "analysis": {
        "exploitability_evidence": "Source: request.getHeaders(\"BenchmarkTest00329\") -> param, URL-decoded. This value is assigned to 'bar' only in the else branch of 'if ((7 * 42) - num > 200) bar = \"This_should_always_happen\"; else bar = param;'. Evaluating the condition: 7*42=294, num=86, 294-86=208, and 208>200 is always true. Therefore the if-branch is always taken and bar is always set to the constant string \"This_should_always_happen\" \u2014 the else branch assigning the tainted 'param' to 'bar' is dead code and never executes. Consequently 'sql = \"{call \" + bar + \"}\"' always uses the constant value, and the CallableStatement is built from a fixed, non-attacker-controlled string. The tainted param never reaches the sink in any real execution path.",
        "verdict": "False Positive",
        "confidence": "high",
        "severity_assessment": "low",
        "root_cause": "The branch condition is a constant arithmetic expression that always evaluates to true, making the assignment of tainted user input to the SQL-building variable 'bar' unreachable dead code. The SAST tool's taint tracker does not evaluate constant folding and flags the unreachable else-branch as if it executes.",
        "impact": "None in practice: since the vulnerable code path can never execute, there is no way for an attacker to inject SQL via this endpoint. If the constant logic were ever changed to allow the else branch to execute, then unsanitized header input would flow directly into a CallableStatement built via string concatenation, enabling SQL injection.",
        "remediation": [
          "Even though currently unreachable, avoid using string concatenation to build SQL/CallableStatement text; use parameterized queries or PreparedStatement/CallableStatement placeholders (?) for any value that could originate from user input.",
          "Remove or refactor the dead-code always-true conditional so that future maintenance does not inadvertently expose the tainted branch.",
          "If dynamic call statements are required, validate/allowlist the header value against expected stored procedure names before use."
        ]
      }
