      "analysis": {
        "exploitability_evidence": "Source: request.getHeaders(\"BenchmarkTest00335\") returns attacker-controlled HTTP header value, stored in param. It is URL-decoded (java.net.URLDecoder.decode) which does not sanitize for SQL. The if-condition (500/42)+196>200 always evaluates true (11+196=207>200), so bar=param unconditionally, meaning bar is fully attacker-controlled. This bar is directly concatenated into the SQL string: \"SELECT * from USERS where USERNAME=? and PASSWORD='\" + bar + \"'\". This concatenated string is passed to connection.prepareStatement(sql, ...) - the injection is baked into the query text itself before the JDBC driver ever parses it as a parameterized query. Only the USERNAME placeholder is a real bind parameter (statement.setString(1,\"foo\")); the PASSWORD value is raw string concatenation with no escaping, encoding, or validation applied anywhere on the path from getHeaders() to the SQL string. This is a textbook SQL injection sink.",
        "verdict": "True Positive",
        "confidence": "high",
        "severity_assessment": "high",
        "root_cause": "Attacker-controlled HTTP header value is concatenated directly into a SQL query string instead of being passed as a bound parameter, and no sanitizer/encoder for SQL context is applied on the path.",
        "impact": "An attacker can inject arbitrary SQL via the 'BenchmarkTest00335' HTTP header (e.g., closing the quote and appending OR/UNION clauses) to bypass authentication, exfiltrate data from the USERS table, or manipulate/destroy data depending on DB privileges.",
        "remediation": [
          "Use a fully parameterized query: bind both USERNAME and PASSWORD as PreparedStatement parameters instead of concatenating bar into the SQL string.",
          "Do not build SQL strings via concatenation with any user-supplied input, including HTTP headers.",
          "Apply strict input validation/allow-listing on the header value if it must be used, in addition to parameterization.",
          "Enable and enforce use of PreparedStatement.setString for all variable parts of the query."
        ]
      }
    },
