      "analysis": {
        "exploitability_evidence": "Source: request.getParameter(\"BenchmarkTest00359\") assigned to param (line 43). The if/else construct at lines 49-50 always evaluates true (500/42 + 196 = 11+196=207 > 200), so bar = param unconditionally, meaning bar is fully attacker-controlled with no sanitization. bar is then passed directly into 'new java.io.File(bar)' at line 53, which is the flagged sink for CWE-22 path traversal. The subsequent code only calls fileTarget.exists() to check for existence and encodes the string for HTML output via ESAPI encodeForHTML - this encoding is for XSS prevention on the response body, not for path traversal sanitization, and does not restrict or validate the file path itself. There is no canonicalization, no restriction to a base directory, and no rejection of '../' sequences before the File object is constructed and its existence checked.",
        "verdict": "True Positive",
        "confidence": "high",
        "severity_assessment": "medium",
        "root_cause": "User-supplied HTTP parameter is used to construct a java.io.File path without any validation, canonicalization, or restriction to a safe base directory, allowing directory traversal sequences (e.g., '../') to reference arbitrary filesystem locations.",
        "impact": "An attacker can supply path traversal sequences to probe for the existence of arbitrary files on the filesystem (information disclosure via existence oracle), and if this pattern is reused elsewhere for read/write operations, it could lead to arbitrary file read/write outside the intended directory.",
        "remediation": [
          "Validate and canonicalize the user-supplied filename against an allow-list of expected values or a fixed base directory using File.getCanonicalPath() and verifying it starts with the intended base path.",
          "Use org.apache.commons.io.FilenameUtils.getName(...) to strip any directory components from user input before constructing the File object.",
          "Reject any input containing '..' or path separators before using it in file operations."
        ]
      }
    },
