      "analysis": {
        "exploitability_evidence": "Attacker-controlled source: request.getParameter(\"BenchmarkTest00360\") assigned to param. It is passed through Base64.encodeBase64() then Base64.decodeBase64() and wrapped in new String(...) - a round trip that returns the exact original string content, providing no sanitization. This value (bar) is directly concatenated: fileName = Utils.TESTFILES_DIR + bar, then used to construct new java.io.File(fileName) which is opened via FileInputStream. No path traversal validation (no check for '../', no canonicalization, no FilenameUtils.getName()) is applied anywhere on this path. The only sanitization present (ESAPI encodeForHTML) is applied later to the fileName string only for HTML output display, not before the file access - it does not affect the path traversal sink at all. Thus a value like '../../../../etc/passwd' would flow unmodified into the FileInputStream constructor.",
        "verdict": "True Positive",
        "confidence": "high",
        "severity_assessment": "high",
        "root_cause": "User-supplied HTTP parameter is concatenated into a file path with no path traversal sanitization (no canonicalization, no allow-list, no FilenameUtils.getName()) before being used to open a file via FileInputStream. The Base64 encode/decode round-trip is a no-op and does not sanitize the input.",
        "impact": "An attacker can supply a parameter value containing '../' sequences to read arbitrary files on the filesystem accessible to the application process (e.g., configuration files, credentials, or other sensitive data), leading to information disclosure.",
        "remediation": [
          "Canonicalize the resulting file path (e.g., File.getCanonicalPath()) and verify it resides within the intended TESTFILES_DIR before opening it.",
          "Use org.apache.commons.io.FilenameUtils.getName() to strip any directory components from the user-supplied value before concatenation.",
          "Maintain an allow-list of permitted file names/paths and reject any input not matching the allow-list.",
          "Reject inputs containing path traversal sequences such as '../' or absolute path indicators."
        ]
      }
