      "analysis": {
        "exploitability_evidence": "Source: request.getParameter(\"BenchmarkTest00362\") assigned to `param`/`bar`. This value is directly concatenated into `fileName = Utils.TESTFILES_DIR + bar` with no validation. `fileName` is then passed unmodified into `new java.io.File(fileName)` and used to construct a `FileOutputStream`, which is the sink. The only encoding present, `ESAPI.encoder().encodeForHTML(fileName)`, is applied afterward purely to safely render the filename string in the HTTP response (HTML-context encoding) \u2014 it does not affect the file path already used to open the file and is not a path-traversal sanitizer. There is no call to FilenameUtils.getName, path canonicalization/normalization, or an allow-list check on `bar` before it reaches the file system sink, so a value like '../../etc/passwd' or similar would let an attacker write outside TESTFILES_DIR.",
        "verdict": "True Positive",
        "confidence": "high",
        "severity_assessment": "high",
        "root_cause": "User-supplied HTTP parameter is concatenated into a file path and used to open a FileOutputStream without any path traversal sanitization (no canonicalization, no filename-only extraction, no allow-list validation).",
        "impact": "An attacker can supply a path traversal sequence (e.g., ../../) in the request parameter to write files outside the intended TESTFILES_DIR directory, potentially overwriting arbitrary files on the server file system, leading to data corruption, code execution (if writable paths are executable), or denial of service.",
        "remediation": [
          "Use org.apache.commons.io.FilenameUtils.getName(bar) or similar to strip any directory components before building the file path.",
          "After constructing the File object, canonicalize it (File#getCanonicalPath) and verify the resulting path is still within the intended base directory (TESTFILES_DIR) before performing any file operation.",
          "Reject or sanitize the parameter value with an allow-list of expected file name characters instead of relying on output encoding for HTML.",
          "Avoid exposing raw file system paths to arbitrary user-supplied names; use indirect references (e.g., IDs mapped server-side to fixed file names)."
        ]
      }
