      "analysis": {
        "exploitability_evidence": "The tainted source is request.getParameter(\"BenchmarkTest00406\") assigned to param, then via an if/else bar is set to either the constant \"This_should_always_happen\" or param. The condition governing this branch is (7*42)-num > 200 with num=86: 7*42=294, 294-86=208, and 208>200 evaluates to true always. Therefore the true branch (bar = constant) is always taken, and the else branch (bar = param, i.e., tainted user input) is unreachable dead code. bar is later concatenated into args passed to Runtime.exec, but bar never actually contains attacker-controlled data because the constant assignment always executes. Hence the exec sink never receives tainted input in this code path.",
        "verdict": "False Positive",
        "confidence": "high",
        "severity_assessment": "low",
        "root_cause": "The branch that assigns tainted user input to the variable used in the exec() call is unreachable due to a hardcoded arithmetic condition that always evaluates to true, causing the constant branch to always execute instead of the tainted branch.",
        "impact": "None in current code state; if the condition were ever changed to allow the else branch to execute, the tainted param would flow unsanitized into Runtime.exec, enabling OS command injection.",
        "remediation": [
          "Remove or fix the dead-code branch so behavior is deterministic and reviewed.",
          "If user input must reach exec(), apply strict allow-list validation on the parameter before use.",
          "Avoid passing raw request parameters into ProcessBuilder/Runtime.exec argument arrays."
        ]
      }
