      "analysis": {
        "exploitability_evidence": "Source: request.getParameter(\"BenchmarkTest00408\") assigned to param. However, bar (which flows into args -> Runtime.exec) is set via: int num = 86; if ((7*42)-num > 200) bar = \"This_should_always_happen\"; else bar = param;. Evaluating the constant expression: 7*42-86 = 208, and 208>200 is always true, so the if-branch is always taken and bar is always assigned the hardcoded string \"This_should_always_happen\", never param. The else branch that would assign the tainted param value to bar is unreachable dead code. Consequently args passed to r.exec() never contain attacker-controlled data from the HTTP request; the ProcessBuilder/exec sink only ever receives constant strings.",
