      "analysis": {
        "exploitability_evidence": "Source: request.getParameter(\"BenchmarkTest00409\") -> param (line 43). The switch statement is deterministic (guess=\"ABC\", switchTarget=guess.charAt(2)=='C'), so it always executes case 'C'/'D': bar = param; -> bar directly holds attacker-controlled input with no validation. On non-Windows path (line ~78-80): cmd = Utils.getOSCommandString(\"ls \") returns \"ls \" (no user data), then args = {\"sh\", \"-c\", cmd + bar} -> the raw, unsanitized param is concatenated into a shell command string that is executed via `sh -c <cmd+bar>` through Runtime.exec(args, argsEnv, dir) at line 90. No encoding, escaping, or allowlist check is applied to param/bar anywhere in the flow (Utils.getOSCommandString only prepends a fixed prefix based on OS, doesn't sanitize appended user data). Because the command is invoked with `sh -c`, shell metacharacters (;, |, `` ` ``, $()) in param would be interpreted, enabling command injection.",
        "verdict": "True Positive",
        "confidence": "high",
        "severity_assessment": "high",
        "root_cause": "User-supplied HTTP parameter is assigned to a variable that is concatenated directly into a shell command string executed via Runtime.exec with 'sh -c', without any sanitization, escaping, or allowlisting of shell metacharacters.",
        "impact": "An attacker can inject arbitrary OS commands executed with the privileges of the web application process, potentially leading to full system compromise, data exfiltration, or lateral movement.",
        "remediation": [
          "Avoid invoking a shell (sh -c) with concatenated user input; use Runtime.exec or ProcessBuilder with a fixed executable and pass user data only as separate, non-shell-interpreted arguments.",
          "Validate/allowlist the parameter value against a strict set of expected values before use in any command.",
          "If shell invocation is unavoidable, properly escape/quote the user-supplied value for the target shell, or reject input containing shell metacharacters.",
          "Consider using safer APIs (e.g., Java NIO Files APIs) instead of shelling out to OS commands like 'ls' or 'echo'."
        ]
      }
    },
