      "analysis": {
        "exploitability_evidence": "Source: request.getParameter(\"BenchmarkTest00425\") assigned to `param` (attacker-controlled, no validation on null-check only). This value flows into `sbxyz2192.append(...)` producing `bar`, which is passed directly to `request.getSession().putValue(\"userid\", bar)` at line 50 \u2014 the flagged sink. No sanitizer or validator is applied to `bar` before this putValue call; the only sanitization present (`Utils.encodeForHTML`) is applied afterward, solely for the HTML output in the response, not before the session storage. Thus raw, unvalidated user input is stored as a session attribute, satisfying the trust boundary violation pattern the rule detects.",
        "verdict": "True Positive",
        "confidence": "high",
        "severity_assessment": "low",
        "root_cause": "User-supplied HTTP request parameter is stored into the HttpSession via putValue/setAttribute without any validation, sanitization, or type-checking, blurring the boundary between trusted session data and untrusted user input.",
        "impact": "Untrusted data persisted in the session can be later trusted implicitly by other parts of the application, potentially leading to session poisoning, logic bypass, or injection issues if the session attribute is later used in a sensitive context (e.g., authorization decisions, HTML rendering, or database queries) without re-validation.",
        "remediation": [
          "Validate and constrain the input (e.g., allow-list characters, length limits, expected format) before storing it in the session.",
          "Avoid storing raw user input directly as session attributes; store validated/typed values instead.",
          "If the value must be redisplayed, ensure encoding is applied every time it is used, not just once at output.",
          "Consider using session attribute names/values that are generated or mapped server-side rather than directly derived from user input."
        ]
      }
    },
