        "exploitability_evidence": "Source: request.getParameter(\"BenchmarkTest00431\") is stored in 'param'. The ternary condition (7*42)-106>200 evaluates to false at compile time (294-106=188, not >200), so 'bar' is always assigned 'param' unmodified - this is just benchmark obfuscation, not a sanitizer. 'bar' is then directly concatenated into the SQL string: \"... PASSWORD='\" + bar + \"'\". This raw string is passed to DatabaseHelper.JDBCtemplate.query(sql, rowMapper) with no parameterization (no '?' placeholders, no PreparedStatement args). No validation, escaping, or allow-listing is applied to 'param'/'bar' anywhere on this path. The only sanitization visible (ESAPI encodeForHTML) is applied to output values (rs.getString results, or the sql string in an error message), not to the input before it reaches the SQL sink. This is a direct, unsanitized taint flow from HTTP parameter to SQL query execution.",
