        "confidence": "high",
        "severity_assessment": "high",
        "root_cause": "User-supplied HTTP parameter is concatenated directly into a SQL query string without parameterization or sanitization, and the resulting string is executed via Spring's JdbcTemplate.batchUpdate(String), which runs it as literal SQL.",
        "impact": "An attacker can inject arbitrary SQL through the BenchmarkTest00434 parameter to bypass authentication, extract or manipulate database contents, or in some database configurations execute further malicious SQL statements, since batchUpdate executes the string as-is.",
        "remediation": [
          "Use parameterized queries (PreparedStatement or JdbcTemplate.update(sql, params...)) instead of string concatenation for the SQL command.",
          "Never build SQL by concatenating raw user input; use bind variables for the PASSWORD (and any other user-controlled) value.",
          "If dynamic SQL construction is unavoidable, apply strict allow-list validation and proper escaping specific to the target SQL dialect before use in the query."
        ]
      }
