        "severity_assessment": "high",
        "root_cause": "User-supplied HTTP parameter is concatenated directly into a SQL query string and executed via a plain java.sql.Statement instead of a parameterized PreparedStatement; the intervening ternary condition is a constant expression that always resolves to pass the tainted value through unchanged.",
        "impact": "An attacker can inject arbitrary SQL via the BenchmarkTest00438 parameter (e.g., in the PASSWORD field) to bypass authentication, extract, modify, or delete data, or in some DBs execute further malicious commands.",
        "remediation": [
          "Use PreparedStatement with bind parameters instead of building SQL via string concatenation.",
          "Validate/whitelist input format if a raw Statement must be retained.",
          "Remove reliance on the always-false ternary as a security control since it does not sanitize input.",
          "Apply least-privilege database credentials to limit blast radius of injection."
        ]
      }
