          "decision": "escalate",
          "reason": "User-controlled request parameter flows through doSomething() into SQL string concatenation without prepared statements, enabling SQL injection.",
          "error": null,
          "elapsed_seconds": 1.05
        }
      },
      "analysis": {
        "exploitability_evidence": "Source: request.getParameter(\"BenchmarkTest00441\") -> param. param is passed to thing.doSomething(param) where thing is either Thing1 or Thing2 (both are simple pass-through: Thing1 returns 'i' unchanged, Thing2 does StringBuilder(i).toString() which is also just a copy of the input). The result 'bar' is directly concatenated into the SQL string: \"INSERT INTO users (username, password) VALUES ('foo','\" + bar + \"')\". This sql string is then executed via statement.executeUpdate(sql, ...) using a raw java.sql.Statement (not PreparedStatement). No validation, escaping, or parameterization occurs anywhere on this path. An attacker can inject a single quote to break out of the string literal and inject arbitrary SQL.",
        "verdict": "True Positive",
        "confidence": "high",
        "severity_assessment": "high",
        "root_cause": "User-controlled HTTP parameter is concatenated directly into a SQL statement string and executed via a raw Statement object instead of using a parameterized PreparedStatement, with no sanitization or escaping applied to the tainted value.",
        "impact": "An attacker can manipulate the SQL query executed against the database, potentially reading, modifying, or deleting arbitrary data, bypassing authentication, or in some database configurations executing further commands.",
        "remediation": [
          "Use java.sql.PreparedStatement with parameterized placeholders (?) instead of concatenating values into the SQL string.",
          "Bind the 'bar' value using setString() or the appropriate typed setter on the PreparedStatement.",
          "Apply input validation/allow-listing on the parameter as defense in depth.",
          "Avoid using Statement.executeUpdate with dynamically built SQL strings."
        ]
      }
    },
