          "key": "org/owasp/benchmark/testcode/BenchmarkTest00442.java:70:13-70:88:java.lang.security.audit.xss.no-direct-response-writer.no-direct-response-writer",
          "decision": "escalate",
          "reason": "User-controlled request parameter is assigned to 'bar' and directly concatenated into XPath expression and written to response without HTML encoding in the success path, enabling both XPath injection and XSS vulnerabilities.",
          "error": null,
          "elapsed_seconds": 1.569
        }
      },
      "analysis": {
        "exploitability_evidence": "Source: request.getParameter(\"BenchmarkTest00442\") is assigned to 'param', then the ternary `bar = (7*42)-num>200 ? \"...\" : param;` evaluates 294-106=188, which is not >200, so bar==param (fully attacker-controlled, no sanitization). 'bar' is concatenated directly into the XPath expression string: `\"/Employees/Employee[@emplid='\" + bar + \"']\"`. This expression is evaluated via `xp.evaluate(expression, xmlDocument)` and the result is assigned to 'result'. On the success path, line 70 writes `response.getWriter().println(\"Your query results are: \" + result + \"<br/>\")` with no HTML encoding applied to 'result' (unlike the catch block, which does call `ESAPI.encoder().encodeForHTML(bar)`). Because the attacker fully controls the XPath predicate, they can manipulate which XML node(s) are selected, and craft injections (e.g. breaking out of the quoted attribute value and using union/boolean operators) to influence which text ends up in 'result', which is then reflected to the browser unescaped. No output encoder or HTML sanitizer is applied on this success-path sink, so any content that ends up in 'result' is rendered as raw HTML/JS in the response.",
        "verdict": "True Positive",
        "confidence": "medium",
        "severity_assessment": "medium",
        "root_cause": "User-supplied request parameter is used unsanitized to build an XPath query, and the query's result is written directly to the HTTP response via getWriter().println() without any HTML output encoding on the success path (encoding is only applied in the exception-handling branch), enabling injected/attacker-influenced content to reach the response unescaped.",
        "impact": "If exploited, an attacker could get attacker-influenced content reflected into the HTML response, resulting in a reflected Cross-Site Scripting condition in browsers rendering the page, in addition to a separate XPath Injection issue in the query construction itself.",
        "remediation": [
          "HTML-encode the 'result' value with ESAPI.encoder().encodeForHTML() (or an equivalent context-aware encoder) before writing it to the response, matching what is already done in the catch block.",
          "Avoid building XPath expressions via string concatenation with user input; use parameterized XPath (e.g. XPathVariableResolver) to prevent XPath Injection.",
          "Adopt a templating/view technology that auto-escapes output instead of writing directly to the response Writer."
        ]
      }
    },
