      "analysis": {
        "exploitability_evidence": "The sink is `new java.io.File(fileName)` where fileName = Utils.TESTFILES_DIR + bar. bar is assigned inside a switch statement on `switchTarget = guess.charAt(1)`, where guess is the hardcoded literal \"ABC\". Thus switchTarget is always the constant char 'B', so execution always takes case 'B' (bar = \"bob\") \u2014 the case 'A' and case 'C'/'D' branches that assign bar = param (the tainted request parameter) are unreachable dead code. Since bar is always the safe constant \"bob\", the tainted parameter never actually reaches the FileOutputStream sink despite the taint being read into `param`.",
        "verdict": "False Positive",
        "confidence": "high",
        "severity_assessment": "low",
        "root_cause": "The switch statement's discriminant is derived from a hardcoded string literal (\"ABC\").charAt(1), which always evaluates to the constant 'B'. This makes the branches that would assign the tainted user parameter to `bar` unreachable dead code; only the safe branch (bar = \"bob\") ever executes, so the file path is never influenced by user input.",
        "impact": "None in practice \u2014 the sink always operates on the constant string \"bob\", so no attacker-controlled path traversal is possible via this code path.",
        "remediation": [
          "No fix required for actual exploitability, but if the code is refactored so switchTarget becomes dynamic/user-influenced, sanitize `param` with FilenameUtils.getName() or validate against a canonical directory before use in File construction.",
          "Remove dead/misleading branches to avoid confusing future maintainers or scanners."
        ]
      }
    },
