          "decision": "escalate",
          "reason": "User-controlled request parameter is Base64 encoded then decoded (no sanitization), and directly written to response via printf without HTML escaping, enabling XSS via format string injection.",
          "error": null,
          "elapsed_seconds": 1.554
        }
      },
      "analysis": {
        "exploitability_evidence": "Source: request.getParameterMap() retrieves the 'BenchmarkTest00473' parameter into 'param' (attacker-controlled). It flows into a Base64 encode+decode round-trip: Base64.decodeBase64(Base64.encodeBase64(param.getBytes())) then wrapped in new String(...) to produce 'bar'. This encode/decode round trip is a no-op with respect to content \u2014 it does not strip, encode, or validate any characters; 'bar' ends up equal to the original 'param' bytes reinterpreted as a String. 'bar' is then passed directly to response.getWriter().printf(bar, obj), which writes to the raw HTTP response OutputStream/Writer bypassing any HTML-escaping template layer. There is no HTML/JS output encoding, no allow-list validation, and no use of a safe view technology on this path. Thus attacker input reaches the sink unescaped.",
        "verdict": "True Positive",
        "confidence": "high",
        "severity_assessment": "medium",
        "root_cause": "User-supplied HTTP parameter is passed through a Base64 encode/decode round-trip that provides no actual sanitization, and the resulting string is written directly to the response writer via printf without any HTML/JS output encoding.",
        "impact": "An attacker can craft a request parameter containing HTML/JavaScript payload (and format specifiers) that will be reflected verbatim in the HTTP response, resulting in reflected Cross-Site Scripting (XSS) that could execute arbitrary script in the victim's browser session, leading to session hijacking, credential theft, or defacement.",
        "remediation": [
          "HTML-encode/escape the value before writing it to the response (e.g., use OWASP Java Encoder or Apache Commons Text StringEscapeUtils.escapeHtml4).",
          "Avoid writing directly to response.getWriter()/OutputStream with user-controlled data; use a templating engine (JSP/JSF/Thymeleaf) that auto-escapes output.",
          "Do not treat Base64 encode+decode as a sanitizer \u2014 it does not neutralize any characters used in the original input.",
          "Apply context-aware output encoding based on where the data is placed (HTML body, attribute, JS, URL, etc.).",
          "Set a strict Content-Security-Policy header to reduce impact of any residual XSS."
        ]
      }
    },
