      "analysis": {
        "exploitability_evidence": "Source: request.getParameterMap() retrieves user-controlled parameter 'BenchmarkTest00480' into 'param'. This flows to 'bar' via a ternary condition (7*42-106=188, which is never >200, so bar always equals param, making this a non-functional guard, not a sanitizer). 'bar' is then concatenated into the string \"echo \" + bar and added to argList. This list is passed to ProcessBuilder via 'sh -c' (or 'cmd.exe /c' on Windows), which means the entire concatenated string is interpreted by a shell. No sanitization, escaping, or whitelist validation is applied to 'bar' before it reaches the shell command sink. An attacker supplying a value like '; cat /etc/passwd' or '$(malicious_command)' as the parameter can inject and execute arbitrary OS commands. The downstream Utils.printOSCommandResults call only HTML-encodes the command's stdout/stderr, which is irrelevant to preventing command injection at the ProcessBuilder sink.",
        "verdict": "True Positive",
        "confidence": "high",
        "severity_assessment": "high",
        "root_cause": "User-supplied HTTP parameter is concatenated directly into a shell command string executed via ProcessBuilder with 'sh -c' / 'cmd.exe /c', without any sanitization, escaping, or whitelist validation of special shell metacharacters.",
        "impact": "An attacker can inject arbitrary OS commands to be executed with the privileges of the web application process, potentially leading to full system compromise, data exfiltration, or denial of service.",
        "remediation": [
          "Avoid invoking a shell (sh -c / cmd.exe /c) with user input concatenated into the command string; instead pass arguments as separate list elements to ProcessBuilder without shell interpretation.",
          "Validate and whitelist the expected input format (e.g., alphanumeric only) before using it in any OS command.",
          "If shell invocation is unavoidable, use proper escaping libraries or APIs designed for safe command construction and avoid string concatenation.",
          "Consider using safer alternatives that do not require shell command execution for the intended functionality."
        ]
      }
    },
