      "analysis": {
        "exploitability_evidence": "Source: request.getParameterMap() value stored in 'param'. This is assigned to 'bar' only in the else-branch of 'if ((7 * 42) - num > 200) bar = \"This_should_always_happen\"; else bar = param;'. Evaluating the constant expression: 7*42=294, num=86, 294-86=208, and 208>200 is true, so the if-branch is always taken and bar is always hardcoded to \"This_should_always_happen\". The else branch that would assign the tainted 'param' to 'bar' is unreachable. Therefore argList.add(\"echo \" + bar) and the subsequent ProcessBuilder.start() always execute with the constant string, never with attacker-controlled input.",
        "verdict": "False Positive",
        "confidence": "high",
        "severity_assessment": "low",
        "root_cause": "The branch condition is a constant arithmetic expression that always evaluates to true, so the code path assigning tainted user input ('param') to the variable used in the OS command ('bar') is dead code and never executes.",
        "impact": "None in the current code, since the tainted value never reaches the command execution sink due to the always-true constant guard. If the constant condition were ever changed to allow the else branch, unsanitized user input would flow directly into a shell command via ProcessBuilder, enabling OS command injection.",
        "remediation": [
          "Remove reliance on the dead-code path and confirm intended control flow.",
          "If user input must ever be used in an OS command, apply strict allow-listing/validation of the value (e.g., only permit expected alphanumeric strings) before use.",
          "Avoid concatenating user input directly into shell commands; prefer passing arguments as separate ProcessBuilder list elements without invoking a shell.",
          "Add unit tests to ensure the branch handling user input cannot be silently bypassed by unrelated constant tweaks."
        ]
      }
