      },
      "analysis": {
        "exploitability_evidence": "Source: request parameter names via request.getParameterNames() - 'name' is attacker-controlled (an HTTP parameter name). The code loops through parameter names/values and sets param = name when a parameter's value equals 'BenchmarkTest00526', so 'bar' becomes an attacker-controlled parameter name. This is passed unsanitized into 'new java.io.File(bar, \"/Test.txt\")' at line 62, which is the flagged path-traversal sink. No path/name validation (e.g., FilenameUtils.getName, canonicalization checks, whitelist) is applied to 'bar' before it's used to build the File path. The only sanitization present, ESAPI encoder().encodeForHTML(...), is applied to the resulting file path string purely for HTML output encoding when writing the response - it does not restrict or validate the directory/path itself and provides no protection against path traversal in the File constructor. Thus an attacker can supply a parameter name like '../../../etc' (with a value of 'BenchmarkTest00526') to control 'bar' and cause fileTarget to point outside the intended directory, and the response then discloses whether that arbitrary path/file exists.",
        "verdict": "True Positive",
        "confidence": "high",
        "severity_assessment": "medium",
        "root_cause": "User-controlled input (an HTTP parameter name) is used directly to construct a File path via 'new File(bar, \"/Test.txt\")' without any path traversal sanitization (no canonicalization, no whitelist, no use of FilenameUtils.getName). The HTML encoding applied afterward only protects against XSS in the response, not path traversal.",
        "impact": "An attacker can manipulate the file path to escape the intended directory (path traversal), potentially checking for the existence of arbitrary files on the filesystem, and depending on how the resulting File object might be used elsewhere, could lead to information disclosure of file system structure or unauthorized file access.",
        "remediation": [
          "Validate and sanitize the parameter used to build the file path against a strict allow-list of expected values before use.",
          "Use FilenameUtils.getName() or similar to strip any directory traversal sequences from user input before constructing File objects.",
          "Canonicalize the resulting File path and verify it resides within an expected base directory before performing any file operations.",
          "Avoid using raw user-supplied parameter names/values directly as file path components; consider using an indirect mapping (e.g., a lookup table) instead."
        ]
      }
    },
