      "analysis": {
        "exploitability_evidence": "Source: request.getParameterNames()/getParameterValues() - the servlet iterates over all submitted parameter names and picks the name of whichever parameter has value 'BenchmarkTest00527', assigning it to 'param'. This is fully attacker-controlled (attacker can send a request with an arbitrary parameter name, e.g. '../../../etc/passwd', with value 'BenchmarkTest00527'). 'param' is passed to ThingFactory.createThing().doSomething(param); both possible implementations (Thing1.doSomething and Thing2.doSomething) are pure pass-throughs that return the input unchanged with no validation or sanitization. The resulting 'bar' is used directly as the parent path in 'new java.io.File(bar, \"/Test.txt\")' (the flagged sink). No path-traversal sanitizer (e.g., FilenameUtils.getName, canonicalization+prefix check, whitelist) is applied anywhere on this path. The subsequent ESAPI.encoder().encodeForHTML() call only HTML-encodes the file path string for display in the response body \u2014 it does not restrict or validate the actual filesystem path used to construct/query the File object. fileTarget.exists() is then evaluated against the attacker-influenced path, allowing directory traversal to probe for arbitrary file existence outside the intended directory.",
        "verdict": "True Positive",
        "confidence": "high"
      }
    },
