          "signal:path_traversal": 0.2,
          "signal:xss": 0.15,
          "taint_source": 0.1,
          "sanitizer_hint": -0.1
        }
      },
      "analysis": {
        "exploitability_evidence": "Source: request.getParameterNames() -> the code iterates all submitted parameter names and picks the name whose value equals 'BenchmarkTest00529', assigning it to 'param' then 'bar'. This 'bar' is fully attacker-controlled since parameter names in an HTTP request are arbitrary strings, e.g. an attacker can send '?../../../etc/passwd=BenchmarkTest00529'. Sink: fileName = Utils.TESTFILES_DIR + bar; new java.io.FileInputStream(fileName) \u2014 no path canonicalization, no getName()/whitelist check, no rejection of '..' sequences occurs on this path. The only sanitization present is org.owasp.esapi.ESAPI.encoder().encodeForHTML(fileName), which is applied to the value written back to the HTTP response for XSS protection \u2014 it does not run before the FileInputStream call and does not restrict path traversal semantics at all. Thus the file path used to open the FileInputStream is directly attacker-controlled and unsanitized against directory traversal.",
        "verdict": "True Positive",
        "confidence": "high",
        "severity_assessment": "high",
        "root_cause": "User-controlled parameter name is concatenated directly into a file path used to open a FileInputStream without any path traversal sanitization (e.g., canonicalization, whitelist, or FilenameUtils.getName).",
        "impact": "An attacker can supply a parameter name containing '../' sequences to read arbitrary files on the filesystem accessible to the application process, potentially exposing sensitive configuration, credentials, or other confidential data.",
        "remediation": [
          "Use FilenameUtils.getName() or similar to strip any directory components from the user-supplied name before building the file path.",
          "Canonicalize the resulting path (File.getCanonicalPath()) and verify it still resides within the intended base directory (Utils.TESTFILES_DIR).",
          "Maintain a whitelist of allowed file names/identifiers instead of trusting arbitrary parameter names.",
          "Reject requests where the resolved path escapes the expected directory."
        ]
      }
    },
    {
