          "error": null,
          "elapsed_seconds": 1.296
        }
      },
      "analysis": {
        "exploitability_evidence": "Source: request.getParameterNames()/getParameterValues() enumerates all submitted parameter names/values. When a parameter's value equals 'BenchmarkTest00542', its name is captured into 'param' (attacker fully controls the parameter name, e.g. by sending a request with a crafted parameter name and value 'BenchmarkTest00542'). 'guess' is the constant \"ABC\", so switchTarget = guess.charAt(2) = 'C', making the switch always take case 'C', so bar = param unconditionally. This unsanitized, attacker-controlled string is then passed directly into response.getWriter().format(Locale.US, bar, obj) at line 83 as the format string itself. No HTML encoding, escaping, or validation is applied to 'param'/'bar' anywhere on this path, and format() writes raw bytes straight to the HTTP response, bypassing any templating/auto-escaping layer. This satisfies both the CWE-79 XSS sink requirement (raw writer output) and injects attacker data as the format string, which is also directly reflected in the response body.",
        "verdict": "True Positive",
        "confidence": "high",
        "severity_assessment": "high",
        "root_cause": "User-controlled HTTP parameter name is used as the format string argument to Writer.format() with no output encoding or validation, allowing arbitrary HTML/JS to be reflected into the response.",
        "impact": "An attacker can craft a request with a parameter name containing malicious HTML/JavaScript and a value of 'BenchmarkTest00542'; this value is reflected verbatim in the HTTP response, resulting in reflected Cross-Site Scripting (XSS) that can lead to session hijacking, credential theft, or arbitrary actions in the victim's browser context.",
        "remediation": [
          "Never use unsanitized user input as a format string; use a fixed format string and pass user data only as substitution arguments.",
          "HTML-encode/escape any user-supplied data before writing it to the response (e.g., using OWASP ESAPI Encoder or context-aware auto-escaping).",
          "Prefer a templating engine (JSP/JSF) that automatically escapes output instead of writing directly to the response writer.",
          "Validate/allow-list expected parameter names rather than deriving output content from arbitrary submitted parameter names."
        ]
      }
    },
