          "reason": "User-controlled request parameter name is extracted into 'bar' and passed directly to response.getWriter().printf() without sanitization, enabling XSS via format string injection.",
          "error": null,
          "elapsed_seconds": 1.447
        }
      },
      "analysis": {
        "exploitability_evidence": "Source: request.getParameterNames() / request.getParameterValues(name) \u2014 both entirely attacker-controlled (an HTTP client can send any parameter name it wants). The code iterates parameter names; when a parameter's value equals the literal 'BenchmarkTest00547', it sets param = name (the attacker-supplied parameter NAME, not a fixed value). 'bar' is then derived via param.split(\" \")[0], which is still attacker-controlled if the injected parameter name contains no spaces (e.g., '<script>alert(1)</script>'). This 'bar' string is passed as the format argument directly into response.getWriter().printf(Locale.US, bar, obj) \u2014 no HTML encoding, no output escaping, and no allow-list validation is performed on 'bar' anywhere in the shown code. The response content type is text/html, so any HTML/script content in the parameter name is written verbatim into the HTML response body, resulting in reflected XSS. No sanitizer/encoder adequate for an HTML-writer sink is present on this path.",
        "verdict": "True Positive",
        "confidence": "high",
        "severity_assessment": "high",
        "root_cause": "User-controlled HTTP parameter name is used to build a string that is written directly to the response Writer via printf without any HTML-escaping or output encoding, bypassing template/view-layer protections.",
        "impact": "An attacker can craft a request with a specially named parameter (containing HTML/JavaScript) whose value equals 'BenchmarkTest00547'; the resulting response reflects the attacker-controlled string unescaped into the HTML page, enabling reflected Cross-Site Scripting (script execution in victim's browser, session/cookie theft, defacement, etc.).",
        "remediation": [
          "HTML-encode/escape any user-controlled data (including parameter names) before writing it to the response body, e.g. use org.owasp.encoder.Encode.forHtml() or a JSP/JSF templating engine that auto-escapes output.",
          "Avoid using raw parameter names as format strings passed to printf; treat them as untrusted data only, never as format/control strings.",
          "Validate/allow-list expected parameter names against a known set instead of trusting arbitrary request parameter names.",
          "Use response.getWriter().write() with encoded content instead of printf on unsanitized input."
        ]
      }
    },
