        "exploitability_evidence": "Source: request.getParameterNames() enumerates all parameter names supplied by the client (fully attacker-controlled, since a client can name a query parameter anything, e.g. ?<script>alert(1)</script>=BenchmarkTest00549). The code loops through names, and if a parameter's value equals the literal string 'BenchmarkTest00549', it sets param = name (the attacker-controlled parameter name). The subsequent ternary (7*42)-106=188, which is not >200, so bar = param always when the loop matched. bar is then written directly via response.getWriter().println(bar.toCharArray()) with no HTML-encoding, escaping, or validation applied anywhere on the path. The X-XSS-Protection header is even disabled (set to '0'). This is a direct reflected XSS sink with an attacker-controlled parameter name flowing untouched to the response writer.",
