      "analysis": {
        "exploitability_evidence": "Source: request.getParameterNames() enumerates all submitted parameter names, which are fully attacker-controlled (e.g., an attacker can submit a parameter named '<script>alert(1)</script>' with value 'BenchmarkTest00555'). The loop sets param = name when the corresponding value equals 'BenchmarkTest00555', so param becomes the attacker-chosen parameter name. bar is then assigned via a ternary whose condition (7*42-106=188>200) is always false, so bar always equals param. Finally response.getWriter().write(bar.toCharArray()) writes bar directly to the raw response stream with no HTML encoding, escaping, or validation applied anywhere on this path. This bypasses any templating/auto-escaping and matches the OWASP Benchmark CWE-79 pattern exactly.",
        "verdict": "True Positive",
        "confidence": "high",
        "severity_assessment": "medium",
        "root_cause": "Attacker-controlled HTTP parameter name is propagated through a ternary expression (which always selects it) directly into response.getWriter().write() without any output encoding or sanitization, allowing injection of arbitrary HTML/JS into the response body.",
        "impact": "An attacker can craft a request with a malicious parameter name (e.g. containing a <script> payload) whose value is 'BenchmarkTest00555', causing the payload to be reflected verbatim in the HTTP response, resulting in reflected Cross-Site Scripting (XSS) that can execute arbitrary JavaScript in victims' browsers, leading to session hijacking, credential theft, or defacement.",
        "remediation": [
          "HTML-encode/escape the value before writing it to the response (e.g., use OWASP Java Encoder's Encode.forHtml() or a JSP/JSF view layer with auto-escaping).",
          "Avoid writing raw request-derived data directly via getWriter(); use a templating engine that performs contextual output encoding.",
          "Validate/whitelist parameter names against expected values before use.",
          "Set appropriate Content-Security-Policy headers as defense-in-depth."
        ]
      }
