      "analysis": {
        "exploitability_evidence": "Source: HTTP parameter names (attacker controlled) from request.getParameterNames()/getParameterValues() \u2014 an attacker can send a request with any parameter name whose value is 'BenchmarkTest00568' to make 'param' equal to that arbitrary attacker-chosen name. 'param' is passed to ThingFactory.createThing().doSomething(param); both Thing1.doSomething and Thing2.doSomething return the input unchanged (no sanitization). The result 'bar' is then used to build the exec args: on non-Windows systems it is directly string-concatenated into 'cmd + bar' which becomes an argument to `sh -c`, and this array is passed to Runtime.exec(args, argsEnv) at line 87. No validation, whitelist, or shell-metacharacter encoding is applied anywhere on this path before reaching the exec sink, so an attacker-supplied parameter name containing shell metacharacters (e.g. '; rm -rf /') would be executed by the shell.",
        "verdict": "True Positive",
        "confidence": "high",
        "severity_assessment": "high",
        "root_cause": "User-controlled HTTP parameter name flows unsanitized through a pass-through helper method into a string concatenated with a shell command, then executed via Runtime.exec with sh -c, allowing shell metacharacter injection.",
        "impact": "An attacker can execute arbitrary OS commands on the server with the privileges of the web application, potentially leading to full system compromise, data exfiltration, or lateral movement.",
        "remediation": [
          "Avoid invoking a shell (sh -c / cmd.exe /c) with user-influenced input; use exec with a fixed, parameterized command array without concatenating attacker data into a single command string.",
          "Validate/whitelist the parameter name or value against an allow-list of expected identifiers before using it in any command construction.",
          "If shell execution is unavoidable, strictly escape or reject shell metacharacters (;, |, &, $, `, etc.) before use.",
          "Prefer Java APIs that avoid shell invocation entirely, or use ProcessBuilder with a fixed command and individual arguments rather than concatenated strings."
        ]
      }
