        "exploitability_evidence": "Source: request.getParameterNames()/getParameterValues() \u2013 the code searches for a request parameter whose VALUE equals 'BenchmarkTest00573' and captures its NAME into 'param' (fully attacker-controlled, since an attacker chooses both parameter names and values). 'bar = param' is then placed into 'argsEnv = {bar}' with no validation/encoding. This flows directly into 'r.exec(cmd, argsEnv)' at line 70, which is the flagged sink. 'cmd' itself is a fixed path returned by Utils.getInsecureOSCommandString (not attacker-controlled), but argsEnv (containing the tainted 'bar') is passed as the second argument, which Runtime.exec(String,String[]) treats as the environment array (envp) for the spawned process rather than as literal command-line arguments. No sanitizer, allow-list, or encoder is applied to 'bar' anywhere on this path (ESAPI encoding is only used later on process output for HTML, not on the input to exec). Because unsanitized attacker data reaches an OS process-creation API and can be used to inject/override environment variables (e.g. PATH, LD_PRELOAD-style variables) that influence the executed script's behavior, this constitutes an insecure, unsanitized flow into a command-execution API, matching the intended CWE-78 vulnerable pattern used throughout this benchmark suite.",
        "verdict": "True Positive",
        "confidence": "medium",
        "severity_assessment": "medium",
        "root_cause": "User-supplied HTTP parameter name (fully attacker-controlled) is used without any validation or sanitization as an element of the environment array passed to Runtime.exec(String, String[]), allowing environment-variable injection into the spawned OS process.",
        "impact": "An attacker can inject arbitrary/malformed environment variable strings into the executed subprocess. Depending on the OS/script behavior, this can be leveraged to alter process behavior (e.g., PATH hijacking, library preloading, or malformed environment causing unexpected script logic), potentially leading to information disclosure, denial of service, or in more permissive shell scripts, further command execution.",
        "remediation": [
          "Never build process environment or command arguments directly from unsanitized user input.",
          "If specific values must be passed to the subprocess, use a strict allow-list of expected values instead of raw parameter names/values.",
          "Prefer ProcessBuilder with a fixed, vetted argument list and avoid passing user data as environment variables unless it is validated to match a strict 'KEY=safe_value' pattern with a whitelist of allowed keys.",
          "Run subprocesses with a minimal, explicitly-defined environment rather than passing through attacker-influenced strings.",
          "Apply centralized input validation/canonicalization on all parameter names and values before they can influence any OS-level operation."
        ]
      }
