      "analysis": {
        "exploitability_evidence": "Source: request.getParameterNames()/getParameterValues() - the parameter *name* whose value equals a fixed marker string is captured into `param`, which is attacker-controlled since parameter names in HTTP requests are fully attacker-controlled. `param` flows into `thing.doSomething(param)`. Depending on which Thing implementation is loaded (Thing1 or Thing2 shown), the value is either directly returned unmodified or wrapped in a StringBuilder and returned unmodified \u2014 no sanitization/validation/whitelisting occurs in either path. The tainted result `bar` is concatenated with `cmd` and passed directly into `r.exec(cmd + bar, argsEnv, ...)`, which is the flagged OS command execution sink. No encoding, whitelist check, or command-argument separation is applied to `bar` before it reaches exec. This is a direct, unsanitized source-to-sink path.",
