        "severity_assessment": "high",
        "root_cause": "User-controlled HTTP parameter name is passed through pass-through helper methods (Thing1/Thing2.doSomething) with no sanitization, then concatenated into a string executed via Runtime.exec, allowing OS command injection.",
        "impact": "An attacker who controls the HTTP request parameter names can inject arbitrary OS commands or command arguments, potentially leading to remote code execution, data exfiltration, or full system compromise depending on privileges of the running process.",
        "remediation": [
          "Avoid using Runtime.exec/ProcessBuilder with any user-influenced input; use fixed command strings and pass user data only as validated arguments.",
          "If dynamic values are unavoidable, use a strict whitelist of allowed values (e.g., only known-safe parameter names) rather than accepting arbitrary parameter names.",
          "Use ProcessBuilder with an argument array (not string concatenation) so the OS shell does not interpret metacharacters, and validate/escape all inputs before use.",
          "Run external commands with least privilege and consider sandboxing to limit impact if injection occurs."
        ]
      }
