      "analysis": {
        "exploitability_evidence": "Source: request.getParameterNames()/getParameterValues() - an attacker fully controls both parameter names and values in an HTTP request. The code loops through all parameter names, and for whichever parameter has a value equal to the literal 'BenchmarkTest00590', it assigns 'param = name' (i.e., the attacker-controlled parameter NAME becomes the tainted value). This 'param' is then assigned to 'bar' via a tautological if/else (the else branch is unreachable, but that doesn't affect taint). 'bar' is directly concatenated into 'sql = \"{call \" + bar + \"}\"' with no encoding, escaping, whitelisting, or parameterization. This raw string is passed to connection.prepareCall(sql, ...) and executed via statement.executeQuery(). No sanitizer (e.g., ESAPI encoder, allow-list, PreparedStatement parameter binding) is applied to 'bar' before it reaches the SQL sink -- ESAPI encoding is only used later in printResults() for HTML output, which does not protect against SQL injection. Thus attacker-supplied parameter names flow unsanitized into a SQL statement string used to build a CallableStatement.",
        "verdict": "True Positive",
        "confidence": "high",
        "severity_assessment": "high",
        "root_cause": "User-controlled HTTP parameter name is concatenated directly into a SQL command string ('{call ' + bar + '}') without parameterization, escaping, or input validation, then executed via CallableStatement.executeQuery().",
        "impact": "An attacker can supply a crafted parameter name containing SQL/stored-procedure injection payloads, potentially allowing unauthorized data access, data modification, or execution of arbitrary stored procedures/commands depending on the underlying database engine and permissions.",
        "remediation": [
          "Use parameterized queries/prepared statements with bind variables instead of string concatenation for the SQL/call statement.",
          "Avoid using raw HTTP parameter names as part of SQL logic; if dynamic identifiers are required, validate them against a strict allow-list of expected values.",
          "If calling stored procedures dynamically is unavoidable, validate the procedure name against a fixed set of permitted procedure names before building the CALL statement.",
          "Apply the principle of least privilege to the database account used by the application to limit the impact of any injection."
        ]
      }
