      "analysis": {
        "exploitability_evidence": "Source: request.getParameterNames()/getParameterValues() - the code iterates all submitted request parameter names and captures the parameter NAME whose value equals 'BenchmarkTest00591' into variable 'param'. This param is fully attacker-controlled (an attacker can name a parameter anything they want). It flows to thing.doSomething(param) \u2014 both possible implementations (Thing1.doSomething returns input unchanged, Thing2.doSomething just copies via StringBuilder) perform no sanitization or validation. The result 'bar' is then directly concatenated into the SQL string: 'SELECT * from USERS where USERNAME=? and PASSWORD=\\'' + bar + '\\''. Although a PreparedStatement is used, only the first placeholder (USERNAME) is bound via statement.setString(1, \"foo\"); the PASSWORD value is embedded directly in the SQL text via string concatenation, so PreparedStatement provides no protection for that portion. This sql string is then passed to connection.prepareStatement(sql, ...) and executed. No encoding, escaping, or allow-list validation of 'param'/'bar' occurs anywhere on this path before it reaches the SQL sink.",
        "verdict": "True Positive",
        "confidence": "high",
        "severity_assessment": "high",
        "root_cause": "User-controlled input (an arbitrary HTTP parameter name matched against a fixed value) is concatenated directly into a SQL query string instead of being bound as a parameter; the use of PreparedStatement is negated because the tainted value is embedded into the raw SQL text before the statement is prepared.",
        "impact": "An attacker can inject arbitrary SQL by naming a request parameter with a value containing SQL metacharacters (e.g., closing the quoted PASSWORD literal and appending malicious SQL), potentially bypassing authentication, extracting or modifying arbitrary data in the USERS table, or executing further SQL injection attacks depending on DB permissions.",
        "remediation": [
          "Bind the value via a second PreparedStatement placeholder (e.g., ... PASSWORD=?) and use statement.setString(2, bar) instead of concatenating it into the SQL string.",
          "Never build SQL text by string concatenation with any part of user-controlled input, even indirectly through helper methods.",
          "Apply strict allow-list validation on parameter names/values if they must influence query logic, and ensure any helper transformation (Thing1/Thing2) does not simply pass through untrusted data unchanged."
        ]
      }
    },
