        "confidence": "high",
        "severity_assessment": "high",
        "root_cause": "User-controlled input (an HTTP request parameter name) is concatenated directly into a SQL query string instead of being bound as a PreparedStatement parameter, allowing SQL injection despite the use of PreparedStatement for an unrelated placeholder.",
        "impact": "An attacker can inject arbitrary SQL by crafting a request parameter name (e.g., closing the quote and appending malicious SQL), potentially bypassing authentication, exfiltrating data from the USERS table, or modifying/deleting data depending on database permissions.",
        "remediation": [
          "Bind the 'bar' value using a PreparedStatement parameter (e.g., statement.setString(2, bar)) instead of concatenating it into the SQL string.",
          "Avoid deriving security-sensitive values from parameter names; validate/whitelist expected parameter names before use.",
          "Use a strict allow-list or parameterized query for all user-influenced values that reach SQL statements."
        ]
      }
