        "exploitability_evidence": "Source: request.getParameterNames() enumerates HTTP request parameter names; for any parameter whose value equals literal 'BenchmarkTest00595', the parameter NAME itself is captured into 'param' (line ~48-53). This name is fully attacker-controlled since HTTP parameter names can be arbitrary strings. The ternary (7*42)-106 > 200 evaluates to 188 > 200 which is always false, so 'bar' is unconditionally assigned 'param' (line 64). This tainted 'bar' is then concatenated directly into the SQL string: sql = \"SELECT * from USERS where USERNAME=? and PASSWORD='\" + bar + \"'\" (line 66). Although a PreparedStatement is created and statement.setString(1, \"foo\") binds the '?' placeholder for USERNAME, the PASSWORD value is embedded as a raw string literal in the SQL text itself via string concatenation, not via a bind parameter. No sanitization, encoding, or escaping is applied to 'bar' before concatenation. This is a textbook SQL injection sink: an attacker can submit a parameter name like foo' OR '1'='1 with value 'BenchmarkTest00595' to break out of the string literal.",
        "verdict": "True Positive",
        "confidence": "high",
        "severity_assessment": "high",
        "root_cause": "User-controlled input (an HTTP parameter name) is concatenated directly into a SQL query string that is then executed, while only one of the two placeholders in the query is actually parameterized. The PASSWORD value is embedded as raw text in the SQL statement rather than bound as a parameter.",
        "impact": "An attacker can inject arbitrary SQL by choosing a malicious parameter name (e.g., containing a single quote to close the PASSWORD string literal and append additional SQL), potentially bypassing authentication, exfiltrating data from the USERS table, or executing further SQL injection attacks depending on database permissions.",
        "remediation": [
          "Bind the PASSWORD value as a second prepared statement parameter (e.g., use '...PASSWORD=?' and call statement.setString(2, bar)) instead of concatenating it into the SQL string.",
          "Avoid using unvalidated HTTP parameter names as data; if the parameter name must be used as a value, treat it with the same rigor as any other untrusted input.",
          "Adopt strict allow-list validation for any input that influences SQL query construction.",
          "Enable a SQL injection detection/prevention layer or ORM that enforces parameterized queries uniformly."
        ]
      }
