      "analysis": {
        "exploitability_evidence": "The tainted value 'param' is derived from request.getParameterNames()/getParameterValues() (attacker-controlled). However, 'bar' is assigned via a ternary: bar = (7*18)+num > 200 ? \"This_should_always_happen\" : param; with num=106, 7*18+106=232 which is always >200, so the true branch is always taken and bar is always assigned the constant string \"This_should_always_happen\". The tainted 'param' value is never assigned to bar, so it never reaches the SQL string concatenation ('sql = ... + bar + ...') that flows into statement.addBatch(sql). The vulnerable branch is dead code \u2014 the condition is a constant-foldable expression that always evaluates to true.",
        "verdict": "False Positive",
        "confidence": "high",
        "severity_assessment": "info",
        "root_cause": "The conditional expression controlling whether tainted user input (param) or a constant is assigned to 'bar' always evaluates to true due to fixed numeric literals (126+106=232>200), making the tainted branch unreachable. This is a classic Benchmark 'false positive' test case designed to look vulnerable to naive taint analysis but is not exploitable in practice.",
        "impact": "None \u2014 the SQL query executed always uses the hardcoded string \"This_should_always_happen\" instead of user input, so no SQL injection is possible via this code path.",
        "remediation": [
          "No fix required for actual exploitability since the branch is unreachable, but for code clarity, remove dead/unreachable conditional logic.",
          "If the intent was to allow conditional user input, replace string concatenation with parameterized queries (PreparedStatement) regardless.",
          "Add static analysis suppression comments or refactor to avoid triggering scanner false positives on always-true/false conditions."
        ]
      }
