      "analysis": {
        "exploitability_evidence": "Source: request parameter names/values (attacker-controlled HTTP request parameters) are scanned; when a value equals 'BenchmarkTest00603', its parameter name is captured into 'param'. This is passed to thing.doSomething(param) via ThingFactory.createThing(), which returns either Thing1 or Thing2 depending on properties file - both implementations shown are pure pass-through (Thing1 returns input unchanged; Thing2 wraps in StringBuilder unchanged), so 'bar' equals the raw attacker-controlled parameter name. This value is directly concatenated into a SQL string: 'SELECT * from USERS where USERNAME=\\'foo\\' and PASSWORD=\\'' + bar + '\\'' with no escaping or parameterization, then executed via statement.execute(sql, ...) using a plain java.sql.Statement (not PreparedStatement). No sanitizer, validator, or encoder is applied anywhere on this path.",
        "verdict": "True Positive",
        "confidence": "high",
        "severity_assessment": "high",
        "root_cause": "User-controlled input (an HTTP request parameter name) is concatenated directly into a SQL query string without parameterization or escaping, then executed via Statement.execute, enabling classic SQL injection.",
        "impact": "An attacker can manipulate the parameter name in the HTTP request to inject arbitrary SQL, potentially bypassing authentication, exfiltrating data from the USERS table, or modifying/deleting data depending on database permissions.",
        "remediation": [
          "Replace java.sql.Statement with java.sql.PreparedStatement and bind the parameter using setString() instead of string concatenation.",
          "Avoid using raw parameter names as data; validate/whitelist expected parameter names.",
          "Apply least-privilege database credentials to limit damage from injection.",
          "Enable centralized input validation and use static analysis / query linting in CI to catch string-built SQL."
        ]
      }
    },
