        "confidence": "high",
        "severity_assessment": "high",
        "root_cause": "User-controlled input (an HTTP parameter name, fully attacker-controlled) is concatenated directly into a SQL query string without parameterization. The Base64 encode/decode round-trip is not a sanitizer \u2014 it returns the original bytes unchanged \u2014 so it provides no protection against SQL metacharacters like single quotes.",
        "impact": "An attacker can inject arbitrary SQL via the parameter name (e.g., a name containing a single quote and SQL syntax) to bypass authentication, exfiltrate data from the USERS table, or manipulate/destroy data, since the query is executed with a raw Statement against the database.",
        "remediation": [
          "Use java.sql.PreparedStatement with a parameterized query (e.g., 'SELECT * from USERS where USERNAME=? and PASSWORD=?') and bind 'bar' via setString instead of string concatenation.",
          "Do not rely on Base64 encode/decode as a sanitization step; it does not neutralize SQL metacharacters.",
          "Apply strict input validation/allow-listing on any value derived from user-controllable request parameters (including parameter names) before using them in security-sensitive operations."
        ]
      }
