        "exploitability_evidence": "Source: request.getParameterNames()/getParameterValues() - the parameter NAME itself is attacker controlled (attacker can send any parameter name, e.g. ?'; DROP TABLE...--=BenchmarkTest00606). The code iterates all submitted parameter names/values; when a value equals literal 'BenchmarkTest00606', it sets param = name (the attacker-supplied parameter name). Then num=196, (500/42)+196 = 11+196 = 207 > 200 is always true, so bar = param unconditionally. The tainted bar is concatenated directly into sql = \"INSERT INTO users (username, password) VALUES ('foo','\" + bar + \"')\" with no escaping, parameterization, or ESAPI validation. This string is then passed straight to statement.executeUpdate(sql) via DatabaseHelper.getSqlStatement(), which just returns a raw java.sql.Statement. No sanitizer sits on this path (outputUpdateComplete's ESAPI encodeForHTML is applied later for HTML output only, not before the SQL execution). Thus attacker-controlled parameter name flows unsanitized into a SQL INSERT statement executed via Statement.executeUpdate.",
        "verdict": "True Positive",
        "confidence": "high",
        "severity_assessment": "high",
        "root_cause": "User-controlled input (the name of an arbitrary HTTP request parameter) is concatenated directly into a SQL INSERT statement string without parameterization or escaping, then executed via java.sql.Statement.executeUpdate.",
        "impact": "An attacker can inject arbitrary SQL by choosing a malicious parameter name (with a value of 'BenchmarkTest00606' to satisfy the trigger condition), enabling data manipulation, unauthorized inserts, or potentially broader SQL injection attacks (e.g., stacked queries depending on JDBC driver/DB support), leading to data integrity loss, information disclosure, or further compromise of the database.",
        "remediation": [
          "Use a PreparedStatement with bind parameters instead of string concatenation for the INSERT statement.",
          "Never use raw HTTP parameter names to build SQL statements; if parameter names must be used, validate them against a strict allow-list before use.",
          "Apply input validation/whitelisting on both parameter names and values before using them in any SQL context.",
          "Enforce least-privilege database accounts and enable prepared-statement caching only with bound values to eliminate injection vectors."
        ]
      }
