        "exploitability_evidence": "Source: request.getParameterNames()/getParameterValues() \u2013 param is set to an attacker-chosen parameter *name* whenever any parameter's value equals 'BenchmarkTest00607', so the attacker fully controls the string stored in param (they can name a parameter arbitrarily and add a second parameter with the magic value). param flows unmodified through ThingFactory.createThing()->Thing1.doSomething()/Thing2.doSomething() (both are pure identity/copy operations, no sanitization) into 'bar'. 'bar' is concatenated directly into the XPath expression string ('/Employees/Employee[@emplid=' + bar + ']') with no escaping, so the attacker fully controls the syntax of the expression passed to xp.evaluate(). Because XPath 1.0 evaluate() with default STRING return type will return the string result of any top-level string-producing expression (e.g., via concat()/string() functions), an attacker can craft 'bar' to make the whole expression evaluate to an arbitrary literal string of their choosing, independent of the XML file's actual content. That resulting 'result' variable is written to the client with response.getWriter().println(\"Your query results are: \" + result + \"<br/>\") at line 81 with no HTML encoding at all. Note the exception-path branch does encode 'bar' via ESAPI.encodeForHTML, but that only executes on XPathExpressionException/parser errors, not on the normal success path that is flagged. Hence the sink at line 81 receives attacker-influenced, unescaped output.",
        "verdict": "True Positive",
        "confidence": "medium",
        "severity_assessment": "medium",
        "root_cause": "User-controlled input (HTTP parameter name) is passed unsanitized into an XPath expression, and the resulting XPath evaluation output is written directly to the HTTP response via response.getWriter().println() without any HTML encoding, allowing XPath-injection-crafted content to reach the browser unescaped.",
        "impact": "An attacker able to control the returned XPath evaluation string (via XPath expression injection using functions like concat()) can inject arbitrary HTML/JavaScript into the response, resulting in reflected Cross-Site Scripting against victims who submit or are tricked into submitting the crafted request.",
        "remediation": [
          "HTML-encode the 'result' value before writing it to the response (e.g., ESAPI.encoder().encodeForHTML(result)) as is already done in the exception handler.",
          "Avoid building XPath expressions via string concatenation of user input; use XPath variable binding (XPathVariableResolver) or parameterized queries to prevent XPath injection.",
          "Adopt a templating/view technology that auto-escapes output instead of writing directly to the raw response Writer."
        ]
      }
