      },
      "analysis": {
        "exploitability_evidence": "Source: request.getParameter(\"BenchmarkTest00622\") via SeparateClassRequest.getTheParameter -> param. This flows to 'bar' only inside case 'A', 'C', or 'D' of a switch on switchTarget. switchTarget is computed from a hardcoded literal: String guess = \"ABC\"; char switchTarget = guess.charAt(1); which always evaluates to the constant 'B'. Consequently the switch statement deterministically executes case 'B': bar = \"bob\"; the branches that assign the tainted 'param' to 'bar' (cases 'A', 'C', 'D') are unreachable dead code. The File object fileTarget = new File(TESTFILES_DIR, bar) is therefore always constructed with the constant string \"bob\", never with attacker-controlled data, so the path-traversal sink never receives tainted input.",
        "verdict": "False Positive",
        "confidence": "high",
        "severity_assessment": "info",
        "root_cause": "The switch statement's controlling expression is derived from a compile-time constant string (\"ABC\".charAt(1) == 'B'), making the branch that assigns user input to the file-path variable unreachable. The scanner's taint analysis does not account for this constant-folding/control-flow reachability, producing a false positive.",
        "impact": "None in the current code path since the tainted value never reaches the sink. If the hardcoded 'guess' value were ever changed or made configurable/user-influenced, the vulnerability would become live, but as shown the code is not exploitable.",
        "remediation": [
          "No immediate fix required since the vulnerable branch is dead code, but consider removing the misleading unreachable case blocks to avoid confusion.",
          "If this pattern is intentional test scaffolding (OWASP Benchmark), no action needed; otherwise ensure any future dynamic switchTarget still routes user input through path canonicalization and directory allow-listing before use in File construction.",
          "As defense in depth, validate 'bar' against a whitelist or use FilenameUtils.getName()/File.getCanonicalPath() checks before constructing File objects from any user-influenced value."
        ]
      }
    },
