      "analysis": {
        "exploitability_evidence": "Source: request.getParameter(\"BenchmarkTest00623\") via SeparateClassRequest.getTheParameter(). The value flows into 'param', then through a base64 encode/decode round-trip (Base64.encodeBase64 then decodeBase64) which is a no-op transformation that returns the original string unchanged, no traversal sequences are stripped. The result 'bar' is used directly as the parent directory in 'new java.io.File(bar, \"/Test.txt\")', and the code then calls fileTarget.exists() to check for file presence and reports whether the file exists via HTTP response. No sanitizer (e.g., canonicalization, path allow-listing, or FilenameUtils validation) is applied to 'bar' before it's used to construct the File path. An attacker can supply '../../etc' or absolute paths in the parameter to make the servlet check for file existence in arbitrary directories outside the intended base directory, and the response leaks this information back to the client.",
        "verdict": "True Positive",
        "confidence": "high",
        "severity_assessment": "medium",
        "root_cause": "User-supplied request parameter is used to construct a java.io.File path without any validation or canonicalization; the base64 encode/decode round-trip does not sanitize path traversal sequences.",
        "impact": "An attacker can probe the file system to determine whether arbitrary files/directories exist on the server (information disclosure), and depending on downstream use of fileTarget could potentially escalate to reading or writing files outside the intended directory.",
        "remediation": [
          "Canonicalize the resulting File path (getCanonicalPath()) and verify it resides within an expected base directory before use.",
          "Reject or strip path traversal sequences ('../', absolute paths) from user input before constructing file paths.",
          "Use an allow-list of permitted file names or a mapping from parameter values to specific safe paths instead of directly using user input in path construction.",
          "Consider using org.apache.commons.io.FilenameUtils.getName() to strip any directory components from user input."
        ]
      }
    },
