      "analysis": {
        "exploitability_evidence": "Source: request.getParameter(\"BenchmarkTest00624\") via SeparateClassRequest.getTheParameter (direct pass-through, no validation). This tainted string flows into ThingFactory.createThing().doSomething(param) - both Thing1.doSomething and Thing2.doSomething are pure identity/pass-through functions that return the input unchanged (Thing2 wraps in StringBuilder but preserves the value including any '../' sequences). The result 'bar' is concatenated with a fixed prefix: fileName = TESTFILES_DIR + bar, then passed directly to `new java.io.File(fileName)` and used to construct a FileOutputStream (the sink) at line 58. There is no path traversal sanitization anywhere on this path \u2014 no canonicalization, no check for '../' or absolute paths, no use of FilenameUtils.getName as suggested by the rule. The only encoding present, ESAPI.encoder().encodeForHTML(fileName), is applied afterward purely for safely rendering the filename in the HTTP response body \u2014 it does not sanitize the path used to open the file, since the FileOutputStream is already constructed with the raw fileName before this call.",
        "verdict": "True Positive",
        "confidence": "high",
        "severity_assessment": "high",
        "root_cause": "User-supplied HTTP parameter is concatenated onto a base directory path and used unsanitized to construct a File/FileOutputStream, allowing directory traversal sequences ('../') to escape the intended directory.",
        "impact": "An attacker can supply a parameter value containing '../' sequences (or absolute paths on some platforms) to write files outside the intended TESTFILES_DIR, potentially overwriting arbitrary files reachable by the web application process, leading to data corruption, code injection (e.g., overwriting web-accessible files or config files), or denial of service.",
        "remediation": [
          "Canonicalize the resulting file path (File.getCanonicalPath()) and verify it still resides within the intended base directory before use.",
          "Reject or strip path traversal sequences and path separators from the user-supplied parameter, or use FilenameUtils.getName() to reduce input to a bare filename.",
          "Maintain an allow-list of permitted filenames or use a generated/mapped identifier instead of directly using user input in file paths.",
          "Apply CWE-22 specific validation (not just HTML output encoding) prior to any filesystem operation."
        ]
      }
