      "analysis": {
        "exploitability_evidence": "Source: request.getParameter(\"BenchmarkTest00625\") via SeparateClassRequest.getTheParameter, assigned to param. However, bar is set based on `if ((7 * 42) - num > 200) bar = \"This_should_always_happen\"; else bar = param;`. Evaluating: 7*42=294, num=86, 294-86=208, and 208>200 is always true. Therefore the if-branch is always taken and bar is always assigned the constant string \"This_should_always_happen\" \u2014 the else branch that assigns the tainted param to bar is dead code and never executes. fileName = TESTFILES_DIR + bar therefore always uses the constant value, never the user-controlled param, before reaching the FileOutputStream sink at line 61.",
        "verdict": "False Positive",
        "confidence": "high",
        "severity_assessment": "low",
        "root_cause": "The tainted parameter is only assigned to `bar` in an else branch that is unreachable because the guarding condition `(7*42) - 86 > 200` (i.e., 208 > 200) is a compile-time-constant true expression, making the constant assignment always execute instead. This is an intentional obfuscated dead-code pattern in the OWASP Benchmark test suite that prevents user input from ever reaching the file path.",
        "impact": "None in practice: the file path used in FileOutputStream is always the hardcoded string, so no attacker-controlled path traversal is possible via this code path.",
        "remediation": [
          "If similar patterns exist elsewhere with genuinely reachable tainted branches, sanitize the parameter using FilenameUtils.getName() or canonicalize and validate against an allow-listed base directory before use in FileOutputStream.",
          "Add unit/integration tests to confirm which branch is actually reachable to avoid relying on obfuscated constant-folding logic for security decisions.",
          "Remove dead code branches to improve clarity and prevent future maintenance errors that could inadvertently make the tainted branch reachable without proper sanitization."
        ]
      }
