          "confidence:MEDIUM": 0.1,
          "impact:MEDIUM": 0.05,
          "likelihood:HIGH": 0.1,
          "cwe_top25": 0.1,
          "subcategory:vuln": 0.1,
          "signal:path_traversal": 0.2,
          "sanitizer_hint": -0.1
        }
      },
      "analysis": {
        "exploitability_evidence": "Source: HttpServletRequest parameter 'BenchmarkTest00627' obtained via SeparateClassRequest.getTheParameter -> request.getParameter(p). This value 'param' is base64-encoded then immediately base64-decoded (bar = decode(encode(param.getBytes()))), which is a no-op round-trip that does not alter or sanitize the string content (no removal of '../' or path separators). The resulting 'bar' is concatenated directly to Utils.TESTFILES_DIR to form 'fileName', which is passed unmodified into 'new java.io.FileOutputStream(fileName, false)' - the flagged sink. The only sanitization present (ESAPI encodeForHTML) is applied to the fileName only when writing it into the HTTP response body for display, not before it is used in the FileOutputStream constructor. Therefore user input reaches a file-system write sink without any path traversal validation (no canonicalization, no check for '..', no restriction to a base directory).",
        "verdict": "True Positive",
        "confidence": "high",
        "severity_assessment": "high",
        "root_cause": "User-supplied request parameter is concatenated into a file path used to open a FileOutputStream without validating or canonicalizing the path to prevent directory traversal sequences like '../'.",
        "impact": "An attacker can supply a parameter value containing '../' sequences to write files outside the intended TESTFILES_DIR, potentially overwriting arbitrary files on the filesystem, leading to data corruption, application compromise, or in some environments remote code execution.",
        "remediation": [
          "Canonicalize the resulting file path (e.g., using File.getCanonicalPath()) and verify it still resides within the intended base directory before opening the FileOutputStream.",
          "Reject or strip path traversal sequences ('..', absolute path indicators) from the user-supplied filename component.",
          "Use a whitelist approach: only allow a restricted set of characters (e.g., alphanumeric) in the filename portion, or map user input to a safe filename via a lookup table.",
          "Consider using java.nio.file.Path with normalization and a strict prefix check against the allowed base directory."
        ]
      }
    },
