        "small_model": {
          "key": "org/owasp/benchmark/testcode/BenchmarkTest00630.java:82:21-82:49:java.lang.security.audit.tainted-ldapi-from-http-request.tainted-ldapi-from-http-request",
          "decision": "escalate",
          "reason": "HTTPServletRequest parameter flows through switch statement into LDAP filter string concatenation without sanitization, enabling LDAP injection regardless of which switch case executes.",
          "error": null,
          "elapsed_seconds": 1.426
        }
      },
      "analysis": {
        "exploitability_evidence": "Source: request.getParameter(\"BenchmarkTest00630\") via SeparateClassRequest.getTheParameter -> param. Flow: guess=\"ABC\", switchTarget=guess.charAt(2)='C', so the switch deterministically executes case 'C', setting bar = param (untrusted). Sink: filter = \"(&(objectclass=person)(uid=\" + bar + \"))\" is passed directly to idc.search(base, filter, sc) with no LDAP-specific encoding/escaping (e.g., no escaping of '(', ')', '*', '\\\\', NUL) applied to bar before concatenation. The only sanitization present in the file is ESAPI.encoder().encodeForHTML(...) applied to output data for the HTTP response, which is irrelevant to the LDAP filter sink. Therefore attacker-controlled input reaches the LDAP search filter unsanitized.",
        "verdict": "True Positive",
        "confidence": "high",
        "severity_assessment": "high",
        "root_cause": "User-supplied HTTP parameter is concatenated directly into an LDAP search filter string without any LDAP-specific escaping/sanitization before being used in DirContext.search().",
        "impact": "An attacker can inject LDAP filter metacharacters (e.g., '*', ')', '(', '|', '&') to manipulate the search filter logic, potentially bypassing intended query constraints, enumerating or exfiltrating LDAP directory entries, or altering the boolean logic of the query (classic LDAP injection).",
        "remediation": [
          "Use a vetted LDAP encoding library (e.g., OWASP ESAPI's encodeForLDAP, or Apache Directory API's Rdn/Filter escaping utilities) to escape special characters in the untrusted value before building the filter string.",
          "Prefer parameterized/programmatic filter construction APIs (e.g., javax.naming with proper escaping, or Spring LDAP's LdapQueryBuilder with filter arguments) instead of manual string concatenation.",
          "Apply strict allow-list input validation on the parameter (e.g., restrict to expected characters such as alphanumeric) before using it in the filter.",
          "Avoid trusting client-controlled branching (switch on hardcoded values) as a security control; ensure sanitization is applied uniformly regardless of code path."
        ]
      }
    },
