        "root_cause": "The switch statement's target character is derived from a hardcoded string literal (\"ABC\".charAt(1) == 'B'), making the branches that would propagate the tainted HTTP parameter into 'bar' unreachable dead code. The scanner's static taint tracker does not evaluate this constant condition and flags the sink even though the tainted value can never actually flow into it at runtime.",
        "impact": "None in practice: the exec call always executes with the fixed string \"bob\" (plus a safe, non-user-controlled 'echo' command prefix on Windows), so no attacker-controlled data reaches the OS command sink.",
        "remediation": [
          "No functional fix required since the vulnerable branch is unreachable, but if this pattern is intentional test/benchmark code, no action needed.",
          "If similar patterns exist in production code, remove dead/unreachable branches or ensure the discriminant used in conditional/switch statements is not effectively constant when tainted data is expected to flow through them.",
