      "analysis": {
        "exploitability_evidence": "Source: request.getParameterValues(\"BenchmarkTest00700\") -> param (attacker-controlled). param is Base64-encoded then immediately Base64-decoded (Base64.decodeBase64(Base64.encodeBase64(param.getBytes()))), which is a no-op round trip and does not strip or validate '../' or other path characters -> bar equals param unchanged. fileName = Utils.TESTFILES_DIR + bar is then passed directly to `new java.io.FileOutputStream(new java.io.File(fileName))` at line 63. No path canonicalization, allow-list check, or FilenameUtils.getName() call is applied to fileName before it reaches the file-write sink. The only encoding used (ESAPI encodeForHTML) is applied to the fileName only when writing it into the HTTP response body for display, not to the value used in the FileOutputStream call, so it provides no protection against path traversal.",
        "verdict": "True Positive",
        "confidence": "high",
        "severity_assessment": "high",
        "root_cause": "User-supplied request parameter is concatenated onto a base directory path and used to open a FileOutputStream without any path traversal sanitization (e.g., canonicalization, filename-only extraction, or rejection of '../' sequences); the Base64 encode/decode round trip performed on the value has no sanitizing effect.",
        "impact": "An attacker can supply a parameter value containing '../' sequences (or an absolute path) to write files outside the intended TESTFILES_DIR, potentially overwriting arbitrary files on the filesystem reachable by the application process, leading to data corruption, code execution (e.g. overwriting webshells/config files), or denial of service.",
        "remediation": [
          "Use org.apache.commons.io.FilenameUtils.getName(bar) or java.nio.file.Path canonicalization to strip directory components before building the file path.",
          "Resolve the final path against the intended base directory and verify (via Path.normalize()/toRealPath() and startsWith check) that it remains within that base directory before use.",
          "Reject or encode any path separators or '..' sequences in the user-supplied parameter.",
          "Avoid deriving file system paths directly from user input; use an indirect reference (e.g., a whitelist/id-to-filename map) instead."
        ]
      }
    },
