      },
      "analysis": {
        "exploitability_evidence": "Source: request.getParameterValues(\"BenchmarkTest00728\") -> values[0] assigned to param (attacker-controlled, no validation). param is copied to bar via an always-true arithmetic condition ((500/42)+196 > 200), so bar always equals param. bar is then written directly with response.getWriter().println(bar) with no HTML encoding, escaping, or sanitization applied anywhere on this path. The sink is a raw Writer output, bypassing any view-layer escaping, so a payload like <script>alert(1)</script> supplied via the request parameter would be reflected verbatim in the response body.",
        "verdict": "True Positive",
        "confidence": "high",
        "severity_assessment": "medium",
        "root_cause": "User-supplied HTTP request parameter is written to the HTTP response body via response.getWriter().println() without any output encoding or sanitization, allowing injected script/HTML to be reflected back to the browser.",
        "impact": "Reflected Cross-Site Scripting (XSS): an attacker can craft a URL/query parameter containing malicious script that will be executed in the context of the victim's browser session, potentially leading to session hijacking, credential theft, or other client-side attacks.",
        "remediation": [
          "Encode the parameter value with an HTML/context-aware encoder (e.g., OWASP Java Encoder's Encode.forHtml()) before writing it to the response.",
          "Avoid writing directly to the raw Writer/OutputStream for user-controlled data; use a templating engine (JSP/JSF) that auto-escapes output.",
          "Validate/allow-list the expected format of the parameter (e.g., restrict to alphanumeric) if only limited values are legitimate.",
          "Set appropriate Content-Security-Policy headers as defense in depth."
        ]
      }
    },
