      "analysis": {
        "exploitability_evidence": "Source: request.getParameterValues(\"BenchmarkTest00738\") assigned to param, then bar is set via: if ((7*42)-86 > 200) bar='This_should_always_happen'; else bar=param. Evaluating the constant expression: 7*42=294, 294-86=208, and 208>200 is always true, so the if-branch always executes and bar is always assigned the hardcoded string, never the tainted 'param' value. The else branch containing bar=param is dead code, unreachable at runtime. Consequently, the value flowing into args (used in Runtime.exec via ProcessBuilder-style array) is always the constant, not user input, so the exec sink at line 78 never receives attacker-controlled data.",
        "verdict": "False Positive",
        "confidence": "high",
        "severity_assessment": "low",
        "root_cause": "The conditional guarding assignment of the tainted parameter to 'bar' contains a constant arithmetic expression that always evaluates to true, making the branch that assigns tainted input to 'bar' unreachable dead code. This is a classic OWASP Benchmark 'safe' test pattern using an always-true condition to prevent taint from reaching the sink.",
        "impact": "None in practice: no attacker input reaches the exec sink because the code path assigning tainted data to the variable used in the command is unreachable.",
        "remediation": [
          "No functional fix required since the tainted branch is unreachable, but for code clarity remove the dead branch or the constant expression to avoid confusion.",
          "If the intent was to actually use user input in some paths, replace exec with an API that avoids shell interpretation and validate/whitelist input strictly."
        ]
      }
