      "analysis": {
        "exploitability_evidence": "Source: request.getParameterValues(\"BenchmarkTest00755\") assigns to `param`. However, `bar` is set via: if ((7*42)-86 > 200) bar = \"This_should_always_happen\"; else bar = param;. Evaluating the condition: (7*42)-86 = 294-86 = 208, and 208 > 200 is always true, so the true branch always executes and bar is always the hardcoded constant \"This_should_always_happen\". The else branch that would assign the tainted `param` to `bar` is dead code, unreachable at runtime. Therefore `request.getSession().putValue(\"userid\", bar)` always stores a constant string, never user input, despite the taint tracker flagging it due to static data-flow analysis not evaluating the constant-folded condition.",
        "verdict": "False Positive",
        "confidence": "high",
        "severity_assessment": "info",
        "root_cause": "The conditional branch that would propagate tainted user input to `bar` is statically unreachable because the guard condition (7*42-86>200) always evaluates to true, so `bar` is always assigned the hardcoded string literal rather than the parameter value. The SAST tool's taint engine does not evaluate the constant arithmetic condition and thus over-approximates the flow as tainted.",
        "impact": "None in this instance \u2014 no actual user input reaches the session attribute. If similar patterns without the dead-code guard existed elsewhere, unvalidated user input placed into session state could enable trust boundary violations, potentially leading to session fixation or trusted-data misuse.",
        "remediation": [
          "No fix required for this instance since the code path is dead; however, remove the misleading dead-code pattern for clarity.",
          "If real user input must be stored in session attributes, validate/sanitize it against an allow-list before calling setAttribute/putValue.",
          "Treat all session attribute values originating from user input as untrusted throughout the application to avoid trust boundary violations."
        ]
      }
