      "analysis": {
        "exploitability_evidence": "The flagged sink is response.getWriter().println(\"Your results are: \" + String.valueOf(results)) at line 57. Tracing back: 'results' is declared as 'Long results = ...queryForObject(sql, Long.class)'. Although the SQL string 'sql' is built by concatenating the unsanitized request parameter 'bar' (from request.getParameterValues), the value written to the writer is NOT the raw parameter or the SQL string \u2014 it is the return value of the JDBC query coerced to type Long. String.valueOf(Long) can only produce digits and an optional leading '-', so no HTML/JS metacharacters can reach the response body via this statement, regardless of what SQL injection the query itself may be vulnerable to. The other branches in the same method that do write attacker-influenced strings (the EmptyResultDataAccessException branch) explicitly wrap the value in ESAPI's encoder().encodeForHTML(sql) before writing, showing the developer intentionally sanitized string outputs, but that branch is not the one flagged. Thus at the specific flagged line, there is no reflected attacker string reaching the writer.",
        "verdict": "False Positive",
        "confidence": "high",
        "severity_assessment": "info",
        "root_cause": "The scanner flags any use of response.getWriter()/println with a nearby tainted variable in scope, but at the specific sink line the actual value written (String.valueOf(Long)) is type-constrained to numeric characters and cannot carry HTML/JS payloads, so no XSS is possible even though the underlying SQL query construction is vulnerable to a different vulnerability class (SQL injection, CWE-89), not XSS.",
        "impact": "None for XSS via this specific statement \u2014 a Long-derived numeric string cannot inject script content into the HTML response.",
        "remediation": [
          "No change needed for XSS at this line since the value is numeric-only.",
          "Separately fix the SQL injection by using PreparedStatement/parameterized queries instead of string concatenation for building 'sql'.",
          "As defense-in-depth, consider consistently applying output encoding (e.g., ESAPI encodeForHTML) to all writer output regardless of perceived type safety."
        ]
      }
