      "analysis": {
        "exploitability_evidence": "Source: request.getParameterValues(\"BenchmarkTest00765\") -> param (attacker-controlled HTTP parameter). param flows into thing.doSomething(param) which resolves via ThingFactory to either Thing1.doSomething (returns input unchanged) or Thing2.doSomething (wraps in StringBuilder, still returns unchanged value) - neither performs any escaping or validation. The result 'bar' is directly concatenated into the SQL string: \"SELECT userid from USERS where USERNAME='foo' and PASSWORD='\" + bar + \"'\". This raw string is passed to DatabaseHelper.JDBCtemplate.queryForObject(sql, Long.class), executing it as a SQL statement with no parameterization or bind variables. The only encoding present (ESAPI encodeForHTML) is applied later, only in the EmptyResultDataAccessException catch block, and only for HTML output display purposes \u2014 it does not run before the query executes and would not neutralize SQL metacharacters anyway. Thus attacker input reaches the SQL sink completely unsanitized.",
        "verdict": "True Positive",
        "confidence": "high",
        "severity_assessment": "high",
        "root_cause": "User-supplied HTTP parameter is concatenated directly into a SQL query string without parameterization or sanitization before being executed via queryForObject.",
        "impact": "An attacker can inject arbitrary SQL via the BenchmarkTest00765 parameter to bypass authentication logic, extract or manipulate data, or in some DB configurations execute further malicious SQL, leading to data breach or integrity compromise.",
        "remediation": [
          "Use parameterized queries / PreparedStatement with bind variables instead of string concatenation for the SQL command.",
          "If using Spring JDBC, use NamedParameterJdbcTemplate or JdbcTemplate.queryForObject(sql, Object[] args, Class<T> requiredType) with placeholders instead of building the SQL string manually.",
          "Apply strict input validation/allow-listing on the parameter if it must be used in a dynamic context.",
          "Do not rely on HTML encoding (ESAPI encodeForHTML) as a SQL injection defense; it only protects output rendering, not query construction."
        ]
      }
    },
