      "analysis": {
        "exploitability_evidence": "Source: request.getParameterValues(\"BenchmarkTest00767\") -> param (unsanitized). The switch statement is based on switchTarget = \"ABC\".charAt(2) = 'C' (hardcoded, always 'C'), so the code deterministically executes case 'C'/'D': bar = param. Thus bar is always the raw user-supplied parameter. This flows directly into: String sql = \"SELECT userid from USERS where USERNAME='foo' and PASSWORD='\" + bar + \"'\"; which is then executed via org.owasp.benchmark.helpers.DatabaseHelper.JDBCtemplate.queryForObject(sql, Long.class) \u2014 a raw SQL string execution, not a parameterized query. No sanitizer, escaper, or parameterized binding is applied to bar/param before it is concatenated into the SQL string. The only encoding present (ESAPI encodeForHTML) is applied to the sql string in an exception handler for HTML output, not to neutralize SQL metacharacters before query execution, and it occurs after the query has already run. This is a textbook classic SQL Injection via string concatenation with attacker-controlled HTTP parameter.",
        "verdict": "True Positive",
        "confidence": "high",
        "severity_assessment": "high",
        "root_cause": "User-supplied HTTP parameter (param) is assigned to bar through a switch statement whose branch condition is always constant/true ('C'), then concatenated directly into a SQL raw SQL query string without any sanitization, escaping, or parameterized binding before being executed via queryForObject.",
        "impact": "An attacker can inject arbitrary SQL through the BenchmarkTest00767 parameter, potentially bypassing authentication (the query checks USERNAME/PASSWORD), extracting arbitrary data from the USERS table, or leveraging further SQL injection techniques (union-based extraction, boolean/time-based blind injection) depending on the underlying DB and permissions.",
        "remediation": [
          "Use parameterized queries / PreparedStatement with bind variables for the SQL query instead of string concatenation.",
          "If using Spring's JdbcTemplate, use queryForObject(sql, Long.class, param) with '?' placeholders rather than building the SQL string manually.",
          "Apply strict input validation/allow-listing on the parameter if it represents a known format (e.g., alphanumeric username).",
          "Avoid using ESAPI HTML encoding as a substitute for SQL injection protection; it is irrelevant to this sink."
        ]
      }
