        "exploitability_evidence": "Source: request.getParameterValues(\"BenchmarkTest00774\") assigned to 'param'. This flows into 'bar' only in switch cases 'A', 'C', or 'D'. However switchTarget = guess.charAt(1) where guess=\"ABC\", so switchTarget is always the constant 'B', which unconditionally executes case 'B': bar = \"bob\" (a hardcoded string). The tainted 'param' value is never assigned to 'bar' since the branches that would do so ('A','C','D') are unreachable given the fixed guess string. Consequently the SQL string built at line 74 (INSERT INTO users ... VALUES ('foo','\" + bar + \"')) always contains the constant \"bob\", not attacker input, before reaching statement.executeUpdate(sql).",
        "verdict": "False Positive",
        "confidence": "high",
        "severity_assessment": "low",
        "root_cause": "The switch statement's controlling variable is derived from a hardcoded string (\"ABC\".charAt(1)), so it always evaluates to case 'B', which assigns a constant to 'bar' instead of the tainted 'param'. The branches that would allow the tainted parameter to reach the SQL sink are dead code, so no attacker-controlled data actually flows into the SQL string despite the surface-level match on 'param' being used inside a switch that also touches 'bar' used in the query.",
        "impact": "None in the current code path \u2014 the SQL query is always built with the constant string \"bob\", so no injection is possible via this endpoint as written.",
        "remediation": [
          "If this switch logic is intentional test scaffolding, no fix needed since it's dead code for the tainted branch; if it's meant to reflect real routing logic, replace string concatenation with a PreparedStatement and bind 'bar' as a parameter to eliminate the class of vulnerability entirely, regardless of which branch executes.",
          "Remove or fix the always-taken 'B' branch if the intent was to test other paths, and use parameterized queries in all branches to be safe against future logic changes."
        ]
      }
