        "exploitability_evidence": "Source: request.getQueryString() -> extracted into 'param' via substring operations locating the 'BenchmarkTest00807=' key, then URL-decoded via java.net.URLDecoder.decode(param, \"UTF-8\") (decoding does not sanitize/escape HTML). The value is assigned to 'bar' (bar = param.split(\" \")[0]) and passed directly as the format string to response.getWriter().printf(java.util.Locale.US, bar, obj) at line 75. No HTML-encoding, output escaping, or allowlist validation is applied to 'bar' anywhere on this path. Since printf writes bar's content directly to the raw response OutputStream/Writer, an attacker can inject '<script>...</script>' or other markup via the query string parameter, and it will be reflected unescaped in the HTTP response body \u2014 a classic reflected XSS.",
