          "decision": "escalate",
          "reason": "User-controlled query parameter is extracted, URL-decoded, concatenated with a string, and written directly to response via getWriter().println() without HTML encoding, creating a direct XSS vulnerability.",
          "error": null,
          "elapsed_seconds": 1.653
        }
      },
      "analysis": {
        "exploitability_evidence": "Source: request.getQueryString() (line 43) is fully attacker-controlled (raw HTTP query string). The code extracts the substring after 'BenchmarkTest00810=' (paramLoc/ampersandLoc logic, lines 55-64), URL-decodes it via java.net.URLDecoder.decode(param, 'UTF-8') (line 66) \u2014 decoding does not sanitize/escape HTML, it only reverses percent-encoding, so a payload like %3Cscript%3E survives as <script>. The decoded value is placed into a StringBuilder and appended with the constant '_SafeStuff' (lines 68-69), producing 'bar' which still contains the raw attacker payload as a prefix. This value is then written directly to the response via response.getWriter().println(bar.toCharArray()) (line 74) with no HTML encoding, escaping, or output-context validation applied anywhere on the path. The X-XSS-Protection header is explicitly disabled ('0'), removing a legacy browser mitigation. No sanitizer/encoder adequate for an HTML sink is present.",
        "verdict": "True Positive",
        "confidence": "high",
        "severity_assessment": "medium",
        "root_cause": "User-supplied query string parameter is URL-decoded and written directly to the HTTP response body via getWriter().println() without any HTML/JS output encoding or validation, allowing injection of arbitrary markup/script.",
        "impact": "An attacker can craft a URL whose query string parameter contains HTML/JavaScript; when a victim visits the crafted link, the payload is reflected unescaped into the response and executed in the victim's browser context, enabling session hijacking, credential theft, defacement, or further client-side attacks (reflected XSS).",
        "remediation": [
          "HTML-encode the decoded parameter value before writing it to the response (e.g., use OWASP Java Encoder's Encode.forHtml() or a JSP/JSF templating engine that auto-escapes output).",
          "Avoid writing directly to the raw OutputStream/Writer for dynamic content; use a view technology (JSP/JSF) with automatic contextual escaping.",
          "Validate/allow-list expected parameter format (e.g., alphanumeric) if the value is not meant to contain arbitrary characters.",
          "Do not disable the X-XSS-Protection header; consider a strong Content-Security-Policy as defense in depth."
        ]
      }
    },
