      "analysis": {
        "exploitability_evidence": "Source: request.getQueryString() (attacker-controlled). Value is parsed to extract the 'BenchmarkTest00815' parameter, URL-decoded via URLDecoder.decode, and assigned to 'bar' with no other transformation. 'bar' is then concatenated directly into a shell command string: args = {a1, a2, \"echo \" + bar}, where a1/a2 are 'sh'/'-c' or 'cmd.exe'/'/c'. This args array is passed to pb.command(args) and pb.start() executes it via a shell interpreter (sh -c or cmd.exe /c). Because the shell (-c) is invoked, any shell metacharacters (;, |, `` ` ``, $(), &&, etc.) in the URL parameter will be interpreted by the shell, allowing arbitrary command injection. No sanitizer, allow-list, or escaping is applied to 'bar' before it is embedded in the command string.",
        "verdict": "True Positive",
        "confidence": "high",
        "severity_assessment": "high",
        "root_cause": "User-supplied query string parameter is decoded and directly concatenated into a shell command string ('echo ' + bar) that is executed via ProcessBuilder using a shell interpreter (sh -c / cmd.exe /c), without any neutralization of shell metacharacters.",
        "impact": "An attacker can inject arbitrary OS commands by supplying shell metacharacters in the query parameter, leading to remote code execution with the privileges of the web application process, potentially resulting in full system compromise, data exfiltration, or further lateral movement.",
        "remediation": [
          "Avoid invoking a shell (sh -c / cmd.exe /c) with user-controlled input; instead pass the command and its arguments as separate array elements to ProcessBuilder without shell interpretation.",
          "If a shell must be used, strictly validate/allow-list the input against a narrow expected pattern (e.g., alphanumeric only) before use.",
          "Use platform APIs or higher-level libraries that avoid spawning shell processes for this functionality.",
          "Apply the principle of least privilege to the process running the web application to limit the impact of any command execution."
        ]
      }
    },
