      "analysis": {
        "exploitability_evidence": "Source: request.getQueryString() -> param extracted from query string, URL-decoded, then stored into HashMap map34260 under key 'keyB-34260' and immediately retrieved back into 'bar' (bar = (String) map34260.get(...)), overwriting the initial safe literal 'safe!'. No sanitization or validation is applied to param before or after the map round-trip. This tainted 'bar' is then concatenated into args[2] = \"echo \" + bar, and args is passed to pb.command(args) followed by pb.start(). Because the command is executed via 'sh -c' (or 'cmd.exe /c'), the entire concatenated string 'echo <bar>' is interpreted by the shell, so shell metacharacters (e.g. ; | & $() ) in the user-supplied query parameter will be executed as additional commands. No encoder/sanitizer for shell metacharacters is applied anywhere on this path (ESAPI encodeForHTML is only used later on the *output* of the command, not on the input).",
        "verdict": "True Positive",
        "confidence": "high",
        "severity_assessment": "high",
        "root_cause": "User-controlled HTTP query parameter is stored/retrieved via a HashMap (a no-op pass-through) and then concatenated directly into a shell command string that is executed by ProcessBuilder using 'sh -c'/'cmd.exe /c', without any validation, allow-listing, or escaping of shell metacharacters.",
        "impact": "An attacker can inject arbitrary OS commands by supplying shell metacharacters in the query string parameter, leading to arbitrary command execution on the server with the privileges of the web application process (full remote code execution).",
        "remediation": [
          "Avoid invoking a shell interpreter (sh -c / cmd.exe /c) with concatenated user input; instead pass the command and its arguments as separate array elements to ProcessBuilder without shell interpretation.",
          "Strictly validate/allow-list the expected input (e.g., only alphanumeric characters) before using it in any command construction.",
          "If shell invocation is unavoidable, use a vetted OS command escaping/sanitization library appropriate for the target shell before including user input.",
          "Apply the principle of least privilege to the process running the command to limit impact if injection occurs."
        ]
      }
    },
