        "exploitability_evidence": "Source: request.getQueryString() -> param extracted and URL-decoded. However, param is only assigned to 'bar' in switch cases 'A','C','D'; the switch variable switchTarget is derived from a hardcoded literal guess=\"ABC\", so switchTarget = guess.charAt(1) = 'B' always. This forces execution into case 'B', which sets bar = \"bob\" (a constant), never bar = param. The SQL string 'sql' is built by concatenating this constant 'bar' value, and the query is actually executed via a PreparedStatement with parameter binding for the '?' placeholder anyway. Since the tainted 'param' variable can never reach the 'bar' variable that feeds into the SQL string (the 'A'/'C'/'D' branches are dead code given the constant switchTarget), the flagged sink never receives attacker-controlled data at runtime.",
        "verdict": "False Positive",
        "confidence": "high",
        "severity_assessment": "low",
        "root_cause": "The switch statement's controlling variable is derived from a hardcocded constant string (\"ABC\").charAt(1), which always evaluates to 'B'. This makes the branches that assign the tainted 'param' to 'bar' unreachable dead code; 'bar' is always the literal \"bob\" when the program actually runs, so the SQL query never contains attacker-controlled input.",
        "impact": "None in practice - the vulnerable code path is unreachable due to the constant switch condition, so no SQL injection is possible via this method as written.",
        "remediation": [
          "If this code path is intended to be reachable (e.g., in a corrected version), use PreparedStatement parameter binding for 'bar' instead of string concatenation.",
          "Remove dead/unreachable code branches to avoid confusion and static analysis noise.",
          "Validate that switchTarget is genuinely dynamic if user-controlled branching is intended."
        ]
      }
