      "analysis": {
        "exploitability_evidence": "Source: request.getQueryString() (line 43) is fully attacker-controlled (raw query string). It is parsed to extract 'param' via substring operations, then URL-decoded (java.net.URLDecoder.decode) - decoding is not sanitization for SQL context. The value is assigned to 'bar' via a ternary condition (7*42-num>200 ? ... : param) which always evaluates false (188>200 is false), so bar always equals the tainted param. This 'bar' is directly concatenated into a raw SQL string: \"SELECT * from USERS where USERNAME='foo' and PASSWORD='\" + bar + \"'\" (line 76-77 area, sink at line 80 per finding). This concatenated string is passed to DatabaseHelper.JDBCtemplate.batchUpdate(sql), which executes it as a raw SQL statement rather than using parameterized queries/PreparedStatement with bind variables. No SQL-specific escaping, allowlist validation, or parameterization occurs anywhere on this path - only URL decoding and HTML encoding (encodeForHTML) which is applied to a different string (the response output) after the fact, not before the SQL execution, and HTML encoding is irrelevant to SQL injection anyway.",
        "verdict": "True Positive",
        "confidence": "high",
        "severity_assessment": "high",
        "root_cause": "User-supplied query string parameter is decoded and directly concatenated into a SQL query string without parameterization or SQL-specific escaping, then executed via a batch update call that runs raw SQL.",
        "impact": "An attacker can inject arbitrary SQL via the query string parameter, potentially bypassing authentication, exfiltrating or modifying data in the USERS table, or in more permissive database configurations execute stacked queries or other SQL commands.",
        "remediation": [
          "Use parameterized queries / PreparedStatement with bind variables instead of string concatenation for the SQL command.",
          "If using Spring JdbcTemplate, use the overload that accepts a SQL string with '?' placeholders and an array of parameter values (or NamedParameterJdbcTemplate) rather than building the SQL string manually.",
          "Apply strict input validation/allowlisting on the parameter before use, in addition to parameterization.",
          "Avoid using batchUpdate with a manually concatenated SQL string; use PreparedStatement-based batch operations."
        ]
      }
    },
