      "analysis": {
        "exploitability_evidence": "Source: request.getQueryString() \u2192 param extracted via substring/indexOf logic \u2192 URLDecoder.decode(param, 'UTF-8') \u2192 stored into a List and retrieved unchanged as 'bar' (the add/remove operations do not sanitize, they just obfuscate the flow) \u2192 sql = \"SELECT * from USERS where USERNAME='foo' and PASSWORD='\" + bar + \"'\" \u2192 java.sql.Statement (not PreparedStatement) via DatabaseHelper.getSqlStatement() \u2192 statement.addBatch(sql); statement.executeBatch(). No validation, escaping, or parameterization is applied to 'bar' before it is concatenated into the SQL string and executed as a batch statement.",
        "verdict": "True Positive",
        "confidence": "high",
        "severity_assessment": "high",
        "root_cause": "User-supplied query string parameter is URL-decoded and directly concatenated into a SQL query string that is executed via a raw java.sql.Statement instead of a parameterized PreparedStatement, with no input validation or escaping applied.",
        "impact": "An attacker can inject arbitrary SQL via the query string parameter (e.g., closing the quote and appending malicious SQL), potentially bypassing authentication, exfiltrating data, or modifying/deleting database records.",
        "remediation": [
          "Use java.sql.PreparedStatement with parameterized queries instead of building SQL via string concatenation.",
          "Bind the user-supplied value using setString() rather than embedding it directly in the SQL text.",
          "Apply strict input validation/allow-listing on the parameter if it must be used outside of a bind variable.",
          "Avoid using Statement.addBatch/executeBatch with dynamically constructed SQL containing user input."
        ]
      }
    },
