      "analysis": {
        "exploitability_evidence": "The sink is `new java.io.File(fileName)` at line ~57, where fileName = Utils.TESTFILES_DIR + bar, and bar = thing.doSomething(param), and param = scr.getTheValue(\"BenchmarkTest00866\"). Tracing back to the source: SeparateClassRequest.getTheValue(String p) is shown to simply `return \"bar\";` \u2014 it ignores its input parameter and always returns the hardcoded literal string \"bar\", regardless of any HTTP request parameter. Thing1.doSomething and Thing2.doSomething merely pass the input through unchanged (return i or a StringBuilder copy of i), so bar is always the constant \"bar\" in this code path. Consequently fileName is always TESTFILES_DIR + \"bar\", a fully constant, application-controlled path with no attacker input reaching the File constructor. There is no user-controlled data on this path as shown.",
        "verdict": "False Positive",
        "confidence": "medium",
        "severity_assessment": "low",
        "root_cause": "The helper method getTheValue, as shown, does not read from the HttpServletRequest at all \u2014 it returns a hardcoded string literal (\"bar\") ignoring its parameter, so no attacker-controlled data ever enters the file path construction. The doSomething implementations are simple pass-throughs, so they don't introduce taint either.",
        "impact": "None in this exact code path since the file name is a compile-time constant; if a real deployment version of getTheValue actually read a request parameter, the resulting path traversal into java.io.File would allow arbitrary file read/write outside the intended TESTFILES_DIR, but that is not what the shown code does.",
        "remediation": [
          "If getTheValue is intended to retrieve real user input in production, ensure the returned value is validated against a canonical directory using something like FilenameUtils.getName() or Path.normalize()+startsWith(baseDir) checks before use in File construction.",
          "Add an allow-list check on the resulting canonical path against the intended TESTFILES_DIR before performing file operations.",
          "Confirm test/mock helper implementations (like the hardcoded getTheValue) are not shipped to production, since they mask any real path traversal risk in this analysis."
        ]
      }
