      "analysis": {
        "exploitability_evidence": "The flagged sink is `new ProcessBuilder(argList)` where argList contains \"echo \" + bar. Tracing back, bar is derived from param = scr.getTheValue(\"BenchmarkTest00895\"). The shown body of SeparateClassRequest.getTheValue ignores its parameter and unconditionally returns the literal string \"bar\" (`return \"bar\";`). It does not read any HttpServletRequest parameter, header, or other attacker-controlled input. Consequently param is always the constant \"bar\", and bar/argList content is fully deterministic, not influenced by user input. No sanitizer is needed because there is no tainted data reaching the sink.",
        "verdict": "False Positive",
        "confidence": "high",
        "severity_assessment": "low",
        "root_cause": "The helper method that is supposed to source user input (getTheValue) is implemented to return a hardcoded constant string instead of reading actual request data, so the value reaching ProcessBuilder is not attacker-controlled.",
        "impact": "None in the current code path since no external input reaches the command construction; however if getTheValue were later changed to actually read user input without sanitization, command injection would become possible.",
        "remediation": [
          "If getTheValue is intended to retrieve real user input in production, ensure any value used in ProcessBuilder command construction is strictly validated/allow-listed against expected characters before use.",
          "Avoid string concatenation of untrusted input into shell commands; use ProcessBuilder with an argument array and no shell interpretation (avoid 'sh -c') to reduce injection risk.",
          "Add unit/integration tests to confirm that getTheValue in production reflects actual request parameters, not a stubbed constant, to avoid false confidence in security testing."
        ]
      }
    },
