      "analysis": {
        "exploitability_evidence": "The flagged sink is `statement.executeUpdate(sql, ...)` at line 64, where sql = \"INSERT INTO users ... '\" + bar + \"')\". Tracing 'bar': it starts as \"alsosafe\", and only changes if `param != null`, where param = scr.getTheValue(\"BenchmarkTest00940\") from org.owasp.benchmark.helpers.SeparateClassRequest. The shown body of SeparateClassRequest.getTheValue is `return \"bar\";` \u2014 a hardcoded constant, completely ignoring the HttpServletRequest object supplied to the constructor and the parameter name argument. Therefore param is always the literal string \"bar\" (never null, never derived from any HTTP request field), and the subsequent list manipulation (add/remove/get) simply reassigns bar to this same fixed literal \"bar\". No attacker-controlled data reaches the SQL string concatenation or the executeUpdate sink in the code as shown.",
        "verdict": "False Positive",
        "confidence": "high",
        "severity_assessment": "low",
        "root_cause": "The helper method SeparateClassRequest.getTheValue is stubbed to always return the constant string \"bar\" instead of reading an actual HTTP request parameter, so the value concatenated into the SQL statement is never attacker-controlled based on the code provided.",
        "impact": "None under the current implementation \u2014 the SQL string is built from constants only, so no injection is possible via this path. If getTheValue were later changed to genuinely retrieve a request parameter, this would become an exploitable SQL injection since string concatenation is used instead of a PreparedStatement.",
        "remediation": [
          "Regardless of the current stubbed source, refactor to use java.sql.PreparedStatement with bind parameters instead of string concatenation for defense in depth.",
          "If SeparateClassRequest.getTheValue is intended to eventually read real request data, ensure it applies proper input validation/allow-listing before use in SQL context.",
          "Add unit/integration tests to confirm the helper class't behavior matches its intended contract, since a hardcoded stub silently defeats security testing here."
        ]
      }
