        "exploitability_evidence": "Source: cookie named 'BenchmarkTest00951' is read from request.getCookies(), URL-decoded via java.net.URLDecoder.decode(...) into 'param'. This attacker-controlled value is passed to new Test().doSomething(request, param) which returns 'bar' (the OWASP Benchmark helper 'Test' classes in this suite are pass-through wrappers that do not sanitize path input \u2014 they only apply benign string transformations). 'bar' is then concatenated directly into a file: URI string together with a fixed TESTFILES_DIR prefix, used to build java.net.URI fileURI and then java.io.File fileTarget = new File(fileURI). No canonicalization, whitelist check, or FilenameUtils.getName()-style sanitization is applied to 'bar' before it is used to construct the File object. The only defensive call present, ESAPI.encoder().encodeForHTML(fileTarget.toString()), is applied solely to the string used in the HTTP response body for XSS protection \u2014 it runs after the File object is already created and has zero effect on the path-traversal sink (new File(fileURI) / fileTarget.exists()). Therefore a value like '../../../../etc/passwd' (URL-encoded in the cookie) would let an attacker control which file is probed for existence, confirming the path traversal sink is reachable with unsanitized input.",
        "verdict": "True Positive",
        "confidence": "medium",
        "severity_assessment": "medium",
        "root_cause": "User-controlled cookie value is decoded and passed through a non-sanitizing helper method directly into file path construction (File object built from a URI containing the tainted segment) without any path traversal sanitization (no canonicalization, no rejection of '../' sequences, no use of getCanonicalPath/FilenameUtils.getName).",
        "impact": "An attacker can manipulate the cookie value to include directory traversal sequences (e.g., '../../') to probe for the existence of arbitrary files on the filesystem outside the intended TESTFILES_DIR, leading to information disclosure about the file system layout and potentially assisting further attacks.",
        "remediation": [
          "Validate/sanitize the decoded cookie value before using it in any filesystem path construction (reject or strip '../', '..\\\\', and absolute path indicators).",
          "Use FilenameUtils.getName(param) or similar to extract only the file name component, discarding any directory traversal segments.",
          "After constructing the File object, call getCanonicalPath() and verify it is still within the intended base directory (TESTFILES_DIR) before performing any filesystem operations such as exists().",
          "Avoid building file paths via URI concatenation with untrusted input; prefer java.nio.file.Path.resolve with strict normalization and containment checks."
        ]
      }
