      "analysis": {
        "exploitability_evidence": "Source: a cookie named 'BenchmarkTest00956' read via request.getCookies(), value URL-decoded into 'param'. This is passed to 'new Test().doSomething(request, param)' (helper body not shown, but in the OWASP Benchmark suite this method is typically a pass-through/identity transform that returns the input unmodified). The result 'bar' is concatenated with a fixed directory constant to form 'fileName', which is then used directly in 'new java.io.FileInputStream(fileName)' \u2014 the flagged path-traversal sink. No path canonicalization, allow-listing, or use of FilenameUtils.getName()/other sanitizer is applied anywhere on this path before the file constructor call. The only downstream treatment (ESAPI encodeForHTML) is applied only when writing the filename to the HTTP response for display, not as sanitization before the file open, so it does not mitigate the traversal.",
        "verdict": "True Positive",
        "confidence": "medium",
        "severity_assessment": "high",
        "root_cause": "User-controlled cookie value is decoded and concatenated into a file path with no validation, canonicalization, or restriction to a safe base directory before being used to open a file (FileInputStream), allowing '../' sequences to escape the intended directory.",
        "impact": "An attacker can supply a crafted cookie value containing directory traversal sequences to read or trigger access to arbitrary files on the filesystem that the application process can access, potentially leading to disclosure of sensitive files (configuration, credentials, etc.) or application errors revealing filesystem structure.",
        "remediation": [
          "Canonicalize the resulting file path (e.g., File.getCanonicalPath()) and verify it resides within the intended base directory (Utils.TESTFILES_DIR) before opening it.",
          "Reject or strip path traversal sequences ('../', absolute paths) from user input, or use org.apache.commons.io.FilenameUtils.getName() to extract only the file name component.",
          "Maintain an allow-list of permitted file names/IDs and map user input to that list rather than directly building a path from user input.",
          "Avoid concatenating raw user input into filesystem paths; use a safe API that binds file access to a fixed directory (e.g., java.nio.file.Path.resolve with strict containment checks)."
        ]
      }
    },
