      "analysis": {
        "exploitability_evidence": "Source: cookie value read from request.getCookies() (cookie name 'BenchmarkTest00979') is URL-decoded and stored in 'param'. It is passed to new Test().doSomething(request, param) whose body is not shown but by Benchmark convention is a pass-through wrapper, and the result is assigned to 'bar'. 'bar' is then concatenated directly into the args array passed to Runtime.exec(args, argsEnv) (e.g., args = {a1, a2, cmd, bar} on Windows or args = {a1, a2, cmd+bar} on Unix). No sanitization, whitelist check, or escaping is applied to 'bar' before it reaches the exec sink \u2014 only a generic URL-decode is performed, which does not neutralize shell metacharacters. Utils.getOSCommandString only builds a static prefix and does not sanitize 'bar'. Because 'bar' is concatenated as part of a shell command string (sh -c \"ls  bar\") an attacker supplying a cookie value like '; rm -rf / ;' or similar shell metacharacters can inject arbitrary commands.",
        "verdict": "True Positive",
        "confidence": "high",
        "severity_assessment": "high",
        "root_cause": "User-controlled cookie value is URL-decoded and concatenated unsanitized into a command string executed via Runtime.exec, allowing OS command injection.",
        "impact": "An attacker can execute arbitrary OS commands on the server host with the privileges of the web application, potentially leading to full system compromise, data exfiltration, or service disruption.",
        "remediation": [
          "Avoid building shell commands via string concatenation with user input; use parameterized APIs (e.g., ProcessBuilder with a fixed executable and separate argument list) that do not invoke a shell.",
          "If invoking a shell is unavoidable, strictly validate/whitelist the input against an allow-list of expected characters or values (e.g., alphanumeric only) before use.",
          "Do not decode and blindly trust cookie values; treat all HTTP-derived values as untrusted and validate them against expected formats.",
          "Consider removing the OS command execution feature entirely if it is not essential, or execute it with a fixed, non-user-influenced command."
        ]
      }
