      "analysis": {
        "exploitability_evidence": "Source: HTTP cookie value 'BenchmarkTest00982' -> URL-decoded into 'param' -> passed through Test().doSomething(request, param) to produce 'bar' (assumed to preserve taint). However, 'bar' is placed into the array 'argsEnv = {bar}', which is passed as the THIRD-position 'envp' argument of Runtime.exec(String[] cmdarray, String[] envp, File dir) \u2014 i.e., Runtime.exec(args, argsEnv, workingDir). The actual command array executed is 'args = {cmd}', where 'cmd' is derived from Utils.getInsecureOSCommandString(), a fixed classpath resource path (insecureCmd.bat/.sh) that is NOT attacker-controlled. Per Java semantics, envp only sets environment variables for the spawned process and is not concatenated into or interpreted as part of the command line; there is no shell invoked to expand metacharacters in 'bar'. Thus the tainted cookie value never reaches a position where it controls the executed program or its arguments \u2014 it only becomes (or fails to become, if malformed) an environment variable for the fixed script. The sink flagged by the rule (Runtime.exec with tainted data) is syntactically matched but the tainted value lands in the non-exploitable envp parameter rather than the cmdarray.",
