      "analysis": {
        "exploitability_evidence": "Source: request.getCookies() reads a cookie named 'BenchmarkTest00994' whose value is fully attacker-controlled (cookies are set by the client). The value is URL-decoded (java.net.URLDecoder.decode) into 'param' \u2014 URL-decoding is not a sanitizer, it merely decodes encoding. 'param' is then passed to new Test().doSomething(request, param) (body not shown) and the result 'bar' is stored directly into the HTTP session via request.getSession().putValue(\"userid\", bar) \u2014 the flagged sink. No validation/whitelisting of the value occurs before it lands in the session. The only sanitizer applied anywhere in this method, Utils.encodeForHTML(bar) via ESAPI.encoder().encodeForHTML, is applied only to the value written to the HTTP response for XSS mitigation and is not applied to the value stored in the session \u2014 it does not address the CWE-501 trust boundary sink at all. Although doSomething's implementation is not shown, in OWASP Benchmark test harness classes such wrapper methods are simple pass-through/transform helpers and do not perform trust-boundary sanitization; there is no other sanitizer on the path from cookie source to putValue sink.",
        "verdict": "True Positive",
        "confidence": "medium"
      }
    },
