      "analysis": {
        "exploitability_evidence": "Source: HTTP cookie 'BenchmarkTest00996' value, read via request.getCookies() and URL-decoded into 'param'. This attacker-controlled string is passed to new Test().doSomething(request, param) whose body is not shown, but its return value 'bar' is directly concatenated into the SQL command string: sql = \"{call \" + bar + \"}\". This concatenated string is passed unmodified to connection.prepareCall(sql) and executed via statement.executeQuery() with no parameter binding, no allow-list validation, and no escaping of SQL metacharacters visible anywhere in the shown code path. The only processing applied (URLDecoder.decode) is not a SQL sanitizer. DatabaseHelper.printResults only performs output encoding (ESAPI encodeForHTML) for the HTML response, which is irrelevant to the SQL injection sink. Because doSomething() is a helper typical of the Benchmark test suite that passes the tainted parameter through largely unchanged (no SQL-specific sanitization observed or implied by naming/usage elsewhere in the file), the tainted cookie value reaches the CallableStatement construction essentially unsanitized.",
        "verdict": "True Positive",
        "confidence": "medium",
        "severity_assessment": "high",
        "root_cause": "User-controlled cookie value is decoded and concatenated directly into a SQL command string used to build a CallableStatement, without parameterized query binding or adequate SQL-specific input validation/escaping.",
        "impact": "An attacker can manipulate the cookie value to inject arbitrary SQL/stored-procedure call syntax, potentially allowing unauthorized data access, data modification, or execution of arbitrary database commands (SQL Injection).",
        "remediation": [
          "Use parameterized queries / PreparedStatement or CallableStatement with bind parameters (e.g., '{call ?}' with setString) instead of string concatenation.",
          "Apply strict allow-list validation on the cookie-derived value (e.g., only allow expected stored procedure names) before using it in SQL.",
          "Avoid passing raw user input into SQL statement construction even through helper methods; ensure any such helper performs SQL-context-aware sanitization or rejects unexpected input.",
          "If dynamic SQL is unavoidable, use a vetted escaping library specific to the database driver in use, in addition to parameterization for all data values."
        ]
      }
    },
