      "analysis": {
        "exploitability_evidence": "Source: HTTP request cookie 'BenchmarkTest00997' value is read via request.getCookies(), URL-decoded, and stored in 'param'. This attacker-controlled string is passed to new Test().doSomething(request, param) whose body is not shown, but the returned value 'bar' is directly concatenated into a SQL statement: sql = \"{call \" + bar + \"}\". This string is then passed unmodified to connection.prepareCall(sql, ...) and executed via statement.executeQuery(). No parameterization (bind variables) is used - the entire callable-statement text is built via string concatenation of tainted input. The only helper method bodies shown (DatabaseHelper.getSqlConnection, DatabaseHelper.printResults) do not perform any input sanitization/escaping relevant to SQL construction; printResults only HTML-encodes output for the response, which is irrelevant to the SQL injection sink. There is no evidence of validation, escaping, or use of PreparedStatement parameter binding anywhere on this path before the tainted value reaches prepareCall/executeQuery.",
