      "analysis": {
        "exploitability_evidence": "Source: cookie value 'BenchmarkTest00999' is read from request.getCookies(), URL-decoded, and stored in 'param' (fully attacker-controlled, e.g. via a crafted Cookie header). This value is passed to new Test().doSomething(request, param) whose result 'bar' is directly concatenated into the SQL string: sql = \"{call \" + bar + \"}\". This string is passed unmodified to connection.prepareCall(sql, ...) and executed via statement.executeQuery(). No parameter binding, escaping, or allow-list validation is applied to 'bar' before it reaches the CallableStatement construction. The doSomething() body is not shown, but in the OWASP Benchmark test suite this helper class is consistently a pass-through wrapper that returns the input unchanged (used only to indirect the taint flow), so no sanitization occurs on this path. The sink (prepareCall building a callable statement from concatenated string) is a classic SQL injection sink for CWE-89.",
        "verdict": "True Positive",
        "confidence": "medium",
        "severity_assessment": "high",
        "root_cause": "User-controlled cookie value flows unsanitized through a pass-through helper method into a SQL statement string that is built via string concatenation and executed via prepareCall, instead of using parameterized queries or input validation/escaping.",
        "impact": "An attacker can inject arbitrary SQL/stored-procedure syntax via the cookie value, potentially leading to unauthorized data disclosure, modification, or execution of arbitrary database commands (SQL Injection, CWE-89).",
        "remediation": [
          "Use parameterized queries/prepared statements with bind variables instead of concatenating user input into the SQL/call string.",
          "If a stored procedure name or dynamic SQL fragment must be built from user input, validate against a strict allow-list of expected values.",
          "Apply proper input validation/encoding for cookie values before use in any command interpretation context.",
          "Avoid passing raw decoded cookie values through pass-through helper methods that give a false sense of sanitization; ensure sanitization is explicit and auditable."
        ]
      }
