        "exploitability_evidence": "Source: HTTP cookie named 'BenchmarkTest01002' read via request.getCookies(), value URL-decoded and stored in 'param'. This is passed to 'new Test().doSomething(request, param)' whose result 'bar' is directly concatenated into the SQL string: 'SELECT * from USERS where USERNAME=? and PASSWORD=\\'' + bar + '\\''. Although a PreparedStatement is created via connection.prepareStatement(sql, ...) and statement.setString(1,\"foo\") binds only the USERNAME placeholder, the PASSWORD value is embedded directly into the SQL text before the statement is prepared, so it is never treated as a bound parameter \u2014 it becomes literal SQL syntax. statement.execute() then executes this raw, attacker-influenced SQL string. No sanitization, escaping, or ESAPI encoding is applied to 'bar' before it enters the SQL text (ESAPI encoding is only used later for HTML output in printResults, which is irrelevant to SQL). The Test().doSomething() body isn't shown, but per OWASP Benchmark conventions this is a pass-through/no-op wrapper that preserves the tainted value; nothing in the visible code neutralizes it for SQL context.",
