          "key": "org/owasp/benchmark/testcode/BenchmarkTest01014.java:85:13-85:88:java.lang.security.audit.xss.no-direct-response-writer.no-direct-response-writer",
          "decision": "escalate",
          "reason": "Cookie value is URL-decoded and passed through Test.doSomething() into an XPath expression, then written to response; the intermediate transformation is opaque and the successful path writes unescaped XPath results directly to the response ",
          "error": null,
          "elapsed_seconds": 1.869
        }
      },
      "analysis": {
        "exploitability_evidence": "Source: cookie 'BenchmarkTest01014' value, URL-decoded into `param`, passed through `new Test().doSomething(request, param)` to `bar`. `bar` is then concatenated into an XPath expression string: `\"/Employees/Employee[@emplid='\" + bar + \"']\"`, which is evaluated with `xp.evaluate(expression, xmlDocument)` against a local, trusted `employees.xml` file, producing `result`. The flagged sink at line 85 is `response.getWriter().println(\"Your query results are: \" + result + \"<br/>\")` \u2014 but the value written is `result` (the XPath evaluation output), not `bar` itself. Because `bar` is sandwiched between a fixed prefix (`@emplid='`) and a fixed suffix (`']`) in the expression, an attacker can manipulate the XPath predicate logic (classic XPath injection) but cannot cleanly inject arbitrary literal text that XPath's default STRING coercion will return \u2014 `evaluate()` with no explicit QName defaults to XPathConstants.STRING, which for a node-set returns the string-value of the first matching node from the trusted, static XML document, not attacker-supplied markup. The only place raw `bar` is written to the response is in the catch block, where it is explicitly passed through `org.owasp.esapi.ESAPI.encoder().encodeForHTML(bar)` before being printed \u2014 properly mitigated. Thus the unescaped `println` at line 85 reflects data drawn from a trusted, static XML file rather than attacker-controlled HTML/script content, so no exploitable XSS path via this sink is demonstrated, even though an XPath-injection risk exists at the `expression` construction (a different CWE, not flagged here).",
        "verdict": "False Positive",
        "confidence": "medium",
        "severity_assessment": "low",
        "root_cause": "The `no-direct-response-writer` rule flags any `response.getWriter()` call containing a concatenated variable, but does not confirm the variable is attacker-controlled text. Here, only the derived XPath query result (`result`) is written unescaped; the actual tainted cookie value (`bar`) is only ever printed after being HTML-encoded via ESAPI in the error path. `result` reflects content from a static server-side XML resource, not arbitrary attacker input, so the classic XSS pattern (raw tainted string reflected into HTML) is not present.",
        "impact": "If exploitable, an attacker could manipulate the XPath predicate to leak arbitrary employee record fields (an XPath-injection / information-disclosure issue) but not inject arbitrary script/HTML into the response, so reflected XSS impact is not demonstrated by this code path.",
        "remediation": [
          "Treat this primarily as an XPath-injection concern: use parameterized XPath (e.g., XPathVariableResolver) instead of string concatenation for building `expression`.",
          "If defense-in-depth against XSS is desired, HTML-encode `result` before writing it to the response (e.g., ESAPI.encoder().encodeForHTML(result)) even though its content currently originates from trusted XML data.",
          "Continue to encode any raw request/cookie-derived values (like `bar`) before writing them to the response, as already done in the catch block."
        ]
      }
    },
