      "analysis": {
        "exploitability_evidence": "Source: HTTP header 'BenchmarkTest01031' obtained via request.getHeader(...), which is fully attacker-controlled. It is URL-decoded (java.net.URLDecoder.decode) which does not perform any path-traversal sanitization (decoding is actually the opposite - it can reveal encoded '../' sequences). The value is passed through new Test().doSomething(request, param) (body not shown, but per repeated OWASP Benchmark suite patterns this helper is a simple pass-through/no-op wrapper for the tainted string) and returned as 'bar'. 'bar' is concatenated directly into fileName = TESTFILES_DIR + bar with no path normalization, no getCanonicalPath()/getCanonicalFile() check, no whitelist, and no use of FilenameUtils.getName() to strip directory components. This fileName is then passed straight into new java.io.FileInputStream(fileName), the flagged sink. The only sanitization present in the method is ESAPI's encodeForHTML() applied to the file name and file content when writing the HTTP response body \u2014 this is an output encoder for XSS protection, not a path-traversal sanitizer, and does not affect the value used to open the file. Thus the sink receives unsanitized attacker input capable of directory traversal (e.g., '../../../etc/passwd').",
        "verdict": "True Positive",
        "confidence": "medium",
        "severity_assessment": "high",
        "root_cause": "User-supplied HTTP header value is URL-decoded and concatenated into a file path without any path traversal sanitization (no canonicalization, no filename-only extraction, no whitelist validation) before being used to open a file via FileInputStream.",
        "impact": "An attacker can supply a header value containing directory traversal sequences (e.g., ../../../../etc/passwd) to read arbitrary files on the filesystem that the application process has permission to access, leading to disclosure of sensitive configuration files, credentials, or other data.",
        "remediation": [
          "Validate and canonicalize the resulting file path and verify it resides within the intended base directory (e.g., compare File.getCanonicalPath() against the canonical base directory) before opening it.",
          "Use org.apache.commons.io.FilenameUtils.getName(...) to strip any directory components from the user-supplied value so only a bare file name is used.",
          "Maintain and enforce an allow-list of valid file names/identifiers rather than accepting arbitrary user input for file path construction.",
          "Reject or strip sequences such as '../' and encoded variants before use, in addition to canonical path validation (do not rely on decoding alone)."
        ]
      }
