      "analysis": {
        "exploitability_evidence": "Source: request.getHeader(\"BenchmarkTest01035\") \u2014 a fully attacker-controlled HTTP header. The value is URL-decoded and passed to new Test().doSomething(request, param) (body not shown, but in the OWASP Benchmark suite these helper methods are typically simple identity/pass-through wrappers that do not sanitize path-traversal characters). The returned 'bar' is concatenated directly into fileName = Utils.TESTFILES_DIR + bar and then used in new java.io.FileOutputStream(fileName) \u2014 the flagged sink. There is no validation, canonicalization, or use of FilenameUtils.getName()/path normalization anywhere on this path. The only sanitization present, ESAPI.encoder().encodeForHTML(fileName), is applied merely for the HTML response text, not before the file write, and HTML-encoding does not neutralize '../' sequences for a filesystem path anyway. Consequently, a header value like '../../etc/passwd%00' (post URL-decode) would allow directory traversal on the FileOutputStream call.",
        "verdict": "True Positive",
        "confidence": "medium",
        "severity_assessment": "high",
        "root_cause": "User-supplied HTTP header value is decoded and concatenated into a file path used to open a FileOutputStream, without any path traversal sanitization (no canonicalization, no restriction to a base directory, no filename-only extraction).",
        "impact": "An attacker can supply a crafted header value to write files outside of the intended TESTFILES_DIR directory, potentially overwriting arbitrary files on the filesystem the application has write access to, leading to data corruption, code execution (e.g., overwriting web-accessible files or configuration), or denial of service.",
        "remediation": [
          "Use FilenameUtils.getName() (or equivalent) to strip any path components from the user-supplied value before use.",
          "Resolve the final path and verify (via canonical path comparison) that it remains within the intended base directory (Utils.TESTFILES_DIR) before opening the FileOutputStream.",
          "Reject any input containing path traversal sequences such as '../' or absolute path prefixes.",
          "Avoid deriving file system paths directly from HTTP headers; use a whitelist or indirect mapping (e.g., ID-to-filename lookup) instead."
        ]
      }
    },
