      "analysis": {
        "exploitability_evidence": "Source: request.getHeader(\"BenchmarkTest01035\") is attacker-controlled data (any client can set arbitrary HTTP headers). It is URL-decoded (which does not sanitize path traversal sequences, and actually decodes encoded traversal patterns like %2e%2e%2f). The decoded value 'param' is passed to new Test().doSomething(request, param) and the return value assigned to 'bar'; in the OWASP Benchmark suite this helper is a pass-through wrapper that returns the tainted input unchanged (its body is not shown here, but no sanitizer/validator call is visible in the focal method for path-traversal purposes). 'bar' is then concatenated directly into fileName = TESTFILES_DIR + bar without any validation (no check for '..', no canonicalization, no FilenameUtils.getName()), and fileName is passed straight into new java.io.FileOutputStream(fileName) \u2014 the flagged sink. The only encoding present, ESAPI.encoder().encodeForHTML(fileName), is applied afterward and only to the string echoed back in the HTTP response, not to the path used to open the file, so it provides no protection at the sink. No sanitizer for path traversal is applied anywhere on the source-to-sink path.",
        "verdict": "True Positive",
        "confidence": "medium",
        "severity_assessment": "high",
        "root_cause": "User-supplied HTTP header value is URL-decoded and concatenated into a file path with no path traversal validation (no canonicalization, no '..' check, no allowlist) before being used to open a FileOutputStream.",
        "impact": "An attacker can supply a header value such as '../../../../etc/passwd' or an absolute path to write/overwrite arbitrary files on the filesystem accessible to the application process, potentially leading to data corruption, configuration tampering, or remote code execution if writable paths are strategically chosen (e.g., web shells, cron files, startup scripts).",
        "remediation": [
          "Canonicalize the resulting file path (e.g., File.getCanonicalPath()) and verify it remains within the intended base directory (TESTFILES_DIR) before use.",
          "Use org.apache.commons.io.FilenameUtils.getName() to strip any directory components from the user-supplied value before concatenation.",
          "Maintain an allowlist of permitted file names/extensions instead of accepting arbitrary user input for file paths.",
          "Reject any input containing path traversal sequences ('../', '..\\\\', absolute path prefixes) prior to constructing the file path."
        ]
      }
    },
