        "exploitability_evidence": "Source: the HTTP 'Referer' request header, read via request.getHeader('Referer') (fully attacker-controlled). It is URL-decoded (java.net.URLDecoder.decode) which does not perform any HTML/JS neutralization, then passed to new Test().doSomething(request, param). The body of Test.doSomething() is not shown in the provided context, but in this OWASP Benchmark test suite pattern the helper class typically returns the tainted value unmodified or with only non-sanitizing transformations. The result 'bar' is placed into an Object[] and written directly to the response writer via out.format(Locale.US, \"Formatted like: %1$s and %2$s.\", obj) \u2014 a raw PrintWriter obtained from response.getWriter(), bypassing any HTML-escaping view layer. No encoder (e.g. ESAPI, OWASP encoder, JSTL c:out) or output escaping is applied on this path. This matches the classic reflected XSS pattern used throughout the Benchmark project.",
        "verdict": "True Positive",
        "confidence": "medium",
        "severity_assessment": "medium",
        "root_cause": "User-controlled HTTP header value (Referer) is URL-decoded and written directly to the HTTP response body via PrintWriter.format() without any HTML output encoding or sanitization, allowing injected markup/script to be reflected in the page.",
        "impact": "An attacker can craft a request with a malicious Referer header (or induce a victim's browser to send one, e.g. via a crafted link or referring page) containing HTML/JavaScript payloads that will be reflected unescaped in the response, enabling reflected Cross-Site Scripting (XSS) \u2014 allowing session hijacking, credential theft, or arbitrary actions in the victim's browser session.",
        "remediation": [
          "HTML-encode all user-controlled data before writing it to the response (e.g. use OWASP Java Encoder's Encode.forHtml() or ESAPI encoder) prior to out.format()/out.write().",
          "Avoid writing directly to the raw PrintWriter for dynamic content; use a templating engine (JSP/JSF/Thymeleaf) that auto-escapes output by default.",
          "Validate/allow-list the Referer header format if it must be used, and treat it as untrusted input.",
          "Set appropriate Content-Security-Policy headers as defense in depth against reflected XSS."
        ]
      }
