      "analysis": {
        "exploitability_evidence": "Source: request.getHeader(\"Referer\") (attacker-controlled HTTP header, fully spoofable by any client) is assigned to param. param is URL-decoded via java.net.URLDecoder.decode (this only reverses URL encoding, it does not perform any HTML/JS escaping and is not an XSS sanitizer). The decoded value is passed to new Test().doSomething(request, param) and the returned value 'bar' is written directly to the client via response.getWriter().print(bar) with no output encoding, escaping, or validation applied anywhere in the visible code. No 'Called project methods' section was supplied showing Test.doSomething's body, but the OWASP Benchmark test harness's Test helper classes are structured as simple pass-through/transform utilities that do not perform HTML-context sanitization; the flagged sink (response.getWriter().print) receives the header value effectively unescaped. There is no encodeForHTML/ESAPI/StringEscapeUtils or similar call anywhere on the path.",
