      "analysis": {
        "exploitability_evidence": "Source: request.getHeader(\"BenchmarkTest01066\") is read directly from the HTTP request, then URL-decoded (still fully attacker-controlled). It is passed as `param` to `new Test().doSomething(request, param)`, whose result `bar` is used without any further sanitization. `bar` is placed into `String[] argsEnv = {bar}` and passed as the `envp` parameter to `Runtime.getRuntime().exec(args, argsEnv)`. Although `args` (the executed program) is a fixed path resolved from the classpath, the `envp` array is fully attacker-controlled, meaning the attacker can inject arbitrary environment variable assignments (e.g., overriding PATH, IFS, LD_PRELOAD, or other variables the invoked script/binary may rely upon), which is a well-known OS command/environment injection vector that can alter or hijack the behavior of the spawned process. No CWE-78-relevant sanitizer, whitelist check, or validation is applied to `param`/`bar` anywhere on this path \u2014 the only encoding present (ESAPI encodeForHTML) is applied solely to error output/stdout for XSS protection and has no bearing on the exec sink.",
