      "analysis": {
        "exploitability_evidence": "Source: request.getHeader(\"BenchmarkTest01067\") \u2192 URL-decoded into 'param' \u2192 passed to new Test().doSomething(request, param) (body not shown, but Benchmark helper 'Test.doSomething' typically returns the tainted value unmodified or with trivial concatenation) \u2192 result stored in 'bar' \u2192 placed into String[] argsEnv = {bar} \u2192 Runtime.getRuntime().exec(cmd, argsEnv) at line 61. Although the second argument to Runtime.exec(String,String[]) is the process *environment* array (envp) rather than command-line arguments, attacker-controlled data is still injected verbatim into the environment passed to the externally-executed script (insecureCmd.bat/.sh). No sanitizer, allow-list, or encoding is applied to 'param'/'bar' before this sink \u2014 URLDecoder.decode is not a security control, and ESAPI encodeForHTML is only applied later to the *output* of the command, not to the input used to build the exec call. This is the classic OWASP Benchmark 'insecure OS command' pattern; injecting attacker-controlled environment variable strings (e.g., overriding PATH or supplying malicious 'NAME=VALUE' entries) can influence the behavior of the invoked script and lead to command/argument injection or execution hijacking.",
        "verdict": "True Positive",
        "confidence": "medium",
        "severity_assessment": "high",
        "root_cause": "User-supplied HTTP header value is URL-decoded and passed through an opaque helper method without any validation or allow-listing, then used to construct the environment array supplied to Runtime.exec(), allowing attacker control over process environment/behavior of the executed script.",
        "impact": "An attacker can inject arbitrary environment variable definitions (or malformed entries) into the executed subprocess, potentially overriding critical variables (e.g. PATH, LD_PRELOAD) to hijack execution flow of the invoked script, leading to arbitrary code/command execution on the server.",
        "remediation": [
          "Never build exec()/ProcessBuilder command or environment arrays directly from user input.",
          "If custom environment variables are required, use a strict allow-list of variable names and values, rejecting anything containing '=', null bytes, or path separators.",
          "Prefer ProcessBuilder with explicit, fixed environment maps rather than raw String[] envp built from request data.",
          "Validate/canonicalize the header value against an expected format before using it anywhere near process execution.",
          "Avoid passing any user-controllable header data into process creation APIs entirely; if unavoidable, sanitize using a strict whitelist rather than URL-decoding alone."
        ]
      }
    },
