      "analysis": {
        "exploitability_evidence": "Sink at line 61: response.getWriter().println(\"Your results are: \" + results). The value concatenated into the output, 'results', is declared as an Integer and is the return value of DatabaseHelper.JDBCtemplate.queryForObject(sql, Integer.class) \u2014 a JDBC template call that is contractually bound to return an Integer object. Although the SQL query 'sql' itself is built by concatenating unsanitized attacker-controlled input (request header 'BenchmarkTest01087' -> URLDecoder.decode -> Test().doSomething() -> string concatenation into the SQL statement), that tainted string is never written to the response at the flagged line; only the resulting Integer (e.g., a row/user id or count) is written. An Integer's toString() cannot contain HTML/script metacharacters, so there is no way for attacker-supplied markup or script to reach the response body via this sink. The other catch blocks in the method either use ESAPI.encoder().encodeForHTML(sql) before output or emit a static string, so they are not exploitable either.",
        "verdict": "False Positive",
        "confidence": "high"
      }
