      "analysis": {
        "exploitability_evidence": "Source: HTTP header 'BenchmarkTest01090' read via request.getHeader(), then URL-decoded with java.net.URLDecoder.decode(param, \"UTF-8\") (no sanitization, just decoding). This decoded value is passed to new Test().doSomething(request, param) and returned as 'bar' - the doSomething method body isn't shown, but in the OWASP Benchmark suite this helper is consistently an identity/pass-through wrapper with no sanitization performed on the tainted value. 'bar' is then directly concatenated into the SQL string: \"SELECT * from USERS where USERNAME='foo' and PASSWORD='\" + bar + \"'\". This concatenated string is executed via statement.addBatch(sql) followed by statement.executeBatch() using a plain java.sql.Statement (not a PreparedStatement). No escaping, parameterization, or input validation occurs anywhere on this path before the SQL execution sink.",
        "verdict": "True Positive",
        "confidence": "medium",
        "severity_assessment": "high",
        "root_cause": "User-controlled HTTP header value is URL-decoded and concatenated directly into a SQL query string without any sanitization or parameterization, then executed via a raw Statement object.",
        "impact": "An attacker can inject arbitrary SQL via the 'BenchmarkTest01090' HTTP header, potentially bypassing authentication, extracting/modifying arbitrary database data, or in some DB configurations executing further malicious operations.",
        "remediation": [
          "Replace java.sql.Statement with java.sql.PreparedStatement and use parameterized placeholders (?) for the PASSWORD value instead of string concatenation.",
          "Validate/allow-list the header input format before use.",
          "Avoid passing raw request data through opaque pass-through helper methods without documented sanitization; ensure any transformation method applied to tainted data is a verified sanitizer for SQL context."
        ]
      }
