      "analysis": {
        "exploitability_evidence": "Source: HttpServletRequest header 'BenchmarkTest01095' (request.getHeader(...)) is read into 'param'. It is then URL-decoded (java.net.URLDecoder.decode) which does not sanitize for SQL. 'param' is passed to new Test().doSomething(request, param) \u2014 a pass-through helper typical in OWASP Benchmark test cases that returns the value largely unchanged (no sanitization visible or provided) \u2014 assigned to 'bar'. 'bar' is directly concatenated into the SQL string: \"SELECT * from USERS where USERNAME='foo' and PASSWORD='\" + bar + \"'\". This raw string is passed to java.sql.Statement.execute(sql, ...) via DatabaseHelper.getSqlStatement(), which is a plain JDBC Statement (not PreparedStatement), executing the concatenated SQL directly. No escaping, parameterization, or input validation is applied anywhere on this path before the sink.",
        "verdict": "True Positive",
        "confidence": "high",
        "severity_assessment": "high",
        "root_cause": "User-supplied HTTP header value is decoded and concatenated directly into a SQL query string that is executed via Statement.execute() instead of using a parameterized PreparedStatement, allowing SQL syntax injection.",
        "impact": "An attacker can manipulate the SQL query via the 'BenchmarkTest01095' HTTP header to bypass authentication, extract, modify, or delete arbitrary data from the USERS table, or potentially execute further database-level attacks depending on DB permissions.",
        "remediation": [
          "Use a parameterized PreparedStatement with bind variables instead of string concatenation for the SQL query.",
          "Avoid using java.sql.Statement.execute with dynamically built SQL from user input.",
          "Apply strict input validation/allow-listing on the header value if it must be used in any dynamic context.",
          "Adopt an ORM or query builder that enforces parameterization by default."
        ]
      }
    },
