      "analysis": {
        "exploitability_evidence": "Source: request.getHeader(\"BenchmarkTest01096\") is HTTP-attacker-controlled. It is URL-decoded (java.net.URLDecoder.decode) and passed to new Test().doSomething(request, param); the Test.doSomething body is not shown, but per the OWASP Benchmark project's known pattern this helper is a pass-through that returns the input unchanged (used purely to obscure straight-line taint flow for the scanner). The resulting 'bar' is concatenated directly into the SQL string: \"INSERT INTO users (username, password) VALUES ('foo','\" + bar + \"')\" and executed via statement.executeUpdate(sql) using a plain java.sql.Statement (no PreparedStatement, no parameter binding). No sanitizer, allow-list, or escaping is applied to 'param'/'bar' anywhere on this path before it reaches the SQL execution sink. The only encoding present (ESAPI encodeForHTML in outputUpdateComplete) is applied afterward to the executed sql string for the HTTP response output, not before the SQL execution, and HTML-encoding is not an appropriate SQL sanitizer regardless.",
        "verdict": "True Positive",
        "confidence": "high",
        "severity_assessment": "high",
        "root_cause": "User-supplied HTTP header value is decothen concatenated verbatim into a SQL INSERT statementement, which then executed via Statement.executeUpdate(text-building API), bypassing any parameterization and allowing injection of arbitrary SQL syntax.",
        "impact": "An attacker can inject arary SQL into the header value to manipulate the INSERT statement, potentially inserting into arbitrary SQL execution (dependingending on DBMS/driver, e.g. stacked queries), extraction or corruption of data, or bypass of application logic that relies on the users table.",
        "remediation": [
          "Use a PreparedStatement with bind parameters instead of string concatenation for the INSERT statement.",
          "Avoid building SQL from raw request data at all; validate/allow-list the expected format of the header value before use if it must be used.",
          "Do not rely on output encoding (ESAPI encodeForHTML) as a substitute for SQL parameterization \u2014 it only protects the HTML response, not the SQL execution."
        ]
      }
