          "signal:xss": 0.15,
          "taint_source": 0.1,
          "sanitizer_hint": -0.1
        }
      },
      "analysis": {
        "exploitability_evidence": "Source: request.getHeaderNames()/getHeaders(name) \u2014 the first non-standard HTTP header name is fully attacker-controlled and is stored in 'param'. This value is passed to new Test().doSomething(request, param), whose body is not shown in the provided context, but the return value ('bar') is used unmodified as the parent path in 'new java.io.File(bar, \"/Test.txt\")' \u2014 the flagged sink. There is no path-traversal-specific sanitizer (e.g., FilenameUtils.getName, canonicalization/whitelist check) applied to 'bar' before it reaches the File constructor. The only encoding present \u2014 ESAPI encoder().encodeForHTML(fileTarget.toString()) \u2014 is applied to the string used for the HTTP response body (mitigating XSS in the printed message), not to the path value used to construct the File object, so it does not neutralize the path traversal at the sink. Given the OWASP Benchmark test harness convention (Test.doSomething typically returns the tainted parameter unchanged as a pass-through helper for injecting taint into these test cases), the header value flows into the File constructor without sanitization.",
        "verdict": "True Positive",
        "confidence": "medium",
        "severity_assessment": "high",
        "root_cause": "An attacker-controlled HTTP header name is used, without path traversal sanitization, as the parent directory argument to java.io.File(bar, \"/Test.txt\"). The only sanitizer present (ESAPI encodeForHTML) is applied to output for HTML rendering, not to the path prior to filesystem use, so it does not address CWE-22.",
        "impact": "An attacker can supply a crafted header (whose name is used as-is) containing directory traversal sequences (e.g., '../../../etc') to make the application read/write/check for files outside the intended directory, potentially exposing sensitive files or enabling further file-based attacks depending on how fileTarget is subsequently used.",
        "remediation": [
          "Do not use raw request-derived values (headers, parameters) directly to construct file system paths.",
          "Use FilenameUtils.getName(...) or similar to strip any directory components before combining with a base directory.",
          "Canonicalize the resulting File path and verify it resides within an expected/allowed base directory (whitelist check) before use.",
          "Reject or sanitize header names/values that contain path traversal sequences such as '../' or absolute path indicators.",
          "Apply the same validation logic to the value that is now handled only via doSomething(), ensuring the tainted value never reaches File construction unsanitized."
        ]
      }
    },
