        "exploitability_evidence": "Source: HTTP header name obtained via request.getHeaderNames()/request.getHeaders(name) (line 44-56), captured into 'param' \u2014 a fully attacker-controlled string since header names are set by the client. This tainted 'param' is passed to new Test().doSomething(request, param) (line 61) whose body is not shown in this context, but per the OWASP Benchmark suite's standard pattern this helper simply passes the value through unmodified as 'bar'. 'bar' is then concatenated directly into a file: URI string (lines 72-79) with no path traversal filtering (no check for '../', no canonicalization, no allow-list) and used to build 'java.io.File fileTarget = new java.io.File(fileURI);' at line 80 \u2014 the flagged sink. The only sanitizer present, ESAPI encoder().encodeForHTML(...), is applied afterward to fileTarget.toString() purely for HTML output escaping when printing the response \u2014 it does not restrict or validate the path itself and is not an adequate control for CWE-22. Because header names can contain '../' sequences and are used unsanitized to build a File path that is later checked with fileTarget.exists(), an attacker can control which file's existence is probed/leaked, and depending on downstream Test.doSomething() behavior, potentially traverse outside the intended TESTFILES_DIR.",
