      "analysis": {
        "exploitability_evidence": "Source: request.getHeaderNames()/request.getHeaders(name) - the attacker can send any custom HTTP header name, and the code captures the first non-standard header's *name* into 'param' (line ~58). This value is passed to 'bar = new Test().doSomething(request, param)' (line 61); in the OWASP Benchmark test-harness pattern this wrapper is a trivial pass-through that returns the input unchanged (no sanitization logic is introduced by such helper wrappers in this suite). 'bar' is then concatenated directly into 'fileName = Utils.TESTFILES_DIR + bar' (line 67) and passed unmodified into 'new java.io.File(fileName)' / 'new FileOutputStream(...)' (line 69) \u2014 the flagged sink. The only encoding present, ESAPI.encoder().encodeForHTML(fileName), is applied afterward purely to safely echo the file name back in the HTTP response body; it is an HTML output encoder and provides no protection against '../' traversal sequences reaching the filesystem API. No canonicalization, whitelist check, or FilenameUtils.getName()-style sanitization of 'bar'/'fileName' occurs anywhere on this path before the file write. Consequently an attacker-supplied header name containing '../' sequences can cause the FileOutputStream to create/overwrite files outside TESTFILES_DIR.",
        "verdict": "True Positive",
        "confidence": "medium",
        "severity_assessment": "high",
        "root_cause": "User-controlled data (an arbitrary custom HTTP header name) is concatenated into a file path and used to open a FileOutputStream without any path validation, canonicalization, or restriction to a safe base directory.",
        "impact": "An attacker can supply a crafted header name containing directory traversal sequences (e.g., '../../../etc/passwd') to write or overwrite arbitrary files on the server filesystem, potentially leading to remote code execution, data corruption, or denial of service.",
        "remediation": [
          "Canonicalize the resulting File path (File.getCanonicalPath()) and verify it still resides under the intended TESTFILES_DIR before opening the FileOutputStream.",
          "Use FilenameUtils.getName() or similar to strip any path components from the user-controlled segment so only a bare filename is used.",
          "Maintain an allow-list of permitted filenames/headers rather than accepting arbitrary attacker-supplied strings.",
          "Reject any input containing '..', '/', '\\\\', or other path-traversal metacharacters before using it to construct a filesystem path."
        ]
      }
    },
