        "exploitability_evidence": "Attacker-controlled source: the raw HTTP header name obtained via request.getHeaderNames()/getHeaders(name) is captured into 'param' (line ~52-56). It is passed to new Test().doSomething(request, param), whose return value 'bar' is concatenated directly into fileName = TESTFILES_DIR + bar (line 68) and used to construct a java.io.File/FileOutputStream (line 70). No path-traversal sanitizer (e.g., FilenameUtils.getName, canonicalization/whitelist check) is applied anywhere on this path \u2014 the only encoding present is ESAPI.encoder().encodeForHTML(fileName), which is an HTML output encoder applied merely to the message written back to the response, and provides no protection against '../' sequences reaching the filesystem API. The body of Test.doSomething() is not shown in this context, but no sanitizing call is visible in the focal method, and typical Benchmark 'Test' helper classes simply return the tainted parameter unmodified. Given the header name is fully attacker-controlled and can contain arbitrary characters including '../', and no adequate sanitizer for a filesystem sink is present in the visible code, this constitutes a path traversal into FileOutputStream.",
