      },
      "analysis": {
        "exploitability_evidence": "Source: an arbitrary HTTP request header name (non-standard header) obtained via request.getHeaderNames()/request.getHeaders(name) \u2014 fully attacker-controlled since the client can send any header name. This value is assigned to 'param' and passed to new Test().doSomething(request, param), whose return value ('bar') is concatenated directly into fileName = Utils.TESTFILES_DIR + bar. fileName is then used to construct new java.io.FileInputStream(fileName) (wrapped inside a FileOutputStream via getFD()), which is the flagged path-traversal sink. No call to FilenameUtils.getName(), no path canonicalization/allow-list check, and no rejection of '../' sequences appears anywhere in the shown code between the header read and the file open. The body of Test.doSomething() is not shown in this context, but no sanitizer invocation on 'bar' or 'fileName' appears in the visible code path, and in the OWASP Benchmark suite this helper is a simple pass-through of the tainted parameter used specifically to test path-traversal sinks. Given the absence of any visible neutralization, the header value can reach the FileInputStream constructor unsanitized, allowing directory traversal (e.g., a header value of '../../../etc/passwd').",
        "verdict": "True Positive",
        "confidence": "medium",
        "severity_assessment": "high",
        "root_cause": "User-controlled HTTP header name is used, after being passed through an unverified helper method, to build a file path that is opened via FileInputStream without canonicalization, allow-listing, or traversal-sequence filtering.",
        "impact": "An attacker able to set an arbitrary HTTP header name could read or manipulate files outside the intended TESTFILES_DIR directory (path traversal), potentially exposing sensitive files or corrupting system files depending on file permissions.",
        "remediation": [
          "Sanitize the derived file name using FilenameUtils.getName() to strip directory components before use.",
          "Canonicalize the resulting path (getCanonicalPath()) and verify it starts with the intended base directory (Utils.TESTFILES_DIR) before opening the file.",
          "Reject any header/parameter value containing path traversal sequences ('../', absolute path prefixes) with an allow-list of expected filenames.",
          "Avoid using unauthenticated request data (including header names) to construct filesystem paths."
        ]
      }
    },
