      "analysis": {
        "exploitability_evidence": "Source: request.getHeaderNames()/getHeaders(name) \u2014 an attacker-controlled HTTP header name is captured into 'param'. This is passed to 'new Test().doSomething(request, param)' whose body is not shown, but the returned 'bar' is used directly at request.getSession().putValue(\"userid\", bar) \u2014 the flagged sink. No sanitizer or validator is applied to 'bar' before it is stored in the session; the only sanitization present, Utils.encodeForHTML(bar), is applied afterward solely for the HTML response output, not for the session storage. Thus untrusted, attacker-influenced data (derived from a header name) is placed into the session without validation, matching CWE-501 Trust Boundary Violation.",
        "verdict": "True Positive",
        "confidence": "medium",
        "severity_assessment": "low",
        "root_cause": "User-controlled HTTP header name flows through an unshown pass-through helper method into HttpSession.putValue without any validation or sanitization specific to session storage, violating the trust boundary between untrusted request data and the session state.",
        "impact": "Attacker-influenced data is trusted and stored as session state without validation, which could later be treated as trusted by other parts of the application, potentially enabling session poisoning, logic bypass, or downstream injection issues if the session value is later used in a sensitive context.",
        "remediation": [
          "Validate and canonicalize the header-derived value against an allow-list before storing it in the session.",
          "Avoid using raw HTTP header names/values as session attribute values without strict validation.",
          "If the value must be stored, apply context-appropriate sanitization/encoding at the point of storage, not just at the point of output.",
          "Consider using session attribute keys/values that are generated or verified server-side rather than derived directly from user input."
        ]
      }
