      "analysis": {
        "exploitability_evidence": "Source: request.getHeaderNames()/request.getHeaders(name) supplies an attacker-controlled HTTP header name, assigned to 'param'. This flows into new Test().doSomething(request, param) which returns 'bar' (a pass-through helper typical of this benchmark test class), and 'bar' is stored directly via request.getSession().setAttribute(\"userid\", bar) with no validation or sanitization applied before this call. The only sanitizer in the method, Utils.encodeForHTML(bar), is applied afterward only when writing the response to the HTTP client, not before the session write. Thus the tainted header value reaches the session-storage sink unsanitized.",
        "verdict": "True Positive",
        "confidence": "high",
        "severity_assessment": "low",
        "root_cause": "Untrusted HTTP header data is stored into the HttpSession via setAttribute without any validation, encoding, or type-checking prior to the session write, violating the trust boundary between untrusted request data and the trusted session store.",
        "impact": "Storing unvalidated attacker-controlled data in the session can lead to trust boundary violations where downstream code assumes session attributes are safe/trusted, potentially enabling session data poisoning, logic errors, or secondary injection issues (e.g., stored XSS) if the value is later used without re-validation in a security-sensitive context.",
        "remediation": [
          "Validate and canonicalize any user-supplied value (e.g., HTTP header name/value) against an allow-list before storing it in the session.",
          "Avoid using raw request-derived data as session attribute values; store only vetted, application-generated data in the session.",
          "If the value must be persisted for later display, apply contextual encoding (as done for the response) at the point of use in addition to sanitizing at the point of storage.",
          "Consider tagging/session attributes that originate from user input, so downstream consumers treat them as untrusted."
        ]
      }
