        "exploitability_evidence": "Source: request.getHeaderNames()/request.getHeaders(name) supplies an attacker-controlled non-standard HTTP header name, captured in variable `param`. This is passed to `new Test().doSomething(request, param)` (implementation not shown) whose return value `bar` is directly stored into the HTTP session via `request.getSession().setAttribute(\"userid\", bar)` on line 64. No validation or sanitization is applied to `bar` before it enters the session. The only sanitization present (`Utils.encodeForHTML`) is applied afterward for the HTML output, not before the session write, and HTML-encoding is not an appropriate control for a trust-boundary violation into session storage anyway. Thus attacker-controlled header data crosses the trust boundary into the session attribute unsanitized.",
