        }
      },
      "analysis": {
        "exploitability_evidence": "Source: request.getHeaderNames()/request.getHeaders(name) \u2014 an attacker-controlled HTTP header name is assigned to 'param'. This is passed to new Test().doSomething(request, param) whose body is not shown, but in the OWASP Benchmark harness this helper is a pass-through that returns the tainted value unchanged (assigned to 'bar'). 'bar' is then stored directly into the session via request.getSession().setAttribute(\"userid\", bar) at line 64 with no validation or sanitization applied to it before this call. The only sanitizer present, Utils.encodeForHTML(bar), is applied afterward and only to the value written to the HTTP response body \u2014 it does not run before or protect the setAttribute call, and even if it did, HTML-encoding is not an appropriate sanitizer for CWE-501 trust-boundary concerns (storing raw untrusted data in a session attribute).",
        "verdict": "True Positive",
        "confidence": "medium",
        "severity_assessment": "low",
        "root_cause": "Untrusted data derived directly from HTTP request header names is stored into the HttpSession without any validation, type-checking, or boundary enforcement, mixing trusted session state with attacker-influenced data.",
        "impact": "Storing unvalidated attacker-controlled data in the session can lead to trust boundary violations where later code assumes session attributes are trustworthy; this can enable logic flaws, session data poisoning, or downstream injection if the session value is later used in a sensitive context without re-validation.",
        "remediation": [
          "Validate/whitelist the header name and any derived value before using it as session data.",
          "Avoid storing raw request-derived data in session attributes; store a vetted, canonical representation instead.",
          "If the value must be stored, mark it as untrusted and re-validate/sanitize at every point of use (not just for HTML output).",
          "Apply strict input validation (e.g., allow-list of expected header names) at the point where 'param' is derived from request.getHeaderNames()."
        ]
      }
    },
