        "exploitability_evidence": "Source: attacker-controlled HTTP header 'BenchmarkTest01156' via request.getHeaders(...) is captured into `param`, URL-decoded, then passed to `new Test().doSomething(request, param)` whose result `bar` is concatenated directly into a file: URI string (`\"file:\" + startURIslashes + TESTFILES_DIR... + bar`) which is used to build `java.net.URI` and then `new java.io.File(fileURI)`. No path-traversal sanitization (e.g., FilenameUtils.getName, canonicalization/prefix check) is applied to `bar` before it is used to construct the file path \u2014 the only encoding present is `ESAPI.encoder().encodeForHTML(...)` applied to the *output* string when printing the resulting file path to the response, which protects against XSS but does nothing to prevent path traversal at the file-system access sink. Since Test.doSomething is the standard OWASP Benchmark passthrough helper (identity transform) and no other transformation is shown, an attacker can supply '../' sequences in the header value to escape TESTFILES_DIR and probe/access files outside the intended directory (fileTarget.exists() reveals existence, and File(URI) can be used to affect what file location is checked/created).",
        "verdict": "True Positive",
        "confidence": "medium",
        "severity_assessment": "medium",
        "root_cause": "User-supplied HTTP header value is URL-decoded and concatenated unsanitized into a file path (via URI construction) that is then used to instantiate a java.io.File object, without any canonicalization, whitelist, or path traversal filtering (e.g., stripping '../' or restricting to a base directory).",
        "impact": "An attacker can manipulate the header value to include directory traversal sequences ('../') to make the application check for or reference files outside the intended TESTFILES_DIR, potentially leading to disclosure of file existence/structure outside the sandboxed directory, and in variants of this pattern (where file contents are read/written) to arbitrary file read/write.",
        "remediation": [
          "Reject or strip path traversal sequences ('../', absolute paths) from user input before using it in file path construction.",
          "Use FilenameUtils.getName() or similar to extract only the file name component from user input, discarding any directory traversal segments.",
          "After constructing the File object, canonicalize the path (File.getCanonicalPath()) and verify it still resides within the intended base directory before use.",
          "Avoid building file paths via string concatenation with untrusted input; use safe APIs that map to a fixed set of allowed files or use an allow-list validation."
        ]
      }
