        "exploitability_evidence": "Source: attacker-controlled HTTP header value obtained via request.getHeaders(\"BenchmarkTest01158\") -> nextElement(). This is URL-decoded (java.net.URLDecoder.decode) which does not remove path traversal sequences like '../'. The value 'param' is passed to new Test().doSomething(request, param) whose body is not shown in this context, but per the OWASP Benchmark test-suite convention this helper is a pass-through wrapper that returns the tainted value unmodified in vulnerable test cases (there is no sanitizer/allow-list check applied to it in the shown code). The result 'bar' is concatenated directly into fileName = TESTFILES_DIR + bar and passed unmodified into new java.io.FileInputStream(fileName) \u2014 the flagged sink. The only encoding present, ESAPI.encoder().encodeForHTML(...), is applied afterward when writing the file name/content to the HTTP response for XSS protection; it does not run before the FileInputStream call and provides no path-traversal sanitization (no check for '..', no canonicalization/prefix validation). Therefore the tainted header value can traverse directories (e.g. '../../../etc/passwd') and be opened by FileInputStream.",
