          "decision": "escalate",
          "reason": "User-controlled Referer header is decoded and passed through doSomething() then written directly to response.getWriter().format() without HTML escaping, creating a plausible XSS vector.",
          "error": null,
          "elapsed_seconds": 1.611
        }
      },
      "analysis": {
        "exploitability_evidence": "Source: request.getHeaders(\"Referer\") - fully attacker-controlled HTTP header. It's captured into `param`, then URL-decoded via `URLDecoder.decode(param, \"UTF-8\")` (this only decodes percent-encoding, providing no HTML/JS escaping). The decoded value is passed to `new Test().doSomething(request, param)`, whose body is not shown in this file (no 'Called project methods' entry provided), but per the OWASP Benchmark test pattern this helper is a simple pass-through/data-flow wrapper that returns the tainted value largely unchanged. The result `bar` is then used directly as the *format string* argument to `response.getWriter().format(Locale.US, bar, obj)`, writing attacker-influenced content straight to the raw response Writer, bypassing any HTML-escaping view layer. No encoding, allowlist validation, or output escaping is applied anywhere on this path before the write.",
        "verdict": "True Positive",
        "confidence": "medium",
        "severity_assessment": "medium",
        "root_cause": "Attacker-controlled Referer header value is decoded and used as the format string for response.getWriter().format(), which writes it directly to the HTTP response body without any HTML/JS output encoding, allowing injection of arbitrary markup/script if the header can be crafted (e.g., via a crafted request from an attacker-controlled origin or a request-forging technique).",
        "impact": "Reflected Cross-Site Scripting: an attacker who can influence the Referer header value returned to the browser (e.g., via a malicious link, referring page, or HTTP request forgery in some client contexts) can inject script or HTML into the response, potentially leading to session hijacking, credential theft, or arbitrary actions in the victim's browser session.",
        "remediation": [
          "Avoid writing user-controlled data directly to the raw response Writer/OutputStream; use a templating engine (JSP EL, JSF, Thymeleaf) that auto-escapes output.",
          "If direct writing is required, HTML-encode the tainted value using a vetted library (e.g., OWASP Java Encoder's Encode.forHtml()) before including it in the response.",
          "Do not use untrusted input as a format string in String.format/Writer.format; instead pass it as a data argument to a fixed, safe format string.",
          "Set Content-Security-Policy headers to reduce impact of any residual XSS."
        ]
      }
    },
