      "analysis": {
        "exploitability_evidence": "Attacker-controlled source: the 'Referer' HTTP request header (fully attacker-controlled) is read via request.getHeaders(\"Referer\") into 'param'. It is then URL-decoded (java.net.URLDecoder.decode) which does not perform any HTML/output encoding, only URL decoding. The value is passed to new Test().doSomething(request, param) whose body is not shown, but the Test helper class in the OWASP Benchmark suite is a pass-through utility that returns the string largely unmodified (no HTML encoding). The result 'bar' is placed into an Object[] and written directly to the client via response.getWriter().format(\"Formatted like: %1$s and %2$s.\", obj) \u2014 this is the flagged sink. No HTML-escaping, output encoding, or contextual sanitizer is applied anywhere on this path before the value reaches the raw Writer, so a Referer header value such as '<script>alert(1)</script>' would be reflected unescaped in the response body, resulting in a classic reflected XSS.",
        "verdict": "True Positive",
        "confidence": "medium",
        "severity_assessment": "medium",
        "root_cause": "Untrusted request header data (Referer) is decoded and written directly through response.getWriter().format() bypassing any HTML-encoding view layer, allowing injected markup/script to be reflected in the HTTP response.",
        "impact": "An attacker can craft a malicious Referer header (e.g., via a link, redirect, or cross-site request causing browser to send a chosen Referer) containing HTML/JavaScript, which will be reflected verbatim in the response and executed in the victim's browser context, enabling session hijacking, credential theft, or other client-side attacks.",
        "remediation": [
          "HTML-encode/escape user-controlled data (the Referer header value) before writing it into the response body, e.g. using OWASP Java Encoder's Encode.forHtml() or a similar context-aware encoder.",
          "Avoid writing directly to response.getWriter()/OutputStream for dynamic content; use a templating engine (JSP/JSF/Thymeleaf) that auto-escapes output.",
          "Do not trust the Referer header for reflected output; if it must be displayed, treat it as untrusted input and sanitize/encode according to the output context (HTML body, attribute, JS, etc.).",
          "Add a strict Content-Security-Policy header to reduce impact of any residual XSS."
        ]
      }
    },
