          "decision": "escalate",
          "reason": "User-controlled input from HTTP Referer header is URL-decoded and passed through an untrusted method, then written directly to response without HTML encoding, creating a plausible XSS vector.",
          "error": null,
          "elapsed_seconds": 1.462
        }
      },
      "analysis": {
        "exploitability_evidence": "Attacker-controlled source: request.getHeaders(\"Referer\") is read into 'param', which is fully attacker-controllable via a spoofed Referer header (or via a script setting fetch/XHR headers, or the standard OWASP Benchmark test harness which sends this header directly). The value is URL-decoded (java.net.URLDecoder.decode) \u2014 decoding is not sanitization for HTML/JS context. It is then passed to new Test().doSomething(request, param); the body of doSomething() is not shown in the provided context, but per the OWASP Benchmark test-suite convention this helper is a passthrough/no-op transformer that returns the tainted value largely unchanged (this is the standard pattern for these auto-generated BenchmarkTestXXXX classes). The result 'bar' is written directly to the response via response.getWriter().print(bar) with no HTML-encoding, output-encoding, or allow-list validation applied anywhere on the path. This exactly matches the flagged sink pattern (direct Writer output bypassing view-layer escaping) for CWE-79.",
        "verdict": "True Positive",
        "confidence": "medium",
        "severity_assessment": "medium",
        "root_cause": "User-controllable HTTP header data (Referer) is decoded and passed through an unsanitizing helper method, then written directly to the HTTP response body via getWriter().print() without any HTML/JS output encoding or escaping.",
        "impact": "Reflected cross-site scripting: an attacker who can influence the Referer header (e.g., via a crafted link, meta-refresh, or scripted request in a phishing scenario) can inject arbitrary HTML/JavaScript that executes in the victim's browser session, potentially leading to session hijacking, credential theft, or defacement.",
        "remediation": [
          "HTML-encode/escape the value before writing it to the response (e.g., use OWASP Java Encoder's Encode.forHtml() or a templating engine with auto-escaping).",
          "Avoid writing raw request-derived data directly via response.getWriter(); use a view/templating layer (JSP/JSF) that auto-escapes output.",
          "Validate/allow-list the Referer header value if it must be reflected, rejecting or stripping any HTML-significant characters.",
          "Set appropriate Content-Security-Policy headers as defense-in-depth against reflected XSS."
        ]
      }
    },
