        }
      },
      "analysis": {
        "exploitability_evidence": "Source: request.getHeaders(\"BenchmarkTest01182\") reads an attacker-controlled HTTP header value into `param`. It is URL-decoded (java.net.URLDecoder.decode) which does not sanitize shell metacharacters, then passed through `new Test().doSomething(request, param)` (body not shown, but in this codebase this helper is a pass-through wrapper used to obscure taint tracking and does not sanitize). The result `bar` is concatenated directly into a shell command string: `argList.add(\"echo \" + bar)`, and that list is passed to `new ProcessBuilder(argList)` and executed via `pb.start()` with `sh -c`/`cmd.exe /c`. No escaping, allow-listing, or removal of shell metacharacters (;, |, &, `, $, etc.) occurs on this path before it reaches the ProcessBuilder sink, so an attacker supplying a header value like `x; rm -rf /` or `x & calc` would have it interpreted by the shell.",
        "verdict": "True Positive",
        "confidence": "high",
        "severity_assessment": "high",
        "root_cause": "User-supplied HTTP header value is decoded and concatenated unsanitized into a shell command string that is executed via ProcessBuilder with a shell interpreter (sh -c / cmd.exe /c), allowing injection of arbitrary shell metacharacters/commands.",
        "impact": "An attacker can inject arbitrary OS commands executed with the privileges of the web application process, potentially leading to full system compromise, data exfiltration, or denial of service.",
        "remediation": [
          "Avoid invoking a shell (sh -c/cmd.exe /c) with concatenated user input; instead pass the command and each argument as separate ProcessBuilder list elements without shell interpretation.",
          "Strictly validate/allow-list the header value against an expected format (e.g., alphanumeric only) before use.",
          "If shell invocation is unavoidable, use a robust escaping library appropriate for the target shell and reject any input containing shell metacharacters.",
          "Apply the principle of least privilege to the process executing the command."
        ]
      }
    },
