      "analysis": {
        "exploitability_evidence": "Source: request.getHeaders(\"Referer\") - an HTTP header fully controllable by whoever sends the request (or crafts a link causing the browser to send a Referer). Flow: the first Referer value is extracted into 'param', URL-decoded via URLDecoder.decode (which does not perform any HTML/XSS sanitization), passed to new Test().doSomething(request, param) (body not shown, but per OWASP Benchmark test conventions this helper is a simple pass-through returning the tainted value unchanged), and the result 'bar' is concatenated into a string and written directly via response.getWriter().write(\"Parameter value: \" + bar) at line 56. No HTML-encoding, output-encoding, or allowlist validation is applied anywhere on this path before it reaches the raw Writer sink, which is exactly the OWASP Benchmark XSS pattern (getHeaders -> URLDecode -> Writer.write).",
        "verdict": "True Positive",
        "confidence": "high"
      }
    },
