      "analysis": {
        "exploitability_evidence": "Source: request.getHeaders(\"BenchmarkTest01190\") yields the raw HTTP header value assigned to 'param'. It is only URL-decoded (java.net.URLDecoder.decode) which does not neutralize shell/environment metacharacters. This is passed to new Test().doSomething(request, param) (body not shown, but per Benchmark convention this is a pass-through helper that returns the tainted value) and stored in 'bar'. 'bar' is placed into argsEnv={bar} and passed as the envp parameter of Runtime.exec(String[] cmdarray, String[] envp) at line 64. The cmdarray itself ('cmd') is derived from a fixed classpath resource path (insecureCmd.bat/.sh) and is not attacker-controlled, but the envp array is fully attacker-controlled and unsanitized. No whitelist, encoding, or validation is applied to 'param'/'bar' before reaching the exec sink. While this doesn't let the attacker replace the executed command, being able to inject arbitrary environment variable strings into the spawned process is still a dangerous, untrusted-data-into-process-execution API call (can affect PATH, LD_PRELOAD, IFS, or script behavior depending on the invoked script), which matches the CWE-78 sink pattern the rule targets.",
        "verdict": "True Positive",
        "confidence": "medium",
        "severity_assessment": "medium",
        "root_cause": "Attacker-controlled HTTP header value flows through URL-decoding (not a security sanitizer) into the envp parameter of Runtime.exec without any validation, whitelisting, or encoding appropriate for process execution contexts.",
        "impact": "An attacker can control environment variables passed to the spawned process. Depending on how the invoked script (insecureCmd.sh/.bat) uses these environment variables, this could lead to command injection, privilege escalation (e.g., via LD_PRELOAD, PATH, or IFS manipulation), or other unintended process behavior.",
        "remediation": [
          "Do not pass user-controlled data as process environment variables or command arguments.",
          "If environment customization is required, use a strict whitelist of allowed variable names/values.",
          "Validate and encode input specifically for the OS/shell context before use in any process-execution API.",
          "Avoid Runtime.exec/ProcessBuilder with untrusted input entirely; use safer APIs or fixed, parameterized commands with no user-supplied environment data."
        ]
      }
