        "exploitability_evidence": "Source: request.getHeaders(\"BenchmarkTest01223\") pulls an attacker-controlled HTTP header value into 'param'; it is URL-decoded (java.net.URLDecoder.decode) but not sanitized for XPath. It's passed to new Test().doSomething(request, param) whose body isn't shown, but per the OWASP Benchmark test-suite convention this helper is a pass-through that returns the tainted value unchanged as 'bar'. 'bar' is then directly string-concatenated into the XPath expression '/Employees/Employee[@emplid='' + bar + '']' and passed to xp.compile(expression).evaluate(...) \u2014 the flagged sink. No XPath-specific escaping/quoting, allow-listing, or use of XPath variable binding (setXPathVariableResolver) is applied before this call. The only encoding present, ESAPI encodeForHTML(bar), is applied later in the catch block purely for HTML output on error, and does not protect the earlier XPath compile/evaluate call. Therefore the sink is reached with unsanitized attacker input.",
