          "key": "org/owasp/benchmark/testcode/BenchmarkTest01225.java:72:13-72:88:java.lang.security.audit.xss.no-direct-response-writer.no-direct-response-writer",
          "decision": "escalate",
          "reason": "User-controlled header input flows through URLDecoder and Test.doSomething() into an XPath expression, then the XPath result is written directly to response without HTML encoding, creating an XSS vulnerability.",
          "error": null,
          "elapsed_seconds": 1.642
        }
      },
      "analysis": {
        "exploitability_evidence": "Source: request.getHeaders(\"BenchmarkTest01225\") -> param -> URLDecoder.decode(param) -> new Test().doSomething(request, param) returns 'bar' (tainted). 'bar' is concatenated unescaped into an XPath expression string ('/Employees/Employee[@emplid=' + bar + ']'), which IS an XPath-injection sink (not shown/flagged here). The flagged sink at line 72 is response.getWriter().println(\"Your query results are: \" + result + \"<br/>\"), where 'result' is the return value of xp.evaluate(expression, xmlDocument) against the local, non-attacker-controlled employees.xml file. Because the XPath expression here selects a node-set and evaluate() returns the string-value of the matched element (its existing text/attribute content from the static XML file), the tainted 'bar' string itself never appears in 'result' \u2014 only pre-existing XML document content does. Note also that in the catch block, the raw 'bar' IS written to the response but is passed through ESAPI encodeForHTML() first, adequately mitigating XSS on that branch. Thus the only place raw, non-XML-derived tainted text could reach a writer is protected; the flagged line only outputs static file content. There is a real XPath Injection risk (CWE-643) via 'bar' unescaped in the query, but that is a different sink/CWE than the one flagged (CWE-79 XSS on the getWriter() call).",
        "verdict": "False Positive",
        "confidence": "medium"
      }
    },
