        "exploitability_evidence": "Source: request.getParameter(\"BenchmarkTest01233\") is read into 'param' (defaulted to \"\" if null). This user-controlled string is passed to new Test().doSomething(request, param) and returned as 'bar' \u2014 the body of Test.doSomething is not shown/available in this context, so no sanitizer or validator can be confirmed on that path. 'bar' is then used unmodified to construct java.io.File(new File(TESTFILES_DIR), bar), which is the flagged path-traversal sink. The only encoding present, ESAPI encoder().encodeForHTML(...), is applied afterward purely to the string written to the HTTP response body (for XSS mitigation) \u2014 it does not sanitize the path used to build 'fileTarget' or affect fileTarget.exists(), so it provides no protection against '../' sequences reaching the filesystem access. Because 'bar' flows straight from an HTTP parameter to a File constructor with no path-canonicalization or allow-list check on the shown code path, this matches the classic OWASP Benchmark path-traversal test pattern where the intermediate helper is a passthrough.",
