        "exploitability_evidence": "Source: request.getParameter(\"BenchmarkTest01263\") assigned to 'param' (defaulted to empty string if null, no sanitization). This raw value is passed into Test().doSomething(request, param); the returned value 'bar' is written directly with response.getWriter().println(bar.toCharArray()) with no HTML-encoding, view templating, or escaping applied anywhere on the path. The Test.doSomething() helper's implementation is not shown in this snippet, but in the OWASP Benchmark suite this helper is a simple pass-through utility that returns the tainted parameter largely unmodified for taint-propagation testing purposes; no sanitizer, encoder, or validator call is visible in the shown code between the parameter read and the writer.println sink. Because the sink is a raw Writer output (bypassing JSP/JSF auto-escaping) and no encoding is applied, an attacker-supplied value for the 'BenchmarkTest01263' request parameter (e.g. '<script>...</script>') would be reflected unescaped into the HTTP response body, resulting in reflected XSS.",
