        "exploitability_evidence": "Source: request.getParameter(\"BenchmarkTest01286\") -> param (only null-checked, not sanitized). This raw string is passed into new Test().doSomething(request, param), whose body isn't shown but per Benchmark test-harness convention is a pass-through returning the same tainted value as 'bar'. 'bar' is then concatenated directly into the OS command string used by sh: `args = new String[] {\"sh\", \"-c\", cmd + bar}` (Unix branch) or appended as a separate exec argument on Windows. This array is passed straight to `Runtime.getRuntime().exec(args)` at line 69. No encoding, whitelist check, or command-injection-specific sanitizer (e.g., stripping shell metacharacters like ;, |, `, $()) is applied anywhere on this path \u2014 the only sanitization present in the file (ESAPI encodeForHTML) is applied later to command *output*, not to the tainted input before exec. Therefore an attacker supplying a value like `; cat /etc/passwd #` in the BenchmarkTest01286 parameter reaches the shell interpreter unfiltered on the Unix branch.",
