      "analysis": {
        "exploitability_evidence": "Source: request.getParameter(\"BenchmarkTest01287\") -> param (attacker-controlled, no validation - null check only sets empty string, does not sanitize non-null values). param is passed to new Test().doSomething(request, param) whose body is not shown, but no called project method demonstrates any sanitization of this value; the OWASP Benchmark 'doSomething' helpers in this family are simple taint-propagating wrappers that return the input largely unchanged. The result 'bar' is placed, entirely attacker-controlled, as the sole element of argsEnv, which is passed as the envp parameter to Runtime.exec(args, argsEnv). While the command itself (args={cmd}) is a fixed path to insecureCmd.sh/.bat resolved via Utils.getInsecureOSCommandString (not user-controlled), the attacker fully controls the entire environment-variable string (not just a value appended to a fixed variable name), enabling injection of environment variables such as PATH/LD_PRELOAD/IFS that can influence how the invoked script executes further commands, i.e., indirect command injection via environment poisoning. No sanitizer, encoder, or whitelist check is applied to bar before it reaches r.exec. printOSCommandResults only HTML-encodes command output for display, which is irrelevant to the injection sink.",
        "verdict": "True Positive",
        "confidence": "medium",
        "severity_assessment": "high",
        "root_cause": "User-supplied HTTP parameter flows unsanitized into the envp array of Runtime.exec(); because the attacker controls the entire environment variable string (not merely a value), it can inject arbitrary environment variables (e.g., PATH, LD_PRELOAD) that influence execution of the invoked script, resulting in de-facto command injection despite the invoked command path itself being fixed.",
        "impact": "An attacker able to control the process environment of a spawned OS process may be able to hijack execution paths (e.g., via PATH or LD_PRELOAD manipulation) used by the invoked script, potentially leading to arbitrary code execution, information disclosure, or full compromise of the host running the web application.",
        "remediation": [
          "Never construct process environment variables directly from untrusted user input; if a variable value must include user data, prefix a fixed, hardcoded variable name and strictly validate/allow-list the value portion.",
          "Avoid using Runtime.exec/ProcessBuilder with any attacker-influenced data in either the command array or the environment array; use safe, parameterized APIs or avoid shell scripts entirely.",
          "If external OS commands are unavoidable, apply strict input validation (allow-list of characters/format) to any data before it is used to build environment variables or command arguments.",
          "Run subprocesses in a locked-down environment (minimal PATH, no LD_PRELOAD, restricted permissions) to reduce the impact of environment variable injection."
        ]
      }
