      "analysis": {
        "exploitability_evidence": "Source: request.getParameter(\"BenchmarkTest01300\") (line 43) is attacker-controlled. It flows through param -> new Test().doSomething(request, param) -> bar (body of doSomething not shown, but no sanitizer is applied to bar before it reaches the sink). bar is then passed unmodified into request.getSession().putValue(\"userid\", bar) at line 49, which is the flagged sink storing raw HTTP request data into a session attribute. The only sanitization present, Utils.encodeForHTML(bar), is applied later at line 54 solely for the HTTP response output, not before the session storage call \u2014 so it does not protect the sink in question. No validation, allow-listing, or type/format check occurs on bar prior to putValue.",
        "verdict": "True Positive",
        "confidence": "medium",
        "severity_assessment": "low",
        "root_cause": "Untrusted HTTP request parameter data is stored directly into the HTTP session (via putValue/setAttribute) without any validation or sanitization specific to session-attribute trust boundaries; the HTML encoding applied afterward is for a different sink (response output) and does not mitigate the trust boundary violation of persisting unvalidated data in the session.",
        "impact": "Storing unvalidated user input in the session blurs the trust boundary between user-controlled and application-trusted data. If this session attribute is later used in a security-sensitive context (e.g., authorization decisions, database queries, or reflected without further encoding), it could enable session poisoning, privilege confusion, or downstream injection attacks.",
        "remediation": [
          "Validate and canonicalize the parameter value against an expected format/allow-list before storing it in the session.",
          "Avoid storing raw request data in session attributes; use vetted, transformed, or typed values instead of pass-through strings.",
          "If the value must be stored, mark it as untrusted and re-validate/re-encode it every time it is used from the session, and apply the same rigor as user input elsewhere.",
          "Consider using session attribute names/values that are generated or validated server-side rather than directly derived from user input."
        ]
      }
