        "exploitability_evidence": "Source: request.getParameter(\"BenchmarkTest01312\") assigned to param (defaulted to \"\" if null, but attacker can supply any string). This is passed to new Test().doSomething(request, param) which returns bar; the body of Test.doSomething is not shown, but no sanitizer/validator method is shown or referenced on this path, and the return value is directly concatenated into a raw SQL string: 'SELECT * from USERS where USERNAME=\\'foo\\' and PASSWORD=\\'' + bar + '\\''. This concatenated string sql is passed to statement.execute(sql, ...) using java.sql.Statement (not PreparedStatement), which does not perform any parameter binding \u2014 the whole string is sent as literal SQL. No encoding, escaping, or allow-list validation is applied to bar before it reaches the sink. This matches the classic OWASP Benchmark SQLi test pattern where the pass-through helper does not sanitize input.",
