      "analysis": {
        "exploitability_evidence": "Source: request.getParameterMap() reads parameter 'BenchmarkTest01328' into 'param' (fully attacker-controlled, GET/POST parameter). This value is passed to new Test().doSomething(request, param) which (per the standard OWASP Benchmark pattern used throughout this suite) returns the parameter unmodified as 'bar' \u2014 no sanitization method body is shown that would alter it. 'bar' is then directly concatenated into a file: URI string ('file:' + startURIslashes + TESTFILES_DIR + bar) with no path-traversal filtering (no check for '../', no canonicalization, no use of FilenameUtils.getName or similar). This URI is used to build java.io.File fileTarget, and fileTarget.exists() is checked and its path echoed back to the client. The only sanitizer present, ESAPI.encoder().encodeForHTML(), is applied to the *output* string for XSS protection when writing to the HTTP response \u2014 it does nothing to prevent path traversal in the underlying File resolution, which is the actual sink for CWE-22. Therefore the tainted parameter flows unsanitized into the File-path sink.",
        "verdict": "True Positive",
        "confidence": "medium",
        "severity_assessment": "medium",
        "root_cause": "User-supplied HTTP parameter is concatenated directly into a filesystem path (via URI) without validating or normalizing away directory-traversal sequences such as '../', allowing an attacker to reference files outside the intended TESTFILES_DIR.",
        "impact": "An attacker can manipulate the 'BenchmarkTest01328' parameter to include path traversal sequences and probe the filesystem for the existence of arbitrary files outside the intended directory, leaking information about the server's file system layout. If similar unsanitized paths are later used for actual file reads/writes elsewhere, this could escalate to arbitrary file disclosure or modification.",
        "remediation": [
          "Canonicalize the resulting File path (File.getCanonicalPath()) and verify it still resides within the intended base directory before use.",
          "Reject or strip path traversal sequences ('../', absolute paths) from user input before building file paths.",
          "Use org.apache.commons.io.FilenameUtils.getName(...) to extract only the file name component from user input, discarding any directory information.",
          "Maintain an allow-list of permitted file names rather than constructing paths dynamically from user input."
        ]
      }
    },
