        "exploitability_evidence": "Source: request.getParameterMap() \u2192 param (attacker-controlled HTTP parameter 'BenchmarkTest01329'). This raw param is passed unmodified into `new Test().doSomething(request, param)`, whose return value 'bar' is concatenated directly into a file path string (with only slash/space normalization, no traversal-sequence filtering) which is turned into a java.net.URI and then a java.io.File object. The file's existence is then reported back to the user via fileTarget.exists(). No validation of 'bar' for '..' sequences, no use of FilenameUtils.getName(), Path canonicalization/containment check, or ESAPI validator is applied before the value reaches the File constructor. The doSomething() helper body is not shown in this context (no 'Called project methods' entry provided), but in the standard OWASP Benchmark harness this helper is a pure pass-through wrapper that does not sanitize input; the ESAPI HTML encoding applied later is only for output encoding to the HTTP response and does not neutralize traversal sequences for the filesystem sink. Therefore the tainted value flows unsanitized to a path-sensitive sink (File construction / existence check), enabling an attacker to supply '../../' sequences to probe arbitrary file existence on the server filesystem.",
