        "confidence": "medium",
        "severity_assessment": "medium",
        "root_cause": "User-supplied HTTP parameter is concatenated into a file path with no path traversal sanitization (no canonicalization check, no rejection of '..', no use of a safe filename utility) before being used to construct a File object.",
        "impact": "An attacker can manipulate the file path to reference arbitrary locations outside the intended directory (e.g., via '../' sequences), allowing them to determine the existence of arbitrary files on the server filesystem, which is an information disclosure vector and a stepping stone to more serious path traversal/file read attacks if the code is later modified to read file contents.",
        "remediation": [
          "Canonicalize the resulting file path (e.g., File.getCanonicalPath()) and verify it is still within the intended base directory before use.",
          "Reject or strip path traversal sequences ('../', absolute paths) from user input before combining with file paths.",
          "Use org.apache.commons.io.FilenameUtils.getName(...) to extract just the file name component from user input, discarding any directory traversal components.",
          "Adopt an allow-list of permitted file names/extensions rather than accepting arbitrary user-supplied path fragments."
        ]
