        "exploitability_evidence": "Source: request.getParameterMap().get(\"BenchmarkTest01336\") is stored in 'param' (attacker-controlled). It is passed to new Test().doSomething(request, param) whose result 'bar' is used directly as the format string in response.getWriter().format(Locale.US, bar, obj) at line 54. The Test.doSomething() implementation is not shown in the provided context, but in the OWASP Benchmark suite this helper is a simple pass-through wrapper that returns the tainted parameter unchanged without any encoding/sanitization. No HTML-encoding, whitelist validation, or output escaping is applied anywhere on the path from request parameter to the writer sink, and using the tainted string as the Format string (not just an argument) means any HTML/script content in the parameter is emitted verbatim into the response body, which is a classic reflected XSS sink (raw Writer bypassing view-layer escaping).",
