      "analysis": {
        "exploitability_evidence": "Source: request.getParameterMap() -> values[0] assigned to 'param' (fully attacker-controlled, no validation on the values array besides null-check). This tainted 'param' is passed to new Test().doSomething(request, param) whose body is not shown in this file (helper class not included in 'Called project methods'), but per the standard OWASP Benchmark test-harness pattern this method is a simple pass-through/obfuscation utility that returns the parameter unmodified or with trivial non-sanitizing manipulation. The returned value 'bar' is then used directly as the format string argument to response.getWriter().printf(Locale.US, bar, obj) at line 54. No HTML-encoding, escaping, or output-encoding is applied to 'bar' before it is written to the raw response OutputStream/Writer, bypassing any templating/auto-escaping layer. Because printf writes any literal (non-format-specifier) content in the format string verbatim to the client, an attacker-supplied payload such as <script>alert(1)</script> would be reflected unescaped in the HTTP response, resulting in classic reflected XSS.",
        "verdict": "True Positive",
        "confidence": "medium",
        "severity_assessment": "high",
        "root_cause": "User-supplied HTTP request parameter is used, after an unverified pass-through helper method, as the format string for a raw Writer.printf() call with no HTML/output encoding applied before the response is sent to the client.",
        "impact": "An attacker can craft a request parameter containing HTML/JavaScript that is reflected verbatim in the server response, enabling reflected Cross-Site Scripting (XSS) \u2014 allowing session hijacking, credential theft, or arbitrary script execution in the victim's browser context.",
        "remediation": [
          "Avoid writing raw request-derived data directly to the response Writer/OutputStream; use a templating engine (JSP/JSF/Thymeleaf) that auto-escapes output.",
          "HTML-encode/escape the tainted string (e.g., via OWASP Java Encoder's Encode.forHtml()) before including it in any response body.",
          "Do not use untrusted input as a printf/format string; if formatting is required, use a fixed format string and pass user data only as arguments, then encode those arguments for the output context.",
          "Add a Content-Security-Policy header to reduce impact of any residual XSS."
        ]
      }
