        "exploitability_evidence": "Attacker-controlled source: request.getParameterMap() -> param (from HTTP parameter 'BenchmarkTest01362'). This value is passed to new Test().doSomething(request, param) whose return value (bar) is used unmodified. On non-Windows path bar is concatenated directly into args[] passed to Runtime.exec(args, ...): args = new String[]{a1, a2, cmd + bar}. On Windows path bar is placed directly as a raw argument to 'cmd.exe /c echo <bar>'. No sanitizer, whitelist, or shell-metacharacter escaping is applied to 'bar' anywhere in the shown code before it reaches Runtime.exec \u2014 the only sanitization visible (ESAPI.encoder().encodeForHTML) is applied to the *output* of the process (in printOSCommandResults) and to error messages, which is HTML output-encoding, not command-injection sanitization, and does not protect the exec() call. Test.doSomething's body is not shown, but Benchmark test-harness classes of this pattern are simple pass-throughs that return the parameter unchanged; nothing in the visible code path introduces validation. Because the tainted value flows unsanitized into Runtime.exec, this is a valid OS command injection sink reachable with attacker input.",
