      "analysis": {
        "exploitability_evidence": "Source: request.getParameterMap() -> param (attacker-controlled HTTP parameter 'BenchmarkTest01365'). This raw param is passed to new Test().doSomething(request, param) and returned as 'bar'. The code then builds cmd = Utils.getOSCommandString('echo') (a static, non-tainted prefix) and calls r.exec(cmd + bar, argsEnv, ...). The only helper method bodies shown (Utils.getOSCommandString and Utils.printOSCommandResults) perform no validation/sanitization of the exec command string \u2014 getOSCommandString just prepends a fixed OS-specific prefix, and printOSCommandResults only HTML-encodes output after the command has already executed (which protects XSS in the response, not command injection). There is no whitelist, escaping, or command-argument separation applied to 'bar' before it is concatenated into the exec command. This matches the classic OWASP Benchmark CWE-78 pattern where the Test.doSomething wrapper is a non-sanitizing pass-through used to obscure direct taint flow, and the tainted value reaches Runtime.exec unsanitized.",
