      "analysis": {
        "exploitability_evidence": "The flagged sink at line 60 is `response.getWriter().println(\"Your results are: \" + results);`. Tracing the value: `results` is declared as `Integer results = ...queryForObject(sql, Integer.class);` (line 57-59), i.e., it is coerced to an Integer type by the JDBC template call. Although the SQL string itself is built by concatenating unsanitized request parameter `bar` (making this a SQL Injection issue, not XSS), the value actually written to the HTTP response writer at the flagged line is strictly an `Integer` object converted via string concatenation. An Integer cannot contain HTML/JS metacharacters, so no attacker-controlled markup can reach the client via this specific println call. The other catch-block writers (lines using `sql` or `ESAPI.encoder().encodeForHTML(sql)`) are not the flagged sink line and are out of scope for this finding.",
        "verdict": "False Positive",
        "confidence": "high",
        "severity_assessment": "info",
        "root_cause": "The scanner's heuristic flags any response.getWriter() call combined with string concatenation involving a variable that traces back (transitively) to tainted input, but on this specific line the concatenated value is an Integer produced by a type-constrained JDBC query result, not raw string data. Integers cannot carry HTML/script payloads, so no reflected XSS is possible at this sink.",
        "impact": "None for this specific line; the underlying SQL query construction (not the flagged sink) is a genuine SQL Injection vulnerability (CWE-89), but that is outside the scope of this XSS finding.",
        "remediation": [
          "No action needed for XSS on this specific println call since the output type is Integer.",
          "Separately, address the SQL Injection risk by parameterizing the query instead of concatenating 'bar' into the SQL string.",
          "If defense-in-depth is desired, apply output encoding universally via a templating engine regardless of data type."
        ]
      }
