      "analysis": {
        "exploitability_evidence": "The flagged sink at line 60 is `response.getWriter().println(\"Your results are: \" + results);`. Tracing back, `results` is declared as `Integer results = ...queryForObject(sql, Integer.class);` \u2014 the value returned from a JDBC query cast to Integer, not a raw string. Although the SQL query itself is built by concatenating unsanitized user input `bar` (from `doSomething(request, param)`), the value written to the response is strictly typed as an Integer. Even if the underlying data were attacker-influenced, an Integer.toString() representation cannot contain HTML/script markup, so no XSS payload can reach the writer via this specific sink. Other println calls in the same method (e.g., the one that echoes `sql` in the EmptyResultDataAccessException branch) do involve raw string concatenation of user input, but that is not the line flagged here (line 60), and that branch even applies ESAPI HTML encoding.",
        "verdict": "False Positive",
        "confidence": "high",
        "severity_assessment": "low",
        "root_cause": "The scanner flags any writer.println() call as a potential XSS sink without checking the actual data type of the value being written. Here the value is an Integer derived from a database query result, which cannot carry an XSS payload regardless of upstream taint.",
        "impact": "No practical XSS impact since the output is a numeric type; an attacker cannot inject script/HTML through this specific sink.",
        "remediation": [
          "No action needed for this specific line since Integer output cannot carry XSS payloads.",
          "If concerned about defense-in-depth, still route all writer output through a view/templating layer that HTML-encodes by default.",
          "Separately review the SQL query construction (line ~52) for SQL injection, which is a distinct and more serious issue not covered by this XSS finding."
        ]
      }
