      "analysis": {
        "exploitability_evidence": "The flagged sink is line 59: response.getWriter().println(\"Your results are: \" + String.valueOf(results)). Tracing back, 'results' is declared as 'Long results = ...queryForObject(sql, Long.class)' \u2014 i.e., a Long value returned by the JDBC query, not the raw user-supplied 'param'/'bar' string. Although 'param' (from request.getParameterMap()) flows unsanitized into the SQL string 'sql' via doSomething() (a SQL injection concern, CWE-89, not the flagged CWE-79 issue), the actual data written to the Writer at the flagged sink is String.valueOf(results), a numeric Long representation. This means the attacker cannot control arbitrary HTML/script content of the output at this specific sink \u2014 only the numeric outcome of the query, which cannot carry an XSS payload. The other catch blocks in this method (lines 62-65) that echo 'sql' actually apply ESAPI encodeForHTML, an adequate encoder for this sink. Since the specific sink flagged (line 59) never outputs raw attacker-controlled text, there is no exploitable XSS path here.",
        "verdict": "False Positive",
        "confidence": "high",
        "severity_assessment": "low",
        "root_cause": "The rule flags any write to response.getWriter() with concatenation, but the actual attacker-influenced parameter never reaches this specific output; the value written (results) is a Long derived from a DB query, not the raw user string, so no HTML/script injection is possible at this sink.",
        "impact": "No practical XSS impact from this specific line since only a numeric value is rendered; any real risk in the surrounding code is SQL injection (CWE-89) via unsanitized concatenation of 'bar' into 'sql', which is a separate issue from the flagged CWE-79 sink.",
        "remediation": [
          "If genuinely necessary to output query-derived text elsewhere, still HTML-encode it before writing (e.g., ESAPI.encoder().encodeForHTML()) for defense in depth.",
          "Address the underlying SQL injection risk by using parameterized queries (PreparedStatement) instead of string concatenation for building 'sql'.",
          "Avoid direct use of response.getWriter() for dynamic content; prefer a templating/view layer that auto-escapes output."
        ]
      }
    },
