      "analysis": {
        "exploitability_evidence": "Source: request.getParameterNames()/getParameterValues() \u2013 the attacker chooses both parameter names and values, so 'param' (the name of whichever parameter has value 'BenchmarkTest01403') is attacker-controlled. This tainted 'param' is passed, along with the raw request, into 'new Test().doSomething(request, param)', whose return value 'bar' is used unmodified to build 'new java.io.File(new java.io.File(TESTFILES_DIR), bar)' \u2013 a classic path-traversal sink (CWE-22). No sanitizer, canonicalization, or FilenameUtils.getName()-style call is applied to 'bar' before it is used in the File constructor; the only encoding present (ESAPI encodeForHTML) is applied afterward to the string used for the HTTP response, which protects against XSS, not path traversal. The body of Test.doSomething is not shown, but in this OWASP Benchmark test-suite pattern the helper class is a thin pass-through that returns the tainted parameter value largely unchanged, so the path from source to the File sink is unobstructed.",
        "verdict": "True Positive",
        "confidence": "medium",
        "severity_assessment": "medium",
        "root_cause": "User-controlled parameter name/value is used directly (via a pass-through helper) to construct a java.io.File path without validating or restricting it to a safe base directory, allowing directory traversal sequences like '../' to escape the intended TESTFILES_DIR.",
        "impact": "An attacker could manipulate the file path to reference arbitrary files on the filesystem, potentially leading to disclosure of file existence/contents outside the intended directory, information disclosure, or in combination with other functionality, arbitrary file read/write.",
        "remediation": [
          "Canonicalize the resulting File path and verify it resides within the intended base directory before use (e.g., compare File.getCanonicalPath() against the canonical base directory).",
          "Use org.apache.commons.io.FilenameUtils.getName() or similar to strip any directory components from the user-supplied value before building the path.",
          "Maintain an allow-list of permitted filenames and reject anything not matching it.",
          "Avoid deriving file names directly from arbitrary request parameter names/values without strict validation."
        ]
      }
    },
