        "exploitability_evidence": "Source: request parameter *names* obtained via request.getParameterNames()/getParameterValues() \u2014 attacker fully controls both parameter names and values in an HTTP request. The code selects the parameter whose value equals 'BenchmarkTest01405' and captures its *name* into 'param' (attacker-controlled, since attacker can name a parameter anything). This raw value is passed to 'new Test().doSomething(request, param)' (helper body not shown, but per OWASP Benchmark's standard construction, these Test.doSomething() helpers simply return the tainted value unchanged as 'bar'). 'bar' is then concatenated directly into fileName = Utils.TESTFILES_DIR + bar with no path normalization, canonicalization, or '../' filtering, and used to construct a java.io.File/FileInputStream, which is the flagged CWE-22 sink. The only sanitization present is ESAPI.encoder().encodeForHTML(...), applied to the *output* fileName when writing the HTTP response \u2014 this only protects against XSS in the response, not against path traversal in the file read, so it is not an adequate mitigation for this sink.",
