      "analysis": {
        "exploitability_evidence": "Source: request.getParameterNames()/getParameterValues() - the code searches all submitted parameter names for one whose value equals a fixed marker string, and assigns the matching parameter NAME (fully attacker-controlled, since an attacker chooses both parameter names and values) to variable 'param'. This is passed to 'new Test().doSomething(request, param)' whose body is not shown, but in this pattern the helper is a pass-through wrapper (no sanitization visible or provided) that returns the tainted value as 'bar'. 'bar' is then concatenated directly onto Utils.TESTFILES_DIR to build 'fileName', which is passed unmodified into 'new java.io.FileInputStream(fileName)' - the flagged sink. The only encoding applied afterward (ESAPI encodeForHTML) is applied to the fileName string when writing it back to the HTTP response body for XSS protection - it does not sanitize or restrict the path before the file-open call, so it provides no protection against path traversal in the file read itself. No path canonicalization, whitelist check, or getName()-style stripping is applied to 'bar' or 'fileName' before the FileInputStream call.",
        "verdict": "True Positive",
        "confidence": "medium",
        "severity_assessment": "high",
        "root_cause": "User-controlled input (an arbitrary HTTP parameter name chosen by the attacker) flows unsanitized into a file path that is used to open a FileInputStream, allowing directory traversal (e.g., '../../etc/passwd' as a parameter name) with no path validation or canonicalization performed before the file access.",
        "impact": "An attacker can read arbitrary files on the server's filesystem that the application process has permission to access, by crafting a parameter name containing '../' sequences, potentially exposing sensitive configuration files, credentials, or source code.",
        "remediation": [
          "Do not use user-supplied strings (including parameter names) directly to construct file paths.",
          "Use org.apache.commons.io.FilenameUtils.getName() or similar to strip any directory components before use.",
          "Canonicalize the resulting path (e.g., File.getCanonicalPath()) and verify it is still within the intended base directory (Utils.TESTFILES_DIR) before opening it.",
          "Maintain an allow-list of permitted file names/identifiers instead of accepting arbitrary path fragments from the client."
