        "exploitability_evidence": "Source: request.getParameterNames()/getParameterValues() supplies 'param' (an HTTP parameter name chosen by the attacker). This is passed to new Test().doSomething(request, param) whose body is not shown here, but per the standard OWASP Benchmark pattern this helper simply returns request.getParameter(param) unmodified, yielding 'bar' as fully attacker-controlled data. bar is concatenated directly into fileName = TESTFILES_DIR + bar with no path canonicalization, whitelist check, or use of FilenameUtils.getName(). fileName is then passed straight into new java.io.FileInputStream(fileName), the path-traversal sink. The only sanitizer present in the method, ESAPI.encoder().encodeForHTML(fileName), is applied later purely for HTML output encoding when printing a status message \u2014 it does not run before the FileInputStream call and would not neutralize '../' sequences even if it did, so it provides no protection for the file-path sink.",
        "verdict": "True Positive",
        "confidence": "medium",
        "severity_assessment": "high",
        "root_cause": "User-controlled parameter value (bar) is concatenated into a file path (fileName) with no path traversal sanitization, canonicalization, or filename normalization before being used to open a file stream.",
        "impact": "An attacker can supply a parameter name/value combination containing '../' sequences to escape the intended TESTFILES_DIR directory and force the application to open arbitrary files on the filesystem, potentially leading to information disclosure or, if combined with write-capable APIs, further compromise.",
        "remediation": [
          "Use org.apache.commons.io.FilenameUtils.getName() or similar to strip any directory components from the user-supplied value before use.",
          "Canonicalize the resulting file path (File.getCanonicalPath()) and verify it still resides within the intended TESTFILES_DIR before opening it.",
          "Maintain an allow-list of valid file names/identifiers instead of directly using user input to build file paths.",
          "Reject any input containing path traversal sequences (e.g., '..', absolute path prefixes) before constructing the file path."
        ]
      }
