        "exploitability_evidence": "Source: request parameter names/values via request.getParameterNames()/getParameterValues() are fully attacker-controlled. The code scans all submitted parameters and, when it finds one whose value equals a marker string, sets 'param' to that parameter's NAME (also attacker-controlled, since an attacker chooses both parameter names and values). This tainted 'param' is passed to new Test().doSomething(request, param) and the result assigned to 'bar'. In the standard OWASP Benchmark 'Test' helper class used throughout this suite, doSomething is a pass-through that returns the input value unmodified with no validation/encoding. 'bar' is then concatenated directly into fileName = Utils.TESTFILES_DIR + bar, which is passed unmodified into new java.io.FileOutputStream(fileName, false) \u2014 the flagged sink. No path canonicalization, allow-listing, or FilenameUtils.getName()-style sanitization is applied anywhere on this path before the file write, so an attacker supplying a parameter name like '../../../etc/passwd' style traversal sequences (with a value equal to the marker string) can control the target file location for writing.",
