        "exploitability_evidence": "Source: request.getParameterNames()/getParameterValues() lets an attacker control both the parameter name and its value; the code selects 'param' = the name of whatever parameter has value 'BenchmarkTest01408', so 'param' is fully attacker-controlled. This value is passed to new Test().doSomething(request, param) and returned as 'bar' (the Test helper in this Benchmark suite is a simple pass-through utility that returns the parameter unchanged). 'bar' is then concatenated directly into fileName = Utils.TESTFILES_DIR + bar with no path traversal sanitization (no call to FilenameUtils.getName, no check for '../', no canonicalization) before being used in new FileOutputStream(fileName,false). The only sanitizer present, ESAPI encodeForHTML, is applied afterward purely for the HTTP response text, not for the filesystem path, so it does not mitigate path traversal at the sink.",
