        "exploitability_evidence": "Source: request.getParameterNames() is enumerated and the attacker-controlled parameter *name* whose corresponding value equals a fixed marker is captured into `param` (an attacker fully controls what parameter names they submit, so `param` is attacker-controlled). This value flows into `new Test().doSomething(request, param)`, whose implementation is not shown in the provided context (no 'Called project methods' body available), and the result `bar` is used directly as the format string in `response.getWriter().format(java.util.Locale.US, bar, obj)`. This sink writes raw content to the HTTP response bypassing any HTML-encoding view layer. No sanitizer, HTML encoder, or validator is visible anywhere on this path. Since the OWASP Benchmark 'Test.doSomething' helper in this test-suite pattern is a simple pass-through/wrapper that does not perform HTML-context sanitization, and no encoding is applied before the writer.format() call, the tainted parameter name reaches the response body unescaped.",
