      "analysis": {
        "exploitability_evidence": "Source: request.getParameterNames()/getParameterValues() are enumerated to find a parameter whose value array contains the literal string 'BenchmarkTest01423'; when found, the *name* of that parameter is stored in 'param'. This 'param' is then passed to new Test().doSomething(request, param), which (per the standard OWASP Benchmark 'Test' helper used across this suite) does 'bar = request.getParameter(param);' \u2014 i.e., it re-reads the request using the discovered parameter name. Because HttpServletRequest.getParameter() returns the FIRST submitted value for a given parameter name, an attacker can submit two values for the same parameter, e.g. foo=<script>alert(1)</script>&foo=BenchmarkTest01423. The loop matches the second value ('BenchmarkTest01423') to select 'foo' as 'param', then doSomething's request.getParameter('foo') returns the attacker XSS payload as 'bar'. This value is then written unescaped straight to the response via response.getWriter().printf(\"Formatted like: %1$s and %2$s.\", obj) with no HTML encoding, sanitization, or use of a templating/auto-escaping mechanism on the path. No sanitizer or encoder relevant to HTML output is applied anywhere in the shown code.",
        "verdict": "True Positive",
        "confidence": "medium",
        "severity_assessment": "medium",
        "root_cause": "User-controlled request parameter value is retrieved indirectly (via a parameter-name lookup helper) and written directly to the HTTP response writer using printf without any HTML/output encoding, bypassing view-layer auto-escaping.",
        "impact": "Reflected Cross-Site Scripting: an attacker can craft a request with a duplicated parameter to inject arbitrary HTML/JavaScript that executes in the victim's browser in the context of the vulnerable application, enabling session hijacking, credential theft, or other client-side attacks.",
        "remediation": [
          "HTML-encode all user-supplied data before writing it into the response (e.g., use OWASP Java Encoder's Encode.forHtml()).",
          "Avoid writing directly to the response Writer/OutputStream; use a templating engine (JSP/JSF) that auto-escapes output.",
          "Validate/allow-list expected parameter names and values before using them to look up further request data.",
          "Set appropriate Content-Security-Policy headers as defense in depth."
        ]
      }
