      "analysis": {
        "exploitability_evidence": "Source: request.getParameterNames()/getParameterValues() are fully attacker-controlled (both parameter names and values are supplied by the HTTP client). The code iterates all parameter names/values and sets `param` to the name of whichever parameter has value 'BenchmarkTest01427' (name is attacker chosen, since a client can supply any parameter name with that value). `param` is then passed into `new Test().doSomething(request, param)` whose body is not shown in this context (no 'Called project methods' were provided), and the returned `bar` is written directly to the response via `response.getWriter().println(bar)` with no HTML-encoding, escaping, or use of a templating/view layer. This is the classic OWASP Benchmark XSS pattern: helper 'Test.doSomething' typically just passes the tainted value through (e.g., returns it unchanged or lightly wrapped) without sanitizing for HTML context. No sanitizer, encoder, or validator is visible anywhere on this path before the getWriter().println() sink, which is a raw stream write that bypasses any templating auto-escaping.",
