      "analysis": {
        "exploitability_evidence": "Source: request.getParameterNames()/getParameterValues() \u2013 attacker fully controls both parameter names and values in an HTTP request. The code iterates all supplied parameter names/values and, if a value equals a fixed marker string, sets 'param' to that parameter's *name* (also attacker-supplied, since HTTP query/POST parameter names are freely chosen by the client). This tainted 'param' is passed to new Test().doSomething(request, param) and the returned 'bar' is concatenated into a string that is written directly via response.getWriter().write(\"Parameter value: \" + bar) at line 63. No HTML encoding, escaping, or output sanitization is applied on this path. The body of Test.doSomething() is not shown in this context, but per the standard OWASP Benchmark test harness pattern this helper is a pass-through that returns the tainted value unmodified. Given the direct write to the raw response Writer with no encoding, this satisfies a CWE-79 reflected XSS pattern.",
        "verdict": "True Positive",
        "confidence": "medium",
        "severity_assessment": "medium",
        "root_cause": "User-controlled request parameter name/value is propagated to a helper method and the result is written directly to the HTTP response body via response.getWriter().write() without any HTML/output encoding, bypassing template-based auto-escaping.",
        "impact": "An attacker can craft a request whose parameter name/value contains HTML/JavaScript payload; if reflected unescaped in the response, this enables reflected Cross-Site Scripting, allowing session hijacking, credential theft, or arbitrary script execution in the victim's browser context.",
        "remediation": [
          "HTML-encode all user-controlled data before writing to the response (e.g., use OWASP Java Encoder's Encode.forHtml()).",
          "Avoid writing directly to the raw OutputStream/Writer with concatenated user input; use a templating engine or JSP/JSF that auto-escapes output.",
          "Validate/allow-list expected parameter names and values where possible instead of reflecting arbitrary user input.",
          "Set appropriate Content-Security-Policy headers as defense-in-depth."
        ]
      }
