      "analysis": {
        "exploitability_evidence": "The attacker-influenced value 'bar' (derived from an HTTP parameter name and passed through Test().doSomething, whose body isn't shown) is placed into the array 'argsEnv' which is passed as the *envp* parameter of Runtime.exec(String[] cmdarray, String[] envp) \u2014 not into 'args' (cmdarray), which contains only the fixed path returned by Utils.getInsecureOSCommandString(). Since envp only sets environment variables for the spawned process and is not parsed as shell command/arguments, tainted data reaching this parameter does not let an attacker alter the command being executed or inject additional shell commands. The actual command executed (cmd) is fully controlled by the application (a fixed script path resolved from the classpath), so the exec sink is not exploitable for OS command injection via this taint path.",
        "verdict": "False Positive",
        "confidence": "medium",
        "severity_assessment": "low",
        "root_cause": "Tainted data flows only into the environment-variable array (envp) of Runtime.exec(cmdarray, envp), not into the command/argument array actually executed. This pattern does not allow shell metacharacter injection into the invoked command, so it is not exploitable as classic OS command injection even though the scanner's taint rule matched HTTP-request data reaching an exec() call.",
        "impact": "None via this specific code path for OS command injection; at most, if the invoked script insecurely trusts specific environment variable names/values, a lesser environment-variable poisoning issue could theoretically exist, but that is not demonstrated by the shown code and is a different vulnerability class.",
        "remediation": [
          "Confirm scanner rule differentiates between cmdarray and envp parameters of Runtime.exec to reduce false positives.",
          "If the executed script (insecureCmd.sh/.bat) reads and uses any environment variable value unsafely, audit and harden that separately.",
          "Continue avoiding direct use of unsanitized request data in any exec-related arrays; use allow-lists even for environment variables to be safe.",
          "Remove or restrict this endpoint's ability to pass arbitrary environment variables to child processes if not required."
        ]
      }
