        "exploitability_evidence": "Source: request.getParameterNames()/getParameterValues() (attacker-controlled query/POST parameters). The code loops through all parameter names/values looking for one whose value equals a fixed literal, and if found sets `param` = the parameter *name* (still attacker-controlled since parameter names are attacker-supplied). This tainted `param` (plus the raw `request` object) is passed to `new Test().doSomething(request, param)`, whose return value `bar` is then passed directly to `request.getSession().putValue(\"userid\", bar)` \u2014 the flagged sink. No validation/whitelisting of `bar` occurs before this call. The only sanitizer present, `Utils.encodeForHTML(bar)`, is applied afterward, only for the HTTP response body write, and is irrelevant to the session-storage sink. Thus untrusted user input reaches the session-attribute sink unsanitized for that specific use.",
