      "analysis": {
        "exploitability_evidence": "Source: request.getParameterNames()/getParameterValues() iterated to find a parameter whose value equals 'BenchmarkTest01457'; the matching parameter NAME is captured into 'param' \u2014 fully attacker-controlled since parameter names are user-supplied. This tainted 'param' is passed to `new Test().doSomething(request, param)` (body not shown, but in this pattern doSomething typically returns/propagates a derived value based on the tainted input) whose result 'bar' is stored via `request.getSession().putValue(\"userid\", bar)`. There is no sanitizer applied to 'bar' before it reaches the session-storage sink; the only encoding present, `Utils.encodeForHTML(bar)`, is applied afterward and only for the HTML response output, not for the session attribute itself. Because the trust-boundary sink (session.putValue) receives unvalidated/unsanitized data derived from user input, the flow reaches the sink without adequate mitigation for CWE-501.",
        "verdict": "True Positive",
        "confidence": "medium",
        "severity_assessment": "low",
        "root_cause": "User-supplied HTTP request parameter data (parameter name matching a target value) flows, via a helper method whose body is not shown but which forwards the tainted parameter, directly into HttpSession.putValue without any validation or sanitization aimed at preventing trust-boundary violations; the only sanitization present (encodeForHTML) is applied later solely for HTML output encoding, not before the session write.",
        "impact": "Storing unvalidated user-controlled data in the session under a trusted attribute name can lead to trust boundary violations: the application may later treat this session value as trusted, enabling logic bypasses, session data poisoning, or privilege confusion; combined with the fact HttpSession.putValue is deprecated, this reinforces poor session data hygiene.",
        "remediation": [
          "Validate and sanitize any data before storing it in the HttpSession, applying an allow-list or strict format check appropriate to how the session value will later be used.",
          "Avoid using deprecated HttpSession.putValue; use setAttribute with vetted, canonicalized values.",
          "Do not derive session values directly from raw request parameter names/values without server-side validation.",
          "If the value must reflect user input, store a validated/canonical form and reserve output encoding (e.g., encodeForHTML) strictly for the rendering context, not as a substitute for input validation before persistence."
        ]
      }
