      "analysis": {
        "exploitability_evidence": "Source: request.getParameterNames()/getParameterValues() iterated to find the parameter name whose value equals 'BenchmarkTest01461'; that parameter NAME (attacker-controlled, since an attacker can send an arbitrary parameter with that value to make its name become 'param') is passed as 'param' into new Test().doSomething(request, param), whose result is assigned to 'bar'. 'bar' is directly concatenated into the SQL/CallableStatement string: sql = \"{call \" + bar + \"}\"; this string is passed unmodified to connection.prepareCall(sql, ...) and executed via statement.executeQuery(). No parameterization (bind variables) is used \u2014 the entire call string is built via string concatenation. The body of Test.doSomething is not shown in the 'Called project methods' section, but no encoding/sanitization call is visible anywhere on this path (ESAPI encoding is only applied later to the *output* HTML in printResults, not to the SQL string), and the OWASP Benchmark 'Test' helper classes in this suite are typically simple pass-through wrappers around request data. Given the sink is a raw string concatenation into prepareCall with no visible neutralization of SQL metacharacters, this constitutes an exploitable SQL injection.",
        "verdict": "True Positive",
        "confidence": "medium",
        "severity_assessment": "high",
        "root_cause": "User-controlled request parameter name/value is concatenated directly into a SQL call string used to build a CallableStatement, rather than using parameterized queries or bind variables, and no sanitizer/encoder appropriate for SQL context is applied before the value reaches the sink.",
        "impact": "An attacker who controls the HTTP parameter name (or crafts a parameter whose value equals the sentinel and whose name contains SQL metacharacters/control sequences) can inject arbitrary SQL into the callable statement, potentially leading to unauthorized data disclosure, data modification, or database compromise.",
        "remediation": [
          "Use PreparedStatement/CallableStatement with bind parameters (?) instead of concatenating user input into the SQL/call string.",
          "Validate/whitelist parameter names and values against a strict allow-list before using them in any SQL construction.",
          "Avoid deriving SQL statement text from arbitrary HTTP parameter names; use a fixed, server-defined set of stored procedure names.",
          "If dynamic call targets are required, map user input to a safe internal identifier via a lookup table rather than directly embedding it in SQL."
        ]
      }
