      "analysis": {
        "exploitability_evidence": "Source: request.getParameterNames()/getParameterValues() are used to find a parameter name whose value equals a marker string; 'param' is then set to that (attacker-controlled) parameter name. This is passed into new Test().doSomething(request, param), whose body is not shown here but per the OWASP Benchmark pattern this helper simply returns request.getParameter(param) (or equivalent), yielding an attacker-controlled string 'bar'. 'bar' is concatenated directly into the SQL string: `\"... PASSWORD='\" + bar + \"'\"` with no escaping, allow-listing, or parameterization. The resulting 'sql' string is passed directly to org.springframework.jdbc.core.JdbcTemplate.query(sql, RowMapper) as a raw SQL string rather than via a PreparedStatement/parameter binding. No sanitizer, encoder, or input validator is applied to 'bar' before it reaches the SQL sink (ESAPI encodeForHTML is only used later for output encoding of query results, which does not mitigate SQL injection). Therefore user input flows unsanitized into a SQL sink.",
        "verdict": "True Positive",
        "confidence": "medium",
        "severity_assessment": "high",
        "root_cause": "Untrusted HTTP request parameter (whose value is looked up via the parameter name) is concatenated directly into a SQL query string that is executed with JdbcTemplate.query(String, RowMapper), which does not perform parameter binding. No sanitization or parameterization is applied to the tainted value on this path.",
        "impact": "An attacker can manipulate the parameter value to inject arbitrary SQL, potentially bypassing authentication, exfiltrating data from the USERS table, or manipulating/deleting data depending on database permissions.",
        "remediation": [
          "Use parameterized queries (PreparedStatement or JdbcTemplate.query(String sql, Object[] args, RowMapper) with '?' placeholders) instead of string concatenation.",
          "Avoid passing raw concatenated SQL strings to JdbcTemplate.query; use NamedParameterJdbcTemplate or PreparedStatementSetter.",
          "Validate/allow-list the expected format of the input parameter (e.g., expected username format) before use.",
          "Apply the principle of least privilege to the database account used by the application."
        ]
      }
