        "exploitability_evidence": "Source: request.getParameterNames()/getParameterValues() are enumerated and if a value equals a fixed marker string, the corresponding parameter NAME is captured into 'param'. This attacker-influenced 'param' (and the raw HttpServletRequest) is passed to new Test().doSomething(request, param), whose return value 'bar' is concatenated directly into a SQL string: \"...PASSWORD='\" + bar + \"'\". The resulting sql string is executed via statement.addBatch(sql)/executeBatch() (Statement, not PreparedStatement) \u2014 no parameterization, escaping, or ESAPI/other input validation is applied to bar before it reaches the SQL sink. Although the body of Test.doSomething is not shown here, in this OWASP Benchmark test-suite pattern this helper is a trivial pass-through that returns request-derived data (typically request.getParameter(param)) unmodified; regardless of its exact internals, the sink itself (raw string concatenation executed via Statement) provides no protection, so any tainted value returned by doSomething flows unsanitized into the SQL command.",
