          "decision": "escalate",
          "reason": "User input from request parameters flows through `bar` variable and is written directly to response via `response.getWriter().println(value.getTextContent())` without HTML encoding, creating an XSS risk despite sanitization attempt in error",
          "error": null,
          "elapsed_seconds": 1.655
        }
      },
      "analysis": {
        "exploitability_evidence": "The flagged sink is `response.getWriter().println(value.getTextContent() + \"<br/>\");` at line 86. Tracing back: `value` is an `org.w3c.dom.Element` obtained from `nodeList.item(i)`, where `nodeList` is the result of evaluating an XPath expression (`/Employees/Employee[@emplid='...bar...']`) against `xmlDocument`, which was parsed from the static file `employees.xml` on the classpath (via `Utils.getFileFromClasspath`). While the request-derived value `bar` (built from `param`, which comes from `request.getParameterNames()`/`getParameterValues()`) is embedded unescaped into the XPath expression string (an XPath-injection issue, CWE-643, not shown as the flagged sink here), the actual text written to the response at the flagged line \u2014 `value.getTextContent()` \u2014 is the *content of matched XML nodes from the trusted local employees.xml file*, not the raw attacker-supplied string itself. An attacker can influence which employee node(s) match via XPath injection, but cannot inject arbitrary HTML/script content into the response through this write, since the printed text is limited to the pre-existing employee record data in the static XML file. There is no path where raw attacker-controlled markup reaches this particular `println` call unescaped. (Separately, the exception-handler branch does escape `bar` via `ESAPI.encoder().encodeForHTML(bar)` before printing it, so that path is safe too.) Because the actual sink content is not attacker-controlled, this specific XSS finding does not hold.",
        "verdict": "False Positive",
        "confidence": "medium",
        "severity_assessment": "low",
        "root_cause": "The Semgrep rule generically flags any `response.getWriter().println(...)` call with concatenated variables as a possible XSS sink, without tracing that the concatenated variable (`value.getTextContent()`) originates from parsing a static, trusted local XML file rather than directly from user input. The user-controlled value (`bar`) is only used to build an XPath filter expression, not the text ultimately written to the response.",
        "impact": "None via this specific code path for reflected XSS; the true underlying issue in this code (unsanitized `bar` in the XPath expression) is XPath Injection (CWE-643), which could allow query manipulation but not direct script injection into the HTML response through this sink.",
        "remediation": [
          "If XPath injection is a concern, use parameterized/XPath variable resolvers instead of string concatenation for `bar`.",
          "If any XML node text content could ever include HTML metacharacters, HTML-encode `value.getTextContent()` before writing to the response as defense in depth.",
          "Consider using a templating engine that auto-escapes output instead of writing directly to the raw response Writer."
        ]
      }
    },
