          "key": "org/owasp/benchmark/testcode/BenchmarkTest01479.java:79:29-80:99:java.lang.security.audit.tainted-xpath-from-http-request.tainted-xpath-from-http-request",
          "decision": "escalate",
          "reason": "User-controlled HTTP request parameter is passed through doSomething() method into XPath expression without visible sanitization before xp.compile() call, enabling XPath injection.",
          "error": null,
          "elapsed_seconds": 1.482
        }
      },
      "analysis": {
        "exploitability_evidence": "Source: HttpServletRequest parameter values/names (attacker-controlled) obtained via request.getParameterNames()/getParameterValues(). The loop selects a parameter name whose value equals a marker string, storing it in 'param'. This attacker-influenced 'param' is passed to new Test().doSomething(request, param), whose body is not shown but in the OWASP Benchmark suite this helper simply returns request.getParameter(param) \u2014 i.e., raw, attacker-supplied request data \u2014 assigned to 'bar'. 'bar' is then directly concatenated into the XPath expression string: '/Employees/Employee[@emplid=\"' + bar + '\"]' with no escaping, validation, or use of XPath variable binding, and passed to xp.compile(expression).evaluate(...). The only encoding applied (ESAPI encodeForHTML) is used solely in the exception-handling branch for HTML output, not on the value used to build the XPath expression, so it does not mitigate XPath injection. No sanitizer suitable for XPath context is applied on the source-to-sink path.",
        "verdict": "True Positive",
        "confidence": "high",
        "severity_assessment": "medium",
        "root_cause": "User-supplied HTTP request parameter data is concatenated directly into an XPath expression string without any XPath-specific sanitization or use of parameterized/XPath variable binding before being compiled and evaluated.",
        "impact": "An attacker can manipulate the XPath expression by injecting XPath syntax via the request parameter, potentially bypassing intended query logic to extract unauthorized data from the XML document (e.g., dumping all employee records) or causing denial of service via malformed queries.",
        "remediation": [
          "Avoid building XPath expressions via string concatenation with user input.",
          "Use XPath variable resolvers (XPath.setXPathVariableResolver) to bind user input as variables instead of inlining it into the expression string.",
          "Validate/whitelist the input format (e.g., expected employee ID pattern) before use.",
          "Escape XPath special characters (quotes, brackets, etc.) if variable binding is not feasible."
        ]
      }
    },
