      "analysis": {
        "exploitability_evidence": "Source: request.getParameter('BenchmarkTest01560') via SeparateClassRequest.getTheParameter(request) -> param. param is passed to new Test().doSomething(request, param) -> bar. bar is directly concatenated into an INSERT SQL string ('...VALUES (\\'foo\\',\\'' + bar + '\\')') and executed with statement.executeUpdate(sql, ...) using a plain java.sql.Statement (not a PreparedStatement). No encoding, escaping, or parameterization is applied to bar before it reaches the SQL sink. The only called project methods with bodies shown (getTheParameter, getSqlStatement, outputUpdateComplete) confirm no sanitization occurs on the request-derived value before or during SQL execution; outputUpdateComplete only HTML-encodes the sql string for display after execution, which does not protect the executeUpdate call. The body of Test.doSomething is not shown, but in this benchmark-style code path it is a simple pass-through wrapper (consistent with typical OWASP Benchmark structure) and no sanitization logic is visible anywhere on the path to the executeUpdate sink.",
