      "analysis": {
        "exploitability_evidence": "Source: request.getParameterValues(\"BenchmarkTest01570\") is fully attacker-controlled and assigned to 'param'. It is passed to new Test().doSomething(request, param), whose body is not shown in the provided context (Test is the typical Benchmark helper class that in this pattern simply forwards the parameter unchanged as 'bar', with no path validation). 'bar' is then used directly as the child path segment in 'new java.io.File(new java.io.File(Utils.TESTFILES_DIR), bar)' \u2014 the flagged sink. The only sanitizer visible on the path is ESAPI's encodeForHTML(), which is applied to the resulting File.toString() purely for safe HTML output in the response, and provides no protection against '../' traversal sequences being resolved by the File constructor. There is no call to FilenameUtils.getName(), Path.normalize()+prefix-check, or any allow-list validation of 'bar' before it is used to build the File object.",
        "verdict": "True Positive",
        "confidence": "medium",
        "severity_assessment": "high",
        "root_cause": "User-supplied request parameter flows, via a pass-through helper method, directly into a java.io.File path without any canonicalization, allow-listing, or path traversal filtering; the only encoding present (encodeForHTMLML) protects the HTTP response body, not the filesystem path resolution.",
        "impact": "An attacker can supply a value like '../../etc/passwd' or other traversal sequences to make the application check for or reveal the existence of arbitrary files outside the intended TESTFILES_DIR, potentially disclosing sensitive file system information; if this pattern were reused with read/write operations elsewhere, it could enable arbitrary file read/write.",
        "remediation": [
          "Canonicalize the resulting File path (getCanonicalPath()) and verify it still resides within the intended base directory before use.",
          "Reject or strip path traversal sequences and path separators from the parameter, or use FilenameUtils.getName() to retain only the file name component.",
          "Prefer an allow-list of permitted file names/IDs mapped server-side rather than trusting client-supplied file path fragments."
        ]
      }
    },
