        "exploitability_evidence": "Source: request.getParameterValues(\"BenchmarkTest01572\") is read into 'param' (attacker-controlled). This is passed to new Test().doSomething(request, param), whose return value 'bar' is directly concatenated with a fixed directory prefix (Utils.TESTFILES_DIR + bar) to build 'fileName', which is then used to construct java.io.File/FileInputStream \u2014 the flagged sink. The Test.doSomething() body is not shown, but per standard OWASP Benchmark test harness conventions this helper is a passthrough that returns the input largely unmodified. No path normalization, whitelist check, or FilenameUtils.getName()-style sanitization is applied to 'bar' or 'fileName' before the file open. The only sanitization present (ESAPI encoder().encodeForHTML(...)) is applied to the fileName string only when writing it back to the HTTP response for display, i.e., after the file has already been opened/read \u2014 it does not prevent path traversal in the file-open sink and is not designed for that purpose. Thus attacker input can include '../' sequences to escape TESTFILES_DIR and read arbitrary files.",
        "verdict": "True Positive",
        "confidence": "medium",
        "severity_assessment": "high",
        "root_cause": "User-supplied HTTP parameter is concatenated into a filesystem path with no path/canonicalization validation before being used to open a file (CWE-22). The only sanitizer on the path (HTML encoding) is for XSS output protection, not path-traversal prevention.",
        "impact": "An attacker can supply a parameter value containing directory traversal sequences (e.g., ../../etc/passwd) to read arbitrary files on the server filesystem accessible to the application process, leading to disclosure of sensitive configuration files, credentials, or source code.",
        "remediation": [
          "Validate and canonicalize the resulting file path and verify it remains within the intended base directory (e.g., using File.getCanonicalPath() and checking it starts with the allowed base directory).",
          "Reject or strip path traversal sequences ('..', absolute path indicators) from user input before use.",
          "Use org.apache.commons.io.FilenameUtils.getName(...) to strip any directory components from user input, allowing only bare filenames.",
          "Maintain an allow-list of permitted filenames or use an indirect reference (e.g., a lookup key mapped to a fixed set of files) rather than directly using user input in file paths."
        ]
      }
