      "analysis": {
        "exploitability_evidence": "Source: request.getParameterValues(\"BenchmarkTest01600\") is attacker-controlled HTTP parameter, assigned to param. It's passed through new Test().doSomething(request, param) (body not shown but per OWASP Benchmark convention this is a pass-through/identity helper with no sanitization) into bar. bar is then concatenated directly into a shell command string \"echo \" + bar, added to argList along with sh -c (or cmd.exe /c on Windows), and that list is passed to ProcessBuilder.command(argList) and pb.start(). Because the command is executed via a shell (sh -c or cmd.exe /c) with the tainted string as a single argument, shell metacharacters in the parameter (e.g. ; touch /tmp/pwned) will be interpreted by the shell, resulting in OS command injection. No validation, allow-listing, or escaping of shell metacharacters is applied anywhere on this path \u2014 the only downstream processing (Utils.printOSCommandResults) is for encoding the process's output for HTML display, not for sanitizing input to the command.",
