        "confidence": "medium",
        "severity_assessment": "low",
        "root_cause": "User-supplied HTTP parameter value flows, without any sanitization or type/format validation, into an HttpSession attribute via setAttribute call, whiching trusted (session) and untrusted (request) data.",
        "impact": "If an application session state is or later trusted logic reads this session attribute assuming it is valider-checked/internal data, an attacker could poison session state to manipulate subsequent authorization, display, or business logic decisions (trust boundary violation), potentially leading to logic bypass or injection into downstream consumers of the session attribute.",
        "remediation": [
          "Validate and whitelist the expected format/values of the request parameter before storing it in the session.",
          "Avoid storing raw, unvalidated user input directly as session attributes; store a validated/canonicalized representation instead.",
          "If the value must be redisplayed, encode it at every output sink separately from the session-storage decision, and treat session data as originating from an interaction boundary that requires its own validation."
        ]
      }
