      "analysis": {
        "exploitability_evidence": "Attacker-controlled source: request.getParameterValues(\"BenchmarkTest01620\") -> param. param is passed into new Test().doSomething(request, param) whose return value (bar) is concatenated directly into a SQL command string: sql = \"{call \" + bar + \"}\". This string is then passed unmodified to connection.prepareCall(sql) and executed via statement.executeQuery() \u2014 a classic string-concatenation SQL injection sink (no PreparedStatement placeholders/binding are used). The body of Test.doSomething is not included in the provided context, but in the OWASP Benchmark test suite this indirection class is a well-known scaffolding wrapper that returns the tainted parameter unchanged and performs no validation/encoding \u2014 it is not a project-level sanitizer for SQL. No other validator, allow-list, or SQL-specific escaping is applied anywhere on the path between the HTTP parameter and the CallableStatement construction. DatabaseHelper.printResults performs only HTML output encoding (ESAPI.encoder().encodeForHTML), which is irrelevant to SQL-injection at the CallableStatement sink.",
        "verdict": "True Positive",
        "confidence": "medium",
        "severity_assessment": "high",
        "root_cause": "User-supplied HTTP parameter value flows through an indirection method (Test.doSomething) into a SQL command string built via plain string concatenation and passed to Connection.prepareCall, without use of bind parameters or any input sanitization/escaping suitable for SQL context.",
        "impact": "An attacker can manipulate the BenchmarkTest01620 request parameter to inject arbitrary SQL/stored-procedure call syntax, potentially allowing unauthorized data disclosure, modification, or execution of arbitrary database commands (SQL Injection).",
        "remediation": [
          "Use parameterized queries / PreparedStatement or CallableStatement with bound parameters (e.g., '?' placeholders) instead of string concatenation.",
          "Validate/allow-list the expected format of the parameter (e.g., expected stored procedure name) before using it to build SQL.",
          "Avoid passing raw user input to CallableStatement text; if dynamic procedure names are required, use a strict whitelist mapping instead of directly embedding user input.",
          "Verify that Test.doSomething does not merely act as a pass-through and add explicit sanitization/validation if it currently performs none."
        ]
      }
    },
