      "analysis": {
        "exploitability_evidence": "The user-controllable value is `param` from `request.getParameterValues(\"BenchmarkTest01625\")`, passed to `new Test().doSomething(request, param)` and stored in `bar`. `bar` is concatenated into a SQL string (`sql`) which is passed to `JDBCtemplate.queryForObject(sql, Long.class)`. The flagged sink at line 57 is `response.getWriter().println(\"Your results are: \" + String.valueOf(results))`. Here `results` is the return value of `queryForObject(...)`, declared as `Long`, not the raw `bar`/`sql` string. Because `results` is strongly typed as `Long`, `String.valueOf(results)` can only ever be a numeric string (or null), so the value actually written to the response output stream cannot contain attacker-supplied HTML/JS payloads regardless of what `bar` contained. The tainted string does reach the SQL query (a potential SQL injection sink), but it does not reach the `response.getWriter()` XSS sink flagged here.",
        "verdict": "False Positive",
        "confidence": "high",
        "severity_assessment": "low",
        "root_cause": "The value written to the response writer is a Long (query result), not the raw tainted parameter string, so the flagged println call cannot emit attacker-controlled markup/script content.",
        "impact": "No XSS impact via this sink since only numeric data derived from a DB query result is written to the response.",
        "remediation": [
          "No action needed for this specific sink; confirm the SQL string construction elsewhere is parameterized to avoid SQL injection.",
          "If future changes make `results` a String or reflect user input directly, apply HTML encoding (e.g., ESAPI.encoder().encodeForHTML) before writing to the response."
        ]
      }
