          "key": "org/owasp/benchmark/testcode/BenchmarkTest01633.java:67:13-67:88:java.lang.security.audit.xss.no-direct-response-writer.no-direct-response-writer",
          "decision": "escalate",
          "reason": "User input from request parameter flows through Test.doSomething() into XPath expression and then directly to response.getWriter().println() without HTML encoding on the success path, enabling XSS.",
          "error": null,
          "elapsed_seconds": 1.429
        }
      },
      "analysis": {
        "exploitability_evidence": "Source: request.getParameterValues(\"BenchmarkTest01633\") -> param. param is passed to new Test().doSomething(request, param) whose body is not shown but per the standard OWASP Benchmark pattern this passthrough helper returns the value largely unmodified (confirmed by the static triage evidence indicating unmodified flow). The returned value 'bar' is concatenated directly into an XPath expression: \"/Employees/Employee[@emplid='\" + bar + \"']\", which is then evaluated via xp.evaluate(expression, xmlDocument). On the success path the resulting string is written directly to the client: response.getWriter().println(\"Your query results are: \" + result + \"<br/>\") with NO HTML encoding. Only the exception-handling branch applies ESAPI.encoder().encodeForHTML(bar); the normal (non-exception) path that is reached whenever the XPath evaluates successfully performs no output encoding. Because 'bar' is concatenated unescaped into the XPath predicate, an attacker can break out of the quoted attribute value and use XPath 1.0 string functions (e.g. concat()) to force the evaluate() call to return an attacker-chosen string independent of the underlying XML content, which is then reflected unescaped into the HTML response, resulting in a classic reflected XSS via XPath injection.",
        "verdict": "True Positive",
        "confidence": "medium",
        "severity_assessment": "medium",
        "root_cause": "User-supplied request parameter is concatenated unsanitized into an XPath expression and the result of evaluating that expression is written directly to the HTTP response via response.getWriter().println() without any HTML output encoding on the success path, only the exception path uses ESAPI encoding.",
        "impact": "An attacker can craft an XPath-injection payload (e.g. using XPath 1.0 string functions such as concat()) to make the evaluated result contain arbitrary attacker-controlled text, including HTML/JavaScript, which is reflected unescaped in the response, leading to reflected Cross-Site Scripting and potential session hijacking, credential theft, or other client-side attacks.",
        "remediation": [
          "HTML-encode the 'result' value (and any other user-influenced output) before writing to the response, e.g. using ESAPI.encoder().encodeForHTML(result) as already done in the catch block.",
          "Avoid building XPath expressions via string concatenation; use parameterized/precompiled XPath queries or an XPath variable resolver (XPathVariableResolver) to bind user input safely.",
          "Apply strict input validation/allow-listing on the 'BenchmarkTest01633' parameter (e.g., restrict to expected emplid format) before using it in the XPath expression.",
          "Use a templating/view technology that auto-escapes output instead of writing directly to the response writer."
        ]
      }
    },
