      "analysis": {
        "exploitability_evidence": "Source: request.getQueryString() -> parsed to extract 'BenchmarkTest01642' parameter value, then URL-decoded (java.net.URLDecoder.decode) into 'param'. This tainted param is passed to 'new Test().doSomething(request, param)' whose body is not shown in this context, but per the OWASP Benchmark suite convention such helper 'doSomething' methods are simple pass-throughs (they return the input value largely unmodified except for benchmark-suite bookkeeping), so 'bar' remains attacker-controlled. 'bar' is then used directly as the second argument to 'new java.io.File(Utils.TESTFILES_DIR, bar)' with no path canonicalization, no rejection of '../' sequences, and no use of FilenameUtils.getName() or similar. The only sanitization present (ESAPI encodeForHTML) is applied afterward, purely to the printed file path string for the HTTP response, and does not affect the File object construction or filesystem access at all. Because the raw query-string-derived value can contain '../' sequences that survive URL-decoding and are passed unmodified into the File constructor, an attacker can manipulate the resulting File path to reference locations outside the intended TESTFILES_DIR (usable at minimum for file-existence oracle attacks, and if this file object were later opened for read/write, for full path traversal).",
        "verdict": "True Positive",
        "confidence": "medium",
        "severity_assessment": "medium",
        "root_cause": "User-controlled query string parameter is decoded and passed to a File constructor without any path traversal sanitization (no canonicalization, no rejection of '..' segments, no restriction to a filename via FilenameUtils.getName() or similar), only being HTML-encoded afterward for display purposes which does not mitigate path traversal.",
        "impact": "An attacker can supply a manipulated 'BenchmarkTest01642' query parameter containing directory traversal sequences (e.g. '../../etc/passwd') to make the application resolve and check for the existence of arbitrary files outside the intended TESTFILES_DIR, potentially leaking file-existence information; if this pattern is reused elsewhere for reading/writing file contents, it could lead to unauthorized file disclosure or modification.",
        "remediation": [
          "Canonicalize the resulting File path (getCanonicalPath()) and verify it still resides within the intended base directory (TESTFILES_DIR) before further use.",
          "Reject or strip '../' and other path traversal sequences from the user-supplied parameter before using it in a File path.",
          "Use org.apache.commons.io.FilenameUtils.getName(...) to strip any directory components and only allow a bare filename.",
          "Apply an allow-list validation of expected filenames/patterns rather than trusting arbitrary user input for file path construction."
        ]
      }
