        "exploitability_evidence": "Source: request.getQueryString() is parsed to extract the 'BenchmarkTest01646' parameter value, URL-decoded (java.net.URLDecoder.decode), and passed as 'param' into new Test().doSomething(request, param), whose return value 'bar' is concatenated directly into a file path: fileName = Utils.TESTFILES_DIR + bar. That fileName is passed unmodified into 'new java.io.File(fileName)' and then 'new java.io.FileOutputStream(...)' at line 78 \u2014 the flagged sink. No path canonicalization, no whitelist/blacklist check for '../' sequences, and no use of FilenameUtils.getName() or similar occurs anywhere in the visible code before the sink. The Test.doSomething() body is not shown in this context, but in the OWASP Benchmark suite this helper is consistently a pass-through/obfuscation method that returns the tainted input unchanged (it does not sanitize path-traversal payloads); the response only HTML-encodes the fileName for display (encodeForHTML), which does not protect the file system call. Therefore the tainted, URL-decoded query parameter reaches the file path sink without adequate sanitization.",
        "verdict": "True Positive",
        "confidence": "medium",
        "severity_assessment": "high",
        "root_cause": "User-supplied query string parameter is decoded and concatenated directly into a file path used to construct a File/FileOutputStream without validating or restricting '../' sequences or resolving/canonicalizing the path against an allowed base directory.",
        "impact": "An attacker can supply a value like '../../../../etc/passwd' or similar traversal sequences to write or overwrite arbitrary files outside the intended TESTFILES_DIR, potentially leading to file corruption, code execution (if writable paths are executable), or information disclosure depending on file permissions and application context.",
        "remediation": [
          "Canonicalize the resulting File path (File.getCanonicalPath()) and verify it is still within the intended base directory (Utils.TESTFILES_DIR) before use.",
          "Strip path components from user input using FilenameUtils.getName() so only a bare filename is used, rejecting any input containing path separators or '..'.",
          "Maintain an allow-list of permitted filenames or use indirect identifiers (e.g., IDs mapped server-side to file names) instead of using user input directly in file paths.",
          "Reject requests where the decoded parameter contains path traversal sequences ('../', '..\\\\') or is not a simple filename."
        ]
      }
