        "confidence": "medium",
        "severity_assessment": "high",
        "root_cause": "User-supplied query string parameter is decoded and concatenated into a file path with no path traversal sanitization (no canonicalization, no rejection of '../' sequences, no allow-list check) before being used to open a FileOutputStream.",
        "impact": "An attacker can supply a query string parameter containing directory traversal sequences (e.g. '../../etc/passwd' or arbitrary absolute paths) to write/overwrite files outside the intended TESTFILES_DIR, potentially leading to arbitrary file write, data corruption, or in some environments remote code execution (e.g., overwriting configuration or executable files).",
        "remediation": [
          "Canonicalize the resulting file path (e.g., File.getCanonicalPath()) and verify it still resides within the intended TESTFILES_DIR before opening the FileOutputStream.",
          "Use org.apache.commons.io.FilenameUtils.getName(...) or similar to strip any directory components from the user-supplied value, allowing only bare filenames.",
          "Maintain an allow-list of permitted filenames or use a UUID/mapping approach instead of directly using user input in file paths.",
          "Reject any input containing '..', absolute path prefixes, or null bytes before use in path construction."
        ]
      }
