        "exploitability_evidence": "Source: request.getQueryString() (fully attacker-controlled). The code extracts the value of the 'BenchmarkTest01647' parameter into 'param', URL-decodes it, then passes it to new Test().doSomething(request, param) whose return value ('bar') is concatenated directly into fileName = Utils.TESTFILES_DIR + bar. That fileName is passed unmodified into new java.io.FileOutputStream(fileName, false) \u2014 the flagged sink. No path traversal sanitization (no normalization, no check for '../', no use of FilenameUtils.getName or similar) is applied anywhere on this path. The only encoding present, ESAPI encodeForHTML(fileName), is applied afterward purely for safely displaying the filename in the HTTP response body \u2014 it does not alter or validate 'fileName' before the FileOutputStream call and provides no protection against path traversal in a filesystem sink. The Test.doSomething helper is not overridden with sanitization logic in the shown code path (consistent with the OWASP Benchmark harness pattern where this helper is an identity pass-through), so the tainted value flows unsanitized to the file write sink.",
