        "exploitability_evidence": "Source: request.getQueryString() is parsed to extract the 'BenchmarkTest01687' parameter value, URL-decoded into `param` (fully attacker-controlled, no sanitization applied). This is passed into `new Test().doSomething(request, param)`, whose body is not shown but based on the Benchmark test harness convention this is a pass-through/no-op wrapper used to move tainted data across a method boundary rather than a sanitizer (its name gives no indication of encoding/validation, and no ESAPI or whitelist call is visible anywhere on this path). The returned value `bar` is then concatenated directly into the command array passed to `Runtime.exec()`: on Linux, `args = {\"sh\", \"-c\", cmd + bar}` builds a single shell command string that `sh -c` will parse, so shell metacharacters (`;`, `|`, `&`, backticks, etc.) in `bar` are interpreted by the shell \u2014 classic OS command injection. On Windows, `args = {\"cmd.exe\",\"/c\",\"echo \", bar}` is likewise re-assembled and interpreted by cmd.exe's command-line parser, which is also vulnerable to injection via metacharacters like `&`, `|`. No adequate command-injection sanitizer (e.g., whitelist filtering of shell metacharacters) is applied to `param`/`bar` before it reaches `Runtime.exec`. The output-encoding calls (ESAPI encodeForHTML) seen in `Utils.printOSCommandResults` only protect the HTTP response rendering of command output, not the command construction itself, so they are irrelevant to this sink.",
