        "root_cause": "User-supplied query string parameter is URL-decoded and, after passing through an opaque pass-through helper method, concatenated unsanitized into a shell command string that is executed via Runtime.exec with a shell interpreter (sh -c / cmd.exe /c), allowing injection of arbitrary shell metacharacters and commands.",
        "impact": "An attacker can inject OS shell metacharacters into the 'BenchmarkTest01687' query parameter to execute arbitrary commands on the host with the privileges of the web application process, leading to full system compromise, data exfiltration, or lateral movement.",
        "remediation": [
          "Avoid invoking a shell (sh -c / cmd.exe /c) with user-influenced strings; call the target program directly with an argument array so no shell parses the input.",
          "If shell invocation is unavoidable, strictly whitelist allowed characters for the parameter (e.g., alphanumeric only) and reject/escape anything else before use.",
          "Use ESAPI's OS command validation (e.g., Validator.getValidInput with a strict regex) on the parameter before building the command.",
          "Prefer safer high-level APIs (e.g., Java NIO/File APIs) instead of shelling out to OS commands like 'ls' or 'echo' for functionality that can be implemented natively."
        ]
      }
