        "confidence": "medium",
        "severity_assessment": "high",
        "root_cause": "User-supplied HTTP query-string data is decoded but never validated/sanitized before being forwarded (through an opaque pass-through helper) into Runtime.exec's parameter list, so it directly reaches an OS process-creation API without any allow-list or encoding suitable for command execution contexts.",
        "impact": "If the untrusted value can influence how the spawned process behaves (e.g., via the environment string being interpreted unsafely by the target script/interpreter, or if the runtime environment differs from the documented envp semantics), an attacker could manipulate subprocess execution, potentially leading to information disclosure, unintended process behavior, or command injection, resulting in remote code execution on the host.",
        "remediation": [
          "Never pass raw, HTTP-derived strings into Runtime.exec/ProcessBuilder in any parameter position (command, args, or environment).",
          "Use ProcessBuilder with a fixed command and a strict allow-list of permitted argument/environment values; reject anything not on the whitelist.",
          "If dynamic values are required, validate them against a strict pattern (e.g., alphanumeric only) using ESAPI Validator or a similar allow-list mechanism before use.",
          "Avoid passing user input as process environment variables; if unavoidable, enforce a strict KEY=VALUE format check and restrict allowed keys/values.",
          "Review the body of Test.doSomething to confirm it performs no unsafe transformation and add explicit sanitization there if it currently passes data through unchanged."
        ]
      }
