      "analysis": {
        "exploitability_evidence": "Source: request.getQueryString() is parsed to extract 'param', which is URL-decoded (java.net.URLDecoder.decode) - fully attacker-controlled. This param is passed to new Test().doSomething(request, param), whose return value 'bar' is stored directly into the HttpSession via request.getSession().setAttribute(\"userid\", bar) at line 73. No validation, allow-listing, or type-checking is applied to 'bar' before it is placed into the session. The only sanitizer present (Utils.encodeForHTML) is applied afterward, only for HTML output in the response.getWriter().println() call, not before the setAttribute call - it does not protect the session-storage sink at all. Thus untrusted, attacker-influenced data flows unimpeded from the HTTP request into session state, matching the CWE-501 Trust Boundary Violation pattern exactly.",
