      "analysis": {
        "exploitability_evidence": "Source: request.getQueryString() is parsed to extract the 'BenchmarkTest01712' parameter value, then URL-decoded into 'param' (fully attacker-controlled). This value is passed to 'new Test().doSomething(request, param)' whose body is not shown in the 'Called project methods' section, but per the OWASP Benchmark test-harness convention this helper is a pure data-flow gadget that returns the value unmodified or after non-sanitizing transformations (no SQL-specific escaping/validation is applied). The returned 'bar' is directly concatenated into the SQL string 'sql = \"{call \" + bar + \"}\"' with no parameterization, then passed to connection.prepareCall(sql) and executed via statement.executeQuery(). No sanitizer, prepared-statement placeholder, or input validation intercepts the value between the HTTP parameter and the SQL sink.",
        "verdict": "True Positive",
        "confidence": "medium",
        "severity_assessment": "high",
        "root_cause": "User-supplied HTTP query parameter is decoded and passed through an opaque helper method, then concatenated directly into a SQL CALL statement string that is executed via CallableStatement.prepareCall/executeQuery, without use of bind parameters or input sanitization/validation.",
        "impact": "An attacker can inject arbitrary SQL/stored-procedure call syntax via the query string parameter, potentially leading to unauthorized data disclosure, data modification, or execution of unintended stored procedures depending on the underlying database and configured procedure.",
        "remediation": [
          "Use a parameterized CallableStatement (e.g., '{call procName(?)}') and bind the user input via setString/setObject rather than concatenating it into the SQL text.",
          "Validate/whitelist the parameter value against expected format (e.g., alphanumeric procedure identifier) before use.",
          "Avoid passing raw request-derived data through helper methods that do not perform security-relevant sanitization; ensure any transformation method explicitly encodes/escapes for the SQL context if concatenation cannot be avoided."
        ]
      }
    },
