      "analysis": {
        "exploitability_evidence": "Source: request.getQueryString() is parsed to extract 'param', which is then URL-decoded (still fully attacker-controlled). This value is passed to new Test().doSomething(request, param) whose return value 'bar' is directly concatenated into the SQL string: \"SELECT * from USERS where USERNAME=? and PASSWORD='\" + bar + \"'\". Although a PreparedStatement is created from this string, only one placeholder (?) exists for USERNAME and it is bound to the constant \"foo\" via statement.setString(1,\"foo\") \u2014 the PASSWORD value (the tainted 'bar') is embedded directly into the SQL text before prepareStatement is even called, so parameterization provides no protection for it. No sanitizer, escaping, or validation is applied to 'param'/'bar' anywhere on this path (URLDecoder.decode is not a SQL sanitizer). The body of Test.doSomething is not shown, but per the OWASP Benchmark suite convention this helper is a pass-through wrapper that returns the tainted value unchanged, consistent with the absence of any sanitizing logic in the visible code. This results in classic string-concatenation SQL injection.",
        "verdict": "True Positive",
        "confidence": "medium",
        "severity_assessment": "high",
        "root_cause": "User-controlled query-string parameter is concatenated directly into a SQL query string before the query is prepared; the PreparedStatement's parameter binding does not cover the concatenated portion, so tainted data becomes part of the executable SQL text without any sanitization or encoding.",
        "impact": "An attacker can manipulate the PASSWORD clause of the SQL query to bypass authentication logic, extract or modify arbitrary data, or in some database configurations execute stacked queries, leading to full compromise of the USERS table data.",
        "remediation": [
          "Use a parameterized query with a placeholder for the PASSWORD value as well, e.g. 'SELECT * from USERS where USERNAME=? and PASSWORD=?' and bind both values via statement.setString(1,...) and statement.setString(2, bar).",
          "Never build SQL strings via concatenation of untrusted input even when a PreparedStatement is eventually used \u2014 the driver only parameterizes values bound through setX methods, not text embedded in the query string.",
          "Validate/allow-list the expected format of the parameter before use, in addition to parameterization.",
          "Apply the principle of least privilege to the DB account used by the application to limit blast radius of any injection."
        ]
      }
    },
