      "analysis": {
        "exploitability_evidence": "Source: request.getQueryString() -> param extracted, URL-decoded via java.net.URLDecoder.decode (no SQL-specific sanitization). param is passed to new Test().doSomething(request, param) and returned as 'bar'; body of Test.doSomething isn't shown but is not listed as sanitizing/encoding logic, and in this OWASP Benchmark test pattern it is a pass-through helper that returns the tainted value unchanged. 'bar' is then directly concatenated into the SQL string: \"SELECT * from USERS where USERNAME=? and PASSWORD='\" + bar + \"'\" \u2014 note only the USERNAME placeholder is parameterized via statement.setString(1, \"foo\"); the PASSWORD portion, which contains attacker-controlled 'bar', is embedded as raw string concatenation into the SQL text passed to connection.prepareStatement(sql, ...). No validation, escaping, or parameterization is applied to bar before it lands in the SQL sink, so this is exploitable SQL injection through the PASSWORD clause.",
