      "analysis": {
        "exploitability_evidence": "The flagged sink is the response.getWriter().println(...) call in the main try block: `response.getWriter().println(\"Your results are: \" + results);`. Tracing back, `results` is an Integer returned from `DatabaseHelper.JDBCtemplate.queryForObject(sql, Integer.class)` \u2014 a numeric database result, not the raw user-controlled string. While `bar`/`param` (derived from `request.getQueryString()` via `URLDecoder.decode`) are attacker-controlled and flow into the `sql` string, that tainted string is only used to build/execute the SQL query; it is never written to the response at this sink. The other getWriter() calls in the method either emit static strings or explicitly HTML-encode the tainted `sql` value via `org.owasp.esapi.ESAPI.encoder().encodeForHTML(sql)` before writing it. Therefore no unsanitized attacker-controlled string reaches an HTTP response writer.",
        "verdict": "False Positive",
        "confidence": "medium",
        "severity_assessment": "low",
        "root_cause": "The semgrep rule flags any direct use of response.getWriter()/OutputStream as a potential XSS sink without confirming that attacker-controlled data actually reaches that specific write call. In this method, the write at the flagged line outputs a coerced Integer (database result count), not raw tainted text, and the only branch that writes the tainted SQL string applies ESAPI HTML encoding first.",
        "impact": "None for this specific line \u2014 no exploitable XSS since the value written is a numeric type or already encoded output.",
        "remediation": [
          "No fix required for this specific sink; the Integer conversion and existing ESAPI encoding in the other branch already prevent XSS.",
          "If defense-in-depth is desired, route all direct getWriter()/println output through a templating engine or consistently apply output encoding (e.g., ESAPI encodeForHTML) to any string derived from user input before writing.",
          "Confirm via dynamic testing that no other code path can inject raw attacker-controlled strings into this writer."
        ]
      }
