      "analysis": {
        "exploitability_evidence": "The flagged sink at line 96 is `response.getWriter().println(value.getTextContent() + \"<br/>\");`. Tracing back: `value` is a `org.w3c.dom.Element` retrieved from `nodeList.item(i)`, and `nodeList` is the result of evaluating an XPath expression against `xmlDocument`, which is parsed from a static file `employees.xml` loaded via `Utils.getFileFromClasspath` (a bundled, trusted classpath resource, not user-controlled). While the user-controlled value `bar` (derived from `request.getQueryString()` via `URLDecoder.decode` and `Test.doSomething`) is concatenated into the XPath expression string (`\"/Employees/Employee[@emplid='\" + bar + \"']\"`), that only affects XPath injection (which node(s) get selected) \u2014 the actual text written to the response (`value.getTextContent()`) is data pulled from the static, trusted employees.xml document, not attacker-supplied content. There is no code path where the raw/decoded query-string value itself is written to the response.getWriter() at line 96. The only place `bar` is written directly to the writer is in the catch block, and there it is passed through `ESAPI.encoder().encodeForHTML(bar)`, which is an adequate encoder for an HTML sink.",
        "verdict": "False Positive",
        "confidence": "high",
        "severity_assessment": "low",
        "root_cause": "The scanner's rule generically flags any response.getWriter()/OutputStream call in a method that also processes user input, without verifying that the specific data written to that sink is attacker-controlled. At line 96 the printed value (`value.getTextContent()`) originates from a static, trusted XML file bundled with the app, not from the request; the only truly tainted variable (`bar`) is HTML-encoded via ESAPI at the location where it is actually written to the response.",
        "impact": "No direct XSS impact from this specific sink since the reflected content is not attacker-controlled; however note there is a legitimate but separate concern of XPath Injection (CWE-643) via unsanitized concatenation of `bar` into the XPath expression, which is out of scope for this CWE-79 finding.",
        "remediation": [
          "No action needed for XSS at this line since the printed content is not attacker-controlled.",
          "Separately, address the XPath Injection risk by using parameterized/precompiled XPath expressions (e.g., XPathVariableResolver) instead of string concatenation of user input into the query."
        ]
      }
