        "exploitability_evidence": "The flagged sink at line 89 is `response.getWriter().println(\"Your query results are: \" + result + \"<br/>\");`. Tracing `result`: it is NOT the raw attacker-controlled `bar`/`param` value, but the return value of `xp.evaluate(expression, xmlDocument)`, where `expression = \"/Employees/Employee[@emplid='\" + bar + \"']\"`. `bar` (derived from the URL-decoded query parameter via `Test.doSomething()`) is only ever embedded inside the quoted predicate of a fixed XPath location-path expression. Because the surrounding expression shape (`/Employees/Employee[@emplid='...']`) is a location path, its evaluation always yields a node-set drawn from the static `employees.xml` document, and `xp.evaluate()` (default String return type) converts that node-set to the string-value of the first matching node in document order. An attacker can manipulate the predicate (classic XPath injection) to change WHICH existing node/employee record is selected, but cannot make the top-level expression itself return an arbitrary attacker-chosen string outside of the document's own static content, since `bar` never reaches a position where it becomes the entire expression or a string-returning function call at the top level. Therefore, while this code is vulnerable to XPath Injection (CWE-643) allowing data disclosure from employees.xml, the value ultimately written unencoded to the response (`result`) is limited to pre-existing static XML content, not attacker-supplied markup/script, so no arbitrary HTML/JS can be reflected through this specific println call. The separate catch-block println at a later line does properly HTML-encode `bar` via `ESAPI.encoder().encodeForHTML(bar)`, reinforcing that the raw tainted value is not the one reaching line 89 unencoded.",
        "verdict": "False Positive",
        "confidence": "medium"
      }
    },
