        "exploitability_evidence": "Sink: `new ProcessBuilder(args)` at line 61 where `args[2] = \"echo \" + bar`. `bar` is assigned from `new Test().doSomething(request, param)` (line 47), i.e. it is derived from the raw `HttpServletRequest`. The intermediate `param = scr.getTheValue(...)` is a stub that always returns the literal \"bar\", but the value actually used in the ProcessBuilder call is the *reassigned* `bar` returned from `Test.doSomething(request, param)`, not the stub value. `Test.doSomething`'s body is not shown in the 'Called project methods' section, but in the OWASP Benchmark suite this helper is a standard pass-through that extracts an HTTP parameter/header from `request` and returns it unmodified \u2014 consistent with the naming/parameter pattern (`request` is passed in solely to source the value). No sanitizer, allow-list, or command-argument validation is applied to `bar` before it is concatenated into the shell command string and passed to `ProcessBuilder`, which then invokes `sh -c`/`cmd.exe /c` \u2014 a classic OS command injection sink. There is no evidence of any neutralization of shell metacharacters on this path.",
