        "exploitability_evidence": "The value ultimately passed as the environment array to Runtime.exec (bar) originates from `param = scr.getTheValue(\"BenchmarkTest01793\")`, whose body is shown as `public String getTheValue(String p) { return \"bar\"; }` \u2014 a hard-coded literal that ignores both the argument and the underlying HttpServletRequest entirely. `param` is then passed into `new Test().doSomething(request, param)`; although Test's body isn't shown, OWASP Benchmark's ubiquitous 'Test' passthrough pattern simply returns the given param unchanged, so `bar` remains the constant \"bar\". The actual command executed (`cmd`) is derived from `Utils.getInsecureOSCommandString`, which loads a fixed script file path from the classpath \u2014 also not attacker-controlled. Since `bar` is only used as the `envp` argument to `Runtime.exec(args, argsEnv)` and is a hard-coded constant (not derived from any HTTP request parameter, header, or cookie), there is no attacker-controlled data reaching the exec sink in this trace.",
