        "exploitability_evidence": "The scanner flags r.exec(args, argsEnv, ...) at line 58. Tracing back: 'param' comes from scr.getTheValue(\"BenchmarkTest01795\") whose shown body is `return \"bar\";` \u2014 a hardcoded literal that ignores its input entirely, so no HttpServletRequest data reaches 'param'. 'bar' (the local var used in argsEnv) is the return of new Test().doSomething(request, param); Test.doSomething's body is not shown, so we cannot confirm it reintroduces request-derived data, but even if it did, the value ends up in argsEnv = {bar}, which is passed as the *envp* (environment variables) parameter of Runtime.exec(String[] cmdarray, String[] envp, File dir) \u2014 not the cmdarray/command itself. The actual command executed, `args = {cmd}`, is built solely from Utils.getInsecureOSCommandString(), which reads a fixed classpath resource path (insecureCmd.bat/.sh) determined by os.name \u2014 no user input at all. Thus neither the executed program path nor (demonstrably) the tainted-request path leads into the command argument that constitutes the OS command; the taint chain to the flagged sink is broken by the hardcoded getTheValue() stub and, independently, by the taint being routed to envp rather than cmdarray.",
