        "small_model": {
          "key": "org/owasp/benchmark/testcode/BenchmarkTest01819.java:54:25-54:53:java.lang.security.audit.sqli.jdbc-sqli.jdbc-sqli",
          "decision": "escalate",
          "reason": "User-controlled parameter flows through SeparateClassRequest and Test.doSomething() into SQL string concatenation without sanitization, creating a SQL injection vulnerability.",
          "error": null,
          "elapsed_seconds": 1.403
        }
      },
      "analysis": {
        "exploitability_evidence": "The SQL query at line 54 is built via string concatenation: `String sql = \"INSERT INTO users ... VALUES ('foo','\" + bar + \"')\"`. The value `bar` originates from `new Test().doSomething(request, param)`, and `param` comes from `scr.getTheValue(\"BenchmarkTest01819\")`. Critically, the shown body of `SeparateClassRequest.getTheValue(String p)` is `return \"bar\";` \u2014 it completely ignores its input argument and always returns the hardcoded literal string \"bar\", never reading from the HttpServletRequest at all. This means the value flowing into `doSomething()` as `param` is a compile-time constant, not attacker-controlled. While the body of `Test.doSomething()` is not shown, it receives this constant `param` in addition to the request object; based on the standard OWASP Benchmark pattern (where this helper typically just echoes/validates the passed-in `param` rather than independently re-extracting request data), there is no evidence in the visible code that user-supplied data reaches the SQL sink. The taint chain is broken at the very first hop by the hardcoded return value.",
        "verdict": "False Positive",
        "confidence": "medium"
      }
    },
