          "decision": "escalate",
          "reason": "User-controlled input from request parameter flows through doSomething() into SQL string concatenation without sanitization or prepared statements.",
          "error": null,
          "elapsed_seconds": 1.307
        }
      },
      "analysis": {
        "exploitability_evidence": "The flagged sink is the string-concatenated SQL passed to statement.executeUpdate(sql,...) at line 54. Tracing back: 'bar' comes from new Test().doSomething(request, param), and 'param' comes from scr.getTheValue(\"BenchmarkTest01820\"). The body of SeparateClassRequest.getTheValue is shown and it does NOT read anything from the HttpServletRequest \u2014 it unconditionally returns the string literal \"bar\". Therefore the value handed to Test.doSomething is a hardcoded constant, not attacker-controlled data. Even though Test.doSomething's body isn't shown, the only input it could taint the SQL with (the 'param' argument) is already fixed to a constant string; the 'request' argument passed alongside is not shown to be used to extract any parameter value here (getTheValue is the designated extraction point in this template and it discards the request). Since the true source of user input never actually reads request data, no attacker-controlled value reaches the sink, so the SQL injection sink is unreachable via untrusted input in this code path.",
        "verdict": "False Positive",
        "confidence": "medium",
        "severity_assessment": "low",
        "root_cause": "The apparent taint source (SeparateClassRequest.getTheValue) is neutered \u2014 it returns a hardcoded literal (\"bar\") instead of extracting a value from the HttpServletRequest, so the string concatenated into the SQL statement is a constant rather than user-controlled input.",
        "impact": "None in current form since input is not attacker-controlled; if the source method were changed to actually parse a request parameter, the same code path would become vulnerable to SQL injection due to string concatenation.",
        "remediation": [
          "Use PreparedStatement with parameterized queries instead of string concatenation, regardless of current source neutrality, to prevent future regressions.",
          "If getTheValue is intended to reflect real user input in production code, ensure it retrieves and validates request parameters properly.",
          "Add unit tests to confirm no code path allows getTheValue to return unsanitized user input directly into SQL construction."
        ]
      }
    },
