          "key": "org/owasp/benchmark/testcode/BenchmarkTest01831.java:79:21-79:58:java.lang.security.audit.tainted-ldapi-from-http-request.tainted-ldapi-from-http-request",
          "decision": "escalate",
          "reason": "Cookie value flows through URLDecoder into doSomething() then directly concatenated into LDAP filter string without sanitization, enabling LDAP injection despite output encoding being present only for results.",
          "error": null,
          "elapsed_seconds": 1.457
        }
      },
      "analysis": {
        "exploitability_evidence": "Attacker-controlled source: HTTP Cookie named 'BenchmarkTest01831' read via request.getCookies(), URL-decoded, and stored in 'param'. This value flows into doSomething(request, param) whose body is not shown but is the standard OWASP Benchmark passthrough helper (Benchmark tests are constructed so this method returns the tainted value largely unmodified). The result 'bar' is directly concatenated into an LDAP filter string: '(&(objectclass=person))(|(uid=' + bar + ')(street={0}))' with no LDAP-specific escaping (e.g., no escaping of parentheses, asterisks, backslashes, or NUL per RFC 4515) before being passed to ctx.search(base, filter, filters, sc). The only sanitization present in the method is ESAPI encodeForHTML() applied to the *output* attributes/results for XSS protection \u2014 this has no bearing on LDAP filter injection at the sink. No LDAP filter encoder (e.g., ESAPI encodeForLDAP) is applied to 'bar' before it reaches the filter string, so a cookie value like ')(uid=*))(|(uid=*' could alter the search filter's logic (LDAP filter injection).",
        "verdict": "True Positive",
        "confidence": "medium",
        "severity_assessment": "medium",
        "root_cause": "User-controlled cookie value is decoded and concatenated directly into an LDAP search filter string without any LDAP-specific escaping/sanitization; the only encoding present (ESAPI encodeForHTML) protects against XSS on output, not LDAP injection on the query construction.",
        "impact": "An attacker can inject LDAP filter metacharacters via the cookie value to alter search semantics, potentially bypassing intended query restrictions, enumerating unauthorized directory entries, or extracting sensitive LDAP data.",
        "remediation": [
          "Use ESAPI's encodeForLDAP() (or equivalent) to escape LDAP filter special characters (\\, *, (, ), NUL) in the 'bar' value before building the filter string.",
          "Prefer parameterized/templated LDAP filters (using SearchControls with filter arguments, e.g., '(uid={0})' with filters array) instead of manual string concatenation for all user-supplied fields, not just the 'street' field.",
          "Validate/allow-list the expected format of the cookie value (e.g., alphanumeric only) before using it in any downstream directory query."
        ]
      }
    },
