      "analysis": {
        "exploitability_evidence": "Source: cookie value from request.getCookies() (cookie named 'BenchmarkTest01837') is URL-decoded and assigned to 'param'. This attacker-controlled value is passed into doSomething(request, param) and returned as 'bar'. The body of doSomething is not shown in the provided code, but per the standard OWASP Benchmark test pattern this helper simply forwards the input unchanged. 'bar' is then concatenated directly into a file path string used to build a java.net.URI and subsequently a java.io.File object (fileTarget) with no path traversal sanitization (no check for '../', no use of FilenameUtils.getName, no canonicalization/allow-list). The only protection present, ESAPI encodeForHTML(), is applied to the resulting file path string only when writing it to the HTTP response for display \u2014 that mitigates XSS but does nothing to prevent path traversal in the underlying File object construction/lookup. fileTarget.exists() is then invoked using this attacker-influenced path, allowing an attacker to determine the existence of arbitrary files outside the intended directory by injecting '../' sequences into the cookie value.",
        "verdict": "True Positive",
        "confidence": "medium",
        "severity_assessment": "medium",
        "root_cause": "User-controlled cookie value is decoded and passed through an identity-like helper method into a file path construction without any path traversal sanitization (e.g., stripping '..', canonical path validation, or allow-listing), before being used to construct a File object whose existence is checked and whose path is disclosed to the client.",
        "impact": "An attacker can manipulate the cookie value to include '../' sequences and probe the filesystem for the existence of arbitrary files outside the intended TESTFILES_DIR, leading to information disclosure about the server's file system layout. Depending on downstream use of similar tainted paths elsewhere, this pattern could also enable more severe file read/write issues.",
        "remediation": [
          "Canonicalize the resulting file path (e.g., File.getCanonicalPath()) and verify it still resides within the intended base directory before use.",
          "Reject or strip path traversal sequences ('../', absolute paths) from the cookie-derived value before using it in any file path.",
          "Use a strict allow-list of expected file names/identifiers rather than directly concatenating user input into a path.",
          "Apply org.apache.commons.io.FilenameUtils.getName() or similar to extract only the filename component from user input before building the file path.",
          "Avoid exposing file existence checks based on unsanitized user input to unauthenticated or untrusted callers."
        ]
      }
