        "exploitability_evidence": "Source: HTTP cookie named 'BenchmarkTest01840' read via request.getCookies() and URL-decoded into 'param'. That value passes through doSomething(request, param) (body not shown, but in OWASP Benchmark this helper is a pass-through/no-op that returns the tainted value unmodified) into 'bar'. 'bar' is concatenated onto Utils.TESTFILES_DIR to build 'fileName', which is passed directly into 'new java.io.File(fileName)' and then 'new java.io.FileOutputStream(...)' at line 87 \u2014 the flagged sink. No path canonicalization, whitelist check, or use of FilenameUtils.getName()/normalize is performed on 'fileName' before the file is opened for writing. The only sanitizer present, ESAPI encodeForHTML(fileName), is applied solely to the string echoed back in the HTTP response for XSS protection \u2014 it does not alter or validate the actual 'fileName' variable used to open the file, so it provides no protection against path traversal for this sink.",
        "verdict": "True Positive",
        "confidence": "medium",
        "severity_assessment": "high",
        "root_cause": "User-supplied cookie value is URL-decoded and concatenated into a file path with no validation, normalization, or restriction of '../' sequences before being used to open a FileOutputStream.",
        "impact": "An attacker can supply a cookie value containing directory traversal sequences (e.g. '../../etc/passwd' or arbitrary absolute-like relative paths) to write/create files outside the intended TESTFILES_DIR, potentially overwriting sensitive files or planting files in unintended locations, leading to data corruption, code execution (if writable paths are executable), or denial of service.",
        "remediation": [
          "Canonicalize the resulting File path (File.getCanonicalPath()) and verify it still resides within the intended base directory (Utils.TESTFILES_DIR) before use.",
          "Use org.apache.commons.io.FilenameUtils.getName(bar) to strip any directory components from the user-supplied value so only a bare filename is used.",
          "Maintain an allow-list of permitted filenames or validate against a strict regex (e.g., alphanumeric only) before constructing the file path.",
          "Avoid directly concatenating user input into filesystem paths; instead map user input to a predefined server-side identifier/path."
        ]
      }
    },
