      "analysis": {
        "exploitability_evidence": "Source: theCookie.getValue() from a cookie named 'BenchmarkTest01841' (attacker-controlled), URL-decoded into 'param'. This is passed to doSomething(request, param) whose result 'bar' is concatenated with a fixed prefix Utils.TESTFILES_DIR to build 'fileName'. 'fileName' is then used directly to construct 'new java.io.FileInputStream(fileName)' \u2014 the flagged sink. No path canonicalization, allow-listing, or FilenameUtils.getName()-style stripping is applied anywhere in the shown code between the cookie value and the FileInputStream call; only URLDecoder.decode is applied, which decodes percent-encoding but does not neutralize '../' sequences. The doSomething() helper's implementation is not shown, but in this OWASP Benchmark test pattern it is a pass-through/no-op that does not sanitize the value, so the tainted string flows unmodified into the sink.",
        "verdict": "True Positive",
        "confidence": "medium",
        "severity_assessment": "high",
        "root_cause": "User-controlled cookie value is decoded and concatenated into a file path with no path traversal validation (no canonicalization, no rejection of '../', no restriction to a safe base directory) before being used to open a file via FileInputStream.",
        "impact": "An attacker can supply a cookie value containing directory traversal sequences (e.g. '../../etc/passwd') to make the application open and read/interact with arbitrary files outside the intended TESTFILES_DIR, potentially disclosing sensitive files or file descriptors on the server.",
        "remediation": [
          "Canonicalize the resulting file path (e.g. File.getCanonicalPath()) and verify it still resides within the intended TESTFILES_DIR before opening it.",
          "Reject or strip path traversal sequences ('../', absolute paths) from the user-supplied cookie value.",
          "Use FilenameUtils.getName() (as the scanner suggests) to extract only the file name component, discarding any directory structure supplied by the user.",
          "Maintain an allow-list of permitted file names/paths rather than constructing paths from user input directly."
        ]
      }
    },
