      "analysis": {
        "exploitability_evidence": "Source: HTTP cookie value read via request.getCookies() -> theCookie.getValue(), URL-decoded and assigned to 'param'. This attacker-controlled value flows into doSomething(request, param) whose body is not shown but which in this test-suite pattern is a passthrough utility returning the value unchanged, and the result is assigned to 'bar'. 'bar' is then directly concatenated into the SQL string: \"... PASSWORD='\" + bar + \"'\" with no escaping or parameterization, and the resulting 'sql' string is executed via DatabaseHelper.JDBCtemplate.batchUpdate(sql) \u2014 a raw SQL execution sink. The only sanitizer present, ESAPI.encoder().encodeForHTML(sql), is applied afterward to the SQL string purely for safely printing it back in the HTTP response body (XSS mitigation) and does not sanitize the value before it is used in the SQL query, so it is not adequate for the SQL injection sink. No parameterized query or SQL-specific escaping is used on the tainted value before it reaches batchUpdate.",
