      "analysis": {
        "exploitability_evidence": "Source: HTTP cookie named 'BenchmarkTest01890' is read via request.getCookies(), URL-decoded (java.net.URLDecoder.decode) into 'param', then passed to doSomething(request, param) whose body is not shown but in this benchmark pattern acts as a pass-through returning 'bar'. 'bar' is directly concatenated into the SQL string: `\"INSERT INTO users (username, password) VALUES ('foo','\" + bar + \"')\"`. This raw SQL string is then executed via `statement.executeUpdate(sql, new String[] {...})` using a plain java.sql.Statement rather than a PreparedStatement with bind parameters. No encoding, escaping, or allow-list validation is applied to 'bar' before it reaches the SQL sink \u2014 the only encoding seen (ESAPI encodeForHTML) is applied afterward, in outputUpdateComplete, for HTML output, which does not protect against SQL injection. Because the cookie value is fully attacker-controlled and reaches an executeUpdate call via string concatenation with no sanitizer for SQL context in between, this is exploitable.",
        "verdict": "True Positive",
        "confidence": "high",
        "severity_assessment": "high",
        "root_cause": "User-controlled cookie value is concatenated directly into a SQL statement string and executed with java.sql.Statement instead of using a parameterized/prepared statement with bound variables, allowing SQL metacharacters in the cookie value to alter the query structure.",
        "impact": "An attacker can inject arbitrary SQL via the cookie value, potentially manipulating the INSERT statement, exfiltrating or corrupting data, bypassing application logic, or in some DB configurations executing additional statements/commands leading to full database compromise.",
        "remediation": [
          "Use java.sql.PreparedStatement with bind parameters (e.g., '?' placeholders) instead of concatenating the cookie value into the SQL string.",
          "Never build SQL queries via string concatenation with attacker-controlled input, even after URL-decoding.",
          "Apply strict input validation/allow-listing on cookie values before use if they must be treated as identifiers or literals.",
          "Adopt an ORM or query builder that enforces parameterization by default.",
          "Enable and enforce a SAST/lint rule that flags any use of Statement.executeUpdate/executeQuery with concatenated strings."
        ]
      }
