        "exploitability_evidence": "Source: HTTP cookie named 'BenchmarkTest01891' read via request.getCookies() and URL-decoded (java.net.URLDecoder.decode) into variable 'param' - fully attacker-controlled. This value is passed through doSomething(request, param) (a trivial pass-through helper typical of OWASP Benchmark test cases, body not overriding taint) into 'bar', which is then directly concatenated into a SQL INSERT string: \"INSERT INTO users (username, password) VALUES ('foo','\" + bar + \"')\". This string is executed via statement.executeUpdate(sql, ...) using a plain java.sql.Statement rather than a PreparedStatement. No validation, escaping, or parameterization occurs anywhere on this path (URLDecoder only decodes URL encoding, it does not sanitize SQL metacharacters like single quotes). The only encoding applied afterward (ESAPI encodeForHTML in outputUpdateComplete) happens after the query already executed and is for HTML output, not SQL protection.",
        "verdict": "True Positive",
        "confidence": "high"
      }
