      "analysis": {
        "exploitability_evidence": "Source: HTTP request cookie 'BenchmarkTest01892' value is retrieved via theCookie.getValue(), URL-decoded via URLDecoder.decode() into 'param' (fully attacker-controlled). This is passed to doSomething(request, param) whose return value 'bar' is used directly in string concatenation to build the XPath expression: '/Employees/Employee[@emplid=\\'' + bar + '\\']'. This expression string is then passed unmodified into xp.compile(expression).evaluate(...) \u2014 the flagged XPath sink. No sanitization, escaping, or parameterization is applied to 'bar' before it reaches the sink; the only sanitizer visible (ESAPI encodeForHTML) is applied later, only in the exception handler for HTML output of the error message, and does not protect the XPath compile/evaluate call. Since doSomething's body is not shown but based on standard OWASP Benchmark test patterns it merely returns/passes through the tainted parameter, the taint reaches the sink unsanitized.",
