        "root_cause": "The scanner's generic 'response writer with tainted data in method' heuristic misattributes taint to value.getTextContent(), which actually originates from a static trusted XML resource file rather than from the tainted 'bar' variable. The only direct write of the tainted variable is HTML-encoded via ESAPI in the exception handler.",
        "impact": "No direct XSS impact from this specific sink since the written data does not include unescaped attacker input; residual risk exists only via XPath injection affecting query results selection, not script injection.",
        "remediation": [
          "Confirm doSomething() does not itself introduce additional risk (review its body if available)",
          "Continue using ESAPI encodeForHTML (or equivalent) for any path that writes user-supplied data directly to the response",
          "Address the underlying XPath injection by using parameterized XPath (e.g., XPathVariableResolver) instead of string concatenation, even though this is a separate CWE from XSS"
        ]
      }
