        "exploitability_evidence": "Source: request.getHeader(\"BenchmarkTest01906\") \u2014 fully attacker-controlled HTTP header. It is URL-decoded and passed through doSomething(request, param) (body not shown but in this test harness such wrapper methods are pass-through with no sanitization) into variable 'bar'. 'bar' is then concatenated directly into fileName = TESTFILES_DIR + bar and used to construct java.io.File/FileOutputStream at line 59. No validation, canonicalization, or whitelist check (e.g., FilenameUtils.getName, path normalization, or check against TESTFILES_DIR) is applied before use. The only encoding applied (ESAPI encodeForHTML) is for the HTTP response body, not for the file path, so it does not mitigate path traversal. An attacker can supply a header value containing '../' sequences to escape TESTFILES_DIR and write files to arbitrary locations.",
