          "key": "org/owasp/benchmark/testcode/BenchmarkTest01914.java:57:9-57:79:java.lang.security.audit.xss.no-direct-response-writer.no-direct-response-writer",
          "decision": "escalate",
          "reason": "User-controlled Referer header is URL-decoded and passed through doSomething() into a format string written directly to response without HTML encoding, creating an XSS risk.",
          "error": null,
          "elapsed_seconds": 1.469
        }
      },
      "analysis": {
        "exploitability_evidence": "Source: request.getHeader(\"Referer\") (attacker-controlled HTTP header). It is URL-decoded (java.net.URLDecoder.decode) which does not perform any HTML/XSS sanitization, then passed as 'param' into doSomething(request, param) whose body is not shown in this context. The result 'bar' is placed into an Object[] and written directly to the response via out.format(...,'%1$s and %2$s.', obj) using a raw PrintWriter obtained from response.getWriter(), bypassing any HTML-escaping view layer. No encoding (e.g., HTML entity encoding) is applied before the write. Since doSomething's implementation is not shown, sanitization cannot be fully confirmed, but URL-decoding is not a sanitizer for HTML context, and the final sink writes raw formatted string to the HTTP response without escaping \u2014 consistent with the classic OWASP Benchmark reflected XSS pattern where doSomething is a pass-through helper.",
        "verdict": "True Positive",
        "confidence": "medium",
        "severity_assessment": "medium",
        "root_cause": "User-controlled Referer header value is URL-decoded (not HTML-encoded) and eventually written to the HTTP response body via PrintWriter.format without any output encoding or escaping, allowing injected HTML/JS to be reflected back to the browser.",
        "impact": "An attacker can craft a malicious Referer header containing HTML/JavaScript that, when reflected in the response, executes in the victim's browser context (reflected XSS), potentially leading to session hijacking, credential theft, or other client-side attacks.",
        "remediation": [
          "HTML-encode all user-controlled data before writing it to the response (e.g., use OWASP Java Encoder's Encode.forHtml() on 'bar' before formatting).",
          "Avoid writing directly to the raw PrintWriter for dynamic content; use a templating engine (JSP/JSF) that auto-escapes output.",
          "Validate/allow-list the Referer header format if it must be used, rejecting or stripping HTML special characters.",
          "Set appropriate Content-Security-Policy headers as defense-in-depth against reflected XSS."
        ]
      }
    },
