          "key": "org/owasp/benchmark/testcode/BenchmarkTest01921.java:55:9-55:75:java.lang.security.audit.xss.no-direct-response-writer.no-direct-response-writer",
          "decision": "escalate",
          "reason": "User-controlled Referer header is URL-decoded and passed through doSomething() then directly written to response.getWriter().printf() without HTML escaping, creating a direct XSS vulnerability.",
          "error": null,
          "elapsed_seconds": 1.454
        }
      },
      "analysis": {
        "exploitability_evidence": "Source: HTTP 'Referer' request header (attacker-controlled) is read via request.getHeader('Referer') into 'param'. It is URL-decoded (decoding does not sanitize/escape HTML) and passed to doSomething(request, param) whose body is not shown but in this test-suite pattern is a pass-through method that returns the input largely unchanged. The result 'bar' is placed into an Object[] and written directly via response.getWriter().printf(\"Formatted like: %1$s and %2$s.\", obj) with no HTML-encoding of the %1$s substitution. No sanitizer, output encoder, or validator is applied anywhere on this path before the sink, and printf() performs no context-aware escaping.",
        "verdict": "True Positive",
        "confidence": "medium",
        "severity_assessment": "medium",
        "root_cause": "User-controlled Referer header value flows unescaped into response.getWriter().printf(), which writes raw content directly to the HTTP response body, bypassing any HTML-escaping view layer.",
        "impact": "An attacker can craft a request with a malicious Referer header (feasible via a link/redirect the attacker controls, or via tools that let attackers set arbitrary headers to victims) containing HTML/JavaScript payload; when reflected unescaped in the response, this results in reflected Cross-Site Scripting (XSS), allowing session hijacking, credential theft, or arbitrary actions in the victim's browser context.",
        "remediation": [
          "HTML-encode all user-controlled data (including headers like Referer) before writing to the response body, e.g., using OWASP Java Encoder (Encode.forHtml()) on 'bar' before the printf call.",
          "Avoid writing directly to response.getWriter()/OutputStream with unescaped dynamic content; use a templating engine (JSP/JSF) that auto-escapes output.",
          "Do not trust the Referer header for any security-sensitive or reflected output; treat all headers as untrusted input.",
          "Set a strict Content-Security-Policy header to reduce impact of any residual XSS."
        ]
      }
    },
