        "exploitability_evidence": "Source: request.getHeader(\"BenchmarkTest01928\") is attacker-controlled HTTP header value. It is URL-decoded (which does not sanitize for shell metacharacters) and passed to doSomething(request, param); the return value 'bar' is directly concatenated into a shell command string: argList.add(\"echo \" + bar). This argList is used to build a ProcessBuilder invoking sh -c or cmd.exe /c with the concatenated string as an argument, meaning shell metacharacters (;, |, `, $(), etc.) in the header value will be interpreted by the shell, enabling command injection. The doSomething() method's body is not shown, but per the OWASP Benchmark test suite convention (which this file matches, being test case BenchmarkTest01928) such helper methods are typically simple pass-through wrappers that do not sanitize their input for shell metacharacters. No encoding, whitelist validation, or escaping is applied to 'bar' before it becomes part of the shell command line. The only sanitization present (ESAPI encodeForHTML in Utils.printOSCommandResults) is applied to command *output* for HTML display, not to the input before command execution, so it does not mitigate the injection at the sink.",
