        "exploitability_evidence": "Attacker-controlled source: HTTP header 'BenchmarkTest01942' obtained via request.getHeader() is assigned to 'param'. It is then only URL-decoded (java.net.URLDecoder.decode) \u2014 a decoding step, not a command-injection sanitizer \u2014 before being passed to doSomething(request, param), whose return value 'bar' is concatenated directly into the command string 'cmd + bar' passed to Runtime.exec(). No whitelist, escaping, or shell-metacharacter filtering is applied anywhere on this path; the only encoding present in the file (ESAPI encodeForHTML) is applied to command *output* in printOSCommandResults, which protects against XSS in the response, not against OS command injection in the exec call. The body of doSomething is not shown, but nothing in the visible code path (URL-decode only) neutralizes shell metacharacters, and Benchmark helper methods of this shape are typically pass-through wrappers. Given the direct string concatenation into Runtime.exec with attacker-supplied header data, this is exploitable.",
