      "analysis": {
        "exploitability_evidence": "Source: request.getHeader(\"BenchmarkTest01961\") -> param, URL-decoded, passed into doSomething(request, param) which (per Benchmark test pattern) returns the value largely unchanged as 'bar'. bar is then directly concatenated into the SQL string: \"SELECT * from USERS where USERNAME=? and PASSWORD='\" + bar + \"'\". Although a PreparedStatement is used, only parameter 1 (a placeholder for USERNAME, set to the constant \"foo\") is bound via setString; the PASSWORD value (bar) is embedded directly in the SQL text before prepareStatement is called, so it is not parameterized at all. No sanitizer, encoder, or validator is applied to bar before concatenation. This allows an attacker to inject arbitrary SQL via the BenchmarkTest01961 HTTP header.",
        "verdict": "True Positive",
        "confidence": "high",
        "severity_assessment": "high",
        "root_cause": "User-controlled HTTP header value is concatenated directly into a SQL query string instead of being passed as a bound parameter to the PreparedStatement, despite the API superficially appearing parameterized.",
        "impact": "An attacker can manipulate the BenchmarkTest01961 HTTP header to inject arbitrary SQL, potentially bypassing authentication, exfiltrating data, or modifying/deleting data in the USERS table.",
        "remediation": [
          "Bind the password value as a PreparedStatement parameter (e.g., use a second '?' placeholder and call statement.setString(2, bar)) instead of concatenating it into the SQL string.",
          "Avoid building SQL strings via concatenation with any user-supplied input.",
          "Apply strict input validation/allow-listing on the header value if it must be used outside of parameter binding.",
          "Use static analysis/regression tests to ensure all user-supplied data reaching SQL sinks are passed exclusively via bound parameters."
        ]
      }
    },
