      "analysis": {
        "exploitability_evidence": "Source: request.getHeader(\"BenchmarkTest01967\") is read into 'param', URL-decoded (java.net.URLDecoder.decode), then passed to doSomething(request, param) whose body is not shown but based on standard Benchmark test harness this is a pass-through wrapper that returns the tainted value as 'bar'. 'bar' is then directly concatenated into the SQL string: \"...PASSWORD='\" + bar + \"'\" with no escaping/parameterization. This concatenated string is passed as the raw SQL text to DatabaseHelper.JDBCtemplate.queryForObject(sql, new Object[] {}, String.class) \u2014 the empty Object[] confirms no bind parameters are used, so 'sql' is executed as literal SQL. No sanitizer, allow-list, or parameterized-query mechanism is applied to 'bar' before it reaches the sink; URL-decoding is not a SQL sanitizer.",
        "verdict": "True Positive",
        "confidence": "medium",
        "severity_assessment": "high",
        "root_cause": "User-controlled HTTP header value is decoded and concatenated directly into a SQL query string that is executed via a JDBC template call without using parameterized/prepared statement bind variables.",
        "impact": "An attacker can inject arbitrary SQL via the 'BenchmarkTest01967' HTTP header, potentially bypassing authentication, exfiltrating data from the USERS table, or manipulating the query logic (classic SQL injection).",
        "remediation": [
          "Use parameterized queries/prepared statements with bind variables instead of string concatenation (e.g., pass 'bar' as a query parameter to queryForObject).",
          "Validate/allow-list the header value format if it is expected to be a simple username/password token.",
          "Avoid constructing SQL from untrusted header/request data; treat all HTTP request data (headers, params) as untrusted input requiring the same protections."
        ]
      }
