        "exploitability_evidence": "Source: request.getHeader(\"BenchmarkTest01974\") -> URL-decoded into 'param' -> passed to doSomething(request, param) (body not shown, but used only to build 'bar'). 'bar' is then concatenated into an XPath expression string (line 67) used to query the local employees.xml file. The sink flagged at line 77 is response.getWriter().println(value.getTextContent() + \"<br/>\") \u2014 this writes the *text content of XML nodes matched by the XPath query*, not the tainted 'bar' value itself. The XML data source (employees.xml) is a fixed classpath resource loaded via Utils.getFileFromClasspath, not attacker-supplied content. The only place 'bar' is written directly to the response is in the catch block, where it is explicitly passed through ESAPI.encoder().encodeForHTML(bar) before being printed \u2014 an adequate encoder for an HTML sink. Therefore, no attacker-controlled string reaches response.getWriter() unescaped on any reachable path; the scanner appears to have flagged the method purely because a tainted variable exists in scope and a writer call appears nearby, not because tainted data actually flows into that specific writer call.",
