        "exploitability_evidence": "Attacker-controlled source: an arbitrary HTTP header name (request.getHeaderNames()) is captured into 'param' when it's not a common header. This untrusted string is passed to doSomething(request, param) (body not shown) whose return value 'bar' is used directly as the second argument to `new java.io.File(Utils.TESTFILES_DIR, bar)` at line 63-64 \u2014 the flagged sink. There is no path-traversal-specific sanitization (no canonicalization, no '..' filtering, no FilenameUtils.getName()) applied to 'bar' before it's used to construct the File object. The only encoding present, ESAPI encodeForHTML(), is applied afterward purely to the string used in the HTTP response body for XSS protection, and does not affect or sanitize the file path used to construct 'fileTarget'. Since header names are fully attacker-controlled and can contain '../' sequences, and no adequate sanitizer intercepts the value before it reaches the File constructor, this is exploitable for path traversal (e.g., discovering existence of arbitrary files outside TESTFILES_DIR via the 'file exists' response behavior).",
