      "analysis": {
        "exploitability_evidence": "Source: attacker-controlled HTTP header name obtained via request.getHeaderNames()/getHeaders(name), assigned to 'param'. This value is passed to doSomething(request, param) (helper body not shown, but no other transformation is applied to it) whose return value 'bar' is directly concatenated: fileName = Utils.TESTFILES_DIR + bar. This fileName is passed unmodified into 'new java.io.File(fileName)' and then opened via new java.io.FileInputStream(...) \u2014 the flagged sink. The only sanitization present, ESAPI encoder().encodeForHTML(fileName), is applied afterward only when writing the file path back into the HTTP response body for display; it is an HTML output encoder and provides no protection against path traversal characters ('../', absolute paths, etc.) reaching the file-open sink. No path canonicalization, whitelist check, or FilenameUtils.getName()-style sanitization exists on this path.",
        "verdict": "True Positive",
        "confidence": "medium",
        "severity_assessment": "high",
        "root_cause": "User-controlled input (an arbitrary HTTP header name) is concatenated into a file path with no path traversal sanitization before being used to open a file via FileInputStream.",
        "impact": "An attacker can supply a crafted header name containing '../' sequences to escape the intended TESTFILES_DIR and read arbitrary files accessible to the server process, leading to disclosure of sensitive files (e.g., configuration files, credentials, source code).",
        "remediation": [
          "Canonicalize the resulting File path (getCanonicalPath()) and verify it still resides within the intended base directory before opening it.",
          "Use org.apache.commons.io.FilenameUtils.getName(...) or similar to strip any directory components from user-supplied input before building the file path.",
          "Maintain and validate against a whitelist of allowed file names rather than trusting arbitrary user input for path construction.",
          "Reject any input containing '..' or path separator characters before use in file operations."
        ]
      }
