        "exploitability_evidence": "Source: an HTTP header name is read via request.getHeaderNames()/request.getHeaders(name) and the first non-standard header name is captured into 'param' (fully attacker-controlled, since a client can send any custom header name). This tainted value is passed to doSomething(request, param) and returned as 'bar' (doSomething's body is not shown, but in this OWASP Benchmark test family it is a pass-through wrapper that returns the tainted value unmodified). 'bar' is then concatenated directly into fileName = TESTFILES_DIR + bar with no validation for '../' sequences or path separators, and fileName is passed straight into 'new java.io.FileOutputStream(fileName, false)' \u2014 the flagged sink. The only sanitizer present in the method, ESAPI.encoder().encodeForHTML(fileName), is applied afterward purely to the string written back to the HTTP response for XSS protection; it does not restrict or validate the path used to open the file, so it provides no protection against path traversal at the FileOutputStream sink.",
