        "confidence": "medium",
        "severity_assessment": "high",
        "root_cause": "User-controlled input (an arbitrary HTTP header name) is concatenated into a file path without validating for directory traversal sequences ('../') or restricting to a filename component, then used directly to open a file for writing.",
        "impact": "An attacker who can set a custom HTTP header name (trivial for any HTTP client) can inject '../' sequences to escape the intended TESTFILES_DIR and write/overwrite arbitrary files on the filesystem, potentially leading to remote code execution (e.g., overwriting web-accessible or config files), data corruption, or denial of service.",
        "remediation": [
          "Never derive file paths directly from user input such as header names; use a fixed allow-list or map the input to a known safe internal identifier.",
          "If a filename must be derived from user input, canonicalize the resulting path (File.getCanonicalPath()) and verify it resides within the intended base directory before use.",
          "Use org.apache.commons.io.FilenameUtils.getName(...) to strip any directory components from the user-supplied value before building the path.",
          "Reject any input containing path traversal sequences ('..', '/', '\\\\') before constructing the file path."
        ]
      }
