        "exploitability_evidence": "Source: request.getHeaderNames()/getHeaders(name) \u2014 the loop grabs the name of the first non-standard HTTP header, which is fully attacker-controlled (an attacker can send any custom header name). This value is stored in 'param' and passed to 'bar = doSomething(request, param)'. The doSomething() body is not shown in this snippet/'Called project methods', but per the standard OWASP Benchmark test pattern this helper is a simple pass-through that returns the tainted parameter unchanged, preserving the taint. 'bar' is then concatenated directly into 'fileName = Utils.TESTFILES_DIR + bar' and passed unmodified to 'new java.io.FileOutputStream(fileName)' at line 82 \u2014 the flagged sink. No path canonicalization, whitelisting, or use of FilenameUtils.getName()/getCanonicalPath() checks against a base directory occurs before the file is opened for writing. The only sanitizer present, ESAPI encodeForHTML(fileName), is applied afterward purely to safely render the fileName string in the HTTP response body (an HTML-encoding sanitizer) \u2014 it does not affect the fileName argument already passed to FileOutputStream and provides no protection against '../' path traversal sequences in a file-system path.",
        "verdict": "True Positive",
        "confidence": "medium"
      }
    },
