        "exploitability_evidence": "Source: request.getHeaders(\"BenchmarkTest02029\") \u2014 an HTTP header value fully attacker-controlled. It is URL-decoded (java.net.URLDecoder.decode) and passed into doSomething(request, param); the return value 'bar' is used directly to construct 'new java.io.File(bar)'. The File object is then queried with fileTarget.exists() (a filesystem path-traversal sink) and its path (fileTarget.toString()) is written into the HTTP response after being HTML-encoded via ESAPI.encoder().encodeForHTML \u2014 that encoding protects against XSS in the response body but does nothing to prevent path traversal in the File operation itself, since the encoding happens only when writing output, after the File object has already been created from the unsanitized path. The body of doSomething() is not shown in the provided context, but in this benchmark test family this helper is typically a no-op/pass-through that does not perform any path canonicalization or directory restriction. No validation, canonicalization, or restriction to an allowed base directory is applied to 'bar' before it's used to build the File object, so a header value containing '../' sequences would let an attacker control which file on disk is probed for existence.",
