      "analysis": {
        "exploitability_evidence": "Source: request.getHeaders(\"BenchmarkTest02087\") retrieves an attacker-controlled HTTP header value into 'param'. It is URL-decoded (no security sanitization, just decoding) and passed to doSomething(request, param) which returns 'bar' (helper body not shown, but per OWASP Benchmark's standard test pattern this is a pass-through/identity function with no sanitization). 'bar' is then directly concatenated into the SQL string: \"...PASSWORD='\" + bar + \"'\". Although a PreparedStatement is created, the query text itself already contains the tainted, unescaped value baked into the SQL string via string concatenation before prepareStatement is called; only a single '?' placeholder (for USERNAME) is bound via setString(1,\"foo\"), while the PASSWORD value is never parameterized. This is the classic PreparedStatement-with-string-concatenation antipattern, which does not protect against SQL injection.",
        "verdict": "True Positive",
        "confidence": "medium",
        "severity_assessment": "high",
        "root_cause": "User-controlled header value flows unsanitized into a SQL query string that is built via string concatenation instead of using a parameterized placeholder for that value; the PreparedStatement is prepared with the tainted string embedded already, defeating the purpose of parameterization.",
        "impact": "An attacker who controls the 'BenchmarkTest02087' HTTP header can inject arbitrary SQL into the PASSWORD clause, potentially bypassing authentication, exfiltrating data, or modifying/deleting database records.",
        "remediation": [
          "Use a second '?' placeholder for the PASSWORD value and bind it via statement.setString(2, bar) instead of concatenating it into the SQL string.",
          "Never build SQL text via string concatenation of user input, even when using PreparedStatement.",
          "Apply strict allow-list validation or escaping if concatenation cannot be avoided, though parameterization is strongly preferred.",
          "Audit the doSomething() helper to ensure it does not silently pass through unsanitized input treated as safe."
        ]
      }
    },
