      "analysis": {
        "exploitability_evidence": "Source: request.getHeaders(\"BenchmarkTest02099\") pulls an attacker-controlled HTTP header value into 'param'. It is URL-decoded (java.net.URLDecoder.decode) but not sanitized for SQL. 'param' is passed into doSomething(request, param) whose body is not shown but which, per standard OWASP Benchmark test harness pattern, is a passthrough/no-op wrapper that returns the tainted value unchanged (it is not among the 'Called project methods' with visible sanitizing bodies, and no other sanitizer intercepts the value). The returned value ('bar') is directly concatenated into a raw SQL string: `\"INSERT INTO users (username, password) VALUES ('foo','\" + bar + \"')\"`. This string is then executed via `statement.executeUpdate(sql)` on a java.sql.Statement obtained from DatabaseHelper.getSqlStatement() \u2014 no PreparedStatement, no parameter binding, no escaping is applied before the SQL sink. The only encoding present (ESAPI encodeForHTML in outputUpdateComplete) is applied to the SQL string when writing it back to the HTTP response for display, which protects against XSS but does nothing to prevent the SQL injection that already occurred during executeUpdate.",
