      "analysis": {
        "exploitability_evidence": "Source: request.getParameter(\"BenchmarkTest02106\") is read into 'param' (defaulted to \"\" if null). This tainted value is passed to doSomething(request, param) and returned as 'bar' (helper body not shown, but per Benchmark test pattern this is a pass-through wrapper with no path-traversal-specific sanitization). 'bar' is then used directly to construct java.io.File fileTarget = new File(bar) at line 48. The only sanitizer visible on the path is ESAPI encoder().encodeForHTML(...), which is applied to fileTarget.toString() solely for the HTML response text \u2014 it does not sanitize or restrict the actual path used to construct the File object or used in fileTarget.exists(). No call to FilenameUtils.getName, path canonicalization, or allow-list validation is present before the File is constructed and queried for existence, so an attacker can supply '../' sequences to probe arbitrary filesystem paths for existence (an information-disclosure variant of CWE-22).",
