        "confidence": "medium",
        "severity_assessment": "medium",
        "root_cause": "User-supplied HTTP parameter is passed through an intermediary helper method and used unsanitized to build a java.io.File, with the only encoding applied being HTML output encoding (irrelevant to path traversal) rather than path canonicalization/allow-listing.",
        "impact": "An attacker can manipulate the file path parameter (e.g., using '../' sequences) to probe the existence of arbitrary files on the server filesystem, leading to information disclosure about the server's directory structure and potentially aiding further attacks.",
        "remediation": [
          "Canonicalize the resulting File path (getCanonicalPath) and verify it resides within an expected base directory before use.",
          "Use org.apache.commons.io.FilenameUtils.getName(...) to strip directory components from user input before constructing the File object.",
          "Apply an allow-list validation on the parameter (e.g., restrict to expected filename patterns) instead of relying on output encoding.",
          "Avoid exposing file-existence checks based on raw user input; if necessary, map user input to a fixed set of permitted file identifiers."
        ]
      }
