        "confidence": "medium",
        "severity_assessment": "high",
        "root_cause": "User-controlled request parameter flows through an unsanitizing helper method into a file path that is concatenated with a base directory and used directly to open a file for writing, without validating or normalizing the path to prevent directory traversal sequences ('../').",
        "impact": "An attacker could supply a value like '../../../../etc/passwd' or similar traversal sequences in the request parameter to write to arbitrary files outside the intended TESTFILES_DIR, potentially overwriting critical files, planting malicious files, or causing denial of service.",
        "remediation": [
          "Canonicalize the resulting file path (e.g., via File.getCanonicalPath()) and verify it resides within the intended base directory before using it.",
          "Use FilenameUtils.getName(bar) or similar to strip any directory components from the user-supplied input before concatenation.",
          "Maintain an allow-list of permitted file names/extensions rather than trusting user input.",
          "Reject any input containing path traversal sequences ('..', absolute path prefixes, etc.) before use in file operations."
