        "confidence": "medium",
        "severity_assessment": "medium",
        "root_cause": "User-supplied HTTP parameter is written directly to the HTTP response body via a raw PrintWriter (out.format/out.write) without any HTML/output encoding, bypassing template-based auto-escaping.",
        "impact": "An attacker can craft a request parameter containing HTML/JavaScript payload that gets reflected unescaped in the response, resulting in reflected Cross-Site Scripting (XSS) that can execute arbitrary script in a victim's browser, leading to session hijacking, credential theft, or defacement.",
        "remediation": [
          "HTML-encode the parameter value before writing it to the response (e.g., use OWASP ESAPI Encoder.encodeForHTML() or Apache Commons Text StringEscapeUtils.escapeHtml4()).",
          "Avoid writing directly to the raw PrintWriter for dynamic content; use a templating engine (JSP/JSF/Thymeleaf) that auto-escapes output.",
          "Set the X-XSS-Protection header appropriately and adopt a strict Content-Security-Policy as defense in depth.",
          "Validate/allow-list the expected format of the 'BenchmarkTest02122' parameter if it should only contain limited characters."
