        "exploitability_evidence": "Attacker-controlled source: request.getParameter(\"BenchmarkTest02137\") assigned to 'param'. This is passed to doSomething(request, param) whose result 'bar' is concatenated directly into a shell command string 'echo ' + bar, which is added to argList and executed via ProcessBuilder with 'sh -c' (or 'cmd.exe /c' on Windows). No validation, whitelist check, or shell-metacharacter escaping is applied to 'bar' before it reaches the ProcessBuilder sink. The doSomething() helper's body is not shown, but no sanitizer/encoder is visible on the path from param to bar to the command string, and the sink is executed via a shell interpreter, meaning shell metacharacters (;, |, &&, $(), etc.) in the parameter would be interpreted by 'sh -c'.",
        "verdict": "True Positive",
        "confidence": "medium",
        "severity_assessment": "high",
        "root_cause": "User-supplied HTTP parameter is concatenated into a shell command string ('echo ' + bar) that is executed via ProcessBuilder invoking a shell ('sh -c' / 'cmd.exe /c') without any input validation, whitelisting, or escaping of shell metacharacters.",
        "impact": "An attacker can inject arbitrary OS commands or shell metacharacters into the 'BenchmarkTest02137' parameter, leading to arbitrary command execution on the server with the privileges of the web application process, potentially resulting in full system compromise, data exfiltration, or denial of service.",
        "remediation": [
          "Avoid invoking a shell (sh -c / cmd.exe /c) with user-supplied data; instead call the target program directly with an argument array so the OS does not perform shell interpretation.",
          "Validate the input against a strict allow-list of expected characters/values before using it in any command construction.",
          "If shell invocation cannot be avoided, use proper escaping/quoting libraries specific to the target shell, and reject any input containing shell metacharacters.",
          "Consider using safer APIs that do not involve OS command execution to accomplish the required functionality."
        ]
      }
    },
