        "exploitability_evidence": "Source: request.getParameter(\"BenchmarkTest02137\") -> param (attacker-controlled). It flows into doSomething(request, param) whose body is not shown, but the OWASP Benchmark test harness in this pattern uses doSomething purely as a pass-through/wrapper (no shell-specific escaping is applied anywhere in the visible code). The result 'bar' is directly concatenated into a shell command string: argList.add(\"echo \" + bar), which is passed to ProcessBuilder via pb.command(argList) and executed with pb.start(). No sanitizer, allow-list validation, or shell-metacharacter escaping is applied to 'bar' before it reaches the ProcessBuilder sink; the only downstream encoding (ESAPI encodeForHTML in Utils.printOSCommandResults) is applied to command *output* for HTML display, not to the command input, and is irrelevant to the injection sink.",
        "verdict": "True Positive",
        "confidence": "medium",
        "severity_assessment": "high",
        "root_cause": "User-supplied HTTP parameter is concatenated directly into a shell command string ('echo ' + bar) that is executed via ProcessBuilder with a shell interpreter (sh -c / cmd.exe /c), without any validation, allow-listing, or escaping of shell metacharacters.",
        "impact": "An attacker can inject arbitrary OS commands or shell metacharacters (e.g., ; | & $() ) via the BenchmarkTest02137 parameter, leading to arbitrary command execution on the server with the privileges of the web application process.",
        "remediation": [
          "Avoid invoking a shell interpreter for user-influenced strings; use ProcessBuilder with a fixed executable and pass user input as a discrete argument (not concatenated into a shell command line).",
          "If shell invocation is unavoidable, strictly validate/allow-list the input (e.g., alphanumeric only) before use.",
          "Use library-level escaping specific to the target shell if concatenation cannot be avoided.",
          "Run the subprocess with the least privileges necessary and apply OS-level sandboxing."
        ]
      }
    },
