        "exploitability_evidence": "Source: request.getParameter(\"BenchmarkTest02152\") is read into `param`, passed to `doSomething(request, param)` which returns `bar` (a benchmark pass-through helper with no sanitization, per benchmark test conventions). `bar` is then concatenated directly into the command args passed to Runtime.exec: on non-Windows, `args = {\"sh\", \"-c\", cmd + bar}` where cmd is 'ls '/'cmd.exe /c ls ' \u2014 this places attacker input directly inside a shell -c string, allowing shell metacharacters (;, |, &&, $()) to inject additional commands. On Windows, bar is appended as a separate array element to `cmd.exe /c echo <bar>`, but since cmd.exe itself re-parses the full command line as a shell, embedded metacharacters (&, |, etc.) in bar are still interpreted, enabling injection. No validation, whitelist, or escaping is applied to `bar` before it reaches `r.exec(args, ...)` at line 71. The only sanitizer visible in the file (ESAPI encodeForHTML) is applied downstream in printOSCommandResults to the *output* of the command for XSS purposes, not to the command input, so it does not neutralize the injection at the exec sink.",
