      "analysis": {
        "exploitability_evidence": "The flagged sink at line 55 is `response.getWriter().println(\"Your results are: \" + String.valueOf(results));`. Tracing 'results': it is a `Long` returned by `queryForObject(sql, Long.class)`, i.e., a numeric database result, not the raw user-supplied `param`/`bar` string. Although `bar` (from `request.getParameter(\"BenchmarkTest02180\")` via `doSomething`) is concatenated unsanitized into the SQL string, that string is only used as the SQL query text and, in this branch, is never written back to the response \u2014 only the resulting Long value is written. `String.valueOf(Long)` can only ever produce digits/minus sign, so no HTML/JS payload can reach the writer through this specific sink. The other println calls in the catch blocks either use `ESAPI.encoder().encodeForHTML(sql)` (properly encoded) or print static/literal text, so they are not exploitable either. No attacker-controlled string data reaches `getWriter().println()` unescaped anywhere in this method.",
        "verdict": "False Positive",
        "confidence": "high",
        "severity_assessment": "low",
        "root_cause": "The value written to the response writer at the flagged line is a numeric Long derived from a database query result, not attacker-controlled string data; therefore no XSS payload can be injected via this sink even though the underlying SQL query construction is separately vulnerable to SQL injection.",
        "impact": "None for XSS via this specific sink; the numeric output cannot carry an XSS payload. (Note: the SQL string concatenation with unsanitized `bar` is a genuine SQL injection risk, but that is a different CWE not covered by this finding.)",
        "remediation": [
          "No action needed for XSS at this line since output is strictly numeric.",
          "Separately, address the SQL injection risk by using parameterized queries/prepared statements instead of string concatenation for the SQL query.",
          "If future changes cause raw string data to be written via getWriter(), apply proper output encoding (e.g., ESAPI encodeForHTML) as already done in the catch block."
        ]
      }
