      "analysis": {
        "exploitability_evidence": "Source: request.getParameterMap().get(\"BenchmarkTest02197\") - fully attacker-controlled HTTP parameter, assigned to 'param'. This flows through 'bar = doSomething(request, param)' (helper body not shown/provided, and OWASP Benchmark's doSomething methods are typically trivial pass-throughs or perform unrelated transformations, not path sanitization). 'bar' is then used directly as the child path in 'new File(new File(TESTFILES_DIR), bar)' at the flagged sink (lines 52-54), with no validation against '../' sequences, no canonicalization/normalization check, and no allow-list of filenames. The only sanitizer present, ESAPI's encodeForHTML(), is applied later to fileTarget.toString() purely for safe HTML output in the response and does not affect the File object construction or the filesystem lookup - it does not mitigate path traversal on the sink itself. Thus the File object can be constructed with attacker-supplied '../../' sequences to escape TESTFILES_DIR, and fileTarget.exists() will reflect the traversal outcome, confirming exploitability (at least for a file-existence oracle) even though the file's contents are not read here.",
        "verdict": "True Positive",
        "confidence": "medium",
        "severity_assessment": "medium",
        "root_cause": "User-supplied HTTP parameter is used to construct a java.io.File path without any path traversal-specific sanitization (no normalization, no rejection of '../' sequences, no allow-listing), only an HTML output encoder (irrelevant to filesystem path safety) is applied further downstream.",
        "impact": "An attacker can manipulate the 'BenchmarkTest02197' parameter to include directory traversal sequences (e.g., '../../etc/passwd') causing the application to reference or probe files outside the intended TESTFILES_DIR directory, potentially disclosing file existence, and if the resulting File object were later opened/read elsewhere, arbitrary file disclosure could occur.",
        "remediation": [
          "Canonicalize the resulting File path (getCanonicalPath()) and verify it is still within the intended base directory (TESTFILES_DIR) before use.",
          "Reject or strip path traversal sequences ('../', absolute path prefixes) from the user-supplied filename before constructing the File object.",
          "Use org.apache.commons.io.FilenameUtils.getName(...) to extract only the filename component, discarding any directory traversal components.",
          "Prefer a safe API that resolves against a fixed base directory and rejects any resolved path escaping that base (e.g., java.nio.file.Path.resolve + normalize + startsWith check)."
        ]
      }
    },
