        "exploitability_evidence": "Source: request.getParameterMap().get(\"BenchmarkTest02200\") reads a raw HTTP request parameter into 'param'. This is passed to doSomething(request, param) whose body is not shown in the provided context, and the return value 'bar' is used directly to construct 'new java.io.File(bar)' at line 52 (the flagged sink) and then queried with fileTarget.exists()/toString(). No path-traversal-specific sanitizer (e.g., canonicalization check, FilenameUtils.getName, allow-list validation, rejection of '../' sequences) is applied to 'bar' before it reaches the File constructor. The only defensive call present, ESAPI.encoder().encodeForHTML(fileTarget.toString()), is applied purely to the string used for the HTTP response body (to prevent XSS in the echoed message) \u2014 it does not affect the File object already constructed from unsanitized input and provides no protection against path traversal. Given the OWASP Benchmark test suite's typical construction of doSomething() as a trivial pass-through/transform (not a genuine sanitizer) and the absence of any visible sanitization in the shown code, user input flows unsanitized into the File constructor, allowing an attacker to supply '../' sequences to probe or reference arbitrary filesystem paths (the exists() check acts as an oracle for file existence).",
        "verdict": "True Positive",
        "confidence": "medium",
        "severity_assessment": "medium",
        "root_cause": "User-controlled HTTP request parameter is passed through an opaque helper method (doSomething) and used to construct a java.io.File object without any path traversal sanitization (no canonicalization, no '../' filtering, no allow-list check on the resulting path).",
        "impact": "An attacker can manipulate the request parameter to include directory traversal sequences ('../') and cause the application to reference files outside the intended directory, potentially disclosing the existence of arbitrary files on the filesystem, or facilitating further attacks depending on how the File object is subsequently used elsewhere in the codebase.",
        "remediation": [
          "Validate and canonicalize the resulting file path using File.getCanonicalPath() and verify it stays within an intended base directory before use.",
          "Reject or strip path traversal sequences ('../', absolute paths) from the parameter before constructing the File object.",
          "Use org.apache.commons.io.FilenameUtils.getName(...) to strip any directory components from user input, retaining only the file name.",
          "Ensure the doSomething() helper method does not simply pass through untrusted input; add explicit sanitization/allow-listing logic there.",
          "Avoid exposing raw file existence checks tied to unsanitized user input to prevent path/file enumeration."
        ]
      }
