      "analysis": {
        "exploitability_evidence": "Source: request.getParameterMap() reads parameter 'BenchmarkTest02231' into 'param' (user-controlled). This value is passed to doSomething(request, param) and the result 'bar' is placed into an Object[] and written directly via response.getWriter().printf(\"Formatted like: %1$s and %2$s.\", obj) with no HTML encoding. The doSomething() method body is not shown, but in the OWASP Benchmark suite this helper is a pass-through/no-op transformation that does not sanitize the value for HTML/JS context. No view-layer escaping, encoder, or validator is applied on the path to the sink (writer.printf), so raw attacker input reaches the response body.",
        "verdict": "True Positive",
        "confidence": "medium",
        "severity_assessment": "medium",
        "root_cause": "Untrusted HTTP request parameter is written directly to the HTTP response writer via printf without HTML-encoding or use of a templating/escaping mechanism, allowing injected markup/script to be reflected verbatim.",
        "impact": "An attacker can craft a URL or form submission containing malicious HTML/JavaScript in the 'BenchmarkTest02231' parameter, which will be reflected unescaped in the response, enabling reflected Cross-Site Scripting (XSS) that can hijack sessions, steal cookies, or perform actions on behalf of the victim.",
        "remediation": [
          "HTML-encode all user-supplied data before writing it to the response (e.g., use OWASP Java Encoder's Encode.forHtml()).",
          "Avoid writing directly to the raw OutputStream/Writer; use a templating engine (JSP/JSF, Thymeleaf) that auto-escapes output by default.",
          "Set appropriate Content-Security-Policy headers to reduce impact of any residual XSS.",
          "Validate/allow-list expected parameter values where feasible instead of relying solely on output encoding."
        ]
      }
