      "analysis": {
        "exploitability_evidence": "Source: request.getParameterMap().get(\"BenchmarkTest02252\") supplies attacker-controlled 'param'. This is passed through doSomething(request, param) (body not shown but is the standard OWASP Benchmark pass-through helper) into 'bar', which is directly concatenated into the shell command: on non-Windows, cmd = Utils.getOSCommandString(\"ls \") returns \"ls \", then args = {\"sh\",\"-c\", cmd + bar} \u2014 so bar is appended raw to the '-c' argument executed by /bin/sh. Since -c string is interpreted by the shell, any shell metacharacters in bar (;, |, `, $(), etc.) are executed. No validation, whitelist, or encoding is applied to 'param'/'bar' before reaching Runtime.exec(args, ...). The Windows branch similarly places bar unescaped as a separate array element after 'echo', which is less exploitable via exec array form, but the sh -c concatenation path is a direct, unmitigated command injection sink.",
