      "analysis": {
        "exploitability_evidence": "Source: request.getParameterMap().get(\"BenchmarkTest02264\")[0] assigned to 'param' - fully attacker-controlled HTTP parameter. This value is passed into doSomething(request, param) and the return value 'bar' is directly concatenated into the SQL string: sql = \"{call \" + bar + \"}\". This concatenated string is passed unmodified to connection.prepareCall(sql) and executed via statement.executeQuery(). No parameterized placeholders (?) are used, and no validation/encoding/escaping is applied to 'param' or 'bar' anywhere in the shown code before it reaches the SQL sink. The doSomething() method body is not shown in the provided context, but per OWASP Benchmark conventions such helper methods are simple pass-through/obfuscation wrappers (not sanitizers) meant to test taint propagation, and its name gives no indication of any security function. The DatabaseHelper methods shown (getSqlConnection, printResults) perform no input sanitization relevant to SQL construction - printResults only HTML-encodes result output, which is irrelevant to the SQL injection sink. Thus the tainted value flows unsanitized from request to the prepareCall sink.",
