        "confidence": "high",
        "severity_assessment": "high",
        "root_cause": "User-controlled input from the request parameter map is concatenated directly into a SQL query string instead of being passed as a bound parameter, allowing an attacker to inject arbitrary SQL syntax via the PASSWORD field.",
        "impact": "An attacker could manipulate the WHERE clause (e.g., closing the string literal and injecting boolean/UNION-based payloads) to bypass authentication, extract arbitrary data from the USERS table, or potentially execute further SQL operations depending on database permissions.",
        "remediation": [
          "Use a fully parameterized query: bind 'bar' via statement.setString(2, bar) instead of concatenating it into the SQL text.",
          "Ensure doSomething() does not bypass or weaken input handling; treat all HTTP request parameters as untrusted.",
          "Apply strict input validation/allow-listing on the parameter if it must remain part of dynamic query construction.",
          "Adopt a consistent coding standard requiring all SQL statements to use bind variables for user-supplied values."
        ]
      }
