      "analysis": {
        "exploitability_evidence": "The flagged sink at line 60 is response.getWriter().println(\"Your results are: \" + results). Tracing back, 'results' is declared as an Integer at line 57-59, populated by JDBCtemplate.queryForObject(sql, Integer.class) \u2014 a JDBC call whose declared return type is Integer.class, meaning it can only ever hold a numeric value or throw an exception. Although the SQL string itself is built by unsafely concatenating the attacker-controlled 'param'/'bar' (a genuine SQL injection issue), the value actually written to the HTTP response at the flagged sink is not the raw attacker string but a numeric Integer result. Since java.lang.Integer.toString() only ever produces digits (and an optional minus sign), no attacker-supplied HTML/JS characters can reach the response body via this statement. The other catch block does write attacker-influenced content but wraps it in ESAPI encoder().encodeForHTML(sql), an adequate encoder for this sink; that catch's println is not the line flagged here anyway.",
