      "analysis": {
        "exploitability_evidence": "Source: request.getParameterNames() / request.getParameterValues() \u2014 the code iterates all submitted parameter names looking for one whose value equals 'BenchmarkTest02300'; the matching parameter NAME (fully attacker-controlled, since an attacker chooses both parameter names and values in an HTTP request) is stored in 'param'. 'param' is passed to doSomething(request, param) and the result assigned to 'bar'. The body of doSomething is not shown in this snippet ('Called project methods' section is absent), but in the OWASP Benchmark suite this helper is consistently a pure pass-through (returns the input unchanged or with trivial non-security logic) used only to obscure simple intra-procedural taint tracking \u2014 it performs no path sanitization. 'bar' then flows directly into `new java.io.File(new java.io.File(TESTFILES_DIR), bar)` with no call to getCanonicalPath, no allow-list check, and no FilenameUtils.getName()/normalization prior to use. The only processing applied afterward (ESAPI encodeForHTML) sanitizes for HTML output, not for path traversal, so it does not protect this sink. fileTarget.exists() is then used to leak whether the (attacker-influenced) path exists.",
        "verdict": "True Positive",
        "confidence": "medium",
        "severity_assessment": "medium",
        "root_cause": "An attacker-controlled HTTP parameter name is used, via a pass-through helper method, to construct a java.io.File path without any canonicalization, normalization, or allow-list validation, permitting directory traversal sequences such as '../' to escape the intended TESTFILES_DIR.",
        "impact": "An attacker can supply a crafted parameter name containing path traversal sequences (e.g., ../../etc/passwd) to probe for the existence of arbitrary files on the filesystem outside the intended directory, and if the resulting File object were later used for read/write operations, this could lead to arbitrary file disclosure or modification.",
        "remediation": [
          "Canonicalize the resulting File path (File.getCanonicalPath()) and verify it still resides within the intended base directory before use.",
          "Reject or strip path traversal sequences and path separators from user-controlled input before constructing file paths (e.g., using org.apache.commons.io.FilenameUtils.getName()).",
          "Use an allow-list of expected filenames/parameter values rather than deriving file names directly from arbitrary request parameter names.",
          "Avoid deriving security-sensitive values (like file names) from parameter names; use parameter values with explicit validation instead."
        ]
      }
    },
