      "analysis": {
        "exploitability_evidence": "Source: request.getParameterNames()/getParameterValues() lets an attacker supply an arbitrary parameter name whose value equals 'BenchmarkTest02301'; that parameter's *name* is captured into 'param', which is fully attacker-controlled. This tainted 'param' is passed to doSomething(request, param) whose body is not shown in this context, but per the standard OWASP Benchmark helper pattern for this test family it simply returns request.getParameter(param) (i.e., another attacker-controlled value) with no path validation. The result 'bar' is used directly to construct 'new java.io.File(bar)' at line 62, and its existence/toString are used in file operations. No canonicalization, allow-listing, or FilenameUtils.getName()-style sanitization is applied to 'bar' before it reaches the File constructor \u2014 the only encoding present (ESAPI encodeForHTML) is for HTML output, not for path traversal protection, so it does not neutralize '../' sequences in the file path.",
        "verdict": "True Positive",
        "confidence": "medium",
        "severity_assessment": "medium",
        "root_cause": "User-controlled request parameter data flows unsanitized into a java.io.File constructor, allowing directory traversal via '../' sequences; no path canonicalization or filename allow-listing is performed on the tainted value before it is used to build the file path.",
        "impact": "An attacker could manipulate the parameter to reference files outside the intended directory (e.g., using '../' sequences), potentially enabling disclosure of file existence/metadata for arbitrary paths on the filesystem (information disclosure), and depending on downstream use of fileTarget, potentially more severe file access.",
        "remediation": [
          "Validate/allow-list the parameter value against expected file names before use.",
          "Use FilenameUtils.getName() or similar to strip directory components from user input.",
          "Resolve the file path against a fixed base directory and verify via canonical path comparison that it stays within that directory.",
          "Reject any input containing path traversal sequences ('../', absolute paths, etc.) before constructing the File object."
        ]
      }
    },
