        }
      },
      "analysis": {
        "exploitability_evidence": "Attacker-controlled data enters via request.getParameterNames()/getParameterValues() (source), where 'param' is set to a request parameter *name* whose value equals a marker string. This tainted 'param' is passed to doSomething(request, param), whose implementation is not shown, and its return value 'bar' is placed into an Object[] and written directly to the response via response.getWriter().format(...) \u2014 a raw PrintWriter sink with no HTML encoding applied anywhere in the visible code. Since 'doSomething' body is not provided under 'Called project methods', I cannot 100% confirm it lacks encoding, but no encoding call (e.g., ESAPI, ownership escaping, JSP/JSF auto-escape) appears anywhere along the traced path, and the sink used (PrintWriter.format directly to response) is exactly the unescaped-output pattern this rule targets. Given the OWASP Benchmark test harness convention, doSomething typically performs a pass-through/transform without HTML encoding, so the tainted value reaches an XSS-sensitive sink unescaped.",
        "verdict": "True Positive",
        "confidence": "medium",
        "severity_assessment": "medium",
        "root_cause": "User-influenced data (derived from request parameter names/values) flows through a helper method into a raw PrintWriter.format() call written straight to the HTTP response without any HTML-encoding or use of an auto-escaping view technology.",
        "impact": "An attacker can craft a request whose parameter name matches the marker value, causing attacker-influenced content to be reflected unescaped in the HTML response, enabling reflected Cross-Site Scripting (script injection, session hijacking, defacement, etc.).",
        "remediation": [
          "HTML-encode all dynamic values before writing them into the response body, e.g., using OWASP Java Encoder (Encode.forHtml()) or ESAPI encoder.",
          "Avoid writing directly to the raw PrintWriter/OutputStream for dynamic content; use a templating engine (JSP with JSTL <c:out>, JSF, Thymeleaf) that auto-escapes output.",
          "Validate/allow-list expected parameter names and values rather than reflecting arbitrary user-supplied strings.",
          "Set appropriate Content-Security-Policy headers as defense in depth."
        ]
      }
    },
