        "exploitability_evidence": "Source: request.getParameterNames()/getParameterValues() iterates over all HTTP request parameter names/values submitted by the client; when a parameter's value equals \"BenchmarkTest02335\", its parameter NAME is captured into `param` (attacker fully controls both parameter names and values in an HTTP request). `param` is then passed to `doSomething(request, param)` (body not shown, but consistent with the OWASP Benchmark convention where this helper simply returns `request.getParameter(param)`), yielding `bar`, which is fully attacker-controlled. `bar` is concatenated directly into a shell command string: `\"echo \" + bar`, which becomes the third argument to `sh -c` (or `cmd.exe /c` on Windows) via `pb.command(args)` and `pb.start()`. No validation, allow-listing, or shell-metacharacter escaping is applied to `bar` anywhere on this path before it reaches the ProcessBuilder sink, and because the command is executed through a shell interpreter (`sh -c`), shell metacharacters in `bar` (e.g., `; rm -rf /`, `$(...)`, backticks) would be interpreted, enabling OS command injection.",
        "verdict": "True Positive",
        "confidence": "medium"
      }
    },
