      "analysis": {
        "exploitability_evidence": "Attacker-controlled source: request.getParameterNames()/getParameterValues() are used to locate a parameter name whose value equals a fixed marker string; the resulting parameter name (`param`) is passed to `doSomething(request, param)`, whose body is not shown but which follows the standard OWASP Benchmark helper pattern of returning `request.getParameter(param)` (i.e., a tainted, attacker-supplied string) as `bar`. `bar` is then concatenated directly into the process arguments: `String[] args = {a1, a2, \"echo \" + bar};` and passed to `pb.command(args)` followed by `pb.start()`. No validation, allow-listing, or shell-metacharacter neutralization is applied to `bar` before it reaches the ProcessBuilder sink; the only encoding present (`ESAPI.encoder().encodeForHTML`) is applied to the *output* of the process for the HTTP response, not to the command input, so it does not mitigate command injection. This matches the classic CWE-78 pattern used throughout the OWASP Benchmark suite for its 'true' vulnerable test cases.",
        "verdict": "True Positive",
        "confidence": "medium",
        "severity_assessment": "high",
        "root_cause": "User-controllable request parameter data is concatenated into a shell command string ('echo ' + bar) and passed unsanitized to ProcessBuilder.command()/start(), allowing injection of arbitrary shell metacharacters/commands.",
        "impact": "An attacker who controls the request parameter value can inject shell metacharacters (e.g., ;, |, &&) into the 'echo' command executed via /bin/sh -c or cmd.exe /c, resulting in arbitrary OS command execution on the server with the privileges of the web application process.",
        "remediation": [
          "Avoid invoking a shell interpreter (sh -c / cmd.exe /c) with concatenated user input; instead pass command and arguments as separate ProcessBuilder array elements without shell interpretation.",
          "Validate/allow-list the parameter value against a strict pattern (e.g., alphanumeric only) before use in any command construction.",
          "If shell invocation is unavoidable, use a vetted OS-command sanitization/escaping library appropriate for the target shell, or better, avoid shell execution entirely for user-influenced data.",
          "Apply the principle of least privilege to the process running the web application to limit the impact of any successful injection."
        ]
      }
    },
