        "exploitability_evidence": "Source: request.getParameterNames()/getParameterValues() \u2013 the code iterates all request parameter names, and when a value matches the literal 'BenchmarkTest02340' it records the parameter *name* into 'param'. That attacker-influenced name is passed to 'doSomething(request, param)' whose body is not shown, but per the established Benchmark pattern this helper typically returns request.getParameter(param) unchanged, meaning 'bar' carries an attacker-supplied value. 'bar' is then concatenated directly into the OS command array: on Windows it's an argument in args[]; on other OSes it's appended to a shell command string ('cmd + bar') that is executed via 'sh -c', meaning shell metacharacters in bar (e.g. ';', '`', '|') would be interpreted by the shell. No sanitization, whitelist check, or escaping of 'bar' is applied before it reaches Runtime.exec(args, argsEnv) on line 85. ESAPI encoding is applied only to the *output* of the command (in printOSCommandResults) for XSS protection, not to the command construction itself, so it does not mitigate CWE-78.",
