      "analysis": {
        "exploitability_evidence": "Source: HTTP request parameter values/names via request.getParameterNames()/getParameterValues(), used to set 'param', then passed to doSomething(request, param) to produce 'bar' (doSomething's body is not shown, but no exec/eval calls are shown within it, so no additional injection point is confirmed there). At the sink, the code calls Runtime.exec(String[] cmdarray, String[] envp, File dir) \u2014 a 3-argument overload where 'args' (cmdarray, the actual command that gets executed) contains only 'cmd', a FIXED path returned by Utils.getInsecureOSCommandString() (resolved from a classpath resource, not attacker-controlled), while the tainted value 'bar' is placed into 'argsEnv', which Java treats as the envp parameter (environment variable settings for the child process), not as part of the executed command line. Since the actual command invoked (args[0]) is not attacker-influenced, and envp entries are not shell-interpreted as additional command tokens, this specific exec() call does not directly allow an attacker to alter or inject the OS command being run.",
