        "confidence": "medium",
        "severity_assessment": "low",
        "root_cause": "The rule flags any tainted data reaching Runtime.exec, but here the tainted value flows into the envp parameter (process environment variables) of the exec(cmdarray, envp, dir) overload, not into the cmdarray that is actually executed. The executed command path itself is derived from a fixed classpath resource (insecureCmd.sh/.bat) and is not attacker-controlled, so classic OS command injection (attacker altering the command or arguments actually executed) is not achievable through this call.",
        "impact": "If exploitable (e.g., if the invoked script insecurely evaluates its own environment variables), an attacker could influence subprocess behavior; however, this depends on the unseen contents of insecureCmd.sh/.bat and is not demonstrated by the shown code, making the direct command-injection impact from this exec call unconfirmed and unlikely under standard exec/envp semantics.",
        "remediation": [
          "Avoid placing user-controlled data into process environment variables passed to exec(); if necessary, strictly validate/whitelist the value.",
          "Do not rely on external scripts that might read arbitrary environment variables and use them unsafely (e.g., via eval) \u2014 audit insecureCmd.sh/insecureCmd.bat for such patterns.",
          "Prefer ProcessBuilder with a fixed, whitelisted command and argument list, and never pass unsanitized request data into any part of process construction (command, arguments, or environment).",
          "If dynamic behavior is required, use a strict allow-list mapping of expected values instead of passing raw request-derived strings."
        ]
      }
