        "exploitability_evidence": "Source: request.getParameterNames()/getParameterValues() \u2014 the code scans all HTTP parameter names/values and sets `param = name` when a value equals a fixed marker string, so `param` is an attacker-controlled parameter name (attacker chooses both name and value of any request parameter). This tainted `param` is passed to `doSomething(request, param)` and returned as `bar` (doSomething is the standard OWASP Benchmark passthrough helper that returns the tainted value largely unmodified). `bar` is then concatenated with a constant `cmd` string (built via Utils.getOSCommandString, which only appends the fixed literal \"echo\") and passed directly into `Runtime.exec(cmd + bar, argsEnv, ...)`. No validation, whitelist check, or shell-metacharacter encoding is applied to `bar` before it reaches the exec sink \u2014 the only sanitization visible in the file (ESAPI encodeForHTML) is applied to command *output* in printOSCommandResults, not to the tainted input reaching exec. This matches the classic OWASP Benchmark OS command injection pattern.",
