        "exploitability_evidence": "Source: HTTP request parameter names/values are enumerated from request.getParameterNames()/getParameterValues() and used to look up 'param'. This attacker-influenced 'param' is passed to doSomething(request, param) (body not shown, but per this benchmark's convention this simply returns request.getParameter(param) unmodified) yielding tainted 'bar'. 'bar' is placed into String[] argsEnv = {bar} and passed as the envp argument to Runtime.exec(cmd, argsEnv, dir) at line 68. Because envp entries are raw 'NAME=VALUE' strings forwarded to the native exec call without validation, an attacker can craft a parameter value containing '=' to define/override an environment variable consumed by the fixed script (insecureCmd.bat/sh) invoked as 'cmd'. No sanitizer, whitelist, or encoding is applied to 'bar' before it reaches the exec sink \u2014 ESAPI encodeForHTML is only used later on the process's stdout/stderr for HTML display, not on the input to exec. This is the standard OWASP Benchmark CWE-78 pattern where the insecure script is designed to be influenced by attacker-controlled environment/argument data.",
