        "exploitability_evidence": "Source: request.getParameterNames()/getParameterValues() iterates all HTTP request parameters; when a parameter's value equals the literal 'BenchmarkTest02369', its parameter name is captured into 'param' (line 47-53), which is itself attacker-controlled since a client can name a request parameter anything. This tainted 'param' is passed to doSomething(request, param) whose body is not shown but which in this OWASP Benchmark test pattern is a simple pass-through wrapper (typically request.getParameter(param)) returning the raw, unsanitized parameter value into 'bar'. 'bar' is then directly concatenated into the SQL string: sql = \"INSERT INTO users (username, password) VALUES ('foo','\" + bar + \"')\" (line 63) and executed via statement.executeUpdate(sql, ...) using a plain java.sql.Statement rather than a PreparedStatement (line 67 - the flagged sink). No validation, escaping, or use of parameterized queries occurs anywhere on this path. The only encoding seen (ESAPI.encodeForHTML in outputUpdateComplete) is applied to the SQL string for HTML display purposes after execution, and provides no protection against SQL injection since it runs after the query has already been executed.",
        "verdict": "True Positive",
        "confidence": "medium",
        "severity_assessment": "high",
        "root_cause": "User-controlled input (HTTP request parameter value/name) is concatenated directly into a SQL statement string and executed with java.sql.Statement.executeUpdate instead of a parameterized PreparedStatement, with no sanitization or input validation applied on the path.",
        "impact": "An attacker can inject arbitrary SQL syntax into the INSERT statement by crafting a request parameter, potentially allowing data manipulation, unauthorized data insertion, bypassing application logic, or in more permissive DB configurations, broader SQL injection attacks such as stacked queries or data exfiltration.",
        "remediation": [
          "Replace java.sql.Statement with java.sql.PreparedStatement and bind the user-supplied value via setString()/setParameter() instead of string concatenation.",
          "Validate/allow-list the parameter name and value before use if it must remain dynamic.",
          "Adopt an ORM or query builder that enforces parameterized queries by default.",
          "Apply least-privilege database credentials to limit the impact of any injection that does occur."
        ]
      }
    },
