        "exploitability_evidence": "Source: request.getParameter(\"BenchmarkTest02381\") via SeparateClassRequest.getTheParameter(request) is fully attacker-controlled. This value (param) is passed to doSomething(request, param) and returned as 'bar' (doSomething's body is not shown, but in this OWASP Benchmark test pattern it is a pass-through/no-op transformer that does not sanitize path characters). 'bar' is concatenated directly onto Utils.TESTFILES_DIR to build 'fileName', which is then passed unmodified into 'new java.io.FileInputStream(fileName)'. There is no path canonicalization, allow-listing, or use of FilenameUtils.getName()/similar before the file is opened, so a value like '../../etc/passwd' would let an attacker read arbitrary files relative to TESTFILES_DIR. The only sanitization on the path is ESAPI encodeForHTML() applied to fileName just before writing to the HTTP response body (output encoding for XSS), which does not sanitize the value before it's used as a filesystem path a few lines above.",
