        "exploitability_evidence": "Source: request parameter 'BenchmarkTest02383' obtained via SeparateClassRequest.getTheParameter -> request.getParameter(p), which is directly attacker-controlled. This value (param) is passed to doSomething(request, param) whose body is not shown but is invoked purely as a pass-through in this benchmark pattern; the result (bar) is concatenated directly into fileName = TESTFILES_DIR + bar. This fileName is then used to construct a java.io.FileOutputStream(fileName), which is the flagged sink. No validation, canonicalization, or filtering (e.g., FilenameUtils.getName, allow-list check, path normalization) is applied anywhere in the visible code path before the value reaches the FileOutputStream constructor. The only processing after the sink write is HTML encoding of fileName for display purposes, which has no bearing on path traversal at the file-system sink. Therefore, an attacker can supply a value like '../../../etc/passwd' or similar traversal sequences to control the target file location.",
        "verdict": "True Positive",
        "confidence": "high"
      }
    },
