        "severity_assessment": "high",
        "root_cause": "User-supplied HTTP parameter flows unsanitized into Runtime.exec(), and on non-Windows systems it is executed via 'sh -c' allowing shell metacharacter interpretation; no input validation or whitelist is applied to the tainted value before it reaches the OS command sink.",
        "impact": "An attacker can inject arbitrary OS commands by supplying shell metacharacters in the request parameter, potentially leading to remote code execution, data exfiltration, or full system compromise under the privileges of the web application process.",
        "remediation": [
          "Avoid invoking a shell (sh -c / cmd.exe /c) with user-controlled input; use ProcessBuilder with a fixed executable and pass arguments as separate array elements without shell interpretation.",
          "Validate and whitelist the parameter value against an expected set of safe values before use.",
          "If dynamic values are required, strictly encode/escape shell metacharacters or use APIs that do not invoke a shell at all.",
          "Apply the principle of least privilege to the process running command execution to limit blast radius if injection occurs."
        ]
