        "confidence": "medium",
        "severity_assessment": "high",
        "root_cause": "User-supplied HTTP parameter is concatenated into a file path with no path traversal validation, canonicalization, or filename allow-listing before being used to open a file, allowing '../' sequences to escape the intended directory.",
        "impact": "An attacker can supply a parameter value like '../../../etc/passwd' (or platform equivalent) to read arbitrary files accessible to the application process, leading to disclosure of sensitive files, source code, or configuration/credentials.",
        "remediation": [
          "Validate the parameter against an allow-list of expected file names or use FilenameUtils.getName() to strip any directory components before use.",
          "After building the path, canonicalize it (File.getCanonicalPath()) and verify it still resides within the intended TESTFILES_DIR before opening.",
          "Reject any input containing '..', path separators, or null bytes.",
          "Consider using a lookup table mapping request values to fixed, pre-approved file paths instead of building paths from user input directly."
        ]
      }
