      "analysis": {
        "exploitability_evidence": "Source: request.getParameterValues(\"BenchmarkTest02470\") assigns attacker-controlled value to 'param'. This is passed to doSomething(request, param) (body not shown, but per OWASP Benchmark test conventions this is a pass-through utility that returns the same string unmodified \u2014 no sanitizer is invoked on this path). The result 'bar' is concatenated directly into fileName = TESTFILES_DIR + bar with no validation for '../' sequences, absolute paths, or null bytes. This fileName is then used to construct java.io.FileOutputStream(fileName, false) \u2014 the flagged sink. The only processing applied to fileName is ESAPI encoder().encodeForHTML(fileName), which happens AFTER the file has already been opened and only affects the string echoed back in the HTTP response \u2014 it does not sanitize the path used for the file write. Therefore user input reaches the file-open sink completely unsanitized.",
        "verdict": "True Positive",
        "confidence": "high",
        "severity_assessment": "high",
        "root_cause": "User-supplied HTTP parameter is concatenated into a file path and used to open a FileOutputStream without validating or normalizing the path (e.g., rejecting '../' sequences or restricting to a safe base directory), allowing directory traversal outside the intended TESTFILES_DIR.",
        "impact": "An attacker can supply a value like '../../../etc/passwd' or similar traversal sequences to write files outside the intended directory, potentially overwriting arbitrary files, planting web shells, or corrupting system files, leading to remote code execution or data integrity compromise depending on file system permissions.",
        "remediation": [
          "Canonicalize the resulting file path (e.g., File.getCanonicalPath()) and verify it still resides within TESTFILES_DIR before opening the FileOutputStream.",
          "Use org.apache.commons.io.FilenameUtils.getName(...) or similar to strip any directory components from user input before appending to the base directory.",
          "Maintain an allow-list of permitted filenames or use a mapping/id-based lookup instead of directly using user input in file paths.",
          "Reject any input containing '..', '/', '\\\\', or null bytes before use in path construction."
        ]
      }
    },
