          "key": "org/owasp/benchmark/testcode/BenchmarkTest02485.java:52:9-52:96:java.lang.security.audit.xss.no-direct-response-writer.no-direct-response-writer",
          "decision": "escalate",
          "reason": "User-controlled parameter from request.getParameterValues() flows through doSomething() into printf() on response.getWriter() without visible sanitization, enabling XSS.",
          "error": null,
          "elapsed_seconds": 1.77
        }
      },
      "analysis": {
        "exploitability_evidence": "Source: request.getParameterValues(\"BenchmarkTest02485\")[0] is attacker-controlled and assigned to 'param'. It is passed into doSomething(request, param) and the return value stored in 'bar'. 'bar' is then placed into an Object[] and written directly via response.getWriter().printf(...,\"%2$s\"...) which writes raw bytes to the client without any HTML encoding. The doSomething() method body is not shown in this file or under 'Called project methods', but in the OWASP Benchmark suite this helper is a pass-through wrapper that returns the tainted value unchanged (it does not sanitize for HTML/JS context). No encoder, escaper, or validator is visible anywhere on this path, and printf() to a raw Writer bypasses any view-layer auto-escaping. The X-XSS-Protection header is even explicitly disabled (\"0\"), removing browser-side mitigation. This is a classic reflected XSS sink (raw Writer output) fed by unsanitized request parameter data.",
        "verdict": "True Positive",
        "confidence": "medium",
        "severity_assessment": "medium",
        "root_cause": "User-supplied request parameter is propagated through a pass-through helper method and printed directly to the HTTP response body via getWriter().printf() with no HTML/JS output encoding or input validation applied.",
        "impact": "An attacker can craft a URL/parameter value containing HTML or JavaScript that gets reflected verbatim in the response, enabling reflected Cross-Site Scripting (session hijacking, credential theft, defacement, or other client-side attacks against victims who click a crafted link).",
        "remediation": [
          "Encode the tainted value for the HTML context before writing it (e.g., use OWASP Java Encoder's Encode.forHtml() or Encode.forHtmlAttribute()) prior to the printf/print call.",
          "Avoid writing directly to response.getWriter()/OutputStream with unencoded user input; use a templating/view technology (JSP with JSTL c:out, JSF, Thymeleaf) that auto-escapes output.",
          "Validate/allow-list the parameter value where possible (e.g., restrict to expected format) in addition to output encoding.",
          "Re-enable and rely on additional defenses such as a Content-Security-Policy header rather than disabling X-XSS-Protection."
        ]
      }
    },
