        "exploitability_evidence": "Source: request.getParameterValues(\"BenchmarkTest02489\") -> param (attacker-controlled). param is passed unmodified into doSomething(request, param); the return value 'bar' is then used directly as the format string in response.getWriter().printf(bar, obj) at line 52. No HTML encoding, escaping, or output sanitization is applied anywhere in the shown code before the value reaches the raw response Writer. The doSomething() helper body is not shown, but in the OWASP Benchmark suite these helper methods are typically pass-through/identity transformations that do not perform any HTML/XSS sanitization, and no sanitizer call is visible on this path. Because printf writes directly to the HttpServletResponse writer (bypassing any view/template auto-escaping) with an attacker-influenced string, a request containing HTML/script payloads (e.g., <script>...</script>) would be reflected unescaped in the response body.",
