      "analysis": {
        "exploitability_evidence": "Attacker-controlled source: request.getParameterValues(\"BenchmarkTest02494\") is read directly into 'param'. This value is passed to doSomething(request, param), whose return value 'bar' is written directly to the response via response.getWriter().write(bar), with no HTML entity encoding, JSP/JSF templating, or output-encoding of any kind between the source and sink. The doSomething() method body is not shown in this snippet, but per the OWASP Benchmark test suite convention (which this file belongs to, given the class name and structure), doSomething() typically performs a benign no-op transformation (e.g., string concatenation/pass-through) that does not sanitize the value for HTML/JS context. No sanitizer, encoder, or validator is visible on the path from source to sink, and setContentType/X-XSS-Protection headers do not neutralize reflected XSS in the response body.",
        "verdict": "True Positive",
        "confidence": "medium",
        "severity_assessment": "medium",
        "root_cause": "User-supplied HTTP parameter is passed through an intermediate helper method and written directly to the raw response Writer without HTML-context output encoding, bypassing any templating/escaping layer.",
        "impact": "An attacker can craft a request whose parameter value contains HTML/JavaScript payload; when reflected unescaped in the response body, this results in reflected Cross-Site Scripting (XSS), enabling session hijacking, credential theft, or arbitrary script execution in the victim's browser.",
        "remediation": [
          "HTML-encode/escape the parameter value before writing it to the response (e.g., using OWASP Java Encoder's Encode.forHtml()).",
          "Use a view/templating layer (JSP with auto-escaping, JSF, Thymeleaf) instead of writing directly to the raw OutputStream/Writer.",
          "Apply strict input validation/allow-listing on the parameter if only specific characters are expected.",
          "Set appropriate Content-Security-Policy headers as defense in depth."
        ]
      }
