      "analysis": {
        "exploitability_evidence": "Source: HttpServletRequest.getParameterValues(\"BenchmarkTest02510\") -> param (attacker-controlled string). param is passed to doSomething(request, param) whose body is not shown, but following OWASP Benchmark's typical pass-through pattern the returned value 'bar' retains the tainted parameter value. 'bar' is then placed into the argsEnv array (String[] argsEnv = {bar};) which is passed as the environment-parameter to Runtime.getRuntime().exec(args, argsEnv). The command itself (args={cmd}) is a fixed script path obtained via Utils.getInsecureOSCommandString, but the environment array passed to the spawned process is fully attacker-controlled and is not validated, encoded, or whitelisted anywhere on the path. No sanitizer/validator call intercepts 'bar' before it reaches exec(). Passing unsanitized user input as process environment entries is a recognized OS command-injection/environment-injection vector (e.g., manipulating PATH, LD_PRELOAD, or other variables read by the invoked script), so the exec sink is reachable with tainted, unsanitized data.",
        "verdict": "True Positive",
        "confidence": "medium",
        "severity_assessment": "high",
        "root_cause": "User-supplied HTTP parameter flows unsanitized into the environment array (argsEnv) of Runtime.exec(String[] cmdarray, String[] envp) without any validation, whitelist check, or encoding suitable for command/environment injection prevention.",
        "impact": "An attacker can inject arbitrary environment variable values into the spawned process, potentially altering its behavior (e.g., PATH hijacking, library preloading via LD_PRELOAD) to achieve command execution or other malicious effects, leading to command injection or privilege escalation depending on how the invoked script (insecureCmd.sh/.bat) uses its environment.",
        "remediation": [
          "Avoid passing user-controlled data directly into process environment arrays; instead use a fixed, hard-coded environment or strictly whitelist allowed variable names/values.",
          "Validate and encode any user input that must influence subprocess behavior using an allow-list of expected values.",
          "Use ProcessBuilder with explicit environment map manipulation rather than raw String[] envp, and reject any input containing unexpected characters (e.g., '=', newlines, shell metacharacters).",
          "Where possible, avoid invoking OS commands with any user-influenced data; use language-level APIs instead of shelling out."
        ]
      }
    },
