        "exploitability_evidence": "Source: request.getParameterValues(\"BenchmarkTest02514\") -> param -> bar = doSomething(request, param) (body not shown, assumed pass-through per Benchmark test conventions) -> argsEnv = {bar}. Sink call: `Process p = r.exec(cmd, argsEnv);` where cmd is Utils.getInsecureOSCommandString(...) \u2014 a fixed script path resolved from the classpath based on OS name (not tainted). Critically, this call matches the Runtime.exec(String command, String[] envp) overload, meaning argsEnv is used as the *environment variable array* for the spawned process, not as command-line arguments appended to the executed command. The command actually executed is the fixed script path with no user-controlled arguments; the tainted 'bar' value is only placed into the subprocess's environment block. Since bar is a raw string (not formatted as NAME=VALUE), it does not correspond to any named/expected environment variable the invoked script would read, and no shell is invoked to interpret the tainted string as shell metacharacters. Therefore the exec call as used does not pass attacker data into the executed command or its arguments, defeating the classic OS command injection primitive that the rule assumes.",
