      "analysis": {
        "exploitability_evidence": "Source: request.getParameterValues(\"BenchmarkTest02518\") -> param -> bar = doSomething(request, param) (body not shown, assumed to pass tainted value through, as is Benchmark convention). Sink flagged: Runtime.exec(cmd, argsEnv, dir) at line 56. However, examining the actual overload used - Runtime.exec(String command, String[] envp, File dir) - the tainted 'bar' is placed into argsEnv, which is passed as the ENVIRONMENT VARIABLE array (envp), not as part of the command string or as command-line arguments. The command actually executed is 'cmd', which is derived from Utils.getInsecureOSCommandString(), a fixed local script file path (insecureCmd.bat/insecureCmd.sh) unrelated to user input. Because envp entries are supposed to be in 'NAME=VALUE' form and 'bar' lacks an '=' (making it a malformed/no-op environment entry in most JVM implementations), the tainted data does not become part of the executed command line and cannot inject additional OS commands or arguments. The exec() call's command component is fully attacker-independent, so no command injection is achievable via this code path.",
        "verdict": "False Positive",
        "confidence": "medium",
        "severity_assessment": "low",
        "root_cause": "The tainted parameter is passed as an environment-variable array (envp) to Runtime.exec(String, String[], File), not as part of the executed command or its arguments. The actual command executed is a hardcoded script path unrelated to user input, so the sink does not use tainted data as command syntax.",
        "impact": "None in this exact code path \u2014 user input cannot alter the executed program or its arguments; at most a malformed environment variable entry could be set, which does not enable command injection.",
        "remediation": [
          "Confirm intended semantics of the exec() overload used; if command-line arguments were intended, use ProcessBuilder with an explicit argument list rather than passing user data via envp.",
          "If any user-controlled data must influence process execution, use a strict allow-list of validated values.",
          "Avoid exposing raw HTTP parameters to any Runtime.exec/ProcessBuilder call paths even indirectly, to reduce future refactor risk."
        ]
      }
