        "confidence": "medium",
        "severity_assessment": "high",
        "root_cause": "User-supplied HTTP parameter is concatenated directly into a SQL command string (used to build a CallableStatement) instead of being passed as a bound parameter, allowing SQL syntax to be altered by attacker input.",
        "impact": "An attacker can inject arbitrary SQL/stored-procedure call syntax via the 'BenchmarkTest02529' request parameter, potentially leading to unauthorized data access, data modification, or execution of unintended database operations.",
        "remediation": [
          "Use parameterized queries/PreparedStatement or CallableStatement with bind variables (e.g., '{call ?}' pattern is not applicable for stored proc names, so validate the input against a strict allow-list of expected values before use).",
          "If the value must be part of the SQL command syntax (e.g., stored procedure name), validate it against a fixed whitelist of allowed procedure names rather than allowing arbitrary user input.",
          "Ensure the doSomething() helper method does not merely pass through tainted data without validation/sanitization suitable for SQL context.",
          "Adopt static/dynamic testing to confirm no unsanitized concatenation reaches SQL execution APIs."
        ]
      }
