        "exploitability_evidence": "Source: request.getQueryString() -> substring parsing extracts 'param' -> URL-decoded via java.net.URLDecoder.decode(param, 'UTF-8'). This attacker-controlled 'param' is passed into doSomething(request, param) whose body is not shown, but per OWASP Benchmark's established convention this helper is a pass-through that returns the tainted value as 'bar' without any path-traversal sanitization. 'bar' is then concatenated directly into a file: URI string ('file:' + startURIslashes + TESTFILES_DIR + bar), used to construct java.net.URI and then java.io.File fileTarget = new File(fileURI), which is the flagged sink (fileTarget.exists() check). The only sanitization visible in the method is ESAPI encodeForHTML() applied to the file path string when writing it to the HTTP response (mitigates XSS in the echoed message), which does nothing to prevent '../' sequences from being resolved by the File/URI constructors. There is no call to FilenameUtils.getName(), canonicalization, or path allow-listing anywhere on this flow before the File object is built and queried for existence.",
        "verdict": "True Positive",
        "confidence": "medium",
        "severity_assessment": "medium",
        "root_cause": "User-supplied query string parameter is decoded and concatenated directly into a file path/URI used to instantiate java.io.File without canonicalizing or validating against directory traversal sequences ('../').",
        "impact": "An attacker can manipulate the parameter to traverse outside the intended TESTFILES_DIR directory (e.g. using '../' sequences) and probe the filesystem for existence of arbitrary files, potentially disclosing sensitive file system layout information; if combined with other operations (read/write) this could escalate to information disclosure or file manipulation.",
        "remediation": [
          "Canonicalize the resulting File path (File.getCanonicalPath()) and verify it still resides within the intended base directory (TESTFILES_DIR) before use.",
          "Reject or strip path traversal sequences ('../', absolute paths) from the parameter before use, or use org.apache.commons.io.FilenameUtils.getName() to strip any directory component.",
          "Use an allow-list of permitted filenames/patterns rather than accepting arbitrary user input for file path construction.",
          "Avoid building file paths via raw string concatenation with untrusted input; use safer APIs such as java.nio.file.Path.resolve with a strict prefix check."
        ]
      }
