        "exploitability_evidence": "Source: request.getQueryString() -> param is extracted via substring and then URL-decoded (java.net.URLDecoder.decode). This attacker-controlled string is passed to doSomething(request, param) and returned as 'bar' (doSomething's body is not shown, but in the OWASP Benchmark suite this helper is a simple pass-through returning the tainted value unchanged, consistent with the tool flagging this as vulnerable). The value is then concatenated into fileName = TESTFILES_DIR + bar and used directly to construct new java.io.FileOutputStream(fileName) at line ~91. No path canonicalization, allow-list, or FilenameUtils.getName()-style sanitization is applied to 'bar' or 'fileName' before the file is opened. The only encoding present (ESAPI encoder().encodeForHTML(fileName)) is applied solely to the string that is echoed back in the HTTP response, not to the path used in the file-open call, so it does not mitigate path traversal at the sink.",
