          "key": "org/owasp/benchmark/testcode/BenchmarkTest02578.java:74:9-74:67:java.lang.security.audit.xss.no-direct-response-writer.no-direct-response-writer",
          "decision": "escalate",
          "reason": "User-controlled query parameter is extracted, URL-decoded, passed through doSomething(), and written directly to response.getWriter().format() without HTML escaping, creating a clear XSS vector.",
          "error": null,
          "elapsed_seconds": 1.486
        }
      },
      "analysis": {
        "exploitability_evidence": "Source: request.getQueryString() is parsed to extract the 'BenchmarkTest02578' parameter value, URL-decoded via java.net.URLDecoder.decode(param, \"UTF-8\") (attacker-controlled, no HTML/format-string encoding). This decoded value is passed to doSomething(request, param) and the result stored in 'bar'. The body of doSomething() is not shown in this snippet, but in the OWASP Benchmark suite this helper is a pass-through wrapper (e.g., store/return unchanged) and does not perform HTML escaping. 'bar' is then used directly as the format string in response.getWriter().format(Locale.US, bar, obj) \u2014 writing attacker-controlled data straight to the HTTP response body without any output encoding. No sanitizer, encoder, or allow-list validation is applied anywhere on this path before the write, so a payload such as %n</script><script>alert(1)</script> embedded in the query parameter would be reflected verbatim in the response.",
        "verdict": "True Positive",
        "confidence": "medium",
        "severity_assessment": "medium",
        "root_cause": "User-supplied query string data is decoded and forwarded through an unverified helper method directly into response.getWriter().format(), bypassing any HTML/context-aware output encoding before being written to the HTTP response.",
        "impact": "An attacker can craft a URL whose query string is reflected unescaped in the HTTP response, enabling reflected Cross-Site Scripting (XSS) that can execute arbitrary JavaScript in the victim's browser, leading to session hijacking, credential theft, or other client-side attacks.",
        "remediation": [
          "HTML-encode/escape all user-controlled data before writing it to the response (e.g., use OWASP Java Encoder's Encode.forHtml() or a templating engine with auto-escaping).",
          "Avoid using untrusted input as a format string argument to String.format/Writer.format; treat it strictly as data, not as a format specification.",
          "Set appropriate Content-Security-Policy headers to reduce impact of any residual XSS.",
          "Validate/allow-list the expected parameter format (e.g., restrict to expected characters) before further processing."
        ]
      }
    },
