        "exploitability_evidence": "Source: request.getQueryString() (attacker-controlled HTTP query string). It is parsed to extract 'param', URL-decoded (URLDecoder.decode only reverses percent-encoding, it does not neutralize shell metacharacters), then passed to doSomething(request, param) whose result 'bar' is directly concatenated into the OS command: on non-Windows, args = {\"sh\", \"-c\", cmd + bar} where cmd is 'ls '; on Windows, args = {\"cmd.exe\",\"/c\",\"echo \", bar}. This array is passed straight to Runtime.exec(args, argsEnv). Because the non-Windows path invokes 'sh -c' with 'bar' concatenated into a single command string, any shell metacharacters (;, |, &, `, $()) in bar are interpreted by the shell, enabling arbitrary command execution. No whitelist, regex validation, or command-injection-specific escaping/sanitizer is applied to 'param'/'bar' anywhere in the shown code (only URL-decoding and, later, HTML-encoding of *output* via ESAPI, which protects against XSS, not command injection). doSomething's body is not shown, but even in the best case where it passes 'param' through unchanged (consistent with the Benchmark suite's typical wrapper pattern), no sanitization occurs before reaching the exec sink.",
