        "exploitability_evidence": "Source: request.getQueryString() is parsed to extract 'param', which is then URL-decoded (java.net.URLDecoder.decode) \u2014 fully attacker-controlled. This param is passed to doSomething(request, param) whose body is not shown, but the return value 'bar' is directly concatenated into the SQL string: `\"SELECT * from USERS where USERNAME=? and PASSWORD='\" + bar + \"'\"`. This concatenated string is then passed as the SQL text to connection.prepareStatement(sql, ...). Although a PreparedStatement object is created, the parameterization only covers the first '?' (USERNAME, bound to the literal \"foo\"), while the PASSWORD value is embedded directly into the query text via string concatenation before the statement is even prepared \u2014 this defeats the protection normally provided by parameterized queries. No sanitizer, encoder, or input validation is applied to 'bar' before it is concatenated into the SQL string. The only encoding present (ESAPI.encoder().encodeForHTML) is applied later in printResults for HTML output, not for SQL context, so it does not mitigate this SQL sink.",
