      "analysis": {
        "exploitability_evidence": "Source: request.getQueryString() -> substring/URLDecoder.decode produces 'param' (fully attacker-controlled, no filtering of quotes/SQL metacharacters). This is passed into doSomething(request, param) to produce 'bar'; doSomething's body isn't shown in the provided context, but no sanitizer/encoder is applied to 'bar' before it is concatenated: sql = \"INSERT INTO users (username, password) VALUES ('foo','\" + bar + \"')\". This concatenated string is then executed via a raw java.sql.Statement.executeUpdate(sql) rather than a PreparedStatement, which is the flagged sink. There is no use of PreparedStatement with bind parameters, no escaping, and no allow-list validation visible on this path. The only encoding present (ESAPI.encoder().encodeForHTML) is applied afterward for HTML output, not before the SQL execution, so it does not mitigate the injection.",
        "verdict": "True Positive",
        "confidence": "medium",
        "severity_assessment": "high",
        "root_cause": "User-supplied query string parameter is decoded and concatenated directly into a SQL statement string that is executed with a plain Statement object instead of a parameterized PreparedStatement, allowing SQL metacharacters to alter query structure.",
        "impact": "An attacker can inject arbitrary SQL via the request query string parameter, potentially reading, modifying, or deleting data in the users table, bypassing authentication, or in some DB configurations executing further malicious SQL.",
        "remediation": [
          "Use java.sql.PreparedStatement with bind variables instead of building SQL via string concatenation.",
          "Validate/allow-list the expected format of the parameter before use.",
          "Apply the principle of least privilege to the DB account used by the application.",
          "Verify doSomething() does not silently pass through unsanitized input; enforce sanitization/parameterization at the query construction point regardless of upstream processing."
        ]
      }
