          "source_sink_combo": 0.2
        }
      },
      "analysis": {
        "exploitability_evidence": "The value that reaches ProcessBuilder is built from `bar = doSomething(request, param)`, where `param = scr.getTheValue(\"BenchmarkTest02699\")`. The shown body of `SeparateClassRequest.getTheValue` is a hardcoded literal: `return \"bar\";` \u2014 it does not read `request.getParameter(...)` or any other attacker-controlled input despite its request-derived name. Consequently, regardless of what `doSomething` does with this constant, the value concatenated into the ProcessBuilder args (`\"echo \" + bar`) is not attacker-controlled; it is always the fixed string 'bar'. There is no tainted source flowing to the ProcessBuilder sink in the code shown.",
        "verdict": "False Positive",
        "confidence": "high",
        "severity_assessment": "low",
        "root_cause": "The apparent user-input source (SeparateClassRequest.getTheValue) is stubbed to return a hardcoded constant string instead of reading any request parameter, so no attacker-controlled data reaches the ProcessBuilder call despite the string concatenation pattern the scanner flagged.",
        "impact": "None in current form \u2014 since the source is a hardcoded literal, no command injection is possible via this code path. If getTheValue were later changed to actually pull from request parameters, this would become exploitable OS command injection, allowing arbitrary command execution on the host.",
        "remediation": [
          "No immediate action required as the data path is not attacker-controlled in the shown code.",
          "If getTheValue is intended to reflect real user input in production, ensure any user-supplied value used in ProcessBuilder args is validated against a strict allow-list before use.",
          "Avoid constructing shell commands via string concatenation; prefer passing arguments as separate array elements without invoking a shell interpreter.",
          "Add regression tests to confirm request-derived values never reach ProcessBuilder unsanitized if the stub is replaced with real request handling."
        ]
      }
    },
