          "sanitizer_hint": -0.1
        }
      },
      "analysis": {
        "exploitability_evidence": "Sink is Runtime.exec(args, argsEnv, dir) at line 58, where argsEnv={bar}. Tracing 'bar' back: bar = doSomething(request, param), and param = scr.getTheValue(\"BenchmarkTest02713\"). The shown body of SeparateClassRequest.getTheValue is 'return \"bar\";' \u2014 it ignores its input parameter entirely and returns a hardcoded constant string, not any value derived from the HttpServletRequest. Thus 'param' passed into doSomething is a fixed literal ('bar'), not attacker-controlled data. doSomething's own body is not shown, but per the pervasive Benchmark obfuscation pattern it is a simple pass-through/transform of 'param' rather than an independent extraction of request data; even if it also receives 'request', there is no visible evidence in the shown code that it pulls a new tainted value from it. The command itself (args={cmd}) comes from Utils.getInsecureOSCommandString, a fixed script path unrelated to user input. Because the only visible source method returns a hardcoded literal, the taint chain from the HTTP request to the exec sink is broken in the code shown.",
        "verdict": "False Positive",
        "confidence": "medium",
        "severity_assessment": "low",
        "root_cause": "The apparent taint source (SeparateClassRequest.getTheValue) is stubbed to always return the constant string \"bar\" regardless of its input, so the value that eventually reaches Runtime.exec via argsEnv is not derived from attacker-controlled HTTP request data in the code shown.",
        "impact": "If doSomething were shown to reintroduce request-derived data, this could allow injection into environment variables consumed by the executed script; but based on the visible flow, no exploitable command injection is demonstrated.",
        "remediation": [
          "Confirm doSomething's implementation to ensure it does not independently extract raw request parameters and use them in the exec call.",
          "If any user input is intended to reach the process arguments/environment, apply strict input validation/whitelisting before use.",
          "Avoid passing user-influenced strings into Runtime.exec's environment array without validation.",
          "Use ProcessBuilder with a fixed command and validated arguments instead of dynamic environment injection."
        ]
      }
    },
